/* AbiLearn — Science MCQ Bank — 1300 Questions (100 per chapter) */
const SCIENCE_MCQS = {

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════
     CH 1 — CHEMICAL REACTIONS AND EQUATIONS
  ══════════════════════════════════════ */
  1: [
    { q: "Which observation is NOT evidence of a chemical reaction?", opts: ["Evolution of a gas", "Change in colour", "Change in state (e.g. melting)", "Formation of a precipitate"], ans: 2, exp: "Melting/boiling are physical changes — no new substance is formed. Gas evolution, colour change, and precipitate formation indicate new substances and hence chemical reactions." },
    { q: "The correct balanced equation for burning of magnesium is:", opts: ["Mg + O₂ → MgO", "2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO", "Mg + O → MgO", "4Mg + O₂ → 2Mg₂O"], ans: 1, exp: "2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO. Check: Mg 2=2, O 2=2. ✓ Magnesium burns with a brilliant white flame." },
    { q: "CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ is an example of which type of reaction?", opts: ["Decomposition", "Displacement", "Exothermic combination", "Endothermic combination"], ans: 2, exp: "Two reactants combine to form one product (combination). The reaction is highly exothermic — the water hisses and steam is produced." },
    { q: "The thermal decomposition of CaCO₃ is industrially called:", opts: ["Slaking", "Calcination", "Roasting", "Smelting"], ans: 1, exp: "CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. Heating a carbonate/hydroxide ore in limited air to expel CO₂ is calcination. Roasting is heating a sulphide ore in excess air." },
    { q: "Iron nails dipped in CuSO₄ solution turn brown because:", opts: ["CuSO₄ oxidises iron", "Iron displaces copper — Fe is more reactive", "Copper displaces iron", "A double displacement occurs"], ans: 1, exp: "Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu. Iron is higher in the reactivity series, so it displaces copper. The brown deposit is metallic copper." },
    { q: "2FeSO₄ is heated strongly. The residue is:", opts: ["Fe", "FeO", "Fe₂O₃", "FeS"], ans: 2, exp: "2FeSO₄ → Fe₂O₃ + SO₂ + SO₃ on strong heating. The reddish-brown residue is ferric oxide (Fe₂O₃)." },
    { q: "Pb(NO₃)₂(aq) + 2KI(aq) → ? The precipitate formed is:", opts: ["White PbI₂", "Yellow PbI₂", "White KNO₃", "Brown PbO"], ans: 1, exp: "Lead iodide (PbI₂) is a bright yellow insoluble precipitate. This is a double displacement (precipitation) reaction." },
    { q: "2AgCl → 2Ag + Cl₂ when exposed to sunlight. This is:", opts: ["Thermal decomposition", "Electrolytic decomposition", "Photochemical decomposition", "Combination"], ans: 2, exp: "Energy from sunlight (photons) drives the decomposition of silver chloride. Used in black-and-white photography." },
    { q: "In the thermite reaction Fe₂O₃ + 2Al → Al₂O₃ + 2Fe, iron oxide is:", opts: ["Oxidised", "Reduced", "Acts as a catalyst", "Neither oxidised nor reduced"], ans: 1, exp: "Fe₂O₃ loses oxygen (→ Fe), so iron is reduced. Simultaneously Al gains oxygen → Al is oxidised. Al is the reducing agent." },
    { q: "Rancidity of fats can be delayed by flushing food packets with nitrogen because:", opts: ["N₂ reacts with fat to stabilise it", "N₂ is inert and prevents O₂ contact", "N₂ lowers the temperature", "N₂ acts as an antioxidant"], ans: 1, exp: "Rancidity is caused by oxidation of unsaturated fats. Replacing air with inert nitrogen removes the oxygen needed for oxidation." },
    { q: "Which reaction is endothermic?", opts: ["CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂", "Combustion of LPG", "Thermal decomposition of CaCO₃", "Neutralisation of NaOH with HCl"], ans: 2, exp: "CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ requires continuous heating — heat is absorbed, making it endothermic. The other three are exothermic." },
    { q: "In electrolysis of water, H₂ and O₂ are produced in what volume ratio?", opts: ["1 : 2", "2 : 1", "1 : 1", "4 : 1"], ans: 1, exp: "2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂. Volume ratio H₂ : O₂ = 2 : 1 (by the equation stoichiometry and equal molar volumes at same T and P)." },
    { q: "Balancing: P₄ + O₂ → P₂O₅. The coefficient of O₂ is:", opts: ["4", "5", "8", "10"], ans: 1, exp: "P₄ + 5O₂ → 2P₂O₅. Check: P 4=4, O 10=10. ✓" },
    { q: "An oxidising agent in a redox reaction:", opts: ["Gets oxidised itself", "Gets reduced itself", "Supplies hydrogen", "Removes oxygen from another"], ans: 1, exp: "An oxidising agent causes oxidation of another substance and is itself reduced (gains electrons / gains hydrogen / loses oxygen)." },
    { q: "What does the symbol ↓ in a chemical equation indicate?", opts: ["A gas is produced", "A precipitate (insoluble solid) is formed", "The reaction is endothermic", "The reaction is reversible"], ans: 1, exp: "A downward arrow (↓) after a substance indicates it is an insoluble precipitate. An upward arrow (↑) indicates a gaseous product that escapes." },
    { q: "The reducing agent in CuO + H₂ → Cu + H₂O is:", opts: ["CuO", "H₂", "Cu", "H₂O"], ans: 1, exp: "H₂ removes oxygen from CuO, causing reduction of copper. A substance that causes reduction is called a reducing agent; H₂ itself gets oxidised (forms H₂O)." },
    { q: "Green patina on copper statues is mainly:", opts: ["CuO", "Cu₂O", "Basic copper carbonate [CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂]", "CuS"], ans: 2, exp: "Copper reacts with CO₂, O₂ and moisture over time to form a green layer of basic copper carbonate (verdigris). The Statue of Liberty has this green coating." },
    { q: "Balancing: C₃H₈ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O. Coefficient of O₂ is:", opts: ["3", "4", "5", "6"], ans: 2, exp: "C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O. Check: C 3=3, H 8=8, O 10=10. ✓" },
    { q: "Which of the following is a double displacement reaction?", opts: ["Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu", "Na₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + 2NaCl", "2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO", "CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂"], ans: 1, exp: "Na₂SO₄ + BaCl₂: two compounds exchange ions to form BaSO₄ (precipitate) and NaCl. This is a double displacement (metathesis) reaction." },
    { q: "MnO₂ + 4HCl → MnCl₂ + 2H₂O + Cl₂. MnO₂ acts as:", opts: ["Reducing agent", "Oxidising agent", "Catalyst", "Base"], ans: 1, exp: "MnO₂ oxidises HCl (Cl⁻ → Cl₂). It is the oxidising agent and itself gets reduced (Mn⁴⁺ → Mn²⁺)." },
    { q: "When excess CO₂ is passed through lime water, the milky solution first forms then clears because:", opts: ["CaCO₃ is soluble", "Excess CO₂ forms soluble Ca(HCO₃)₂", "Lime water runs out", "CO₂ evaporates"], ans: 1, exp: "CaCO₃ + CO₂ + H₂O → Ca(HCO₃)₂ (soluble, so the milkiness disappears). Heating Ca(HCO₃)₂ reverses the reaction." },
    { q: "The process of slow oxidation of fats and oils giving a rancid smell is called:", opts: ["Corrosion", "Rancidity", "Combustion", "Fermentation"], ans: 1, exp: "Rancidity: unsaturated fatty acids in food react with atmospheric O₂ (and sometimes light/moisture) producing compounds with unpleasant smell and taste." },
    { q: "Antioxidants are added to food to prevent:", opts: ["Bacterial growth", "Rancidity", "Colour change", "Crystallisation"], ans: 1, exp: "Antioxidants (vitamin E, BHA, BHT) inhibit oxidation of fats. They are preferentially oxidised themselves, protecting the food lipids." },
    { q: "The balanced equation for photosynthesis is:", opts: ["CO₂ + H₂O → CH₂O + O₂", "6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂", "6CO + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 3O₂", "6CO₂ + H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + O₂"], ans: 1, exp: "6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂. Verify: C 6=6, H 12=12, O 18=18. ✓" },
    { q: "Decomposition of H₂O₂ in presence of MnO₂ is:", opts: ["Photochemical decomposition", "Thermal decomposition", "Catalytic decomposition", "Electrolytic decomposition"], ans: 2, exp: "2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂ with MnO₂ as catalyst. MnO₂ is not consumed. This is catalytic decomposition." },
    { q: "In 2PbO + C → 2Pb + CO₂, carbon is:", opts: ["Oxidised (reducing agent)", "Reduced (oxidising agent)", "Acts as catalyst", "Neither"], ans: 0, exp: "C gains oxygen (→ CO₂) → C is oxidised. Being oxidised, C acts as the reducing agent (causes reduction of PbO → Pb)." },
    { q: "Iron reacts with steam (not cold water) to form:", opts: ["Fe(OH)₂ + H₂", "Fe₂O₃ + H₂", "Fe₃O₄ + H₂", "FeO + H₂"], ans: 2, exp: "3Fe + 4H₂O(steam) → Fe₃O₄ + 4H₂. The product is magnetic iron oxide (Fe₃O₄), also called loadstone." },
    { q: "Which of the following is an example of a reversible reaction?", opts: ["Burning of wood", "Rusting of iron", "N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃", "Baking a cake"], ans: 2, exp: "The Haber process (N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃) is a reversible reaction — the double arrow shows both forward and backward reactions occur under given conditions." },
    { q: "The number of atoms is NOT balanced in:", opts: ["H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl", "N₂ + H₂ → NH₃", "2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O", "CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O"], ans: 1, exp: "N₂ + H₂ → NH₃ is unbalanced. Correct form: N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃. Check: N 2=2, H 6=6. ✓" },
    { q: "When iron reacts with dilute H₂SO₄, the salt formed is:", opts: ["Fe₂(SO₄)₃", "FeSO₄", "FeS", "FeSO₃"], ans: 1, exp: "Fe + H₂SO₄ → FeSO₄ + H₂↑. Dilute acid reacts with iron to give iron(II) sulphate (ferrous sulphate) and hydrogen gas." },
    { q: "A precipitate forms in a double displacement reaction because:", opts: ["Heat is absorbed", "One product is insoluble in the solvent", "Electrons are transferred", "A covalent bond forms"], ans: 1, exp: "In a double displacement reaction in aqueous solution, if one product is insoluble (does not dissolve in water), it separates as a solid precipitate." },
    { q: "Balancing: Al + O₂ → Al₂O₃. Which is the balanced form?", opts: ["2Al + O₂ → Al₂O₃", "4Al + 3O₂ → 2Al₂O₃", "Al + O₂ → AlO", "2Al + 3O → Al₂O₃"], ans: 1, exp: "4Al + 3O₂ → 2Al₂O₃. Check: Al 4=4, O 6=6. ✓" },
    { q: "Silver bromide is used in photographic film because:", opts: ["It is very stable", "It decomposes in light, producing metallic silver (darkens image)", "It dissolves in developer solution", "It has a high melting point"], ans: 1, exp: "2AgBr → 2Ag + Br₂ in light. The dark metallic silver at exposed areas forms the photographic image." },
    { q: "Electroplating spoons with silver: the spoon is connected as the:", opts: ["Anode", "Cathode", "Electrolyte", "Neither — it floats in solution"], ans: 1, exp: "The object to be plated is the cathode (negative electrode). Silver anode dissolves and deposits on the cathode spoon. Electrolyte is silver nitrate solution." },
    { q: "Na₂SO₄(aq) + BaCl₂(aq) → BaSO₄↓ + 2NaCl. What precipitate colour is observed?", opts: ["Yellow", "Brown", "White", "Black"], ans: 2, exp: "BaSO₄ (barium sulphate) is a white insoluble precipitate. This test is used to detect sulphate ions." },
    { q: "A substance that speeds up a chemical reaction without being consumed is called:", opts: ["Reactant", "Product", "Catalyst", "Inhibitor"], ans: 2, exp: "A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with lower activation energy, speeding up the reaction without being consumed. MnO₂ is a catalyst for H₂O₂ decomposition." },
    { q: "Zinc reacts with silver nitrate solution. The observation is:", opts: ["No reaction — Zn is less reactive than Ag", "Shiny silver deposit forms — Zn displaces Ag", "Blue colour appears", "Grey precipitate of Ag₂O forms"], ans: 1, exp: "Zn + 2AgNO₃ → Zn(NO₃)₂ + 2Ag. Zinc is more reactive than silver, so it displaces silver from the solution. A silvery deposit forms on the zinc." },
    { q: "The brown fumes produced when Pb(NO₃)₂ is heated are:", opts: ["CO₂", "SO₂", "NO₂", "H₂O"], ans: 2, exp: "2Pb(NO₃)₂ → 2PbO + 4NO₂ + O₂. Brown fumes of nitrogen dioxide (NO₂) are a characteristic observation." },
    { q: "What type of reaction occurs when quick lime (CaO) is added to water?", opts: ["Endothermic combination", "Exothermic combination", "Decomposition", "Displacement"], ans: 1, exp: "CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + heat. A large amount of heat is released. This is an exothermic combination reaction." },
    { q: "Which observation confirms that hydrogen gas is produced in a reaction?", opts: ["Turns limewater milky", "Burns with a squeaky pop sound when a burning splint is brought near", "Relights a glowing splint", "Turns litmus blue"], ans: 1, exp: "Hydrogen burns in air producing a characteristic squeaky pop sound. Oxygen relights a glowing splint. CO₂ turns limewater milky." },
    { q: "The substance that is both oxidised and reduced in the same reaction undergoes:", opts: ["Combination", "Displacement", "Disproportionation", "Photochemical reaction"], ans: 2, exp: "In disproportionation, a single substance simultaneously acts as both oxidising and reducing agent. Example: 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂ (O is both oxidised 0 and reduced −2)." },
    { q: "Corrosion of aluminium produces a thin layer of Al₂O₃. This layer:", opts: ["Accelerates further corrosion", "Flakes off like rust", "Protects the metal from further corrosion", "Dissolves in water"], ans: 2, exp: "Al₂O₃ forms a tough, adherent, impermeable layer that prevents further oxidation. This is why aluminium is used outdoors despite being reactive." },
    { q: "Which gas is produced at the anode during electrolysis of water?", opts: ["Hydrogen", "Oxygen", "Ozone", "Nitrogen"], ans: 1, exp: "At the anode (positive electrode), oxidation occurs: 2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻. Oxygen gas is produced. Hydrogen forms at the cathode." },
    { q: "Balancing: Fe₂O₃ + CO → Fe + CO₂. The coefficient of CO is:", opts: ["2", "3", "4", "6"], ans: 1, exp: "Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂. Check: Fe 2=2, O 3+3=6 (left: 3+3=6, right: 6). C 3=3. ✓" },
    { q: "Combustion is always:", opts: ["Endothermic", "Exothermic", "A decomposition reaction", "A double displacement"], ans: 1, exp: "Combustion is always exothermic — it releases heat (and light). It is a rapid oxidation reaction of a fuel in the presence of oxygen." },
    { q: "Rusting of iron requires the presence of:", opts: ["Only O₂", "Only H₂O", "Both O₂ and H₂O (moisture)", "CO₂ only"], ans: 2, exp: "Rusting (Fe₂O₃·xH₂O) requires both oxygen and moisture. Iron does not rust in dry air or in water that has no dissolved oxygen." },
    { q: "Digestion of food in the human body is an example of:", opts: ["Exothermic decomposition", "Endothermic decomposition (biochemical)", "Combination", "Displacement"], ans: 1, exp: "Digestion involves hydrolysis of complex molecules (proteins, fats, carbohydrates) into simpler ones. These biochemical decomposition reactions are endothermic (require enzyme activation energy)." },
    { q: "In a redox reaction, the species that gains electrons is:", opts: ["The oxidising agent — it gets reduced", "The reducing agent — it gets oxidised", "The catalyst", "The solvent"], ans: 0, exp: "Gain of electrons = reduction. The species that accepts electrons is the oxidising agent and it itself gets reduced." },
    { q: "The word 'photosynthesis' implies:", opts: ["Making food in soil", "Making food using light (photo = light, synthesis = making)", "Breaking down with light", "Respiration in light"], ans: 1, exp: "Photo = light (Greek), synthesis = putting together. Photosynthesis means using light energy to make food (glucose) from CO₂ and H₂O." },
    { q: "Balancing: Mg₃N₂ + H₂O → Mg(OH)₂ + NH₃. Coefficient of H₂O is:", opts: ["3", "4", "6", "8"], ans: 2, exp: "Mg₃N₂ + 6H₂O → 3Mg(OH)₂ + 2NH₃. Check: Mg 3=3, N 2=2, H 12=12, O 6=6. ✓" },
    { q: "Which of the following is used as a preservative to prevent rancidity in potato chips?", opts: ["Citric acid", "Salt", "Nitrogen gas flushing", "Sugar"], ans: 2, exp: "Bags of chips are flushed with nitrogen (inert gas) to displace oxygen, preventing oxidation (rancidity) of the oil in the chips." },
    { q: "In the reaction Zn + H₂SO₄(dil) → ZnSO₄ + H₂, what is oxidised?", opts: ["H (from +1 to 0)", "S (from +6 to +4)", "Zn (from 0 to +2)", "O (from −2 to 0)"], ans: 2, exp: "Zn loses 2 electrons (0 → +2): it is oxidised. H gains electrons (+1 → 0): it is reduced. S stays +6 throughout. This is a classic single displacement redox." },
    { q: "For the Haber process N₂+3H₂⇌2NH₃ (ΔH=−92 kJ), which conditions maximise yield?", opts: ["High T, low P", "Low T, very high P with Fe catalyst", "High T, high P, no catalyst", "Low T, low P"], ans: 1, exp: "Low T favours exothermic product but is too slow; ~450°C is a compromise. High P (150-200 atm) shifts equilibrium right (4 mol gas → 2 mol). Fe catalyst lowers activation energy." },
    { q: "Contact process for H₂SO₄: 2SO₂+O₂→2SO₃ uses catalyst:", opts: ["MnO₂ at 200°C", "V₂O₅ at 450-550°C", "Platinum at 300°C", "Fe₂O₃ at 700°C"], ans: 1, exp: "V₂O₅ (vanadium pentoxide) at 450-550°C catalyses SO₂ → SO₃. Cheaper and more robust than Pt. SO₃ then dissolves in H₂SO₄ → oleum → diluted to give H₂SO₄." },
    { q: "Rusting anode half-reaction is:", opts: ["Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Fe", "Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻", "O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O", "2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂"], ans: 1, exp: "At the anode (iron surface): Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻ (oxidation). Cathode: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻. Fe²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₂ → rust (Fe₂O₃·xH₂O)." },
    { q: "Daniel cell EMF = +1.10 V means:", opts: ["Non-spontaneous reaction", "ΔG < 0 → spontaneous (ΔG = −nFE)", "Cell at equilibrium", "Reversible only at 298 K"], ans: 1, exp: "ΔG = −nFE. E > 0 → ΔG < 0 → spontaneous. For Zn/Cu cell: E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V. Zn is spontaneously oxidised, Cu²⁺ is reduced." },
    { q: "Galvanisation protects iron even when zinc is scratched because:", opts: ["Zinc seals the gap", "Zn is above Fe in reactivity → Zn sacrificially oxidises (cathodic protection)", "Zinc acts as insulator", "Paint prevents rusting"], ans: 1, exp: "Sacrificial protection: Zn (more reactive) oxidises preferentially at the scratch point, protecting Fe from oxidising. Same principle used in Mg blocks protecting ship hulls." },
    { q: "Disproportionation reaction: Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaOCl + H₂O. Cl oxidation states:", opts: ["0 → +1 only", "0 → −1 only", "Cl₂ (0) → NaCl (−1, reduced) and NaOCl (+1, oxidised) simultaneously", "−1 → +1"], ans: 2, exp: "Disproportionation: same element simultaneously oxidised and reduced. Cl₂ (0) gives Cl⁻ (−1, reduced) and OCl⁻ (+1, oxidised). Important: bleaching powder, swimming pool chlorination." },
    { q: "If 4g NaOH (M=40) is dissolved to make 500 mL solution, molarity = ?", opts: ["0.1 M", "0.2 M", "0.4 M", "2.0 M"], ans: 1, exp: "n = 4/40 = 0.1 mol. V = 0.5 L. M = n/V = 0.2 mol/L. Basic concentration calculation — always convert mL to L." },
    { q: "Rate doubles every 10°C. At 20°C rate = x. At 60°C, rate = ?", opts: ["2x", "4x", "8x", "16x"], ans: 3, exp: "60-20 = 40°C rise = 4 intervals of 10°C. Rate = x × 2⁴ = 16x. Biological reactions follow similar rule (Q₁₀ ≈ 2)." },
    { q: "Which represents a spontaneous process at all temperatures?", opts: ["ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0", "ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0 (ΔG = ΔH−TΔS always negative)", "ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0", "ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0"], ans: 1, exp: "ΔG = ΔH − TΔS. If ΔH < 0 (exothermic) and ΔS > 0 (entropy increases), ΔG < 0 at all T → always spontaneous. The most favourable thermodynamic scenario." },
    { q: "Electrolysis of brine: product at cathode is:", opts: ["Cl₂", "NaOH only", "H₂ and NaOH", "O₂"], ans: 2, exp: "Cathode: 2H₂O + 2e⁻ → H₂↑ + 2OH⁻; OH⁻ + Na⁺ → NaOH. Anode: 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂↑ + 2e⁻. Three products: H₂, Cl₂, NaOH (chlor-alkali industry)." },
    { q: "KMnO₄ + HCl redox: Mn goes from +7 to +2. What is oxidised?", opts: ["K (0 to +1)", "Mn (+7 to +2)", "Cl⁻ (−1 to 0 in Cl₂)", "O (−2 to 0)"], ans: 2, exp: "HCl is the reducing agent: Cl⁻ (−1) → Cl₂ (0), Cl is oxidised. KMnO₄ is the oxidising agent: Mn (+7) → Mn²⁺ (+2), Mn is reduced. 5 electrons per Mn, 1 per Cl → 10 HCl per 2 KMnO₄." },
    { q: "Standard electrode potential E°: more positive value means:", opts: ["Stronger reducing agent", "Stronger oxidising agent (higher tendency to gain electrons/be reduced)", "Lower reactivity", "Higher solubility"], ans: 1, exp: "More positive E° = more easily reduced = stronger oxidising agent. F₂ has highest E° (+2.87V) = strongest oxidising agent. Li has most negative E° (−3.04V) = strongest reducing agent." },
    { q: "Object to be electroplated is connected as:", opts: ["Anode", "Cathode (M^n+ ions deposit on it)", "Electrolyte", "Power source"], ans: 1, exp: "Electroplating: object = cathode. Plating metal (e.g., Cu) = anode. Electrolyte = metal salt solution. Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu deposits on cathode object." },
    { q: "Hess's law: ΔH is a _____ function, so route doesn't matter.", opts: ["Path", "State (same start/end state → same ΔH regardless of steps)", "Rate", "Kinetic"], ans: 1, exp: "ΔH is a state function → depends only on initial and final states, not path. Allows calculation of inaccessible ΔH values from known ΔH values via different paths." },
    { q: "2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O: 4g H₂ reacts. Volume O₂ required at STP:", opts: ["11.2 L", "22.4 L", "44.8 L", "5.6 L"], ans: 1, exp: "4g H₂ = 4/2 = 2 mol H₂. Ratio H₂:O₂ = 2:1 → 1 mol O₂ needed. At STP: 1 mol = 22.4 L." },
    { q: "Le Chatelier: adding inert gas (constant volume) to N₂+3H₂⇌2NH₃ equilibrium:", opts: ["Shifts right", "Shifts left", "No effect (partial pressures unchanged)", "Doubles yield"], ans: 2, exp: "Adding inert gas at constant volume: total pressure rises but partial pressures of reactants/products unchanged → no shift in equilibrium. If constant total pressure, equilibrium shifts left (more moles side)." },
    { q: "Activation energy can be measured from the slope of the Arrhenius plot of:", opts: ["k vs T", "ln k vs 1/T (slope = −Eₐ/R)", "rate vs concentration", "k vs [A]"], ans: 1, exp: "Arrhenius: k = Ae^{−Eₐ/RT} → ln k = ln A − Eₐ/RT. Plot ln k vs 1/T: slope = −Eₐ/R. From slope: Eₐ = −slope × R." },
    { q: "Which reaction type has ΔS most positive?", opts: ["Solid → solid + solid", "2 gases → 1 gas", "Solid → gas (sublimation)", "Liquid → solid"], ans: 2, exp: "Entropy increases most when solid → gas: particles gain enormous freedom of movement. Gas has much higher entropy than liquid or solid. Sublimation: ΔS highly positive." },
    { q: "Solvay process produces Na₂CO₃ from:", opts: ["Na + CO₂", "NaCl + NH₃ + CO₂ + H₂O → NaHCO₃ → Na₂CO₃", "NaOH + CO₂", "Na₂SO₄ + CaCO₃"], ans: 1, exp: "Solvay: saturated NaCl + NH₃ + CO₂ → NaHCO₃↓ + NH₄Cl. Heat NaHCO₃: 2NaHCO₃ → Na₂CO₃ + H₂O + CO₂. NH₃ recovered from NH₄Cl + Ca(OH)₂. Environmentally superior to older processes." },
    { q: "Born-Haber cycle: lattice energy is energy released when:", opts: ["Atoms ionise in gas phase", "Gaseous ions form 1 mol of ionic crystal", "Ionic crystal dissolves in water", "Metal is vaporised"], ans: 1, exp: "Lattice energy U: M⁺(g) + X⁻(g) → MX(s) + U. Always exothermic (−ve). Larger lattice energy = more stable ionic compound. NaCl: −787 kJ/mol. MgO: −3791 kJ/mol (higher charge, smaller ions)." },
    { q: "Third Law of Thermodynamics: entropy of perfect crystal at 0 K =", opts: ["Infinity", "Undefined", "Zero (only one microstate)", "−R ln 2"], ans: 2, exp: "S = k_B ln W. At 0 K, perfect crystal has W=1 (one microstate) → S = 0. Sets absolute reference for entropy. Real substances have residual entropy if imperfect." },
    { q: "Beer-Lambert law A = εcl: if concentration triples, absorbance:", opts: ["Triples", "Increases by 1/3", "Stays same", "Decreases"], ans: 0, exp: "A ∝ c (direct proportionality). Triple c → triple A. ε (molar absorptivity) and l (path length) are constant. This is the basis of colorimetric concentration analysis." },
    { q: "Entropy change in dissolving NaCl in water is positive because:", opts: ["NaCl is ionic", "Ordered crystal lattice → dispersed ions → increased disorder (more microstates)", "Water becomes colder", "Endothermic process"], ans: 1, exp: "NaCl crystal (highly ordered) → Na⁺(aq) and Cl⁻(aq) dispersed among water molecules → more microstates → ΔS > 0. Dissolution often endothermic but entropy-driven." },
    { q: "Oxidation state of Cr in K₂Cr₂O₇ (potassium dichromate):", opts: ["+3", "+6", "+4", "+7"], ans: 1, exp: "2(+1) + 2(Cr) + 7(−2) = 0 → 2Cr = +12 → Cr = +6. K₂Cr₂O₇ (orange) is a strong oxidising agent in acidic solution, Cr⁶⁺ → Cr³⁺ (+3, green). Used in breathalyser tests for alcohol." },
    { q: "A catalyst increases reaction rate by:", opts: ["Raising temperature", "Providing alternative pathway with lower activation energy", "Increasing reactant concentration", "Adding energy to products"], ans: 1, exp: "Catalyst: provides alternative reaction pathway with lower Eₐ. More molecules have sufficient energy to react → faster rate. Catalyst is not consumed. Does not shift equilibrium position." },
    { q: "Van't Hoff factor (i) for Na₂SO₄ in dilute solution:", opts: ["1", "2", "3", "4"], ans: 2, exp: "Na₂SO₄ → 2Na⁺ + SO₄²⁻: 3 ions per formula unit → i = 3. Colligative properties (boiling point elevation, osmotic pressure) = i × (ideal value). For NaCl: i = 2." },
    { q: "Enthalpy of neutralisation of strong acid + strong base is always approximately:", opts: ["Zero", "+57 kJ/mol", "−57 kJ/mol (exothermic)", "−100 kJ/mol"], ans: 2, exp: "Strong acid + strong base: essentially H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l). ΔH = −57.1 kJ/mol at 25°C. Same regardless of which strong acid/base. Weak acid/base: smaller value (some energy used to ionise)." },
    { q: "Which phenomenon explains why Fe in HNO₃(conc.) doesn't dissolve?", opts: ["Fe does not react with acids", "Passivation: dense Fe₂O₃ layer blocks further reaction", "HNO₃ is not acidic enough", "Fe is below H in reactivity series"], ans: 1, exp: "Passivation: concentrated HNO₃ (and H₂SO₄) react with Fe/Al surface to form a thin, adherent, impermeable oxide layer. This oxide layer prevents further attack. Also explains why Al cans hold acid drinks." },
    { q: "If Ksp of AgCl = 1.8×10⁻¹⁰, the solubility in pure water is:", opts: ["1.8×10⁻¹⁰ mol/L", "1.34×10⁻⁵ mol/L", "9×10⁻¹¹ mol/L", "3.6×10⁻¹⁰ mol/L"], ans: 1, exp: "AgCl → Ag⁺ + Cl⁻. Let s = solubility. [Ag⁺]=[Cl⁻]=s. Ksp = s² = 1.8×10⁻¹⁰. s = √(1.8×10⁻¹⁰) = 1.34×10⁻⁵ mol/L. Common ion effect reduces solubility further." },
    { q: "In a second-order reaction (rate = k[A]²), if [A] doubles, rate:", opts: ["Doubles", "Quadruples (rate ∝ [A]²)", "Stays same", "Halves"], ans: 1, exp: "Second-order: rate ∝ [A]². If [A] → 2[A]: rate → k(2[A])² = 4k[A]² = 4× original rate. First-order: [A] doubles → rate doubles. Zero-order: rate independent of [A]." },
    { q: "Which process converts SO₃ to H₂SO₄ in the Contact process?", opts: ["Direct addition: SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄ (but forms acid mist)", "Absorption in H₂SO₄ → oleum → then dilution with water", "Reduction with H₂", "Reaction with NaOH"], ans: 1, exp: "SO₃ is not added directly to water (produces acid mist). Instead: SO₃ + H₂SO₄ → H₂S₂O₇ (oleum), then H₂S₂O₇ + H₂O → 2H₂SO₄. Efficient and safe." },
    { q: "Coordination number of Na in NaCl crystal (rock salt structure):", opts: ["4", "6", "8", "12"], ans: 1, exp: "In rock salt (NaCl) structure: each Na⁺ surrounded by 6 Cl⁻, each Cl⁻ surrounded by 6 Na⁺. CN = 6. In CsCl: CN = 8. In ZnS (sphalerite): CN = 4." },
    { q: "The rate-determining step in a multi-step reaction is:", opts: ["The first step", "The last step", "The slowest step (it controls the overall rate)", "The fastest step"], ans: 2, exp: "In a multi-step mechanism, the overall rate = rate of the slowest step (rate-determining step, RDS). All faster steps adjust instantly. Rate law reflects the RDS mechanism." },
    { q: "pOH + pH = 14 at 25°C. If pH = 9, [H⁺] = ?", opts: ["10⁻⁹ mol/L", "10⁻⁵ mol/L", "10⁹ mol/L", "9 mol/L"], ans: 0, exp: "pH = −log[H⁺] = 9 → [H⁺] = 10⁻⁹ mol/L. pOH = 14−9 = 5 → [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁵ mol/L. Basic solution (pH > 7)." },
    { q: "Nernst equation E = E° − (RT/nF)lnQ relates cell potential to:", opts: ["Temperature only", "Concentration of species (via Q, reaction quotient)", "Pressure only", "Activation energy"], ans: 1, exp: "Nernst equation: E = E° − (0.0592/n)logQ at 25°C. When Q=1 (standard conditions), E=E°. At equilibrium, Q=K and E=0. Used for non-standard concentration calculations." },
    { q: "Thermodynamic spontaneity requires ΔG < 0. ΔG = ΔH − TΔS. At low T, spontaneity is mainly determined by:", opts: ["ΔS", "ΔH (enthalpy dominates at low T)", "Activation energy", "Kc value"], ans: 1, exp: "At low T: TΔS term is small → ΔG ≈ ΔH. Exothermic reactions (ΔH < 0) more likely spontaneous at low T. At high T: TΔS dominates → endothermic reactions can be spontaneous if ΔS > 0." },
    { q: "Osmosis is movement of solvent molecules from:", opts: ["High solute concentration to low solute concentration", "Low solute concentration to high solute concentration (across semipermeable membrane)", "High temperature to low temperature", "High pressure to low pressure"], ans: 1, exp: "Osmosis: solvent moves from low solute (high solvent) concentration to high solute (low solvent) concentration through semipermeable membrane. Net movement reduces concentration gradient." },
    { q: "Osmotic pressure π = iMRT. For 0.1 M NaCl (i=2) at 300 K:", opts: ["0.1 × 0.0821 × 300 = 2.46 atm", "2 × 0.1 × 0.0821 × 300 = 4.93 atm", "4 × 0.1 × 0.0821 × 300 = 9.85 atm", "0.1 atm"], ans: 1, exp: "π = iMRT = 2 × 0.1 × 0.0821 × 300 = 4.93 atm. NaCl dissociates into 2 ions (i=2). Osmotic pressure is a colligative property." },
    { q: "Photochemical smog (secondary pollutant) forms when:", opts: ["CO₂ builds up in cities", "NO₂ + UV → NO + O; O + O₂ → O₃; O₃ + hydrocarbons → PAN and other irritants", "SO₂ reacts with water", "Methane burns incompletely"], ans: 1, exp: "Photochemical smog: NO₂ from vehicle exhaust + UV sunlight → NO + O (atomic). O + O₂ → O₃ (ozone). O₃ + unburnt hydrocarbons → PAN (peroxyacetyl nitrate) and aldehydes → eye/lung irritants." },
    { q: "A buffer solution resists pH change because it contains:", opts: ["Only a strong acid", "A weak acid and its conjugate base (or weak base and conjugate acid) in comparable amounts", "High concentration of HCl", "Distilled water"], ans: 1, exp: "Buffer: weak acid (HA) + conjugate base (A⁻). Add H⁺: HA forms. Add OH⁻: HA ionises. Buffer capacity is highest when pH = pKa (Henderson-Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log[A⁻]/[HA])." },
    { q: "Thermal decomposition of CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ is endothermic. This reaction is used in:", opts: ["Manufacture of cement (limestone heated in kilns)", "Production of NaOH", "Making baking soda", "Extracting gold"], ans: 0, exp: "Calcination of limestone (CaCO₃) at >840°C → quicklime (CaO) + CO₂. CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ (slaked lime). Used in cement, mortar, steel making, and reducing soil acidity." },
    { q: "Photodegradation of ozone: Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂; ClO + O → Cl + O₂. The net reaction is:", opts: ["O₃ + Cl → ClO + O₂", "2O₃ → 3O₂ (Cl is catalytic)", "Cl₂ → 2Cl", "O₃ → O₂ + Cl"], ans: 1, exp: "Adding the two steps: Cl + O₃ + ClO + O → ClO + O₂ + Cl + O₂ → 2O₃ → 3O₂ with Cl regenerated. Cl is a catalyst (regenerated). Each Cl atom can destroy ~100,000 O₃ molecules." },
    { q: "Which colloid property causes particles to scatter light (milk appears white)?", opts: ["Adsorption", "Tyndall effect", "Electrophoresis", "Dialysis"], ans: 1, exp: "Tyndall effect: colloidal particles (1-100 nm) scatter light, making the beam visible. Milk (fat droplets in water) scatters all visible wavelengths → appears white. True solutions (ions/molecules) don't show Tyndall effect." },
    { q: "Henderson-Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]). For acetate buffer (pKa=4.76) with equal concentrations:", opts: ["pH = 3.76", "pH = 4.76 (log 1 = 0)", "pH = 5.76", "pH = 7"], ans: 1, exp: "Equal [A⁻] and [HA]: log([A⁻]/[HA]) = log(1) = 0. pH = pKa + 0 = pKa. Buffer has maximum capacity at pH = pKa." },
    { q: "Quantum yield Φ > 1 in a photochemical chain reaction means:", opts: ["More photons needed than molecules reacting", "Each photon initiates a chain of reactions involving many molecules", "Light is emitted", "Reaction is always exothermic"], ans: 1, exp: "Φ > 1: one photon initiates a chain reaction → many molecules react per photon absorbed. H₂ + Cl₂ (chain reaction): Φ ≈ 10⁶. Each photon breaks one Cl₂ → 2 Cl radicals initiating long chain." },
    { q: "Which type of isomerism do [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺ complexes show?", opts: ["Chain isomerism", "Geometric (cis-trans) isomerism: Cl can be cis (adjacent) or trans (opposite)", "Optical isomerism only", "Tautomerism"], ans: 1, exp: "Square planar or octahedral complexes with at least two different ligands show geometric (cis-trans) isomerism. [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺ octahedral: cis (both Cl on same side) vs trans (opposite sides). Different properties/colours." },
    { q: "The degree of unsaturation (DoU) of benzene C₆H₆ is:", opts: ["1", "4", "3", "6"], ans: 1, exp: "DoU = (2C + 2 − H)/2 = (12 + 2 − 6)/2 = 4. One ring (1) + 3 double bonds (3) in Kekulé = 4. Or: benzene has 4 degrees of unsaturation. Correct: DoU = 4." },
    { q: "Molar conductivity increases with dilution because:", opts: ["More ions are produced on dilution", "For strong electrolytes: inter-ionic attractions decrease; for weak electrolytes: degree of ionisation increases", "Temperature increases", "More water molecules conduct electricity"], ans: 1, exp: "Molar conductivity (Λm): increases with dilution. Strong electrolytes: fully ionised, but inter-ionic forces decrease with dilution → ions move faster. Weak electrolytes: degree of ionisation increases greatly on dilution." }
  ]
,

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════
     CH 2 — ACIDS, BASES AND SALTS
  ══════════════════════════════════════ */
  2: [
    { q: "Which natural indicator turns red in base?", opts: ["Litmus", "Phenolphthalein", "Turmeric", "Methyl orange"], ans: 2, exp: "Turmeric (yellow in acid/neutral) turns red-brown in basic conditions. It is a natural indicator found in the kitchen spice." },
    { q: "Arrhenius defined a base as a substance that:", opts: ["Accepts a proton", "Donates a proton", "Produces OH⁻ ions in water", "Produces H⁺ ions in water"], ans: 2, exp: "Arrhenius (1884): bases ionise in water to give OH⁻ ions. Example: NaOH → Na⁺ + OH⁻." },
    { q: "What is the pH of pure water at 25°C?", opts: ["0", "7", "14", "6.5"], ans: 1, exp: "Pure water has [H⁺] = [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁷ mol/L at 25°C, so pH = −log(10⁻⁷) = 7. It is exactly neutral." },
    { q: "Hydrochloric acid (HCl) in the stomach helps:", opts: ["Absorb fats", "Activate pepsinogen to pepsin and kill pathogens", "Produce bile", "Produce insulin"], ans: 1, exp: "HCl in gastric juice (pH 1.5-2) activates the enzyme pepsin for protein digestion and kills most bacteria ingested with food." },
    { q: "Which of the following is a strong acid?", opts: ["CH₃COOH", "H₂CO₃", "H₂SO₄", "H₃PO₄"], ans: 2, exp: "H₂SO₄ (sulphuric acid) is a strong acid — it is nearly completely ionised in water. Acetic and carbonic acids are weak acids (partial ionisation)." },
    { q: "A solution with pH = 11 is:", opts: ["Strongly acidic", "Weakly acidic", "Neutral", "Strongly basic"], ans: 3, exp: "pH > 7 is basic. pH 11 indicates a strongly basic solution ([OH⁻] = 10⁻³ mol/L). NaOH solution has pH ≈ 13-14." },
    { q: "Neutralisation reaction: NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O is:", opts: ["Endothermic", "Exothermic", "Neither", "First endothermic then exothermic"], ans: 1, exp: "All neutralisation reactions are exothermic (heat is released). The mixing of acid and base produces heat — the solution temperature rises." },
    { q: "Washing soda (Na₂CO₃·10H₂O) removes permanent hardness of water by:", opts: ["Precipitating Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ as insoluble carbonates", "Adding more sodium ions", "Making water acidic", "Absorbing CO₂"], ans: 0, exp: "Na₂CO₃ + CaCl₂ → CaCO₃↓ + 2NaCl. Calcium and magnesium ions precipitate as insoluble carbonates, removing hardness." },
    { q: "Baking soda (NaHCO₃) is used as an antacid because:", opts: ["It is acidic and neutralises base", "It is a mild base that neutralises excess HCl in stomach", "It absorbs water", "It is a salt and is neutral"], ans: 1, exp: "NaHCO₃ + HCl → NaCl + H₂O + CO₂. It is a mild, non-toxic base that neutralises excess acid in the stomach without making it too alkaline." },
    { q: "Bleaching powder (CaOCl₂) is prepared by passing Cl₂ gas over:", opts: ["CaO (quick lime)", "CaCO₃ (limestone)", "Ca(OH)₂ (slaked lime)", "CaCl₂"], ans: 2, exp: "Ca(OH)₂ + Cl₂ → CaOCl₂ + H₂O. Dry slaked lime absorbs chlorine to form bleaching powder (calcium hypochlorite)." },
    { q: "Plaster of Paris sets when water is added because:", opts: ["It dissolves and reprecipitates", "It forms hard crystalline gypsum (CaSO₄·2H₂O)", "Water evaporates quickly", "An endothermic reaction occurs"], ans: 1, exp: "CaSO₄·½H₂O + 1½H₂O → CaSO₄·2H₂O. Interlocking crystals of gypsum form a rigid solid, used in fracture casts and wall plaster." },
    { q: "Which of the following salts is formed by a strong acid and strong base?", opts: ["NH₄Cl", "CH₃COONa", "Na₂CO₃", "NaCl"], ans: 3, exp: "NaCl is from strong acid HCl + strong base NaOH. NH₄Cl: strong acid + weak base. CH₃COONa: weak acid + strong base." },
    { q: "When concentrated H₂SO₄ is added to sugar, the sugar turns black because H₂SO₄ is a:", opts: ["Strong oxidising agent", "Strong acid", "Powerful dehydrating agent", "Reducing agent"], ans: 2, exp: "Conc. H₂SO₄ removes H and OH from sugar (C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁) as water, leaving behind black carbon. This demonstrates its powerful dehydrating action." },
    { q: "Formic acid (methanoic acid) is the acid present in:", opts: ["Vinegar", "Ant sting", "Lemon juice", "Curd"], ans: 1, exp: "Ant sting (and nettle leaves) contain formic acid (HCOOH). Rubbing calamine (ZnCO₃, a base) on the sting neutralises the acid and relieves pain." },
    { q: "Which acid is found in tamarind (imli)?", opts: ["Citric acid", "Tartaric acid", "Oxalic acid", "Tartaric + malic acid"], ans: 1, exp: "Tartaric acid is the main organic acid in tamarind, giving it its characteristic sour taste. Tartaric acid is also in grapes." },
    { q: "Phenolphthalein is colourless in acid and turns pink in base. At exact neutralisation point (pH 7), it is:", opts: ["Pink", "Orange", "Colourless", "Yellow"], ans: 2, exp: "Phenolphthalein is colourless at pH < 8.2 (acidic and neutral) and pink at pH > 8.2 (basic). At the neutral point pH 7, it remains colourless." },
    { q: "The pH of fresh milk is about 6 and of sour milk is about 4. This shows that:", opts: ["Milk became more basic as it soured", "Lactic acid produced during souring lowered the pH", "The acidity decreased", "pH and sourness are unrelated"], ans: 1, exp: "Bacteria ferment milk sugar (lactose) to lactic acid (CH₃CHOHCOOH), increasing acid concentration and lowering pH from 6 to ~4." },
    { q: "When CO₂ is passed through lime water Ca(OH)₂, the solution first turns milky then clears with excess CO₂ because:", opts: ["CaCO₃ dissolves in excess water", "Excess CO₂ converts CaCO₃ to soluble Ca(HCO₃)₂", "Ca(OH)₂ runs out", "CO₂ evaporates"], ans: 1, exp: "CaCO₃(s) + CO₂ + H₂O → Ca(HCO₃)₂(aq). Calcium bicarbonate is soluble, clearing the milkiness." },
    { q: "Acid rain is caused mainly by the dissolution of which gases in rainwater?", opts: ["CO₂ and N₂", "SO₂ and NOₓ", "CO and CH₄", "O₃ and Cl₂"], ans: 1, exp: "SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃; 4NO₂ + O₂ + 2H₂O → 4HNO₃. Sulphur and nitrogen oxides from burning coal and vehicle exhaust form acids in rain (pH < 5.6)." },
    { q: "The chlor-alkali process (electrolysis of brine) produces:", opts: ["NaOH + HCl + H₂", "Cl₂ + H₂ + NaOH", "Cl₂ + NaCl + H₂O", "Na + Cl₂ + H₂O"], ans: 1, exp: "2NaCl(aq) + 2H₂O → Cl₂(g) + H₂(g) + 2NaOH(aq). Three industrially important products: chlorine, hydrogen, and caustic soda (NaOH)." },
    { q: "Which of these is an alkali?", opts: ["CuO", "Ca(OH)₂", "Fe(OH)₃", "All of these"], ans: 1, exp: "An alkali is a soluble base. Ca(OH)₂ (lime water) dissolves (slightly) in water and is an alkali. CuO and Fe(OH)₃ are bases but insoluble in water, so not alkalis." },
    { q: "The formula of Plaster of Paris is:", opts: ["CaSO₄·2H₂O", "CaSO₄", "CaSO₄·½H₂O", "Ca₂SO₄·H₂O"], ans: 2, exp: "Plaster of Paris = CaSO₄·½H₂O (calcium sulphate hemihydrate). Gypsum = CaSO₄·2H₂O (dihydrate). POP is made by heating gypsum at ~120°C." },
    { q: "The common name of NaOH is:", opts: ["Baking soda", "Washing soda", "Caustic soda", "Soda ash"], ans: 2, exp: "NaOH = caustic soda. Na₂CO₃·10H₂O = washing soda. NaHCO₃ = baking soda. Na₂CO₃ (anhydrous) = soda ash." },
    { q: "A student tests an unknown liquid with litmus: red litmus unchanged, blue litmus turned red. The liquid is:", opts: ["Basic", "Neutral", "Acidic", "Amphoteric"], ans: 2, exp: "Acids turn blue litmus red. Red litmus stays red in acid. If blue litmus turned red and red litmus stayed red → acidic solution." },
    { q: "Aqueous solution of which salt is basic?", opts: ["NH₄Cl", "NaCl", "Na₂CO₃", "FeSO₄"], ans: 2, exp: "Na₂CO₃ is formed from weak acid (H₂CO₃) and strong base (NaOH). The carbonate ion undergoes hydrolysis: CO₃²⁻ + H₂O → HCO₃⁻ + OH⁻, making the solution basic." },
    { q: "Tooth enamel is corroded when pH of mouth falls below:", opts: ["5.5", "6.0", "7.0", "4.0"], ans: 0, exp: "Tooth enamel (calcium phosphate) dissolves (corrodes) when mouth pH drops below 5.5, due to acids produced by bacteria from sugary food." },
    { q: "Metals above hydrogen in the reactivity series react with dilute acids to give:", opts: ["Metal oxide + Water", "Metal salt + Hydrogen", "Metal salt + Oxygen", "No reaction"], ans: 1, exp: "Metals above hydrogen displace H from acids: Metal + Acid → Salt + H₂↑. Metals below hydrogen (Cu, Hg, Ag, Au) do not react with dilute acids." },
    { q: "Hydrated copper sulphate (CuSO₄·5H₂O) is blue. Anhydrous CuSO₄ is:", opts: ["Green", "Red", "White", "Yellow"], ans: 2, exp: "Anhydrous CuSO₄ is white powder. The blue colour is due to water of crystallisation. Adding water to the white powder turns it blue — used as a test for water." },
    { q: "Equal volumes of 1 M HCl and 1 M NaOH are mixed. The resulting solution pH is:", opts: ["Less than 7", "7", "Greater than 7", "Cannot be determined"], ans: 1, exp: "HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O. Equal moles of strong acid and strong base completely neutralise — NaCl solution is neutral (pH 7)." },
    { q: "Soil acidity can be corrected by adding:", opts: ["Ammonium sulphate fertiliser", "Quick lime (CaO) or slaked lime", "More water", "Nitrogen gas"], ans: 1, exp: "CaO and Ca(OH)₂ are bases that neutralise excess acidity in soil (liming). This raises pH to the optimal 6-7 range for most crops." },
    { q: "The reaction between an acid and a metal oxide is:", opts: ["Combination", "Decomposition", "Neutralisation (salt + water)", "Displacement"], ans: 2, exp: "Acid + Metal oxide → Salt + Water. Example: H₂SO₄ + CuO → CuSO₄ + H₂O. Metal oxides are basic oxides, so this is essentially neutralisation." },
    { q: "Methyl orange turns orange at what approximate pH?", opts: ["2-3", "4-5", "6-7", "8-9"], ans: 1, exp: "Methyl orange is red at pH < 3.1 and yellow at pH > 4.4. In between (≈4) it shows orange. It is best used for strong acid–strong base titrations." },
    { q: "The formula of common salt is:", opts: ["KCl", "NaCl", "Na₂SO₄", "CaCl₂"], ans: 1, exp: "Common salt (table salt) is sodium chloride (NaCl). It is the most common ionic compound and the source material for many industrial chemicals." },
    { q: "Which of these does NOT conduct electricity in solution?", opts: ["HCl", "NaOH", "NaCl", "Glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆)"], ans: 3, exp: "Glucose is a covalent compound and does not ionise in water — no ions are produced, so it does not conduct electricity. Acids, bases, and salts produce ions." },
    { q: "Lime water turns milky in CO₂ but NOT in SO₂. TRUE or FALSE?", opts: ["TRUE", "FALSE — SO₂ also turns lime water milky", "TRUE — only CO₂ affects lime water", "FALSE — neither gas affects lime water"], ans: 1, exp: "FALSE. SO₂ also turns lime water milky: Ca(OH)₂ + SO₂ → CaSO₃↓ (white) + H₂O. Both CO₂ and SO₂ react with lime water to form white precipitates." },
    { q: "The substance formed when slaked lime [Ca(OH)₂] reacts with CO₂ in atmosphere is:", opts: ["CaO", "CaCO₃", "Ca(HCO₃)₂", "CaCl₂"], ans: 1, exp: "Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O. This is why freshly applied lime mortar hardens over time — it slowly absorbs CO₂ from air." },
    { q: "The anion present in hydrochloric acid (HCl) in solution is:", opts: ["H⁺", "Cl⁻", "OH⁻", "Cl₂"], ans: 1, exp: "HCl → H⁺ + Cl⁻ in water. The H⁺ (actually H₃O⁺) is the cation responsible for acidity; Cl⁻ is the anion." },
    { q: "Stomach acid pH is approximately:", opts: ["5-6", "3-4", "1.5-2", "0"], ans: 2, exp: "Gastric juice has pH ≈ 1.5-2, making it very strongly acidic. This acidity is necessary for enzyme pepsin to work." },
    { q: "Calamine (ZnCO₃) lotion is applied on ant bite because:", opts: ["ZnCO₃ is acidic and neutralises formic acid", "ZnCO₃ is basic and neutralises formic acid", "ZnCO₃ cools the skin", "ZnCO₃ is an antibiotic"], ans: 1, exp: "Ant sting injects formic acid (HCOOH). Calamine (ZnCO₃) is a mild base: ZnCO₃ + 2HCOOH → Zn(HCOO)₂ + H₂O + CO₂. Neutralisation relieves pain." },
    { q: "Which of the following salts has water of crystallisation?", opts: ["NaCl", "KNO₃", "CuSO₄·5H₂O", "Na₂SO₄"], ans: 2, exp: "CuSO₄·5H₂O (blue vitriol) has 5 water molecules per formula unit as water of crystallisation. Removing water gives white anhydrous CuSO₄." },
    { q: "An acid that donates more than one proton per molecule is called:", opts: ["Monoprotic", "Diprotic / Polyprotic", "Strong acid", "Weak acid"], ans: 1, exp: "H₂SO₄ is diprotic (donates 2 H⁺), H₃PO₄ is triprotic. HCl and HNO₃ are monoprotic (one H⁺ per molecule)." },
    { q: "The reaction: HNO₃ + KOH → KNO₃ + H₂O produces which salt?", opts: ["Potassium nitrite", "Potassium nitrate", "Potassium sulphate", "Potassium chloride"], ans: 1, exp: "HNO₃ + KOH → KNO₃ + H₂O. KNO₃ is potassium nitrate (also used as fertiliser and in gunpowder)." },
    { q: "Among the following, which is the strongest acid?", opts: ["CH₃COOH (Ka≈1.8×10⁻⁵)", "H₂CO₃ (Ka≈4.3×10⁻⁷)", "HCl (fully ionised)", "HF (Ka≈7.2×10⁻⁴)"], ans: 2, exp: "HCl is a strong acid — it fully ionises in water. The others are weak acids with small Ka values. Higher Ka = stronger acid." },
    { q: "The common name of Ca(OH)₂ is:", opts: ["Quick lime", "Slaked lime", "Lime stone", "Milk of lime"], ans: 1, exp: "Ca(OH)₂ = slaked lime (or hydrated lime). CaO = quick lime. CaCO₃ = limestone. Lime water is dilute Ca(OH)₂ solution." },
    { q: "Acid rain damages marble statues because:", opts: ["Marble (CaCO₃) reacts with acids to dissolve", "Marble absorbs rain and expands", "Acid reduces marble", "Marble is oxidised"], ans: 0, exp: "CaCO₃ + H₂SO₄ → CaSO₄ + H₂O + CO₂. Sulphuric acid in acid rain converts insoluble CaCO₃ (marble) to soluble CaSO₄, which washes away." },
    { q: "Bases that are soluble in water are called:", opts: ["Indicators", "Salts", "Alkalis", "Oxides"], ans: 2, exp: "Alkalis = soluble bases. Examples: NaOH, KOH, Ca(OH)₂ (slightly). All alkalis are bases, but not vice versa — CuO is a base but not an alkali (insoluble)." },
    { q: "The pH of human saliva is approximately:", opts: ["5.0", "6.5-7.5", "8.5", "9.0"], ans: 1, exp: "Human saliva is nearly neutral (pH 6.5-7.5). Salivary amylase works best in this pH range. After eating sugar, bacteria produce acids lowering mouth pH." },
    { q: "Baking powder is a mixture of baking soda and:", opts: ["Salt", "Sugar", "Tartaric acid (or cream of tartar)", "Vinegar"], ans: 2, exp: "Baking powder = NaHCO₃ + tartaric acid (or cream of tartar). When moistened/heated: the acid reacts with baking soda releasing CO₂ for leavening." },
    { q: "Methane gas burns in air to produce:", opts: ["CO + H₂", "CO₂ + H₂O", "CO₂ + H₂", "C + H₂O"], ans: 1, exp: "CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O. Complete combustion of methane (natural gas) gives carbon dioxide and water. It is an exothermic reaction." },
    { q: "The salt formed by reaction of HCl and NaOH is:", opts: ["Acidic salt (pH < 7)", "Basic salt (pH > 7)", "Normal/neutral salt — NaCl (pH ≈ 7)", "Double salt"], ans: 2, exp: "Strong acid + strong base → neutral salt. NaCl has pH ≈ 7 in water. If weak acid + strong base → basic salt (pH > 7, e.g., CH₃COONa). If strong acid + weak base → acidic salt (pH < 7, e.g., NH₄Cl)." },
    { q: "Bronsted-Lowry acid-base theory: acid is a _____ donor.", opts: ["Electron pair", "Proton (H⁺)", "Hydride (H⁻)", "Neutron"], ans: 1, exp: "Bronsted-Lowry (1923): acid = proton donor, base = proton acceptor. Wider than Arrhenius (works in non-aqueous solvents). NH₃ is a base (accepts H⁺). CH₃COOH is an acid (donates H⁺)." },
    { q: "Lewis acid is a _____ acceptor.", opts: ["Proton", "Electron pair (accepts lone pair from Lewis base)", "Hydride", "Neutron"], ans: 1, exp: "Lewis (1923): acid = electron pair acceptor, base = electron pair donor. Broadest definition. Examples: BF₃ (Lewis acid, empty orbital), AlCl₃, Fe³⁺. NH₃, OH⁻ are Lewis bases." },
    { q: "In NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, the conjugate acid-base pairs are:", opts: ["NH₃/OH⁻ and H₂O/NH₄⁺", "NH₃/NH₄⁺ and H₂O/OH⁻", "NH₃/NH₂⁻ and H₂O/H₃O⁺", "NH₄⁺/H₂O and OH⁻/NH₃"], ans: 1, exp: "Conjugate pairs: NH₃ (base) / NH₄⁺ (conjugate acid); H₂O (acid) / OH⁻ (conjugate base). A conjugate pair differs by one H⁺." },
    { q: "pH of 0.01 M HCl (strong acid, fully ionised):", opts: ["1", "2", "−2", "12"], ans: 1, exp: "[H⁺] = 0.01 = 10⁻² M. pH = −log(10⁻²) = 2. Strong acid: fully ionised. [H⁺] = initial concentration." },
    { q: "Buffer capacity is maximum when:", opts: ["pH = 7", "pH = pKa (equal concentrations of weak acid and conjugate base)", "pH = 0", "pH is very high"], ans: 1, exp: "Henderson-Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]). Maximum buffer capacity at [A⁻]=[HA] → pH = pKa. Buffer range: pKa ± 1." },
    { q: "Bleaching powder formula is:", opts: ["Ca(OCl)₂", "CaOCl₂ (mixture: Ca(OCl)Cl or Ca(ClO)Cl)", "NaOCl", "Cl₂"], ans: 1, exp: "Bleaching powder: CaOCl₂, or Ca(ClO)Cl = calcium hypochlorite and calcium chloride mixture. Made: Ca(OH)₂ + Cl₂ → CaOCl₂ + H₂O. Releases Cl₂/HOCl in water → bleaches." },
    { q: "Washing soda (Na₂CO₃·10H₂O) is used to:", opts: ["Bake bread", "Soften hard water by precipitating Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ as carbonates, and as a cleaning agent", "Make glass directly", "Disinfect water"], ans: 1, exp: "Washing soda: Na₂CO₃·10H₂O. Removes temporary AND permanent hardness: Ca²⁺ + CO₃²⁻ → CaCO₃↓. Also used in glass making, soap/detergent manufacture, paper industry." },
    { q: "Plaster of Paris (CaSO₄·½H₂O) sets when mixed with water because:", opts: ["CaSO₄ dissolves", "It reabsorbs water → CaSO₄·2H₂O (gypsum), expanding slightly and hardening", "It decomposes", "A chemical reaction produces CO₂"], ans: 1, exp: "Setting reaction: 2(CaSO₄·½H₂O) + 3H₂O → 2(CaSO₄·2H₂O). Gypsum crystals interlock → hard, rigid material. Slight expansion (0.3%) fills mould perfectly. Used in orthopaedic casts, sculptures." },
    { q: "Hard water fails to form lather with soap because:", opts: ["Soap is impure", "Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ ions react with soap (sodium stearate) → insoluble scum (calcium stearate), wasting soap", "Hard water is too cold", "Soap dissolves in hard water"], ans: 1, exp: "Hard water: Ca²⁺ + 2RCOONa → (RCOO)₂Ca↓ + 2Na⁺. Calcium/magnesium stearate is insoluble scum. Soap wasted until all Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ is precipitated. Detergents (sulfonates) work in hard water." },
    { q: "Zeolite process for water softening uses:", opts: ["Precipitation with Na₂CO₃", "Ion exchange: Zeolite-Na + Ca²⁺ → Zeolite-Ca + 2Na⁺ (removes Ca²⁺)", "Boiling", "Addition of Ca(OH)₂"], ans: 1, exp: "Zeolites (aluminosilicates): porous, exchange Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ for Na⁺ in water softening. Regenerated with brine (NaCl). Also used in detergents to replace phosphates (environmental benefit)." },
    { q: "pKa of acetic acid ≈ 4.76. What is Ka?", opts: ["4.76", "10⁻⁴·⁷⁶ ≈ 1.74×10⁻⁵", "10⁴·⁷⁶", "−4.76"], ans: 1, exp: "pKa = −log Ka → Ka = 10^{−pKa} = 10^{−4.76} ≈ 1.74×10⁻⁵. Weak acid (Ka << 1). Stronger weak acids have lower pKa (HF: pKa = 3.17, Ka = 6.7×10⁻⁴)." },
    { q: "Titration: at equivalence point of strong acid + strong base, pH =", opts: ["7 exactly at 25°C", "pKa of acid", "Below 7 always", "Above 7 always"], ans: 0, exp: "Strong acid + strong base → neutral salt solution (pH = 7 at 25°C). The salt (e.g., NaCl) doesn't hydrolyse. Weak acid + strong base → basic equivalence point (pH > 7). Weak base + strong acid → acidic (pH < 7)." },
    { q: "Phenolphthalein indicator changes colour in range:", opts: ["pH 3.1-4.4 (methyl orange range)", "pH 8.2-10.0 (colourless → pink)", "pH 5-7", "pH 1-2"], ans: 1, exp: "Phenolphthalein: colourless below pH 8.2, pink above 10. Used for weak acid/strong base titrations (equivalence point > 7). NOT suitable for strong acid/weak base titrations (equivalence point < 7)." },
    { q: "Common ion effect: adding NaCl to CH₃COONa/CH₃COOH buffer:", opts: ["Decreases pH", "No significant effect on pH (Cl⁻ is not the common ion)", "Increases buffer capacity", "Destroys the buffer"], ans: 1, exp: "NaCl provides Na⁺ and Cl⁻. Cl⁻ is not involved in the buffer equilibrium. Na⁺ has minimal effect. pH = pKa + log([CH₃COO⁻]/[CH₃COOH]) is unchanged. Common ion effect requires adding a species in the equilibrium." },
    { q: "Baking powder releases CO₂ by:", opts: ["Decomposition on heating only", "Acid-base reaction: NaHCO₃ + tartaric acid → CO₂ (when moistened, enhanced by heat)", "Fermentation", "Oxidation"], ans: 1, exp: "Baking powder: NaHCO₃ + tartaric acid → sodium tartrate + H₂O + CO₂↑. Double acting: reacts when wet AND when heated. CO₂ leavens baked goods." },
    { q: "Sodium carbonate solution is alkaline because Na₂CO₃ hydrolyses:", opts: ["CO₃²⁻ + H₂O ⇌ HCO₃⁻ + OH⁻ (hydrolysis of weak acid anion)", "Na⁺ reacts with water", "CO₂ forms and reacts", "Na₂CO₃ is a strong acid"], ans: 0, exp: "Salt hydrolysis: Na₂CO₃ is a salt of weak acid (H₂CO₃) + strong base (NaOH). Anion CO₃²⁻ hydrolyses: CO₃²⁻ + H₂O ⇌ HCO₃⁻ + OH⁻ → pH > 7. Na⁺ does not hydrolyse." },
    { q: "Which acid is used in car batteries (lead-acid)?", opts: ["HNO₃", "HCl", "H₂SO₄ (dilute, ~30%)", "H₃PO₄"], ans: 2, exp: "Lead-acid battery electrolyte: ~30% H₂SO₄ (dilute). Anode: Pb → PbSO₄; Cathode: PbO₂ → PbSO₄. Both electrodes form PbSO₄ → specific gravity of H₂SO₄ drops when discharged → measurable." },
    { q: "The universal indicator shows purple/dark blue at very high pH because:", opts: ["It contains only phenolphthalein", "It is a mixture of indicators with different colour changes covering pH 1-14", "It reacts with base", "It decomposes at high pH"], ans: 1, exp: "Universal indicator: mixture of several indicators (methyl orange, litmus, bromothymol blue, phenolphthalein, etc.) showing different colours across pH 1-14. Red (very acid) → orange → yellow → green → blue → violet (very basic)." },
    { q: "When NH₄Cl dissolves in water, the solution is acidic because:", opts: ["HCl forms", "NH₄⁺ hydrolyses: NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺ (weak base cation)", "Cl⁻ is acidic", "NH₄Cl is a strong acid"], ans: 1, exp: "NH₄Cl: salt of weak base (NH₃) + strong acid (HCl). NH₄⁺ hydrolyses to give H₃O⁺ → acidic solution (pH < 7). Cl⁻ does not hydrolyse (conjugate base of strong acid)." },
    { q: "Acid-base neutralisation reaction: H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O. This is also a:", opts: ["Redox reaction", "Precipitation reaction", "Double displacement reaction (H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O)", "Combination reaction"], ans: 2, exp: "Neutralisation is a double displacement reaction: acid and base exchange ions. H⁺ from acid + OH⁻ from base → H₂O (net ionic equation: H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O). No oxidation state changes." },
    { q: "pH of a 10⁻⁸ M HCl solution (very dilute) is approximately:", opts: ["8", "−8", "6.96 (water's contribution to H⁺ cannot be ignored)", "1"], ans: 2, exp: "At 10⁻⁸ M HCl, H⁺ from HCl ≈ 10⁻⁸ M, but H⁺ from water = 10⁻⁷ M (dominant!). Total [H⁺] ≈ 10⁻⁷ + 10⁻⁸ = 1.1×10⁻⁷. pH = −log(1.1×10⁻⁷) ≈ 6.96. Cannot ignore water's autoionisation at very high dilution." },
    { q: "Polyprotic acid H₃PO₄ has three Ka values: Ka₁ >> Ka₂ >> Ka₃. This is because:", opts: ["Each step removes H⁺ from increasingly negative/stable anion (harder each time)", "Each step is at different temperature", "Ka₁ = Ka₂ = Ka₃ always", "Ka increases with ionisation step"], ans: 0, exp: "H₃PO₄ → H₂PO₄⁻ (Ka₁=7.5×10⁻³) → HPO₄²⁻ (Ka₂=6.2×10⁻⁸) → PO₄³⁻ (Ka₃=4.8×10⁻¹³). Each successive H⁺ removed from more negatively charged species — electrostatically harder." },
    { q: "Solubility product Ksp defines:", opts: ["Solubility in mol/L directly", "Equilibrium constant for dissolving ionic solid: MX ⇌ M⁺ + X⁻; Ksp = [M⁺][X⁻]", "Rate of dissolution", "Hydration energy"], ans: 1, exp: "Ksp = product of ion concentrations at saturation (equilibrium). For Ca₃(PO₄)₂: Ksp = [Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]². Precipitation occurs when ionic product Q > Ksp. Q < Ksp: unsaturated (dissolves). Q = Ksp: saturated." },
    { q: "Autoionisation of water: H₂O ⇌ H⁺ + OH⁻, Kw = 10⁻¹⁴ at 25°C. At 37°C (body temp), Kw:", opts: ["Stays 10⁻¹⁴", "Decreases", "Increases (endothermic process, more ionisation at higher T)", "Becomes infinity"], ans: 2, exp: "Autoionisation of water is endothermic. Higher T → higher Kw. At 37°C: Kw ≈ 2.4×10⁻¹⁴ → neutral pH at 37°C is about 6.81 (not 7.0). pH meter calibration is temperature-dependent." },
    { q: "Superacids (e.g., \"Magic acid\" SbF₅/HF) are:", opts: ["Dilute acids", "Acids stronger than 100% H₂SO₄, capable of protonating even hydrocarbons", "Weak acids at high concentration", "Bases in disguise"], ans: 1, exp: "Superacids: Hammett acidity function H₀ < −12 (stronger than 100% H₂SO₄). Examples: HF/SbF₅ (\"Magic acid\"), HSO₃F/SbF₅ (\"fluoroantimonic acid\"). Can protonate alkanes, benzene. Used in petrochemistry." },
    { q: "Amphiprotic species can:", opts: ["Only donate H⁺", "Only accept H⁺", "Both donate and accept H⁺ (e.g., HCO₃⁻, H₂O, HPO₄²⁻)", "Neither"], ans: 2, exp: "Amphiprotic: can act as both acid and base. HCO₃⁻: acid (donates H⁺ → CO₃²⁻) or base (accepts H⁺ → H₂CO₃). H₂O is amphiprotic. Important in buffer systems." },
    { q: "Which salt gives a neutral aqueous solution?", opts: ["CH₃COONa (alkaline)", "NH₄Cl (acidic)", "NaCl (neutral — strong acid + strong base)", "Na₂CO₃ (alkaline)"], ans: 2, exp: "NaCl = strong acid HCl + strong base NaOH. Neither Na⁺ nor Cl⁻ hydrolyses. pH = 7. CH₃COONa and Na₂CO₃ are alkaline (anion hydrolyses). NH₄Cl is acidic (cation hydrolyses)." },
    { q: "The degree of neutralisation at equivalence point (mol acid = mol base) gives what indicator reading for NaOH + HCl?", opts: ["Indicator at mid-point of transition, pH = 7", "Only phenolphthalein works", "Any indicator works (steep curve means any indicator in range 4-10 is OK)", "Litmus is most accurate"], ans: 2, exp: "Strong acid/strong base titration: very steep pH curve at equivalence point (pH jump from ~3 to ~11). Any indicator that changes between pH 4-10 works accurately: methyl orange, litmus, phenolphthalein." },
    { q: "Brine electrolysis (industrial chlor-alkali process) produces all EXCEPT:", opts: ["H₂", "Cl₂", "NaOH", "HCl (not directly produced)"], ans: 3, exp: "Chlor-alkali: electrolysis of brine → H₂ (cathode), Cl₂ (anode), NaOH (cathode solution). HCl is NOT a direct product (though it can be made by combining H₂ and Cl₂ separately)." },
    { q: "Acid rain pH is typically below:", opts: ["7.0", "6.5", "5.6 (natural rain CO₂ → H₂CO₃ gives pH ~5.6; below this is acid rain)", "4.0"], ans: 2, exp: "Natural rainwater (CO₂ dissolved) has pH ~5.6. \"Acid rain\" is defined as pH < 5.6, caused by SO₂ and NOₓ producing H₂SO₃/H₂SO₄ and HNO₃. Can reach pH 4 near industrial areas." },
    { q: "Which substance is used to treat excessive stomach acidity (antacid)?", opts: ["HCl", "NaOH (too caustic)", "Mg(OH)₂, Al(OH)₃, or NaHCO₃ (mild bases)", "H₂SO₄"], ans: 2, exp: "Antacids: Mg(OH)₂ (milk of magnesia), Al(OH)₃ (Maalox), CaCO₃ (Tums), NaHCO₃ (baking soda). Neutralise HCl: Mg(OH)₂ + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + 2H₂O. Mild — won't over-alkalize." },
    { q: "In the reaction: CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O, which is the Bronsted acid?", opts: ["NaOH", "CH₃COONa", "CH₃COOH (donates H⁺ to OH⁻)", "H₂O"], ans: 2, exp: "Bronsted acid = proton donor. CH₃COOH donates H⁺ to OH⁻ (the Bronsted base). Product CH₃COO⁻ is the conjugate base; H₂O is the conjugate acid of OH⁻." },
    { q: "Enthalpy of neutralisation for weak acid + strong base is LESS than −57 kJ/mol because:", opts: ["More energy released", "Some energy is used to ionise the weak acid first (ionisation energy is absorbed)", "The reaction is endothermic", "Strong base is wasteful"], ans: 1, exp: "Weak acid ionisation: CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺ (ΔH = +ve). Net neutralisation = ionisation + H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O. Energy absorbed for ionisation reduces overall exothermicity below 57 kJ/mol." },
    { q: "Which acid is used for etching glass?", opts: ["HCl", "HNO₃", "HF (reacts with SiO₂: SiO₂ + 4HF → SiF₄ + 2H₂O)", "H₂SO₄"], ans: 2, exp: "HF is the only acid that attacks glass (SiO₂). SiO₂ + 4HF → SiF₄ + 2H₂O. HF is extremely dangerous (absorbs through skin, causes deep tissue damage and hypocalcaemia). Stored in plastic containers." },
    { q: "Carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) in blood acts as a buffer with:", opts: ["NaOH", "HCO₃⁻/CO₂ buffer system (bicarbonate buffer): H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻", "Phosphate buffer only", "Haemoglobin only"], ans: 1, exp: "Blood bicarbonate buffer (most important): H₂CO₃/HCO₃⁻ system. Normal pH = 7.4. CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻. Lungs control pCO₂, kidneys control [HCO₃⁻]. Together maintain pH 7.35-7.45." },
    { q: "Indicators are weak acids/bases. In phenolphthalein (HIn), the colour change occurs when:", opts: ["Temperature changes", "[HIn] = [In⁻] (transition point) i.e., pH = pKIn (pH around 9)", "Only strong acids are added", "Concentration of indicator is high"], ans: 1, exp: "Indicator: HIn (one colour) ⇌ H⁺ + In⁻ (different colour). Colour changes when pH ≈ pKIn (equal amounts of each form). Human eye detects colour change when ratio [In⁻]/[HIn] > 10 or < 0.1 → transition range = pKIn ± 1." },
    { q: "Hydrofluoric acid (HF) is a weak acid despite F being the most electronegative element because:", opts: ["F is too large", "H−F bond is very strong (short, high bond energy) → low dissociation → weak acid", "F doesn't react with water", "HF is a gas"], ans: 1, exp: "HF: bond enthalpy = 565 kJ/mol (very high). Hard to break → low Ka = 6.6×10⁻⁴. Compare HCl (bond enthalpy 431 kJ/mol, Ka >> 1 = strong acid). Bond strength > electronegativity for acid strength in halogens." },
    { q: "The common ion effect decreases solubility of AgCl in NaCl solution because:", opts: ["Na⁺ precipitates AgCl", "Cl⁻ from NaCl shifts AgCl ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ equilibrium LEFT → less AgCl dissolves", "NaCl increases Ksp", "Temperature decreases"], ans: 1, exp: "Common ion effect: adding Cl⁻ (from NaCl) to saturated AgCl → Q > Ksp → AgCl precipitates until new equilibrium. [Ag⁺] decreases. Solubility of AgCl in 0.1M NaCl ≈ Ksp/0.1 = 1.8×10⁻⁹ mol/L (much less than 1.34×10⁻⁵ in pure water)." },
    { q: "Which substance acts as a desiccant (drying agent) by absorbing moisture?", opts: ["NaCl", "KNO₃", "P₂O₅ or silica gel (SiO₂·xH₂O) — absorbs water strongly", "Na₂SO₄ (anhydrous absorbs water but is milder)"], ans: 2, exp: "Desiccants absorb water. P₂O₅: reacts with water (P₂O₅ + H₂O → H₃PO₄) — powerful desiccant. Silica gel (SiO₂·xH₂O): physical adsorption, indicator changes colour when saturated, can be regenerated. Used in medicine packaging, electronics." },
    { q: "Carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) Ka₁ = 4.3×10⁻⁷. What is the approximate pH of a saturated CO₂ solution (0.034 M H₂CO₃)?", opts: ["3.84", "5.6", "7.0", "4.0"], ans: 1, exp: "[H⁺] ≈ √(Ka × c) = √(4.3×10⁻⁷ × 0.034) = √(1.46×10⁻⁸) ≈ 1.2×10⁻⁴. But actually actual CO₂(aq) ≈ 0.034M. pH = −log(1.2×10⁻⁴) ≈ 3.9. Natural rain ≈ pH 5.6 (much more dilute CO₂). Carbonated drinks: ~pH 3.9." },
    { q: "Hardness of water that can be removed by boiling is called:", opts: ["Permanent hardness", "Temporary hardness (Ca(HCO₃)₂ → CaCO₃↓ + H₂O + CO₂ on boiling)", "Both types", "Neither"], ans: 1, exp: "Temporary hardness: due to Ca(HCO₃)₂ and Mg(HCO₃)₂. Boiling: Ca(HCO₃)₂ → CaCO₃↓ + H₂O + CO₂↑. CaCO₃ removed. Permanent hardness (CaCl₂, CaSO₄): not removed by boiling → needs chemical treatment." },
    { q: "Calculate the pH of a 0.001 M H₂SO₄ solution (assume complete dissociation of both protons).", opts: ["3", "2.7", "Approximately 2.3", "1"], ans: 2, exp: "H₂SO₄ → 2H⁺ + SO₄²⁻. For 0.001 M H₂SO₄: [H⁺] = 2 × 0.001 = 0.002 M = 2×10⁻³ M. pH = −log(2×10⁻³) = −log(2) − log(10⁻³) = −0.301 + 3 = 2.699 ≈ 2.7. Note: at high concentration, the 2nd proton may not fully dissociate, but at 0.001 M we assume complete dissociation of both." },
    { q: "Water of crystallisation in CuSO₄·5H₂O is exactly 5 molecules of H₂O. If this salt is heated gently to about 100°C, it loses 4 water molecules first (forming CuSO₄·H₂O). The colour change observed is:", opts: ["Blue → white (complete anhydrous)", "Blue → pale blue-white (monohydrate) — full white only at ~250°C", "Blue → green → white", "Blue → yellow → white"], ans: 1, exp: "CuSO₄·5H₂O (bright blue) loses 4 H₂O at ~100°C → CuSO₄·H₂O (pale blue/greenish white). The last H₂O is more tightly bound; complete dehydration to white anhydrous CuSO₄ requires ~250°C. Adding water to white anhydrous CuSO₄ → blue colour restored + heat released. This is a test for presence of water." },
    { q: "Which of the following aqueous salt solutions will have the LOWEST pH?", opts: ["0.1 M NaCl", "0.1 M CH₃COONa", "0.1 M NH₄Cl", "0.1 M Na₂CO₃"], ans: 2, exp: "Salt hydrolysis: NaCl: neutral (pH=7, no hydrolysis). CH₃COONa: basic (anion of weak acid hydrolyses → pH >7). NH₄Cl: acidic (cation of weak base hydrolyses: NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺ → pH <7). Na₂CO₃: strongly basic (CO₃²⁻ + H₂O ⇌ HCO₃⁻ + OH⁻ → pH >10). NH₄Cl gives lowest pH." },
    { q: "Industrial production of NaOH (caustic soda) uses which process, and what are its co-products?", opts: ["Solvay process; co-product is Na₂CO₃", "Chlor-alkali process (electrolysis of brine); co-products are Cl₂ and H₂", "Leblanc process; co-product is HCl", "Lime-soda process; co-product is CaCO₃"], ans: 1, exp: "Chlor-alkali process: electrolysis of concentrated NaCl solution (brine). Cathode: 2H₂O + 2e⁻ → H₂↑ + 2OH⁻ (+ Na⁺ → NaOH). Anode: 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂↑ + 2e⁻. Three products: NaOH, Cl₂, H₂ — all industrially valuable. NaOH: soap, paper, textile. Cl₂: PVC, disinfection. H₂: ammonia (Haber process), fuel cells." },
    { q: "Hydrated ferric oxide (rust, Fe₂O₃·xH₂O) formation is an example of:", opts: ["Acid-base reaction", "Salt formation with water of crystallisation (x varies — rust is non-stoichiometric)", "Simple combination reaction", "Decomposition reaction"], ans: 1, exp: "Rust (Fe₂O₃·xH₂O) is a hydrated ferric oxide — it contains variable amounts of water of crystallisation (x is not fixed). This is why rust is written with \"xH₂O.\" Similar to CuSO₄·5H₂O but non-stoichiometric. The water is held in the crystal lattice. Rust is porous (unlike Al₂O₃) and allows further corrosion." },
    { q: "The pH of blood must be maintained at 7.35-7.45. A patient with pH 7.25 is in:", opts: ["Alkalosis (blood too basic)", "Acidosis (blood too acidic — pH below 7.35)", "Normal range", "Neutral pH — no problem"], ans: 1, exp: "Blood pH 7.25 < 7.35 → metabolic or respiratory acidosis. Causes: diabetic ketoacidosis (excess ketone acids), kidney failure (cannot excrete H⁺), severe diarrhoea (loss of HCO₃⁻), or respiratory failure (CO₂ retention → H₂CO₃ excess). pH < 7.0 is life-threatening. Treatment: correct underlying cause; may administer NaHCO₃ IV." },
    { q: "Which of the following is the correct formula and use of washing soda?", opts: ["Na₂CO₃ (anhydrous), used as antacid", "Na₂CO₃·10H₂O, used in glass making, soap/paper industry, water softening, and as a laboratory reagent", "NaHCO₃, used in baking", "NaOH, used as a cleaner"], ans: 1, exp: "Washing soda: Na₂CO₃·10H₂O (sodium carbonate decahydrate). Formula contains 10 water of crystallisation molecules. Uses: (i) removes permanent and temporary hard water (Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ precipitated as carbonates), (ii) glass manufacturing (reduces SiO₂ melting point), (iii) soap/paper/textile industry as alkali, (iv) pH adjustment in swimming pools. Efflorescent in dry air (loses water)." },
    { q: "A student prepares a solution by dissolving 4 g of NaOH (M=40) in water to make 200 mL of solution. What is the pH of this solution?", opts: ["pH = 13", "pH = 1", "pH = 7", "pH = 11"], ans: 0, exp: "Moles NaOH = 4/40 = 0.1 mol. Volume = 0.200 L. [NaOH] = 0.1/0.2 = 0.5 mol/L. NaOH is a strong base: [OH⁻] = 0.5 M. pOH = −log(0.5) = −log(5×10⁻¹) = 1−log5 ≈ 1−0.699 = 0.301. pH = 14 − pOH = 14 − 0.301 ≈ 13.7 ≈ 13. At this concentration, pH ≈ 13." },
    { q: "Plaster of Paris is chemically hemihydrate of calcium sulphate: CaSO₄·½H₂O. It is manufactured by heating gypsum (CaSO₄·2H₂O) to what temperature?", opts: ["100°C (boiling water)", "120-130°C (in a rotating kiln or autoclave)", "500°C", "1000°C"], ans: 1, exp: "Gypsum → Plaster of Paris: 2(CaSO₄·2H₂O) → 2(CaSO₄·½H₂O) + 3H₂O. Heating at ~120-130°C drives off 3 out of 4 water molecules. At higher temperatures (>180°C): soluble anhydrite forms (CaSO₄, slow-setting). At very high temperatures: dead-burned gypsum (insoluble, does not set). Precise temperature control is critical in manufacturing." }
  ]
,

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════
     CH 3 — METALS AND NON-METALS
  ══════════════════════════════════════ */
  3: [
    { q: "The property of metals to be beaten into thin sheets is called:", opts: ["Ductility", "Malleability", "Sonority", "Conductivity"], ans: 1, exp: "Malleability: metals can be hammered/rolled into thin sheets (e.g., gold leaf). Ductility: drawn into thin wires. Sonority: produce a ringing sound when struck." },
    { q: "Which non-metal is a good conductor of electricity?", opts: ["Sulphur", "Phosphorus", "Graphite (carbon)", "Iodine"], ans: 2, exp: "Graphite (an allotrope of carbon) has free electrons in its layered structure and conducts electricity. All other non-metals are poor conductors (insulators)." },
    { q: "Which metal is the best conductor of electricity?", opts: ["Copper", "Gold", "Silver", "Aluminium"], ans: 2, exp: "Silver has the highest electrical conductivity of all metals. Copper is second and is more commonly used due to lower cost." },
    { q: "Mercury is unique among metals because:", opts: ["It is the heaviest metal", "It is liquid at room temperature", "It has the highest melting point", "It is non-lustrous"], ans: 1, exp: "Mercury (Hg) is the only metal that is liquid at room temperature (melting point −39°C). It is used in thermometers and barometers." },
    { q: "The most abundant metal in the Earth's crust is:", opts: ["Iron", "Copper", "Aluminium", "Silicon"], ans: 2, exp: "Aluminium (~8% by mass) is the most abundant metal in Earth's crust. It is found mainly as bauxite (Al₂O₃·2H₂O). Silicon is most abundant overall but is a metalloid." },
    { q: "Which metal can be cut with a knife?", opts: ["Iron", "Copper", "Sodium", "Gold"], ans: 2, exp: "Sodium (and potassium) are very soft metals — they can be cut with a knife. They are stored in kerosene to prevent reaction with moisture and oxygen." },
    { q: "The non-metal that is liquid at room temperature is:", opts: ["Iodine", "Sulphur", "Bromine", "Chlorine"], ans: 2, exp: "Bromine (Br₂) is the only non-metal that is liquid at room temperature. Iodine and sulphur are solids; chlorine is a gas." },
    { q: "When sodium reacts with cold water, the observation is:", opts: ["Sinks and reacts slowly", "Floats, moves about, melts into a silvery ball, and may ignite", "Dissolves quietly", "No reaction"], ans: 1, exp: "2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂↑ (exothermic). Na floats (less dense), the heat melts it into a ball, and the H₂ produced may catch fire (Na is very reactive)." },
    { q: "The reactivity series from most to least reactive is:", opts: ["Au > Cu > Fe > Mg > Na", "Na > Mg > Al > Zn > Fe > Cu > Au", "Fe > Na > Cu > Mg > Au", "Cu > Na > Fe > Al > Mg"], ans: 1, exp: "Correct order: K > Na > Ca > Mg > Al > Zn > Fe > Pb > H > Cu > Hg > Ag > Au. Na is above Mg, which is above Fe, which is above Cu." },
    { q: "Copper does NOT react with dilute HCl because:", opts: ["Copper is a noble metal", "Copper is below hydrogen in the reactivity series", "Copper is too heavy", "HCl is a weak acid"], ans: 1, exp: "Only metals above hydrogen in the reactivity series can displace H₂ from acids. Copper is below H, so it cannot displace hydrogen from dilute acids." },
    { q: "The process of heating a sulphide ore in excess air to convert it to oxide is:", opts: ["Calcination", "Roasting", "Smelting", "Refining"], ans: 1, exp: "Roasting: 2ZnS + 3O₂ → 2ZnO + 2SO₂. Heating a sulphide ore in excess air converts it to oxide. Calcination is for carbonate/hydroxide ores in limited air." },
    { q: "Aluminium is extracted from its ore by:", opts: ["Reduction with carbon", "Reduction with CO", "Electrolysis of molten alumina (Al₂O₃)", "Roasting"], ans: 2, exp: "Aluminium is very reactive — carbon cannot reduce Al₂O₃ at practical temperatures. Electrolysis of molten Al₂O₃ (with cryolite to lower melting point) is used instead." },
    { q: "An alloy is:", opts: ["A pure metal", "A mixture of two or more metals (or metal + non-metal)", "A metallic compound", "A single element in different forms"], ans: 1, exp: "Alloys are homogeneous mixtures. Examples: steel (Fe + C), brass (Cu + Zn), bronze (Cu + Sn), solder (Pb + Sn), amalgam (Hg + another metal)." },
    { q: "Steel is an alloy of iron and:", opts: ["Aluminium", "Copper", "Carbon", "Zinc"], ans: 2, exp: "Steel = iron + carbon (0.02-2%). Carbon increases hardness and strength. Stainless steel also contains chromium and nickel for corrosion resistance." },
    { q: "The thermite reaction (Fe₂O₃ + 2Al → Al₂O₃ + 2Fe) is used to:", opts: ["Extract aluminium", "Weld broken railway tracks", "Make explosives", "Purify iron"], ans: 1, exp: "The thermite reaction releases enormous heat (≈3000°C), melting iron which flows into the gap between broken rails and solidifies on cooling — thermite welding." },
    { q: "Which property distinguishes metals from non-metals most reliably?", opts: ["Colour", "Electrical conductivity", "Melting point", "Solubility"], ans: 1, exp: "Metals are generally good electrical conductors; non-metals are poor conductors (except graphite). Melting points, colours, and solubilities vary widely even within each group." },
    { q: "Ionic compounds conduct electricity:", opts: ["In solid state only", "In molten state and in aqueous solution only", "In all states", "Only in solution"], ans: 1, exp: "Ionic compounds have ions fixed in lattice (solid) — no conduction. In molten state or dissolved in water, ions are free to move and carry current." },
    { q: "The ion formed when a metal loses electrons is called a:", opts: ["Anion", "Cation", "Molecule", "Radical"], ans: 1, exp: "Metals lose electrons to form positively charged ions called cations (e.g., Na → Na⁺ + e⁻). Non-metals gain electrons to form anions (e.g., Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻)." },
    { q: "Rusting of iron is prevented by galvanisation. Galvanisation coats iron with:", opts: ["Tin", "Copper", "Zinc", "Silver"], ans: 2, exp: "Galvanisation = coating iron with zinc. Zinc is more reactive than iron — even if the coating is scratched, zinc corrodes preferentially, protecting the iron (sacrificial protection)." },
    { q: "The ore of aluminium is:", opts: ["Haematite", "Bauxite", "Magnetite", "Galena"], ans: 1, exp: "Bauxite (Al₂O₃·2H₂O) is the main ore of aluminium. Haematite and magnetite are iron ores. Galena (PbS) is a lead ore." },
    { q: "Which metal reacts vigorously with dilute H₂SO₄ among these?", opts: ["Gold", "Silver", "Copper", "Magnesium"], ans: 3, exp: "Mg is well above hydrogen in the reactivity series: Mg + H₂SO₄ → MgSO₄ + H₂↑ (vigorously, with lots of bubbles). Au, Ag, Cu do not react with dilute H₂SO₄." },
    { q: "Non-metals generally form oxides that are:", opts: ["Basic", "Neutral", "Acidic", "Amphoteric"], ans: 2, exp: "Non-metals form acidic oxides: SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃; CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃. Metals form basic oxides (CaO, MgO). Al₂O₃ is amphoteric." },
    { q: "The refining of copper is done by:", opts: ["Froth flotation", "Electrolytic refining", "Distillation", "Magnetic separation"], ans: 1, exp: "Impure copper is the anode; pure copper is the cathode; CuSO₄ solution is the electrolyte. Copper dissolves from anode and deposits as pure copper at cathode. Impurities settle as \"anode mud\"." },
    { q: "Brass is an alloy of:", opts: ["Cu + Sn", "Cu + Zn", "Fe + Ni", "Al + Cu"], ans: 1, exp: "Brass = copper + zinc (typically 70% Cu, 30% Zn). Bronze = copper + tin. Brass is used in musical instruments, locks, and fittings." },
    { q: "Which of the following metals reacts with cold water AND hot water AND steam?", opts: ["Magnesium", "Iron", "Sodium", "Copper"], ans: 2, exp: "Sodium reacts vigorously even with cold water. Magnesium reacts only with hot water or steam slowly. Iron reacts only with steam. Copper does not react with water." },
    { q: "The process of obtaining a metal from its ore involves:", opts: ["Concentration of ore → reduction → refining", "Just heating the ore", "Just dissolving in acid", "Only electrolysis"], ans: 0, exp: "Extraction involves: (1) concentration/dressing of ore, (2) reduction to metal (smelting/electrolysis), and (3) refining (purification) if needed." },
    { q: "Which property of gold makes it ideal for jewellery?", opts: ["High reactivity", "High melting point only", "Lustrous, malleable, and highly unreactive (noble)", "Magnetic properties"], ans: 2, exp: "Gold is lustrous (beautiful sheen), highly malleable (can be shaped), and chemically inert (does not tarnish or corrode) — ideal for long-lasting jewellery." },
    { q: "The anode mud in electrolytic refining contains:", opts: ["Pure copper", "Impurities (like Ag, Au, Pt) that did not dissolve", "The electrolyte salt", "Copper sulphate"], ans: 1, exp: "When impure copper anode dissolves, less reactive/precious metals (Au, Ag, Pt) do not dissolve and collect below the anode as anode mud — economically valuable." },
    { q: "Carbon (as coke) cannot reduce Al₂O₃ because:", opts: ["Al₂O₃ has no oxygen", "Aluminium is more reactive than carbon — Al₂O₃ is more stable than CO₂/CO", "Carbon reacts with aluminium", "The reaction is too slow"], ans: 1, exp: "Reduction with carbon works only when the metal oxide is less stable than CO₂ (i.e., the metal is less reactive than carbon in the reactivity series). Al is more reactive than C, so electrolysis is needed." },
    { q: "Iron reacts with HCl to form:", opts: ["Fe₂Cl₃ + H₂", "FeCl₂ + H₂", "FeCl₃ + H₂O", "FeO + HCl"], ans: 1, exp: "Fe + 2HCl → FeCl₂ + H₂↑. Iron reacts with dilute HCl forming iron(II) chloride (ferrous chloride) and hydrogen. Iron shows +2 state with dilute acids." },
    { q: "Which of these is NOT a characteristic property of metals?", opts: ["Ductile", "Malleable", "Brittle", "Sonorous"], ans: 2, exp: "Brittleness is a property of non-metals (solid non-metals are brittle and shatter when struck). Metals are malleable and ductile (not brittle)." },
    { q: "The process of electroplating uses electrolysis to:", opts: ["Extract metals from ores", "Deposit a metal layer on an object", "Refine metals only", "All of the above"], ans: 1, exp: "Electroplating deposits a thin layer of a desired metal on an object for decoration or protection. The object is the cathode; the plating metal is the anode." },
    { q: "Sodium is stored in kerosene oil because:", opts: ["Kerosene prevents sublimation", "Sodium is less dense than kerosene", "Kerosene prevents reaction with air and moisture", "Kerosene dissolves sodium slowly"], ans: 2, exp: "Sodium is extremely reactive — it tarnishes instantly in air and reacts vigorously with moisture. Kerosene (a hydrocarbon) does not react with sodium, providing safe storage." },
    { q: "Stainless steel contains iron, carbon, and:", opts: ["Copper and zinc", "Chromium and nickel", "Tin and lead", "Aluminium and magnesium"], ans: 1, exp: "Stainless steel = Fe + C + Cr (12-20%) + Ni. Chromium forms a passive Cr₂O₃ layer preventing rusting; nickel adds toughness. Used in kitchen utensils and cutlery." },
    { q: "Which phenomenon is exhibited when a zinc rod is placed in a copper sulphate solution?", opts: ["Corrosion of copper", "Deposition of zinc on copper", "Dissolution of zinc and deposition of copper", "No reaction"], ans: 2, exp: "Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu. Zinc dissolves (goes into solution as Zn²⁺) and copper deposits on the zinc rod. This confirms Zn is more reactive than Cu." },
    { q: "Which metal is used in making of aircraft due to its lightness and strength?", opts: ["Iron", "Copper", "Aluminium (duralumin alloy)", "Gold"], ans: 2, exp: "Duralumin (Al + Cu + Mg + Mn) is lightweight, strong, and corrosion-resistant — ideal for aircraft construction. Pure aluminium is too soft." },
    { q: "In ionic bonding, metals typically:", opts: ["Gain electrons to form anions", "Lose electrons to form cations", "Share electrons", "Do not form bonds"], ans: 1, exp: "Metals have low ionisation energies and lose outer electrons easily. Na → Na⁺ + e⁻. Non-metals (high electronegativity) accept these electrons: Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻." },
    { q: "Copper vessels turn green over time due to formation of:", opts: ["CuO (black)", "Cu₂O (red)", "Basic copper carbonate (green)", "CuS (black)"], ans: 2, exp: "Over time, Cu reacts with CO₂, O₂, and moisture to form green basic copper carbonate [CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂]. This is why old copper coins and statues appear green." },
    { q: "Which property allows aluminium to be used in making cooking utensils?", opts: ["High melting point only", "Good conductor of heat, light, corrosion-resistant", "Magnetic property", "Low melting point"], ans: 1, exp: "Aluminium conducts heat well (fast cooking), is lightweight, corrosion-resistant (Al₂O₃ layer), and food-safe. These combined properties make it ideal for cookware." },
    { q: "The specific property used to separate iron filings from sulphur powder is:", opts: ["Density", "Solubility in water", "Magnetic property of iron", "Colour difference"], ans: 2, exp: "Iron is magnetic; sulphur is not. A magnet separates iron filings from a mixture with sulphur. This is magnetic separation — a physical method." },
    { q: "When magnesium burns in CO₂: 2Mg + CO₂ → 2MgO + C. This shows:", opts: ["CO₂ supports combustion", "Mg is more reactive than carbon — can displace C from CO₂", "CO₂ is a reducing agent", "Mg forms an acid"], ans: 1, exp: "Mg is so reactive it can even burn in CO₂, displacing carbon. This proves CO₂ does NOT always extinguish fires (e.g., Mg fires should not be doused with CO₂)." },
    { q: "The ore galena (PbS) is a mineral of which metal?", opts: ["Gold", "Lead", "Copper", "Mercury"], ans: 1, exp: "Galena (PbS) = lead sulphide — the main ore of lead. Cinnabar (HgS) = mercury ore. Malachite [Cu₂(OH)₂CO₃] = copper ore. Pyrite (FeS₂) = iron ore (fool's gold)." },
    { q: "Non-metals generally have:", opts: ["High melting points", "High density", "Low melting and boiling points", "Metallic lustre"], ans: 2, exp: "Non-metals (except diamond and graphite) generally have low melting and boiling points. They lack metallic lustre and are typically poor conductors." },
    { q: "Which of the following metals is found in native (free) state in nature?", opts: ["Iron", "Aluminium", "Gold", "Sodium"], ans: 2, exp: "Gold is so unreactive (noble metal) that it exists as native gold (free metal) in nature. Reactive metals are always found combined as ores/minerals." },
    { q: "Amalgam is an alloy where one metal is always:", opts: ["Iron", "Copper", "Mercury", "Lead"], ans: 2, exp: "Amalgam is any alloy containing mercury. Examples: tooth filling amalgam (Hg + Ag + Sn + Cu), sodium amalgam. Hg dissolves other metals at or near room temperature." },
    { q: "The main reason metals are good conductors of electricity is:", opts: ["They have large atoms", "They have free/delocalised electrons", "They are dense", "They form ionic bonds"], ans: 1, exp: "In metallic bonding, outer electrons are delocalised (free to move throughout the metal lattice). Under a potential difference, these mobile electrons carry electric current." },
    { q: "Which of these is NOT a use of aluminium?", opts: ["Aircraft bodies", "Electrical wires", "Jewellery (as fine gold)", "Cooking foil"], ans: 2, exp: "Aluminium is used in aircraft, wires (overhead power lines), and packaging foil. It is NOT used as jewellery (unlike gold/silver) because it lacks the same aesthetic/rarity value." },
    { q: "The reaction of a metal with acid producing H₂ is called:", opts: ["Oxidation of acid", "Displacement reaction", "Double displacement", "Combination"], ans: 1, exp: "Metal + Acid → Salt + H₂. The metal displaces hydrogen (H) from the acid. This is a single displacement reaction; only metals above H in reactivity series do this." },
    { q: "Iron corrodes faster in:", opts: ["Dry air", "Distilled water with no oxygen", "Salt water (saline solution)", "Kerosene"], ans: 2, exp: "Salt water accelerates corrosion because dissolved salts increase electrolyte conductivity, speeding up the electrochemical corrosion process. Coastal areas see faster rusting." },
    { q: "The property of metals to produce a ringing sound when struck is:", opts: ["Malleability", "Ductility", "Sonority", "Conductivity"], ans: 2, exp: "Sonority is the property of metals to produce a ringing/bell-like sound when struck. Used in making bells and musical instruments. Non-metals produce a dull sound." },
    { q: "Thermite reaction (Fe₂O₃ + 2Al → Al₂O₃ + 2Fe). Which metal is more reactive?", opts: ["Fe", "Al (higher in reactivity series → displaces Fe)", "Both equally", "Neither reacts"], ans: 1, exp: "Thermite: Al is more reactive than Fe → reduces Fe₂O₃. Extremely exothermic (>2500°C). Used for welding railway tracks, incendiary devices. Also demonstrates aluminothermic reduction." },
    { q: "Froth flotation: ore particles (sulphide ores) are separated because:", opts: ["They are denser than gangue", "Sulphide ore surfaces are hydrophobic → preferentially attach to air bubbles → float as froth", "They dissolve in oil", "Electric fields separate them"], ans: 1, exp: "Froth flotation: water + pine oil + frothing agent + air bubbles. Hydrophobic sulphide ore (PbS, ZnS, Cu₂S) particles wet by pine oil → attach to bubbles → float. Hydrophilic gangue (silica, clay) sinks." },
    { q: "In zone refining (purification of semiconductors), why do impurities concentrate at one end?", opts: ["Impurities are magnetic", "Impurities are more soluble in molten metal than in solid → stay in moving molten zone → carried to one end", "Temperature gradient melts them", "Gravity separates them"], ans: 1, exp: "Zone refining: molten zone moved slowly along metal rod. Impurities (distribution coefficient k < 1) preferentially remain in the molten zone rather than recrystallising. Zone sweeps impurities to one end. Used for ultra-pure Ge, Si." },
    { q: "Calcination involves heating ore:", opts: ["In excess air to convert sulphide to oxide", "In limited air or inert atmosphere to drive off moisture and volatile impurities (CO₂, H₂O)", "With coke and limestone", "In water to dissolve soluble minerals"], ans: 1, exp: "Calcination: heating without air (or limited air) to drive off CO₂, H₂O and other volatile compounds. Example: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. Roasting: heating in air to convert sulphide to oxide. Different processes." },
    { q: "Roasting converts sulphide ores: 2ZnS + 3O₂ → 2ZnO + 2SO₂. The SO₂ produced is:", opts: ["Vented to atmosphere (harmless)", "Used to make H₂SO₄ via Contact process (important industrial link)", "Reduced to S", "Used as a reducing agent"], ans: 1, exp: "SO₂ from roasting sulphide ores is fed to Contact process → H₂SO₄. This integrates smelting with sulphuric acid production, utilizing a by-product that would otherwise be a major air pollutant." },
    { q: "Anodising aluminium increases its corrosion resistance by:", opts: ["Coating it with zinc", "Growing a thicker, harder Al₂O₃ layer by electrolytic oxidation", "Painting the surface", "Alloying with Cu"], ans: 1, exp: "Anodising: Al as anode in H₂SO₄ or H₂C₂O₄ electrolyte. Electrolytic oxidation: Al → Al₂O₃ layer (thicker than natural 4nm → up to 25 μm). Harder, corrosion-resistant. Can be dyed by absorbing dyes into porous layer." },
    { q: "Smelting differs from roasting in that smelting:", opts: ["Uses no heat", "Involves melting the ore/oxide with a reducing agent (coke) to produce the metal", "Only removes water", "Occurs at low temperature"], ans: 1, exp: "Smelting: high-temperature melting of oxide ore with reducing agent (carbon/coke). FeO + CO → Fe + CO₂. Also uses flux (CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃ slag) to remove impurities. Different from roasting (no metal extraction)." },
    { q: "Flux in metallurgy is used to:", opts: ["Reduce the ore", "Combine with gangue → liquid slag (easily separated from molten metal)", "Oxidise the ore", "Cool the furnace"], ans: 1, exp: "Flux: substance added to react with gangue (waste minerals). Acidic gangue (SiO₂) + basic flux (CaO) → CaSiO₃ (slag). Basic gangue (CaO, MgO) + acidic flux (SiO₂) → slag. Slag floats on molten metal." },
    { q: "Electrorefining of copper: impure copper is the _____ and pure copper is the _____.", opts: ["Cathode; anode", "Anode; cathode (pure Cu deposits on cathode)", "Both are anodes", "Electrolyte; electrode"], ans: 1, exp: "Electrorefining: impure Cu anode dissolves (Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻). Pure Cu cathode grows (Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu). Impurities (Ag, Au, Pt) fall as \"anode mud\" below the anode — often more valuable than the copper!" },
    { q: "Stainless steel (Fe + Cr + Ni + C) is corrosion-resistant because:", opts: ["Ni prevents rusting", "Chromium forms a thin Cr₂O₃ passive layer protecting the iron", "High carbon content protects it", "Nickel is below Fe in reactivity"], ans: 1, exp: "Stainless steel: 12-20% Cr. Chromium passivates (forms Cr₂O₃ on surface) → prevents O₂/water reaching Fe → no rusting. Grade 304 (18%Cr, 8%Ni): most common. Grade 316 (with Mo): marine/medical use." },
    { q: "Wrought iron, cast iron, and steel differ mainly in:", opts: ["Source of iron ore", "Carbon content (wrought <0.1%, steel 0.3-2%, cast iron 2-4.5%)", "Iron ore type", "Reduction process"], ans: 1, exp: "Carbon content: wrought iron (<0.1% C) = toughest, most ductile. Mild steel (0.1-0.3% C) = structural. High-carbon steel (up to 2% C) = harder, less ductile. Cast iron (2-4.5% C) = brittle, high compressive strength." },
    { q: "P-block non-metal silicon (Si) is used in semiconductors because:", opts: ["Si is a metal", "Si has 4 valence electrons, band gap 1.1 eV, conductivity tunable by doping", "Si is cheapest element", "Si has no valence electrons"], ans: 1, exp: "Silicon: band gap 1.1 eV (ideal for electronics). Pure Si: semiconductor. Doped n-type (P, As) or p-type (B, Al) → controlled conductivity. 99.9999999% pure Si needed for microchips (zone refining achieves this)." },
    { q: "Which non-metal is liquid at room temperature?", opts: ["Mercury (metal)", "Chlorine (gas)", "Bromine", "Iodine (solid)"], ans: 2, exp: "Bromine (Br₂) is the only non-metal that is liquid at room temperature (mp = −7°C, bp = 58.8°C). Mercury is the only metal liquid at RT. Bromine is a fuming, reddish-brown, toxic liquid." },
    { q: "Activity series (reactivity series): metals below H₂ in the series:", opts: ["Cannot displace H₂ from acid", "Can be displaced from their salt solutions by H₂", "Do not corrode", "React only with alkalis"], ans: 0, exp: "Metals below H₂ (Cu, Hg, Ag, Pt, Au): do not displace H₂ from dilute acids (not reactive enough). But they CAN react with oxidising acids (HNO₃, conc. H₂SO₄). Cu + 4HNO₃(conc.) → Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NO₂ + 2H₂O." },
    { q: "Aluminium is extracted by electrolysis of Al₂O₃ (in molten cryolite) rather than by carbon reduction because:", opts: ["Carbon is expensive", "Al₂O₃ has very high melting point AND Al reduces carbon at high temperatures (Al is too reactive for carbon reduction to work cleanly)", "Electrolysis is faster", "Carbon absorbs Al"], ans: 1, exp: "At temperatures high enough to melt Al₂O₃ (2072°C), Al would reduce CO₂ instead of the reverse. Also, C + Al₂O₃: insufficient energy difference. Molten cryolite (Na₃AlF₆) lowers mp to ~1000°C for Hall-Héroult electrolysis." },
    { q: "The Hall-Héroult process for aluminium uses cryolite (Na₃AlF₆) to:", opts: ["Provide aluminium", "Lower the melting point of Al₂O₃ from 2072°C to ~1000°C, making electrolysis practical", "Act as an electrolyte only", "Reduce Al₂O₃ chemically"], ans: 1, exp: "Pure Al₂O₃ mp = 2072°C — too high for practical electrolysis. Dissolved in molten cryolite (Na₃AlF₆): mp drops to ~1000°C. Cryolite is the solvent, Al₂O₃ is the solute." },
    { q: "Amphoteric oxides react with BOTH acids AND bases. Al₂O₃ is amphoteric:", opts: ["Al₂O₃ + HCl → AlCl₃ + H₂O (acid); Al₂O₃ + NaOH → NaAlO₂ + H₂O (base)", "Al₂O₃ only reacts with water", "Al₂O₃ is a basic oxide only", "Al₂O₃ is an acidic oxide only"], ans: 0, exp: "Al₂O₃: amphoteric. With acid: Al₂O₃ + 6HCl → 2AlCl₃ + 3H₂O. With base: Al₂O₃ + 2NaOH + 3H₂O → 2NaAl(OH)₄ (sodium aluminate). Other amphoteric metals: Zn, Pb, Sn." },
    { q: "The \"tin plague\" phenomenon is:", opts: ["Corrosion of tin cans", "Below 13°C, white tin (β-Sn, tetragonal) slowly converts to grey tin (α-Sn, cubic), crumbling", "Tin dissolving in acid", "Tin alloy failure"], ans: 1, exp: "Tin plague: at T < 13°C, white tin (metallic, β) undergoes allotropic transformation to grey tin (α, powder). Expansion causes crumbling. Reportedly destroyed tin organ pipes in cold churches. Bismuth addition prevents this." },
    { q: "Nitric acid HNO₃ passivates Fe and Al but dissolves Cu because:", opts: ["Cu is below Fe in reactivity", "Fe/Al form protective oxide layer (passivation); Cu doesn't form a stable passive layer and dissolves in oxidising acid", "HNO₃ is a weaker acid for Fe", "Cu is more reactive than Fe"], ans: 1, exp: "Passivation: Fe and Al form impermeable Fe₂O₃/Al₂O₃ layers with conc. HNO₃ → reaction stops. Cu does not passivate: 3Cu + 8HNO₃(dil) → 3Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NO↑ + 4H₂O (Cu readily oxidised by NO₃⁻)." },
    { q: "Noble metals (Au, Pt) dissolve ONLY in aqua regia (3HCl:1HNO₃) because:", opts: ["High temperature needed", "Cl⁻ forms stable complex [AuCl₄]⁻ while HNO₃ oxidises Au — complexation drives dissolution", "Aqua regia is a superacid", "HCl alone dissolves Au slowly"], ans: 1, exp: "Aqua regia: HNO₃ is the oxidising agent (Au → Au³⁺); HCl provides Cl⁻ to form stable [AuCl₄]⁻ complex (removing Au³⁺ from solution → Le Chatelier drives forward). Neither acid alone works." },
    { q: "Malleable = beaten into sheets. Ductile = drawn into wires. Most ductile element is:", opts: ["Aluminium", "Copper", "Gold", "Iron"], ans: 2, exp: "Gold is the most ductile and malleable element. 1 gram of Au can be drawn into 2.5 km of wire or beaten into 1 m² gold leaf (0.0001 mm thick). FCC crystal structure allows layers to slide easily." },
    { q: "Which non-metal is essential for bone formation and is obtained mainly from phosphate rock?", opts: ["Sulphur", "Silicon", "Phosphorus", "Carbon"], ans: 2, exp: "Phosphorus (as phosphate PO₄³⁻): essential for bone (Ca₃(PO₄)₂), ATP (energy), DNA, cell membranes. Phosphate rock (Ca₃(PO₄)₂) mined worldwide. Phosphate fertilisers are critical for agriculture." },
    { q: "White phosphorus vs red phosphorus: white phosphorus is more reactive because:", opts: ["It contains more P atoms", "White P₄ molecules have strained P−P bonds (60° angle); red P is polymeric with more stable structure", "Red phosphorus has a higher formula mass", "White phosphorus has higher melting point"], ans: 1, exp: "White phosphorus (P₄): tetrahedral molecules, very strained 60° P−P−P bond angles → highly reactive, spontaneously flammable in air, toxic. Red phosphorus: more stable polymeric network structure. Same element, different allotropes." },
    { q: "Hydrogen bonding in HF makes it a weaker acid than HCl (in water) because:", opts: ["HF is more covalent", "Strong H-bonding in HF makes it harder to fully ionise → extensive association; while HF(aq) forms FHF⁻ complex", "F is more electronegative", "HCl is less stable"], ans: 1, exp: "HF is weak acid (Ka = 6.6×10⁻⁴) despite F being most electronegative. Strong H−F bond (565 kJ/mol) limits ionisation. Also, HF forms FHF⁻ (bifluoride) ions: HF + F⁻ → HF₂⁻, which actually makes concentrated HF solutions conduct better than expected." },
    { q: "The Ellingham diagram shows ΔG° vs T for metal oxide formation. A metal M whose line lies BELOW another line can:", opts: ["Cannot reduce the other oxide", "Reduce the other oxide (lower ΔG° for oxide formation = more stable oxide = better reducing agent)", "Form an alloy only", "Oxidise the other metal"], ans: 1, exp: "Ellingham: lower ΔG° line → more stable oxide → metal has greater affinity for O₂. Metal M with lower line reduces other metal oxide at that temperature. Carbon line crosses many metal oxide lines at specific temperatures." },
    { q: "Pyrometallurgy uses _____ energy; hydrometallurgy uses _____ energy; electrometallurgy uses _____ energy.", opts: ["Heat; chemical/aqueous; electrical — all correct", "Electrical; heat; chemical", "Chemical; electrical; heat", "All three use heat"], ans: 0, exp: "Pyrometallurgy: high temperature (smelting, roasting). Hydrometallurgy: aqueous chemistry (leaching, precipitation). Electrometallurgy: electrolysis (electrorefining, electrowinning). Different for different metals." },
    { q: "Pig iron from blast furnace has ~4% C. Steel is made by:", opts: ["Adding more C to pig iron", "Reducing C content to <2% by blowing O₂ (Bessemer/BOS process)", "Adding Ni and Cr only", "Slow cooling of pig iron"], ans: 1, exp: "Basic Oxygen Steelmaking (BOS): O₂ blown into molten pig iron → excess C oxidised to CO/CO₂ → C content drops to <2%. Also removes Si, Mn, S impurities. Faster and cheaper than old Bessemer process." },
    { q: "Amalgamation is used to extract gold and silver using:", opts: ["Cyanide solution", "Mercury (Hg) — Au dissolves in Hg → amalgam → Hg vaporised → Au remains", "Acid leaching", "Carbon adsorption"], ans: 1, exp: "Amalgamation: gold/silver dissolves in liquid Hg → gold amalgam. Heat amalgam → Hg evaporates → pure Au remains. Old method now largely replaced by cyanidation (environmental Hg pollution concerns)." },
    { q: "Why is diamond not used as a reducing agent despite being pure carbon?", opts: ["Diamond is reactive", "Diamond is kinetically inert — its C−C bonds in cubic network require too much energy to break at practical temperatures", "Carbon in diamond has different oxidation state", "Diamond doesn't contain carbon"], ans: 1, exp: "Diamond: giant covalent structure. All C−C bonds must be broken for it to react. Extremely high kinetic barrier → doesn't react at ordinary temperatures. Graphite (weaker van der Waals between layers) is used in industry." },
    { q: "The specific property of bismuth (Bi) that makes it unusual among metals:", opts: ["It is liquid at RT", "It expands on solidification (like water/ice) — useful in alloys that need to fill moulds precisely", "It is the most reactive metal", "It is the best conductor"], ans: 1, exp: "Bismuth expands on solidification (similar to water, antimony). Used in Wood's metal (Bi/Pb/Sn/Cd alloy) for sprinkler systems and moulds. Also: Bi has lowest thermal conductivity and electrical conductivity of metals." },
    { q: "In the reactivity series, which metal will NOT displace hydrogen from dilute hydrochloric acid?", opts: ["Magnesium", "Zinc", "Copper", "Iron"], ans: 2, exp: "Copper is below hydrogen in the electrochemical series (E°Cu²⁺/Cu = +0.34 V; E°H⁺/H₂ = 0.00 V). Metals above H₂ in the reactivity series (Mg, Zn, Fe, Al) displace H₂ from dilute acids. Copper, silver, gold, and platinum are below H₂ and do not react with dilute HCl." },
    { q: "The thermite reaction (Fe₂O₃ + 2Al → Al₂O₃ + 2Fe) releases so much heat that iron melts. The temperature reached is approximately:", opts: ["500°C", "1000°C", "2500°C", "5000°C"], ans: 2, exp: "Thermite reaction is highly exothermic (ΔH ≈ −850 kJ/mol). Temperature reaches ~2500°C or above — well above the melting point of iron (1535°C). The molten iron produced is used in aluminothermic welding of railway tracks (Goldschmidt process). The aluminium oxidises preferentially due to its more negative free energy of oxide formation." },
    { q: "Which of the following metals is extracted by the carbon (coke) reduction method in a blast furnace?", opts: ["Aluminium", "Sodium", "Iron from iron ore (Fe₂O₃)", "Titanium"], ans: 2, exp: "Iron is extracted by carbon reduction: Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂ (at ~1500°C in a blast furnace). Aluminium and sodium are too reactive to be reduced by carbon at practical temperatures and are extracted by electrolysis. Titanium reacts with carbon at high temperatures forming TiC — it uses the Kroll process (reduction by Mg or Na)." },
    { q: "The process of converting pig iron (brittle, 4% C) into steel involves which fundamental change?", opts: ["Adding more carbon to increase strength", "Controlled reduction of carbon content to 0.1-2% by oxidation (BOS process)", "Melting and recrystallisation", "Alloying with aluminum only"], ans: 1, exp: "Pig iron: 4-5% C → very brittle (carbon disturbs metallic crystal lattice). Basic Oxygen Steelmaking (BOS): pure O₂ blown into molten pig iron → excess C burned off as CO/CO₂ → C drops to 0.1-2%. Carbon in iron fills interstitial spaces in Fe lattice; too much (cast iron) = very brittle. Optimal steel: 0.3-1.2% C = strong AND relatively ductile." },
    { q: "In electrolytic refining of copper, the \"anode mud\" contains:", opts: ["Impure copper", "Valuable metals: silver, gold, platinum, and selenium that were present as impurities in anode copper", "Copper sulphate", "Lead and zinc"], ans: 1, exp: "Electrorefining Cu: impure Cu anode dissolves. Reactive impurities (Zn, Fe, Ni) also dissolve into CuSO₄ electrolyte. Noble metal impurities (Ag, Au, Pt, Se, Te) do NOT dissolve — they fall below the dissolving anode as \"anode mud.\" This mud is often recovered separately as it may be worth more than the copper itself. A typical anode mud contains 1-3% Au and 5-15% Ag." },
    { q: "Galvanisation of iron with zinc provides \"sacrificial protection\" even if the zinc layer is scratched. The electrochemical reason is:", opts: ["Zinc forms a stronger oxide than iron", "Zinc has a more negative reduction potential than iron (E°Zn²⁺/Zn = −0.76 V vs E°Fe²⁺/Fe = −0.44 V) — Zn acts as the anode (oxidises preferentially)", "Zinc seals the scratch physically", "Zinc reduces water to hydrogen"], ans: 1, exp: "Electrochemical basis: when Zn and Fe are in electrical contact with electrolyte (scratch exposes both), a galvanic cell forms. Zn (more negative E°) acts as anode: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ (oxidation). Fe acts as cathode (protected). Zn sacrifices itself to protect Fe. Compare with tin plating: Sn has less negative E° than Fe — if scratched, Fe becomes the anode and rusts faster." },
    { q: "Which correctly lists the alloy composition of brass?", opts: ["Cu + Sn (bronze)", "Cu + Zn (70% Cu, 30% Zn typically)", "Fe + C (steel)", "Pb + Sn (solder)"], ans: 1, exp: "Brass: copper + zinc alloy (typically 60-90% Cu, 10-40% Zn). Bronze: Cu + Sn. Solder: Pb + Sn (traditional, now often lead-free: Sn + Cu/Ag). German silver (nickel silver): Cu + Ni + Zn (no actual silver). Brass is harder than Cu alone, more machinable, good acoustic properties (used in musical instruments)." },
    { q: "The Goldschmidt aluminothermic process is used in railway track welding. Which metal is obtained?", opts: ["Aluminium", "Iron (molten, poured into the gap between rails)", "Chromium", "Manganese"], ans: 1, exp: "Thermite welding (Goldschmidt process): 8Al + 3Fe₃O₄ → 4Al₂O₃ + 9Fe (or: Fe₂O₃ + 2Al → Al₂O₃ + 2Fe). Reaction temperature ~2500°C → molten iron flows into the gap between rail ends → solidifies → welding without electricity. Produces a continuous weld throughout the rail cross-section. Used worldwide for railroad repairs." },
    { q: "Sodium is stored under kerosene because:", opts: ["Kerosene enhances Na reactivity", "Sodium is so reactive (reacts violently with O₂ and moisture in air) that it must be kept under an inert liquid like kerosene to prevent oxidation and fire", "Kerosene is cheaper than other storage methods", "Sodium dissolves in kerosene, making it easier to handle"], ans: 1, exp: "Sodium (Group 1 alkali metal): reacts vigorously with O₂ (4Na + O₂ → 2Na₂O, then 2Na + O₂ → Na₂O₂) and explosively with water (2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂). Stored under kerosene (hydrocarbons) or mineral oil to prevent air/moisture contact. Similarly, lithium is stored in mineral oil; phosphorus under water." },
    { q: "The ore bauxite (Al₂O₃·2H₂O) is purified by the Bayer process which involves:", opts: ["Roasting to remove water only", "Dissolution in hot NaOH → filtration (removes SiO₂ impurities simultaneously → precipitates Al(OH)₃ on cooling → calcination → Al₂O₃", "Direct electrolysis of bauxite", "Reduction with carbon at high temperature"], ans: 1, exp: "Bayer process: Bauxite + hot concentrated NaOH → Na[Al(OH)₄] (sodium aluminate, soluble) + Fe₂O₃ (red mud, filtered off) + SiO₂ reacts forming insoluble Na₂Al₂Si₂O₈ (filtered). Cooling dilute aluminate solution + seeding → Al(OH)₃ precipitates. Calcination: 2Al(OH)₃ → Al₂O₃ + 3H₂O. Pure Al₂O₃ then electrolysed by Hall-Héroult." },
    { q: "Mercury (Hg) is unique among metals because:", opts: ["It is the most reactive metal", "It is liquid at room temperature (mp = −38.8°C), extremely dense (13.6 g/cm³), forms amalgams with many metals", "It has the highest melting point", "It does not conduct electricity"], ans: 1, exp: "Mercury: only metal liquid at room temperature (mp = −38.8°C, bp = 356.7°C). Very high density (13.6 g/cm³ — iron floats in mercury). Forms amalgams (alloys) with Cu, Ag, Au, Sn, Zn. Used in thermometers, barometers, fluorescent lamps, dental amalgam. Highly toxic (bioaccumulates as methylmercury). Being phased out due to Minamata Convention (2013)." },
    { q: "Stainless steel (containing 12-20% Cr) resists corrosion even in harsh environments because:", opts: ["Chromium is the most reactive metal in the alloy", "Cr forms a thin, self-healing Cr₂O₃ passive oxide layer on the surface that prevents O₂ and moisture reaching the underlying iron", "Nickel content prevents oxidation exclusively", "High carbon content resists corrosion"], ans: 1, exp: "Passivation: in stainless steel, Cr preferentially oxidises at the surface forming a dense, adherent Cr₂O₃ film (2-3 nm thick). This film is self-healing — if scratched, Cr re-oxidises to reform the layer in the presence of O₂. Minimum 10.5% Cr required for the passive layer to form. Grades: 304 (18%Cr, 8%Ni, kitchen), 316 (16%Cr, 10%Ni, 2%Mo, marine/surgical)." },
    { q: "In the refining of metals by zone refining (used for semiconductor-grade silicon), what is the key principle?", opts: ["Impurities have lower melting point than pure Si", "Impurities prefer to remain in the molten phase rather than the solid phase (distribution coefficient K < 1) — molten zone sweeps impurities to one end", "Gravity separates impurities from Si", "Electric field removes metallic impurities"], ans: 1, exp: "Zone refining: coil melts a narrow zone of Si; as zone moves, impurities with K < 1 stay in melt (don't preferentially crystallise). Zone sweeps impurities to the end of the rod. Multiple passes → 99.9999999% pure silicon (\"nine nines\"). Essential for semiconductor and solar cell fabrication where even ppm impurities affect electrical properties." },
    { q: "Titanium (Ti) is extremely corrosion-resistant despite being fairly reactive, because:", opts: ["Ti is below Cu in the reactivity series", "Ti forms a very hard, adherent TiO₂ oxide layer that prevents further corrosion (similar to Al passivation)", "Ti reacts preferentially with N₂, not O₂", "Ti does not conduct electricity so cannot corrode electrochemically"], ans: 1, exp: "Titanium passivation: TiO₂ forms spontaneously on surface → impermeable, adherent → prevents further oxidation. Ti is highly resistant to seawater, aqua regia, chlorine, and many acids. Used in aircraft (light + strong), biomedical implants (biocompatible TiO₂), and marine environments. Kroll process (Mg reduction of TiCl₄) used for extraction due to reactivity with C and N at high temperatures." },
    { q: "Which metal has the highest electrical conductivity at room temperature?", opts: ["Gold", "Copper", "Silver (Ag: 63×10⁶ S/m, highest among metals)", "Aluminium"], ans: 2, exp: "Electrical conductivity order: Ag > Cu > Au > Al > Fe. Silver (Ag) has the highest conductivity due to its electron configuration and crystal structure. Copper (second) is used in electrical wiring because it is far cheaper than silver. Gold is used in connectors for reliability (does not corrode). Al is used in high-voltage transmission lines (lighter than Cu)." },
    { q: "The reactivity series correctly predicts that iron will react with CuSO₄ solution because:", opts: ["Fe is more electronegative than Cu", "Fe has a more negative reduction potential (E°= −0.44 V) than Cu (E°= +0.34 V) → Fe reduces Cu²⁺ spontaneously", "Fe has a lower atomic mass", "Fe and Cu are in the same period"], ans: 1, exp: "Cell potential: E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode = E°Cu²⁺/Cu − E°Fe²⁺/Fe = 0.34 − (−0.44) = +0.78 V. Positive E°cell → spontaneous. Reaction: Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu (Fe displaces Cu). Iron deposits a brown/reddish layer of metallic copper; blue CuSO₄ solution fades to pale green (FeSO₄)." },
    { q: "Alnico (a magnetic alloy) contains:", opts: ["Al, Ni, Co (and Fe, Cu)", "Al, Ni, Cr", "Al, Cu, Zn", "Al, Fe only"], ans: 0, exp: "Alnico: Al (8%), Ni (14%), Co (24%), Cu (3%), rest Fe (~51%). Strong permanent magnet with excellent temperature stability (retains magnetism up to ~540°C). Used in electric motors, guitar pickups, cow magnets. Largely replaced by rare-earth magnets (NdFeB, SmCo) for higher-performance applications but still used where temperature resistance is needed." },
    { q: "Wood's metal (melting point ~70°C) is used in fire-sprinkler systems. Its composition is:", opts: ["Pb only", "Bi (50%) + Pb (25%) + Sn (12.5%) + Cd (12.5%) — low melting eutectic alloy", "Fe + Zn", "Sn + Pb only"], ans: 1, exp: "Wood's metal: eutectic alloy of Bi-Pb-Sn-Cd (proportions may vary slightly by source: roughly 50:25:12.5:12.5). Melts at ~70°C. Used in: fire sprinklers (melts when fire heats the room → releases water), fire safety devices, castings. Bismuth expands on solidification, compensating for other metals' shrinkage → precise castings. Being replaced by cadmium-free alternatives due to Cd toxicity." },
    { q: "Duralumin is an alloy of aluminium used in aircraft. Its composition is:", opts: ["Al + Fe", "Al (93-96%) + Cu (4%) + Mg (0.5-1%) + Mn (0.5-1%)", "Al + Ni + Co", "Al + Ti only"], ans: 1, exp: "Duralumin: approximately 94% Al, 4% Cu, 1% Mg, 0.5% Mn. Much stronger than pure Al (yield strength ~400 MPa vs ~35 MPa for pure Al) while maintaining low density. Precipitation hardened by heat treatment. Used in aircraft fuselages, wings since WWI. Modern aviation uses 2024-T3 (similar composition) and newer Al-Li alloys for even lighter weight." },
    { q: "Which property of metals allows them to be hammered into thin sheets without breaking?", opts: ["Ductility", "Malleability — metallic bonds are non-directional, allowing layers to slide without fracturing", "Hardness", "Brittleness"], ans: 1, exp: "Malleability: ability to be beaten into thin sheets. Metallic bonding: positive metal ions (cations) in a \"sea of delocalised electrons.\" Bonds are non-directional → when force applied, layers of cations can slide past each other while remaining surrounded by electrons. New bonding forms in new position → no fracture. This is why metals don't shatter on impact (unlike ionic or covalent crystals)." }
  ]
,

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════
     CH 4 — CARBON AND ITS COMPOUNDS
  ══════════════════════════════════════ */
  4: [
    { q: "Carbon forms a large number of compounds because of:", opts: ["Its atomic mass 12", "Catenation and tetravalency", "Its non-metallic nature", "High electronegativity only"], ans: 1, exp: "Catenation (ability to bond with other carbon atoms forming chains/rings) + tetravalency (4 bonds possible) allow carbon to form millions of compounds." },
    { q: "Diamond and graphite are allotropes of carbon. They differ in:", opts: ["Chemical composition", "The arrangement of carbon atoms (structure)", "Atomic mass", "Number of protons"], ans: 1, exp: "Both are pure carbon (same composition) but have different structural arrangements. Diamond: tetrahedral 3D network (hardest). Graphite: hexagonal layers (soft, lubricant)." },
    { q: "Graphite conducts electricity because:", opts: ["It is a metal", "Each carbon atom has one free/delocalised electron in its layers", "It is a covalent compound", "It dissolves in water to form ions"], ans: 1, exp: "In graphite, each C forms 3 covalent bonds within a layer; the 4th electron is delocalised across the layer, conducting electricity. Diamond has no free electrons." },
    { q: "The general formula for alkenes is:", opts: ["CₙH₂ₙ₊₂", "CₙH₂ₙ", "CₙH₂ₙ₋₂", "CₙHₙ"], ans: 1, exp: "Alkenes have one C=C double bond: CₙH₂ₙ. E.g., ethene C₂H₄ (2×2=4). Alkanes: CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. Alkynes: CₙH₂ₙ₋₂." },
    { q: "Which of the following is the IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂OH?", opts: ["Methanol", "Ethanol", "Propanol", "Ethanal"], ans: 1, exp: "CH₃CH₂OH has 2 carbons + −OH group → ethanol (eth = 2C, -ol = alcohol). Methanol (CH₃OH) has 1C. Ethanal (CH₃CHO) is an aldehyde." },
    { q: "Ethanoic acid (acetic acid) has a melting point of 17°C, and is called glacial because:", opts: ["It is used in glaciers", "Pure ethanoic acid solidifies near room temperature forming ice-like crystals", "It melts only in glacial conditions", "It is mixed with ice for storage"], ans: 1, exp: "Pure CH₃COOH (mp 17°C) freezes on cold days forming ice-like solid — hence \"glacial acetic acid.\" Vinegar is only 3-5% acetic acid in water." },
    { q: "In the homologous series, each successive member differs by:", opts: ["−CH₃ (mass 15)", "−CH₂− group (mass 14)", "−OH group", "−COOH group"], ans: 1, exp: "Each homologue has one more −CH₂− than the previous member (14 mass units more). They share the same general formula and similar chemical properties." },
    { q: "The functional group −COOH is present in:", opts: ["Alcohols", "Aldehydes", "Carboxylic acids", "Ketones"], ans: 2, exp: "−COOH = carboxyl group, present in carboxylic acids. Example: CH₃COOH (ethanoic acid). −OH = alcohol, −CHO = aldehyde, −CO− = ketone." },
    { q: "The reaction of ethene with bromine water (Br₂) is called:", opts: ["Substitution", "Addition", "Elimination", "Combustion"], ans: 1, exp: "CH₂=CH₂ + Br₂ → CH₂Br−CH₂Br. Unsaturated compounds (alkenes/alkynes) undergo addition reactions across the double/triple bond. Bromine water (orange) is decolourised." },
    { q: "Methane (CH₄) reacts with Cl₂ in sunlight to form CH₃Cl. This reaction is:", opts: ["Addition", "Substitution (halogenation)", "Elimination", "Combustion"], ans: 1, exp: "CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl (in sunlight). One H is replaced by Cl. Alkanes (saturated) undergo substitution reactions. Sunlight initiates the free-radical chain reaction." },
    { q: "Saponification is the reaction of fat/oil with:", opts: ["HCl", "NaOH (alkali)", "H₂SO₄", "CH₃COOH"], ans: 1, exp: "Fat/oil + NaOH (or KOH) → soap + glycerol. This is saponification (Latin sapo = soap). The soap is the sodium/potassium salt of a long-chain fatty acid." },
    { q: "Hard detergents are preferred over soap because:", opts: ["They are cheaper", "They work in hard water (don't form scum with Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ ions)", "They are more acidic", "They are biodegradable"], ans: 1, exp: "Soaps form insoluble curds (scum) with Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ in hard water. Synthetic detergents do not — they work well in hard water. Note: branched detergents are NOT biodegradable." },
    { q: "The micelle in soap cleaning has:", opts: ["Hydrophilic tails surrounding the oil, hydrophobic heads facing water", "Hydrophobic tails surrounding oil, hydrophilic heads facing water", "Both ends hydrophobic", "Both ends hydrophilic"], ans: 1, exp: "Soap molecules: hydrophilic (water-loving) −COONa⁺ head faces water; hydrophobic (oil-loving) long hydrocarbon tail points inward surrounding the oil/grease droplet." },
    { q: "Ethanol is oxidised to ethanoic acid using:", opts: ["H₂SO₄", "NaOH", "Alkaline KMnO₄ or K₂Cr₂O₇", "HCl"], ans: 2, exp: "C₂H₅OH + [O] → CH₃COOH. Oxidising agents like acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ or KMnO₄ oxidise ethanol to ethanoic acid." },
    { q: "The organic compound formed during fermentation of glucose by yeast is:", opts: ["Methanol", "Ethanol", "Ethanoic acid", "Propan-1-ol"], ans: 1, exp: "C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂ (yeast, no O₂). Ethanol (ethyl alcohol) is produced by anaerobic fermentation of glucose. Used in alcoholic beverages." },
    { q: "Which of the following represents a homologous series of alcohols?", opts: ["CH₃OH, C₂H₅OH, C₃H₇OH", "CH₃COOH, C₂H₅COOH", "CH₄, C₂H₆, C₃H₈", "C₂H₄, C₃H₆, C₄H₈"], ans: 0, exp: "Methanol (CH₃OH), ethanol (C₂H₅OH), propan-1-ol (C₃H₇OH) form the alcohols homologous series (general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH, all have −OH group)." },
    { q: "The test for unsaturation (C=C or C≡C) in a compound is:", opts: ["Bromine water is decolourised", "Litmus turns red", "Limewater turns milky", "Hydrogen gas is evolved"], ans: 0, exp: "Bromine water (brown/orange) is decolourised by unsaturated compounds — bromine adds across the double/triple bond. Saturated compounds do NOT decolourise bromine water." },
    { q: "The IUPAC name of the simplest carboxylic acid (HCOOH) is:", opts: ["Methanoic acid", "Ethanoic acid", "Propanoic acid", "Formic acid"], ans: 0, exp: "HCOOH = methanoic acid (1 carbon). Its common name is formic acid (Latin formica = ant). CH₃COOH = ethanoic acid (2 carbons) = acetic acid." },
    { q: "Fullerene C₆₀ was named Buckminsterfullerene because:", opts: ["Discovered by Buckminster Fuller", "Its structure resembles geodesic domes designed by architect R. Buckminster Fuller", "Found in a place called Buckminster", "Named after its 60-carbon structure"], ans: 1, exp: "C₆₀ has 60 carbon atoms arranged in pentagons and hexagons like a football — resembling geodesic domes of architect Buckminster Fuller. Also called \"Buckyball\"." },
    { q: "Propane (C₃H₈) belongs to which homologous series?", opts: ["Alkenes", "Alkynes", "Alkanes", "Alcohols"], ans: 2, exp: "C₃H₈: using CₙH₂ₙ₊₂: n=3, 2(3)+2=8. ✓ Propane is an alkane (all single bonds, saturated). It is a component of LPG (liquefied petroleum gas)." },
    { q: "Which of the following shows isomerism with n-butane (C₄H₁₀)?", opts: ["Propane (C₃H₈)", "Isobutane (2-methylpropane, C₄H₁₀)", "Pentane (C₅H₁₂)", "Ethane (C₂H₆)"], ans: 1, exp: "Structural isomers have the same molecular formula but different structural arrangements. Isobutane (C₄H₁₀) and n-butane (C₄H₁₀) are isomers of each other." },
    { q: "The functional group in ketones is:", opts: ["−OH", "−CHO", ">C=O (between two carbon atoms)", "−COOH"], ans: 2, exp: "Ketone functional group: >C=O (carbonyl group attached to two carbon atoms). Example: CH₃COCH₃ (propanone/acetone). Aldehyde −CHO has carbonyl at end of chain." },
    { q: "Denatured alcohol (methylated spirit) is ethanol mixed with:", opts: ["Methanol (to make it unfit for drinking)", "Water", "Sodium hydroxide", "Glucose"], ans: 0, exp: "Methanol (poisonous) is added to industrial ethanol to make it unfit for consumption (denaturing). This avoids misuse and reduces taxation on industrial alcohol." },
    { q: "Complete combustion of hydrocarbons produces:", opts: ["CO + H₂O", "CO₂ + H₂O + heat", "C + H₂", "CO₂ + H₂ + heat"], ans: 1, exp: "Complete combustion: CₙHₘ + excess O₂ → n CO₂ + m/2 H₂O + heat. Incomplete combustion (limited O₂) produces CO and/or C (soot)." },
    { q: "The carbon-carbon bond in ethyne (acetylene, C₂H₂) is a:", opts: ["Single bond (σ)", "Double bond (σ+π)", "Triple bond (σ+2π)", "No bond"], ans: 2, exp: "Ethyne C≡C: one sigma bond + two pi bonds = triple bond. General formula for alkynes CₙH₂ₙ₋₂. For n=2: C₂H₂₍₂₎₋₂ = C₂H₂. ✓" },
    { q: "LPG (Liquefied Petroleum Gas) used for cooking mainly consists of:", opts: ["Methane only", "Butane and propane", "Ethanol", "Acetylene"], ans: 1, exp: "LPG is mainly a mixture of butane (C₄H₁₀) and propane (C₃H₈) stored as liquids under pressure. It has high calorific value and burns cleanly." },
    { q: "The \"−ol\" suffix in IUPAC nomenclature denotes:", opts: ["An aldehyde", "A carboxylic acid", "An alcohol", "An alkene"], ans: 2, exp: "−ol indicates the −OH (hydroxyl) functional group: methanol, ethanol, propanol. −al = aldehyde, −oic acid = carboxylic acid, −one = ketone." },
    { q: "Which property of carbon leads to the formation of ring compounds like benzene (C₆H₆)?", opts: ["Catenation with ring formation", "High electronegativity", "Low valency", "Metallic character"], ans: 0, exp: "Carbon's catenation ability allows it to form not just chains but also rings. Benzene, cyclopentane, cyclohexane are all ring (cyclic) compounds." },
    { q: "Vinegar (sirka) is a dilute aqueous solution of:", opts: ["Ethanol", "Methanol", "Ethanoic acid (acetic acid)", "Citric acid"], ans: 2, exp: "Vinegar is 3-5% ethanoic acid (acetic acid, CH₃COOH) in water. It is produced by bacterial oxidation of wine/ethanol." },
    { q: "The reaction CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O is:", opts: ["Combustion", "Esterification", "Neutralisation of a carboxylic acid by a base", "Saponification"], ans: 2, exp: "Ethanoic acid (weak acid) reacts with NaOH (base) in a neutralisation reaction to form sodium ethanoate (sodium acetate, a salt) and water." },
    { q: "Buckyballs (C₆₀) belong to which class of carbon allotropes?", opts: ["Diamond allotropes", "Graphite allotropes", "Fullerenes", "Amorphous carbon"], ans: 2, exp: "Fullerenes are cage-like structures of carbon. C₆₀ (Buckminsterfullerene) is the most famous. Diamond, graphite, and fullerenes are the three main allotropes of carbon discussed at this level." },
    { q: "When ethanol is heated with conc. H₂SO₄ at 170°C, the product is:", opts: ["Ethanoic acid", "Diethyl ether", "Ethene (dehydration)", "Methane"], ans: 2, exp: "CH₃CH₂OH → CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O (at 170°C with conc. H₂SO₄). Conc. H₂SO₄ acts as a dehydrating agent, removing water to form ethene. At 140°C: ether forms." },
    { q: "Ester functional group is formed by reaction of:", opts: ["Two alcohols", "Alcohol + carboxylic acid (esterification)", "Acid + water", "Two carboxylic acids"], ans: 1, exp: "CH₃OH + CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COOCH₃ + H₂O (with H₂SO₄ catalyst, heated). Esters have fruity/flowery smells — used as flavouring agents and perfumes." },
    { q: "Which compound is used as a solvent in nail polish remover?", opts: ["Ethanol", "Methanol", "Propanone (acetone)", "Benzene"], ans: 2, exp: "Propanone (acetone, CH₃COCH₃) is the solvent in nail polish remover. It is a ketone with a low boiling point (56°C) and dissolves nail lacquer (polymer)." },
    { q: "What is wrong with the following structural formula: H₃C−C(=O)−CH₃?", opts: ["Nothing wrong — it is propanone", "Carbon has 5 bonds", "It has no functional group", "It is an acid"], ans: 0, exp: "H₃C−CO−CH₃ is propanone (acetone), a ketone with the carbonyl group (>C=O) between two methyl groups. Nothing is wrong — it is the correct structure of propanone." },
    { q: "In a soap molecule, the hydrophilic (water-loving) part is:", opts: ["The long hydrocarbon chain", "The −COO⁻Na⁺ (carboxylate) head", "The entire molecule", "Neither part"], ans: 1, exp: "The ionic −COO⁻Na⁺ head is attracted to water (polar/ionic interactions). The long hydrocarbon tail (hydrophobic) is attracted to grease and oils." },
    { q: "The number of covalent bonds in a molecule of methane (CH₄) is:", opts: ["1", "2", "4", "8"], ans: 2, exp: "CH₄: carbon forms 4 single covalent bonds with 4 hydrogen atoms. Total covalent bonds = 4." },
    { q: "Ethyne (C₂H₂) is used in welding because:", opts: ["It produces a very hot flame when burned with oxygen (>3000°C)", "It is cheap", "It produces a colourful flame", "It is non-flammable"], ans: 0, exp: "Oxy-acetylene flame: C₂H₂ + O₂ produces a flame >3000°C — hot enough to cut and weld metals. This is an important industrial application of ethyne." },
    { q: "The compound CH₃CHO belongs to which functional group class?", opts: ["Ketone", "Alcohol", "Aldehyde", "Carboxylic acid"], ans: 2, exp: "CH₃CHO = ethanal. The −CHO group at the END of the carbon chain is the aldehyde functional group. (Ketone has C=O between two C atoms)." },
    { q: "Why does carbon not form ionic compounds by losing 4 electrons?", opts: ["Carbon has no electrons to lose", "It would need too much energy to remove 4 electrons; covalent bonding is more stable", "Carbon prefers to gain electrons", "It forms C⁴⁺ easily"], ans: 1, exp: "Removing 4 electrons requires enormous energy (4 ionisation energies). Carbon finds it much more energetically favourable to share electrons via covalent bonds." },
    { q: "Which carbon compound is used as a fuel in vehicles (petrol)?", opts: ["Methane only", "A mixture of hydrocarbons (C₅-C₁₀ alkanes and cycloalkanes)", "Ethanol only", "Propane only"], ans: 1, exp: "Petrol (gasoline) is a complex mixture of C₅-C₁₀ hydrocarbons (alkanes, cycloalkanes, aromatics) obtained by fractional distillation of petroleum." },
    { q: "A structural isomer of ethanol (C₂H₆O) is:", opts: ["Methanol", "Dimethyl ether (CH₃−O−CH₃)", "Ethanoic acid", "Propan-1-ol"], ans: 1, exp: "Ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH) and dimethyl ether (CH₃OCH₃) both have formula C₂H₆O but different structural arrangements (C−O−H vs. C−O−C). They are structural isomers." },
    { q: "Soap is made by heating fat/vegetable oil with concentrated NaOH. This process is called:", opts: ["Hydrogenation", "Saponification", "Esterification", "Denaturation"], ans: 1, exp: "Saponification: fat/oil (triglyceride) + NaOH → soap (sodium salt of fatty acid) + glycerol. The reaction is alkaline hydrolysis of an ester." },
    { q: "Which gas produces \"sooty flame\" suggesting incomplete combustion?", opts: ["Methane", "Ethyne (acetylene)", "Hydrogen", "Butane"], ans: 1, exp: "Ethyne (C₂H₂) has a high C:H ratio (2:2) — insufficient O₂ leads to incomplete combustion and sooty (carbon) flame. Methane has lower C:H ratio and burns more cleanly." },
    { q: "The property of carbon that allows it to form chains of any length is called:", opts: ["Tetravalency", "Catenation", "Isomerism", "Valency"], ans: 1, exp: "Catenation: C−C bond is strong and stable, allowing carbon to form chains of any length: propane (3C), hexane (6C), up to millions of carbons in polymers." },
    { q: "Condensed structural formula of ethanoic acid is:", opts: ["CH₃OH", "CH₃CHO", "CH₃COOH", "HCOOH"], ans: 2, exp: "Ethanoic acid: CH₃COOH (2 carbons, carboxyl group −COOH). Methanol: CH₃OH. Ethanal: CH₃CHO. Methanoic acid: HCOOH (1 carbon)." },
    { q: "Kerosene is used as a fuel for:", opts: ["Cars (petrol engines)", "Jet aircraft and lanterns", "LPG stoves", "Diesel engines"], ans: 1, exp: "Kerosene (C₁₀-C₁₆ hydrocarbons) is used in jet aircraft (aviation turbine fuel, ATF) and traditional lanterns/lamps." },
    { q: "An alcohol reacts with sodium to produce:", opts: ["Salt + H₂O", "Sodium alkoxide + H₂ gas", "Ester + H₂O", "Aldehyde + NaOH"], ans: 1, exp: "2C₂H₅OH + 2Na → 2C₂H₅ONa + H₂↑. Alcohols react with active metals like Na to give sodium alkoxide (sodium ethoxide here) and hydrogen gas." },
    { q: "Which compound is used as a preservative for biological specimens?", opts: ["Ethanol (dilute)", "Methanol", "Formaldehyde (40% solution, formalin)", "Acetone"], ans: 2, exp: "Formalin (40% aqueous HCHO — methanal/formaldehyde) is used to preserve biological specimens. It denatures and fixes proteins, preventing decomposition." },
    { q: "The process of converting vegetable oils (unsaturated) into solid fats (saturated) using hydrogen is called:", opts: ["Oxidation", "Saponification", "Hydrogenation (using Ni catalyst)", "Esterification"], ans: 2, exp: "Hydrogenation: vegetable oil + H₂ (Ni catalyst, heat) → vegetable fat (vanaspati/margarine). C=C double bonds are reduced to C−C single bonds → oil solidifies. Same principle makes oils more shelf-stable." },
    { q: "IUPAC name of CH₃−CH(OH)−CH₃:", opts: ["Propan-1-ol", "Propan-2-ol", "2-methylpropan-1-ol", "Methyl propanol"], ans: 1, exp: "OH on carbon-2 of a 3-carbon chain = propan-2-ol (or isopropanol). Propan-1-ol: OH on C-1 (n-propanol). Longest chain = 3C (propan), OH on C-2 = -2-ol." },
    { q: "How many structural isomers does C₄H₁₀ have?", opts: ["2 (n-butane and isobutane)", "3", "4", "1"], ans: 0, exp: "C₄H₁₀ has 2 isomers: n-butane (CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃) and isobutane/2-methylpropane (CH₃CH(CH₃)₂). Different connectivity of carbon atoms." },
    { q: "Which compound shows geometric (cis-trans) isomerism?", opts: ["Ethane (C₂H₆)", "But-2-ene (CH₃−CH=CH−CH₃)", "Propene", "Ethyne"], ans: 1, exp: "But-2-ene: restricted rotation around C=C double bond + different groups on each C of double bond → cis (same groups same side) and trans isomers. Must have: (i) C=C double bond (ii) two different groups on each double-bonded carbon." },
    { q: "Dehydration of ethanol with conc. H₂SO₄ at 140°C gives:", opts: ["Ethene", "Diethyl ether (CH₃CH₂OCH₂CH₃)", "Ethanoic acid", "Ethyne"], ans: 1, exp: "At 140°C: 2C₂H₅OH → C₂H₅OC₂H₅ + H₂O (intermolecular dehydration → ether). At 170°C: C₂H₅OH → CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O (intramolecular dehydration → alkene). Temperature is key." },
    { q: "The addition reaction of HBr to propene (Markovnikov's rule): CH₃CH=CH₂ + HBr → ?", opts: ["1-bromopropane (Br on C-1)", "2-bromopropane (Br on more substituted carbon, C-2)", "Equal mixture", "Propane"], ans: 1, exp: "Markovnikov's rule: H adds to C with more H atoms (C-1), Br adds to C with fewer H atoms (C-2). Product: CH₃CHBrCH₃ (2-bromopropane). The more stable carbocation intermediate (secondary) is favoured." },
    { q: "Benzene doesn't undergo addition reactions readily because:", opts: ["It is saturated", "Delocalised π electrons form an aromatic system (resonance energy ~150 kJ/mol) — disrupting it is costly; prefers substitution", "No double bonds", "Too reactive for addition"], ans: 1, exp: "Benzene's 6π electrons are delocalised in a ring → extra stability (resonance/aromatic stabilisation energy ≈ 150 kJ/mol). Addition would destroy aromaticity. Electrophilic substitution preserves the aromatic ring." },
    { q: "Nitration of benzene: C₆H₆ + HNO₃ → C₆H₅NO₂ + H₂O. The electrophile is:", opts: ["NO₂⁻ (nitrite ion)", "NO₂⁺ (nitronium ion, formed from HNO₃ + H₂SO₄)", "HNO₃ molecule", "N₂O₄"], ans: 1, exp: "Nitronium ion NO₂⁺ (from HNO₃ + H₂SO₄ → NO₂⁺ + HSO₄⁻ + H₂O) is the electrophile in benzene nitration. NO₂⁺ attacks benzene ring → substitutes H → nitrobenzene. Important for making explosives, dyes, drugs." },
    { q: "Cracking of petroleum produces smaller molecules from larger alkanes. This is:", opts: ["Thermal reaction or catalytic process (zeolite catalyst) breaking C−C bonds in large alkanes → alkenes + smaller alkanes", "Addition reaction", "Polymerisation", "Esterification"], ans: 0, exp: "Cracking: large alkane → smaller alkane + alkene. E.g., C₁₀H₂₂ → C₅H₁₂ + C₅H₁₀. Thermal cracking: 700-900°C. Catalytic cracking: ~500°C with zeolite catalyst. Produces petrol, plastics feedstocks." },
    { q: "The degree of polymerisation (DP) of PVC if molecular weight = 62,500 and monomer (vinyl chloride) MW = 62.5:", opts: ["1000", "625", "62500", "100"], ans: 0, exp: "DP = Polymer MW / Monomer MW = 62500 / 62.5 = 1000. DP indicates how many monomer units link together. Higher DP → longer chains → higher tensile strength." },
    { q: "Nylon-6,6 is a condensation polymer formed from:", opts: ["Only one monomer", "Hexamethylenediamine + adipic acid (two different monomers, 6 C each)", "Propylene", "Styrene"], ans: 1, exp: "Nylon-6,6: condensation (polyamide). Hexamethylene diamine (H₂N(CH₂)₆NH₂) + adipic acid (HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH) → nylon-6,6 + H₂O. The 6,6 refers to 6 carbons in each monomer. Strong synthetic fibre." },
    { q: "PTFE (Teflon) is made from monomer:", opts: ["CH₂=CH₂ (ethylene)", "CF₂=CF₂ (tetrafluoroethylene)", "CH₂=CHCl (vinyl chloride)", "C₆H₅CH=CH₂ (styrene)"], ans: 1, exp: "PTFE: (−CF₂−CF₂−)ₙ. Monomer: CF₂=CF₂. C−F bonds are very strong → PTFE is extremely chemically inert, non-stick, high-temperature resistant. Used in non-stick cookware, lab equipment, plumbing tape." },
    { q: "What makes coal tar an important raw material?", opts: ["Burns cleanly as fuel", "Contains hundreds of aromatic compounds (benzene, toluene, naphthalene, anthracene etc.) used as feedstocks for dyes, drugs, polymers", "High calorific value as solid fuel", "Used directly as lubricant"], ans: 1, exp: "Coal tar (destructive distillation of coal): complex mixture of aromatic hydrocarbons. Fractionated: light oil (benzene, toluene), middle oil (naphthalene, pyridine), heavy oil, anthracene oil, pitch. Foundation of synthetic organic chemistry." },
    { q: "Lucas test distinguishes primary, secondary, tertiary alcohols using:", opts: ["K₂Cr₂O₇/H₂SO₄", "Anhydrous ZnCl₂ + conc. HCl: 3° → immediate cloudiness (alkyl chloride); 2° → cloudiness in 5 min; 1° → no cloudiness at RT", "Na metal", "Fehling's solution"], ans: 1, exp: "Lucas reagent (ZnCl₂/conc. HCl): reaction rate reflects stability of carbocation intermediate. 3° alcohol → stable 3° carbocation → instant. 2° → 5 min. 1° → no reaction at RT (needs heating). Used for C3-C6 alcohols." },
    { q: "Tollens' reagent (ammoniacal AgNO₃) distinguishes aldehydes from ketones because:", opts: ["Ketones are more acidic", "Aldehydes reduce Ag⁺ to Ag (silver mirror); ketones do NOT (ketones are weaker reducing agents)", "Tollens' reacts with C=C double bonds", "Tollens' reacts with OH groups"], ans: 1, exp: "Tollens' test: RCHO + 2[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ + 2OH⁻ → RCOO⁻ + 2Ag↓ + 4NH₃ + H₂O. Silver mirror forms (Ag metal deposited). Ketones (R₂C=O) don't react. Aldehydes: primary oxidation product is carboxylic acid." },
    { q: "Fehling's solution (blue) detects aliphatic aldehydes: positive result is:", opts: ["Blue stays blue", "Red/brick-red precipitate of Cu₂O (Cu²⁺ reduced to Cu⁺)", "Silver mirror", "Yellow precipitate"], ans: 1, exp: "Fehling's solution (Cu²⁺ in alkaline tartrate): aliphatic aldehydes reduce Cu²⁺ → Cu₂O (brick-red precipitate). Aromatic aldehydes (benzaldehyde) do NOT react with Fehling's but DO react with Tollens'." },
    { q: "The iodoform test (I₂/NaOH): positive with yellow CHI₃ precipitate indicates:", opts: ["Primary alcohol only", "Methyl ketone (CH₃COR) or secondary alcohol with CH₃CH(OH)R or ethanol", "All aldehydes", "Carboxylic acids"], ans: 1, exp: "Iodoform test: CH₃COR + 3I₂ + 3NaOH → CHI₃↓ (yellow, antiseptic smell) + RCOONa. Positive for: CH₃CHO, CH₃COCH₃ (acetone), CH₃CH₂OH (ethanol), CH₃CHOH−R. Identifies the CH₃CO− group." },
    { q: "Esterification equilibrium: CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O. Yield is increased by:", opts: ["Increasing water", "Removing water or ester as formed, or using excess alcohol", "Removing acid catalyst", "Cooling the mixture"], ans: 1, exp: "Le Chatelier applied to esterification: removing product (water by molecular sieves or removing ester by distillation) shifts equilibrium right → more ester formed. Excess of cheaper alcohol also shifts equilibrium right." },
    { q: "Which functional group is present in CH₃COCH₃ (acetone)?", opts: ["Aldehyde (−CHO)", "Ketone (>C=O between two C atoms)", "Ester (−COO−)", "Carboxylic acid (−COOH)"], ans: 1, exp: "Acetone (propanone): CH₃−CO−CH₃. Carbonyl group C=O with two alkyl groups on either side = ketone. Aldehydes: C=O at end of chain (R−CHO). Ketones: C=O in middle (R−CO−R')." },
    { q: "SN2 reaction is favoured by:", opts: ["Bulky (tertiary) substrates", "Primary substrates (unhindered) + strong nucleophile (backside attack, inversion)", "Weak nucleophiles", "High temperature thermal conditions"], ans: 1, exp: "SN2 (bimolecular nucleophilic substitution): one-step backside attack by nucleophile → inversion of configuration (Walden inversion). Favoured by: primary/methyl substrates (less steric hindrance), strong nucleophile, polar aprotic solvent." },
    { q: "Grignard reagent RMgX is used to:", opts: ["Reduce aldehydes", "Add to carbonyl compounds to make alcohols (R group acts as carbanion/nucleophile)", "Oxidise ketones", "Halogenate alkanes"], ans: 1, exp: "Grignard: RMgX (organomagnesium halide, very reactive). R⁻ is a powerful nucleophile: attacks C=O of aldehydes → 2° alcohols; of ketones → 3° alcohols; of CO₂ → carboxylic acids. Key synthetic reagent." },
    { q: "E1 elimination (versus SN1 substitution) is favoured by:", opts: ["High nucleophile concentration", "High temperature and/or bulky base (removal of β-H is favoured)", "Low temperature", "Polar protic solvent with weak nucleophile"], ans: 1, exp: "E1 vs SN1 competition: both form carbocation intermediate. High T favours elimination (entropy-driven). Bulky base (tert-BuO⁻) approaches H more easily than C → E1. Weak nucleophile: SN1 if RT, E1 if high T." },
    { q: "Which polymer is classified as a thermosetting plastic?", opts: ["Polyethylene (melts and reshapes)", "Nylon (thermoplastic)", "Bakelite (phenol-formaldehyde resin) — crosslinked, cannot be remelted", "PVC (thermoplastic)"], ans: 2, exp: "Thermosetting: crosslinked 3D network → cannot be remelted or reshaped. Bakelite (phenol + formaldehyde), melamine-formaldehyde, epoxy resins. Thermoplastics: linear/branched chains, can be remelted (PE, PP, PVC, nylon)." },
    { q: "Natural rubber (polyisoprene) is vulcanised with sulfur because:", opts: ["Sulfur adds colour", "S crosslinks the polymer chains → more elastic, harder, less sticky, wider temperature range", "Sulfur adds odour", "S prevents UV degradation only"], ans: 1, exp: "Vulcanisation: sulfur crosslinks C=C bonds in polyisoprene chains (S bridges). Properties improved: elasticity, tensile strength, resistance to heat/cold/solvents. 2-3% S: soft rubber. >30% S: ebonite (very hard). Discovered by Goodyear (1844)." },
    { q: "Optical isomers (enantiomers) differ in that they:", opts: ["Have different molecular formulas", "Have different melting points", "Are non-superimposable mirror images (differ in arrangement around chiral centre)", "Differ in degree of unsaturation"], ans: 2, exp: "Enantiomers: mirror image molecules that cannot be superimposed. Require a chiral centre (C bonded to 4 different groups). Identical physical properties (except: rotate plane-polarised light in opposite directions). Biological activity often differs (thalidomide case)." },
    { q: "Biodiesel is produced by transesterification of:", opts: ["Coal + methanol", "Vegetable oil/animal fat + methanol (with NaOH catalyst) → fatty acid methyl esters (FAME) + glycerol", "Crude oil + ethanol", "Sugars + yeast"], ans: 1, exp: "Transesterification: triglyceride + 3CH₃OH → 3 FAME (biodiesel) + glycerol. NaOH/KOH catalyst. FAME has similar combustion properties to diesel. Glycerol by-product used in cosmetics/food. Renewable, reduced CO₂ (compared to fossil diesel)." },
    { q: "The carbocation stability order in organic reactions:", opts: ["1° > 2° > 3°", "3° > 2° > 1° > methyl (alkyl groups stabilise by hyperconjugation/induction)", "Methyl > 1° > 2°", "All equally stable"], ans: 1, exp: "Carbocation stability: 3° > 2° > 1° > CH₃⁺. Alkyl groups donate electron density by induction and hyperconjugation → stabilise positive charge. Determines product in E1, SN1, and Markovnikov reactions." },
    { q: "Petrochemicals vs petroleum fuels: petrochemicals are:", opts: ["Fuels for burning", "Chemical feedstocks derived from petroleum for making plastics, solvents, drugs, synthetic fibres", "Lubricants only", "Liquefied petroleum gas only"], ans: 1, exp: "Petrochemicals: ethylene, propylene, benzene, toluene, xylene (BTX) — derived from crude oil. Used to make plastics (PE, PP), synthetic rubber, solvents (acetone), drugs, fibres (polyester, nylon). ~90% of organic chemicals derive from petroleum." },
    { q: "Saponification value of a fat indicates:", opts: ["Iodine content", "Amount of KOH (in mg) needed to saponify 1g of fat — related to average fatty acid chain length", "Rancidity level", "Melting point"], ans: 1, exp: "Saponification value (SV): mg KOH to saponify 1g fat. Higher SV → shorter chain fatty acids (more ester groups per gram). Coconut oil has high SV (shorter C8-C12 chains). Important in quality control of fats/oils." },
    { q: "Iodine value of a fat indicates:", opts: ["Degree of saponification", "Degree of unsaturation (g I₂ absorbed per 100g fat) — more C=C → higher iodine value", "Rancidity", "Chain length"], ans: 1, exp: "Iodine value (IV): g I₂ absorbed by 100g fat via addition to C=C double bonds. Saturated fats (butter, coconut): low IV. Unsaturated oils (linseed, sunflower): high IV. Used in edible oil quality testing." },
    { q: "What is a zwitterion in amino acids?", opts: ["An uncharged amino acid", "A species with both positive and negative charges (−NH₃⁺ and −COO⁻) at isoelectric point", "An amino acid dimer", "A cyclic amino acid"], ans: 1, exp: "Zwitterion: amino acid at its isoelectric point (pI). −NH₂ is protonated to −NH₃⁺ (positive), −COOH is deprotonated to −COO⁻ (negative). Net charge = 0. Most crystalline amino acids exist as zwitterions." },
    { q: "Peptide bond formation: amino acid + amino acid → dipeptide + water. Peptide bond is:", opts: ["C−N bond formed by esterification", "Amide bond (−CO−NH−) formed by condensation between −COOH of one and −NH₂ of another amino acid", "Disulfide bond (−S−S−)", "Hydrogen bond"], ans: 1, exp: "Peptide bond: −CO−NH−. Formed by condensation: R₁−COOH + H₂N−R₂ → R₁−CO−NH−R₂ + H₂O. Partial double bond character → planar, trans configuration preferred. Chain of peptide bonds = polypeptide → protein." },
    { q: "Which reaction confirms presence of carbon-carbon double bond in an organic compound?", opts: ["Na metal test", "Ferric chloride test", "Bromine water decolourisation (Br₂/CCl₄ addition across C=C)", "Silver mirror test"], ans: 2, exp: "Bromine water test: C=C + Br₂ → dibromoalkane. Brown bromine water becomes colourless (decolouration). Simple and specific for alkenes and alkynes. Benzene doesn't decolourise bromine water (aromatic, needs catalyst for substitution)." },
    { q: "Delocalization in benzene means that the C−C bond length (140 pm) is:", opts: ["Same as C−C single bond (154 pm)", "Same as C=C double bond (134 pm)", "Intermediate between C−C (154 pm) and C=C (134 pm) — all equivalent", "Shorter than triple bond"], ans: 2, exp: "Benzene C−C: 140 pm = intermediate between single (154 pm) and double (134 pm). All 6 bonds are equivalent due to electron delocalisation. This provides aromatic stability (resonance energy ~150 kJ/mol)." },
    { q: "Industrial synthesis of ethanoic acid from methanol (Monsanto process):", opts: ["CH₃OH + CO₂ catalyst → CH₃COOH (low yield)", "CH₃OH + CO → CH₃COOH (carbonylation, Rh or Ir catalyst, high yield)", "Fermentation of glucose", "Oxidation of ethanol only"], ans: 1, exp: "Monsanto (Rh catalyst) and Cativa (Ir catalyst) processes: CH₃OH + CO → CH₃COOH. ~90% selectivity. Replaced older fermentation and wacker processes for industrial scale. Produces >10 million tonnes/year worldwide." },
    { q: "Optical rotation of a compound is measured by:", opts: ["Refractometer", "Polarimeter (measures rotation of plane-polarised light)", "Spectrometer for colour", "Mass spectrometer"], ans: 1, exp: "Polarimeter: polarised light passes through solution of chiral compound. Enantiomers rotate light equally but in opposite directions (+, dextrorotatory or −, levorotatory). Racemic mixture (50:50 enantiomers): zero net rotation." },
    { q: "What is the IUPAC name of the compound CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₂OH?", opts: ["2-methylbutan-1-ol", "3-methylbutan-1-ol", "2-methylbutan-4-ol", "Isoamyl alcohol"], ans: 1, exp: "Structure: HO−CH₂−CH(CH₃)−CH₂−CH₃. Longest chain containing OH: 4 carbons (butanol). OH on C-1, methyl branch on C-3 → 3-methylbutan-1-ol. Numbering from the OH end: C1(OH)−C2−C3(CH₃)−C4. IUPAC: choose longest chain containing the principal functional group (OH), number from end nearest OH." },
    { q: "Which of the following pairs are functional group isomers (same molecular formula, different functional groups)?", opts: ["Ethanol and dimethyl ether (C₂H₆O)", "Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol (C₃H₈O)", "Ethane and propane", "Ethene and ethyne"], ans: 0, exp: "Functional group isomers: same molecular formula, different functional groups. Ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH, alcohol) and dimethyl ether (CH₃OCH₃, ether) are both C₂H₆O but have different functional groups (−OH vs −O−). Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol are positional isomers (same functional group, different position). Chain isomers and positional isomers are other categories." },
    { q: "Ethene (CH₂=CH₂) reacts with Cl₂ in the presence of UV light vs in dark. What products form under each condition?", opts: ["Same product (1,2-dichloroethane) in both", "UV/light: HCl + CHCl=CH₂ (substitution); dark: Cl₂ addition → 1,2-dichloroethane", "Dark: Cl₂ addition → ClCH₂CH₂Cl; UV: substitution → CH₂=CHCl + HCl", "Cl₂ never reacts with ethene"], ans: 2, exp: "With alkenes: Cl₂ in dark → electrophilic addition across C=C → 1,2-dichloroethane (ClCH₂CH₂Cl). In UV light → free radical substitution of allylic H → vinyl chloride (CH₂=CHCl) + HCl. Mechanism determines product: addition (dark, ionic) vs substitution (UV, radical). Important distinction for higher examinations." },
    { q: "Why does soap NOT work effectively in hard water, whereas synthetic detergents do?", opts: ["Soap is more acidic than detergent", "Soap (sodium stearate, RCOO⁻Na⁺) forms insoluble Ca/Mg scum with hard water ions; detergents (sodium dodecylbenzene sulphonate) have sulphonate group (−SO₃⁻) that forms soluble Ca/Mg salts", "Detergents are cheaper", "Soap does not have a hydrophobic tail"], ans: 1, exp: "Hard water: Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺. Soap: RCOO⁻ + Ca²⁺ → (RCOO)₂Ca↓ (insoluble scum — clogs fabric pores, wastes soap). Synthetic detergents: R−C₆H₄−SO₃⁻ + Ca²⁺ → Ca(R−C₆H₄−SO₃)₂ (SOLUBLE — maintains cleaning action). The −SO₃⁻ group forms more soluble calcium/magnesium salts than −COO⁻. However, branched-chain detergents (ABS) were non-biodegradable → foam in rivers → now replaced by linear chain (LAS) which is biodegradable." },
    { q: "The IUPAC name of CH₃COOC₂H₅ (ethyl acetate) is:", opts: ["Ethyl ethanoate", "Methyl propanoate", "Ethyl methanoate", "Propyl ethanoate"], ans: 0, exp: "Ethyl acetate: CH₃−CO−O−CH₂CH₃. IUPAC ester naming: alkyl (from alcohol) + carboxylate (from acid). Alcohol part: ethyl (C₂H₅−). Acid part: acetic acid = ethanoic acid → ethanoate. Name: ethyl ethanoate. Methyl propanoate: CH₃CH₂COOCH₃. Ethyl methanoate: HCOOC₂H₅." },
    { q: "The degree of unsaturation (also called the index of hydrogen deficiency, IHD) of C₆H₅COOH (benzoic acid) is:", opts: ["3", "4", "5", "6"], ans: 1, exp: "IHD = (2C + 2 + N − H − X) / 2 = (2×7 + 2 − 6) / 2 = (14 + 2 − 6) / 2 = 10/2 = 5. Wait: C₆H₅COOH = C₇H₆O₂. IHD = (2×7 + 2 − 6)/2 = (16−6)/2 = 10/2 = 5. Benzene ring = 4 (3 double bonds + ring), carboxylic acid C=O = 1. Total = 5. Correct." },
    { q: "Addition polymerisation of propylene (CH₃CH=CH₂) produces polypropylene. The initiator in free-radical polymerisation breaks down to give:", opts: ["H⁺ ions", "Free radicals from peroxides (e.g., benzoyl peroxide → 2 PhCO₂• → 2 Ph• + 2CO₂)", "Anions from BF₃", "Transition metal complexes"], ans: 1, exp: "Free radical polymerisation: initiator (peroxide, ROOR → 2RO•; or AIBN → 2R• + N₂). Initiation: R• + CH₂=CH(CH₃) → R−CH₂−C•H(CH₃). Propagation: chain grows by adding monomers. Termination: two radicals combine or disproportionate. Produces atactic (random stereo) PP. For isotactic/syndiotactic PP (stronger): Ziegler-Natta catalyst (TiCl₄/AlEt₃)." },
    { q: "Saponification of an ester (ethyl ethanoate) with NaOH gives:", opts: ["Ethanol + Acetic acid", "Ethanol + Sodium ethanoate (sodium acetate) — base hydrolysis is irreversible", "Ethyl sodium + Acetic acid", "Sodium ethoxide + Acetic acid"], ans: 1, exp: "Saponification (base hydrolysis): CH₃COOC₂H₅ + NaOH → CH₃COONa + C₂H₅OH. Unlike acid hydrolysis (reversible), base hydrolysis is irreversible because the carboxylate anion (CH₃COO⁻) does not re-react with alcohol. The sodium salt of the carboxylic acid + alcohol are the products. This is how soap is made from fats + NaOH." },
    { q: "Markovnikov's rule can be explained mechanistically by:", opts: ["The addition of H to the more substituted carbon because H is smaller", "Formation of the more stable (more substituted) carbocation intermediate — secondary or tertiary carbocation more stable than primary", "Random addition of H across the double bond", "The thermodynamic stability of the product alkane"], ans: 1, exp: "Markovnikov mechanistic basis: addition of H⁺ to the double bond can form two possible carbocation intermediates. H adds to give the more stable (higher-substitution) carbocation (tertiary > secondary > primary), which forms faster (lower activation energy → Hammond postulate). Nucleophile (Br⁻/OH⁻) then attacks the more stable carbocation. Anti-Markovnikov addition occurs with HBr + peroxides (free radical, not ionic mechanism)." },
    { q: "The difference between a monomer, polymer, and copolymer is that a copolymer:", opts: ["Is a single repeating monomer", "Is made from two or more different monomers in the same chain", "Has no repeating units", "Forms only by condensation"], ans: 1, exp: "Homopolymer: single monomer (polyethylene from ethylene, PVC from vinyl chloride). Copolymer: two or more different monomers. Alternating copolymer: ABABAB (nylon-6,6). Random copolymer: AABABBBAB. Block copolymer: AAABBB. Graft copolymer: backbone of A with branches of B. SBR rubber: styrene-butadiene copolymer. ABS plastic: acrylonitrile-butadiene-styrene terpolymer." },
    { q: "Which test would BEST distinguish between propan-1-ol (primary alcohol) and propan-2-ol (secondary alcohol) in a school lab?", opts: ["Bromine water test", "Lucas test (ZnCl₂/conc. HCl): propan-2-ol gives cloudiness in 5 min; propan-1-ol gives NO cloudiness at room temperature", "Iodoform test (both are positive)", "Sodium metal test (both react similarly)"], ans: 1, exp: "Lucas test (ZnCl₂/conc. HCl): reaction rate reflects carbocation stability. Propan-2-ol (2°): forms secondary carbocation → turbidity in 5 min. Propan-1-ol (1°): slow SN1 at room temperature → no cloudiness (or very slow). Note: iodoform test: propan-2-ol = positive (CH₃CHOH- group); propan-1-ol = negative (no CH₃CO- group). So iodoform test ALSO distinguishes them — both are valid answers, but Lucas test is the classic distinction." },
    { q: "The van der Waals forces in alkanes increase with chain length. Which physical property directly reflects this trend?", opts: ["Chemical reactivity (decreases with chain length)", "Boiling point (increases with chain length due to greater surface area for van der Waals interactions)", "Solubility in water (increases with chain length)", "Flammability (decreases with chain length)"], ans: 1, exp: "Van der Waals (London dispersion) forces in alkanes: proportional to molecular surface area and polarisability. Longer chain = more electrons = stronger van der Waals = more energy to vaporise = higher boiling point. Methane: −162°C. Butane: −1°C. Decane: 174°C. Branched alkanes have lower boiling points than straight-chain isomers (less surface contact). Solubility in water decreases with chain length." },
    { q: "In a substitution reaction of methane with chlorine (UV light): CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl, what type of mechanism operates?", opts: ["Electrophilic substitution", "Free radical substitution (chain mechanism: initiation → propagation → termination)", "Nucleophilic substitution", "Ionic elimination"], ans: 1, exp: "Free radical chain mechanism: Initiation: Cl₂ + UV → 2Cl• (homolytic fission). Propagation: Cl• + CH₄ → CH₃• + HCl; CH₃• + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•. Termination: Cl• + Cl• → Cl₂ or CH₃• + Cl• → CH₃Cl etc. The chain repeats thousands of times per initiating Cl• radical. Products: CH₃Cl, CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃, CCl₄ in proportions dependent on Cl₂:CH₄ ratio." },
    { q: "Biodegradable vs non-biodegradable polymers in environmental context: which correctly identifies PLA (polylactic acid)?", opts: ["Non-biodegradable synthetic polymer", "Biodegradable biopolymer derived from corn starch/sugarcane (lactic acid monomer) — breaks down by microorganisms in industrial composting", "A type of nylon", "A petroleum-based plastic"], ans: 1, exp: "PLA (polylactic acid): monomer is lactic acid (CH₃CHOHCOOH) from fermentation of corn starch, sugarcane. Biodegradable under industrial composting conditions (>60°C, microorganisms). Used in food packaging, medical sutures (dissolves in body). Degrades into CO₂ + H₂O in ~6 months (industrial compost). However, requires special facilities — does NOT biodegrade easily in home compost or landfill." },
    { q: "Soap forms micelles in water. The correct description of soap micelle structure is:", opts: ["Hydrophilic tails cluster inward, hydrophobic heads point outward", "Hydrophobic long-chain tails cluster inward (away from water), ionic/polar hydrophilic heads (−COO⁻) point outward toward water", "Spherical structure of only water molecules", "Random arrangement of soap molecules"], ans: 1, exp: "Micelle structure: soap molecule (RCOONa) has: (i) non-polar hydrophobic hydrocarbon tail (R, 12-18 C) and (ii) polar hydrophilic carboxylate head (−COO⁻Na⁺). In water above critical micelle concentration (CMC): tails cluster inward (avoid water), heads point outward (interact with water). Oily/greasy dirt is trapped in the hydrophobic core. Micelles are ~4-5 nm diameter, carrying ~60-100 soap molecules. Dirt-loaded micelles are rinsed away with water." }
  ]
,

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════
     CH 5 — LIFE PROCESSES
  ══════════════════════════════════════ */
  5: [
    { q: "Which of the following is NOT considered a life process?", opts: ["Nutrition", "Respiration", "Locomotion", "Excretion"], ans: 2, exp: "Life processes are processes essential for maintaining life: nutrition, respiration, transportation, excretion, growth, reproduction. Locomotion (movement from place to place) is NOT mandatory for life — plants don't locomote." },
    { q: "In which organelle does photosynthesis occur?", opts: ["Mitochondria", "Chloroplast", "Ribosome", "Nucleus"], ans: 1, exp: "Chloroplasts contain the green pigment chlorophyll and are the site of photosynthesis. Mitochondria are the site of aerobic respiration (cellular)." },
    { q: "The equation for photosynthesis is: 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + ?", opts: ["6CO₂", "6H₂", "6O₂", "H₂O"], ans: 2, exp: "6CO₂ + 6H₂O + light energy → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂. Oxygen is released as a by-product when water molecules are split (photolysis) during the light reaction." },
    { q: "Stomata in leaves are primarily responsible for:", opts: ["Photosynthesis only", "Gaseous exchange (CO₂ in, O₂ out) and transpiration", "Absorption of water", "Support of the leaf"], ans: 1, exp: "Stomata are pores in the epidermis controlled by guard cells. They allow CO₂ to enter (for photosynthesis) and O₂ to exit, and water vapour to transpire." },
    { q: "Which type of nutrition does an organism use when it synthesises its own food using inorganic sources?", opts: ["Heterotrophic", "Holozoic", "Autotrophic", "Saprotrophic"], ans: 2, exp: "Autotrophic nutrition: organism makes its own food from CO₂ and H₂O using light (photosynthesis) or chemical energy (chemosynthesis). Green plants and some bacteria." },
    { q: "Holozoic nutrition involves which sequence?", opts: ["Ingestion → Digestion → Absorption → Assimilation → Egestion", "Absorption → Digestion → Ingestion → Egestion", "Digestion → Ingestion → Assimilation", "Egestion → Digestion → Absorption"], ans: 0, exp: "Holozoic (animals): food is taken in (ingestion), broken down (digestion), absorbed, used (assimilation), and undigested waste expelled (egestion). Humans use holozoic nutrition." },
    { q: "In Amoeba, food is engulfed by a process called:", opts: ["Pinocytosis", "Phagocytosis (forming a food vacuole)", "Osmosis", "Diffusion"], ans: 1, exp: "Amoeba extends pseudopodia around food particles and engulfs them, forming a food vacuole — this is phagocytosis. Digestive enzymes in the vacuole break down the food." },
    { q: "The enzyme salivary amylase acts on:", opts: ["Proteins", "Fats", "Starch (converts to maltose)", "Cellulose"], ans: 2, exp: "Salivary amylase (ptyalin) in saliva begins starch digestion in the mouth, converting starch → maltose (a disaccharide). Optimal pH ~7 (neutral)." },
    { q: "Bile is produced by the liver and stored in the:", opts: ["Pancreas", "Gall bladder", "Stomach", "Duodenum"], ans: 1, exp: "Bile is produced in the liver and stored in the gall bladder. It is released into the duodenum (small intestine) to emulsify fats — breaking large fat globules into smaller droplets." },
    { q: "Which enzyme in gastric juice digests proteins?", opts: ["Lipase", "Amylase", "Pepsin", "Trypsin"], ans: 2, exp: "Pepsin (activated from pepsinogen by HCl) digests proteins in the stomach at acidic pH (~2). Trypsin works in the small intestine; amylase digests starch; lipase digests fat." },
    { q: "Absorption of digested food occurs mainly in:", opts: ["Stomach", "Large intestine", "Small intestine (villi)", "Oesophagus"], ans: 2, exp: "Small intestine has millions of finger-like villi (and microvilli) that vastly increase surface area for absorption. Blood capillaries and lacteals absorb nutrients." },
    { q: "Lacteals in the villi of small intestine absorb:", opts: ["Glucose and amino acids", "Fatty acids and glycerol (digested fats)", "Minerals", "Water"], ans: 1, exp: "Lacteals are lymph capillaries that absorb fatty acids and glycerol. Glucose and amino acids are absorbed by blood capillaries. Fat goes into the lymphatic system first." },
    { q: "The main function of the large intestine (colon) is:", opts: ["Protein digestion", "Fat absorption", "Absorption of water and formation of faeces", "Secretion of bile"], ans: 2, exp: "Large intestine reabsorbs water from undigested material, concentrating it into semi-solid faeces (stored in rectum). No significant digestion occurs here." },
    { q: "During aerobic respiration, glucose is completely oxidised to:", opts: ["Lactic acid + energy", "Ethanol + CO₂ + energy", "CO₂ + H₂O + energy (ATP)", "Pyruvate only"], ans: 2, exp: "Aerobic respiration: C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + 38 ATP. Complete oxidation occurs, releasing maximum energy. Occurs in mitochondria." },
    { q: "In which part of the cell does glycolysis (first step of respiration) occur?", opts: ["Mitochondria", "Nucleus", "Cytoplasm", "Ribosome"], ans: 2, exp: "Glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm of the cell (not in mitochondria). Glucose → 2 pyruvate + 2 ATP + 2 NADH. This is common to both aerobic and anaerobic respiration." },
    { q: "Anaerobic respiration in yeast produces:", opts: ["Lactic acid + CO₂", "CO₂ + H₂O", "Ethanol + CO₂ + energy", "Only ATP"], ans: 2, exp: "Yeast (anaerobic): glucose → ethanol + CO₂ + 2 ATP. This fermentation is used in bread making (CO₂ makes dough rise) and alcohol production." },
    { q: "Anaerobic respiration in human muscle cells (during heavy exercise) produces:", opts: ["Ethanol + CO₂", "Lactic acid (causing muscle cramp)", "Pyruvate + O₂", "CO₂ + H₂O"], ans: 1, exp: "In muscle cells, when O₂ is insufficient: glucose → lactic acid + 2 ATP. Lactic acid accumulation causes the burning sensation and cramp. Repaid by \"oxygen debt.\"" },
    { q: "Which organelle is called the \"powerhouse of the cell\"?", opts: ["Chloroplast", "Nucleus", "Mitochondria", "Golgi apparatus"], ans: 2, exp: "Mitochondria produce ATP (adenosine triphosphate) via aerobic respiration (Krebs cycle + oxidative phosphorylation). ATP is the energy currency of the cell." },
    { q: "The respiratory pigment in human blood that carries oxygen is:", opts: ["Myoglobin", "Haemoglobin (in RBCs)", "Fibrinogen", "Albumin"], ans: 1, exp: "Haemoglobin in red blood corpuscles (RBCs) carries O₂. Each Hb molecule has 4 haem groups, each binding one O₂. ~97% of O₂ is carried this way." },
    { q: "In humans, breathing rate is controlled by the concentration of:", opts: ["O₂ only", "CO₂ (and resulting pH drop) in blood", "N₂ in blood", "Glucose in blood"], ans: 1, exp: "Rising CO₂ dissolves in blood → carbonic acid → H⁺ → pH drops. Chemoreceptors in the medulla oblongata detect this and increase breathing rate to expel CO₂." },
    { q: "The process by which plants lose water vapour through stomata is called:", opts: ["Transpiration", "Guttation", "Evaporation", "Osmosis"], ans: 0, exp: "Transpiration is the evaporation of water from aerial parts of plants (mainly through stomata). It creates a transpiration pull that helps draw water upward from roots." },
    { q: "Water and dissolved minerals are transported in plants through:", opts: ["Phloem", "Xylem", "Both equally", "Parenchyma"], ans: 1, exp: "Xylem transports water and dissolved minerals from roots to leaves (unidirectional, upward). Movement driven by transpiration pull and root pressure. Dead cells." },
    { q: "Phloem transports:", opts: ["Water only", "Minerals only", "Food (sucrose and amino acids) from leaves to all parts", "Oxygen"], ans: 2, exp: "Phloem transports food (mainly sucrose) from leaves (source) to roots, fruits, seeds (sinks) — bidirectional. This is called translocation. Uses living sieve tube cells." },
    { q: "The force that pushes water up from roots is called:", opts: ["Transpiration pull", "Root pressure", "Osmotic pressure", "Both root pressure and transpiration pull"], ans: 3, exp: "Both forces contribute: Root pressure (active pumping of ions → water follows by osmosis) + Transpiration pull (evaporation from leaves creates tension that pulls water column up)." },
    { q: "In humans, the heart has how many chambers?", opts: ["2", "3", "4", "5"], ans: 2, exp: "Human heart: 4 chambers — 2 atria (right and left) + 2 ventricles (right and left). Double circulation: pulmonary (heart-lungs) and systemic (heart-body)." },
    { q: "Which blood vessel carries oxygenated blood FROM the lungs TO the heart?", opts: ["Pulmonary artery", "Pulmonary vein", "Aorta", "Vena cava"], ans: 1, exp: "Pulmonary vein carries oxygenated blood from lungs to left atrium. (Unique: a vein carrying oxygenated blood.) Pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood from right ventricle to lungs." },
    { q: "The \"lub-dub\" sounds of the heart are caused by:", opts: ["Blood hitting chamber walls", "Closure of heart valves (AV valves then semilunar valves)", "Contraction of ventricles", "Electrical signals"], ans: 1, exp: "\"Lub\" = closure of AV valves (bicuspid + tricuspid) at start of systole. \"Dub\" = closure of semilunar valves (aortic + pulmonary) at end of systole." },
    { q: "Platelets (thrombocytes) in blood are responsible for:", opts: ["Carrying O₂", "Immunity (antibodies)", "Blood clotting", "Digestion of pathogens"], ans: 2, exp: "Platelets are cell fragments that aggregate at wound sites and release clotting factors → fibrinogen → fibrin mesh trapping RBCs → clot formation stops bleeding." },
    { q: "In which blood vessels does exchange of materials with body tissues occur?", opts: ["Arteries", "Veins", "Capillaries", "Venules"], ans: 2, exp: "Capillaries have walls only one cell thick, allowing diffusion of O₂, nutrients (out) and CO₂, wastes (in). Arteries and veins are too thick for exchange." },
    { q: "Blood pressure is measured as systolic/diastolic. Normal is approximately:", opts: ["80/120 mmHg", "120/80 mmHg", "100/60 mmHg", "140/100 mmHg"], ans: 1, exp: "Normal blood pressure ≈ 120/80 mmHg. Systolic (120) = pressure when heart contracts (ventricles pump). Diastolic (80) = pressure when heart relaxes between beats." },
    { q: "The main excretory product in humans is:", opts: ["CO₂", "Urea", "Uric acid", "Ammonia"], ans: 1, exp: "Humans excrete mainly urea (from amino acid catabolism in the liver). Urea is less toxic than ammonia; excreted dissolved in water as urine. CO₂ and water are also excreted." },
    { q: "The functional unit of the kidney is the:", opts: ["Ureter", "Neuron", "Nephron", "Collecting duct"], ans: 2, exp: "Nephron: the structural and functional unit of the kidney. ~1 million nephrons per kidney. Each consists of a Bowman's capsule, glomerulus, proximal/distal tubule, and loop of Henle." },
    { q: "What is the first step in urine formation in the kidney?", opts: ["Tubular secretion", "Ultrafiltration (in glomerulus)", "Selective reabsorption", "Excretion"], ans: 1, exp: "Ultrafiltration: high pressure in the glomerular capillaries filters blood → glomerular filtrate enters Bowman's capsule. Filters water, glucose, salts, urea (not blood cells/proteins)." },
    { q: "Glucose is present in the glomerular filtrate but NOT in urine (normally) because:", opts: ["It is too large to filter", "It is completely reabsorbed in the proximal tubule", "It is destroyed in the kidney", "Glucose cannot enter the kidney"], ans: 1, exp: "All glucose filtered is selectively reabsorbed (active transport) in the proximal convoluted tubule → returns to blood. Glucose appears in urine only when blood sugar is very high (diabetes)." },
    { q: "The loop of Henle in the nephron is responsible for:", opts: ["Filtration of blood", "Concentration of urine (water reabsorption)", "Glucose reabsorption", "Urea secretion"], ans: 1, exp: "Loop of Henle (especially the descending limb) reabsorbs water, and the ascending limb pumps out salts, creating an osmotic gradient that concentrates the urine." },
    { q: "Dialysis (kidney machine) mimics which kidney function?", opts: ["Hormone production", "Filtration of waste from blood (replacing non-functional kidneys)", "Red blood cell production", "Vitamin D synthesis"], ans: 1, exp: "Dialysis uses a semi-permeable membrane to remove urea, creatinine, and excess salts from blood — mimicking kidney filtration for patients with kidney failure." },
    { q: "Which of the following is an excretory organ in humans?", opts: ["Pancreas", "Skin (sweat glands)", "Spleen", "Thyroid"], ans: 1, exp: "Skin excretes sweat (water, salts, urea). Other excretory organs: kidneys (urea in urine), lungs (CO₂, water vapour), liver (bile pigments)." },
    { q: "Plants excrete oxygen during the day as a by-product of:", opts: ["Respiration", "Transpiration", "Photosynthesis", "Excretion"], ans: 2, exp: "Plants release O₂ as a by-product of photosynthesis (from photolysis of water in light reactions). During the night, plants only respire and release CO₂." },
    { q: "Resin and gum are excretory products stored in:", opts: ["Roots", "Leaves", "Old xylem vessels and bark of plants", "Flowers"], ans: 2, exp: "Plants store excretory products (resins, gums, tannins, latex) in bark, old xylem (heartwood), and resin canals. Unlike animals, plants do not have a specialised excretory system." },
    { q: "The opening and closing of stomata is regulated by:", opts: ["Mesophyll cells", "Guard cells (via osmosis)", "Epidermal cells", "Root hair cells"], ans: 1, exp: "Guard cells are kidney-shaped cells that flank each stoma. When turgid (water-filled), they bow outward and stomata open. When flaccid (water loss), stomata close." },
    { q: "Which is NOT a function of the liver?", opts: ["Bile production", "Urea synthesis", "Production of RBCs in adults", "Detoxification of alcohol"], ans: 2, exp: "In adults, RBCs are produced in bone marrow (not the liver). In foetal life, the liver does produce RBCs. The liver produces bile, synthesises urea, detoxifies drugs/alcohol." },
    { q: "Photosynthesis occurs in two main stages. The light-independent stage is called:", opts: ["Light reaction", "Dark reaction (Calvin cycle)", "Glycolysis", "Kreb's cycle"], ans: 1, exp: "Calvin cycle (dark/light-independent reaction) uses ATP and NADPH (from light reactions) + CO₂ → glucose. Occurs in the stroma of chloroplasts." },
    { q: "Chlorophyll absorbs light mainly in which wavelengths?", opts: ["Green and yellow", "Red and blue-violet (reflects green)", "UV only", "Infrared only"], ans: 1, exp: "Chlorophyll absorbs red (~680nm) and blue-violet (~430nm) light for photosynthesis. Green light is reflected → plants appear green." },
    { q: "Which process helps in the movement of water and minerals from roots to leaves against gravity?", opts: ["Root pressure + transpiration pull + capillary action", "Osmosis alone", "Diffusion alone", "Active transport in xylem"], ans: 0, exp: "Water ascent in plants: Root pressure (push), transpiration pull (tension), and capillary action together drive water up through xylem, sometimes 100m in tall trees." },
    { q: "In aerobic respiration, the Krebs cycle occurs in the:", opts: ["Cytoplasm", "Nucleus", "Matrix of mitochondria", "Inner mitochondrial membrane"], ans: 2, exp: "Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle) occurs in the mitochondrial matrix. Pyruvate enters, CO₂ is released, and NADH/FADH₂ are produced for electron transport chain." },
    { q: "The net gain of ATP molecules from one molecule of glucose in aerobic respiration is approximately:", opts: ["2 ATP", "4 ATP", "36-38 ATP", "100 ATP"], ans: 2, exp: "Aerobic respiration: ~36-38 ATP per glucose (2 from glycolysis, 2 from Krebs, 32-34 from oxidative phosphorylation). Anaerobic gives only 2 ATP per glucose." },
    { q: "Haemoglobin combines with CO₂ (in tissues) to form:", opts: ["Oxyhaemoglobin", "Carboxyhaemoglobin", "Carbaminohaemoglobin", "Methaemoglobin"], ans: 2, exp: "CO₂ binds to the protein part (globin) of haemoglobin → carbaminohaemoglobin (~20% of CO₂ transport). Most CO₂ (~70%) is carried as bicarbonate ions (HCO₃⁻) in plasma." },
    { q: "Which blood group is the \"universal donor\"?", opts: ["AB", "B", "O", "A"], ans: 2, exp: "Blood group O (O negative, specifically O−) is the universal donor — no A or B antigens on RBCs, so no agglutination reaction. AB is the universal recipient." },
    { q: "The hepatic portal vein carries blood from:", opts: ["Heart to liver", "Intestines to liver (nutrient-rich blood)", "Liver to heart", "Kidneys to heart"], ans: 1, exp: "Hepatic portal vein carries nutrient-rich blood from the intestines (after absorption of digested food) to the liver for processing, storage (glycogen), and detoxification." },
    { q: "What is the pH of gastric juice in the stomach?", opts: ["6-7", "7-8", "1-2 (strongly acidic)", "8-9"], ans: 2, exp: "Gastric juice (HCl + pepsin + mucus) has pH ~1-2, highly acidic. HCl activates pepsinogen → pepsin, kills bacteria in food, and provides optimal pH for pepsin." },
    { q: "During the light reactions of photosynthesis, water is split (photolysis) at which photosystem?", opts: ["Photosystem I (P700)", "Photosystem II (P680)", "Both PS I and PS II equally", "The electron transport chain, not a photosystem"], ans: 1, exp: "Photolysis of water (2H₂O → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ + O₂) occurs at the oxygen-evolving complex associated with Photosystem II (P680). The electrons released replace those lost by P680 upon excitation. PS I does not split water." },
    { q: "In the Calvin cycle (C3 pathway), the primary CO₂ acceptor molecule is:", opts: ["Oxaloacetate (OAA)", "Phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP)", "Ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate (RuBP)", "3-phosphoglycerate (3-PGA)"], ans: 2, exp: "RuBP (5-carbon) is carboxylated by RuBisCO with CO₂ → two molecules of 3-PGA (3-carbon). In C4 plants, PEP first captures CO₂ → OAA in mesophyll cells, but the Calvin cycle still uses RuBP." },
    { q: "A plant is kept in a closed, transparent container for 24 hours. After 12 hours of light followed by 12 hours of dark, the net change in O₂ concentration will be:", opts: ["Always net increase", "Always net decrease", "Zero only if gross photosynthesis equals total respiration over 24h", "Unchanged because photosynthesis and respiration cancel exactly"], ans: 2, exp: "During the light period, gross photosynthesis minus respiration = net O₂ released. During dark, only respiration consumes O₂. Net change is zero only when O₂ gained in light equals O₂ consumed in 24 h of respiration — the compensation point condition." },
    { q: "In aerobic respiration, the electron transport chain (ETC) is located on the:", opts: ["Outer mitochondrial membrane", "Mitochondrial matrix", "Inner mitochondrial membrane (cristae)", "Cytoplasm"], ans: 2, exp: "The protein complexes (I, II, III, IV) of the ETC are embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane (cristae). Electron flow drives proton pumping from matrix to intermembrane space, creating the proton gradient that drives ATP synthase." },
    { q: "Glycolysis converts one glucose molecule into two pyruvate molecules. The NET gain of ATP in glycolysis is:", opts: ["4 ATP", "2 ATP (4 produced minus 2 consumed in the investment phase)", "38 ATP", "36 ATP"], ans: 1, exp: "Glycolysis: 2 ATP consumed in steps 1 and 3 (investment phase), 4 ATP produced in steps 7 and 10 (payoff phase). Net = 4 − 2 = 2 ATP per glucose. Also produces 2 NADH and 2 pyruvate." },
    { q: "Which of the following is the correct sequence of events in the Krebs cycle starting from acetyl-CoA?", opts: ["Acetyl-CoA + oxaloacetate → citrate → isocitrate → α-ketoglutarate → succinyl-CoA → succinate → fumarate → malate → oxaloacetate", "Acetyl-CoA → pyruvate → oxaloacetate → malate → fumarate", "Citrate → acetyl-CoA → succinate → oxaloacetate", "Oxaloacetate → pyruvate → acetyl-CoA → citrate → oxaloacetate"], ans: 0, exp: "Each turn of the Krebs cycle: Acetyl-CoA + oxaloacetate → citrate (6C) → isocitrate → α-ketoglutarate (5C) → succinyl-CoA (4C) → succinate → fumarate → malate → oxaloacetate (regenerated). Produces 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂, 1 GTP per turn." },
    { q: "In oxidative phosphorylation, ATP synthesis is driven by:", opts: ["Substrate-level phosphorylation (enzyme-catalysed)", "Proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane (chemiosmosis)", "Direct transfer of electrons to ADP", "Hydrolysis of NADH"], ans: 1, exp: "Mitchell's chemiosmosis theory: ETC pumps H⁺ from matrix to intermembrane space → electrochemical proton gradient. H⁺ flows back through ATP synthase (Complex V) → drives rotation of F₁ subunit → phosphorylates ADP to ATP." },
    { q: "Pyruvate dehydrogenase converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA. This reaction also produces:", opts: ["O₂ and NADH", "CO₂ and NADH", "CO₂ and FADH₂", "Water and ATP"], ans: 1, exp: "Pyruvate + CoA + NAD⁺ → Acetyl-CoA + CO₂ + NADH (pyruvate dehydrogenase complex). This irreversible oxidative decarboxylation occurs in the mitochondrial matrix and is the link reaction between glycolysis and the Krebs cycle." },
    { q: "In anaerobic fermentation in yeast, NADH is reoxidised to NAD⁺ by reducing:", opts: ["Oxygen to water", "Pyruvate to lactate", "Acetaldehyde to ethanol", "CO₂ to glucose"], ans: 2, exp: "In yeast fermentation: pyruvate → acetaldehyde (by pyruvate decarboxylase, releasing CO₂) → acetaldehyde is reduced to ethanol by alcohol dehydrogenase using NADH → NAD⁺ regenerated, allowing glycolysis to continue." },
    { q: "The stomatal opening mechanism in guard cells at dawn is primarily driven by:", opts: ["Increased CO₂ inside the leaf", "K⁺ influx into guard cells → water enters by osmosis → turgor pressure increases → stomata open", "Temperature rise only", "Abscisic acid stimulation"], ans: 1, exp: "In light: blue light activates H⁺-ATPase pumps in guard cell membranes → protons expelled → membrane hyperpolarised → K⁺ channels open → K⁺ and Cl⁻ enter → water follows by osmosis → guard cells become turgid → stomata open. ABA causes stomatal closure (opposite)." },
    { q: "The transpiration pull theory (cohesion-tension theory) for water ascent in xylem relies on which property of water?", opts: ["Low viscosity and high density", "High cohesion (hydrogen bonding between water molecules) and adhesion to xylem walls", "High surface tension at roots only", "Capillarity alone is sufficient for tall trees"], ans: 1, exp: "Dixon and Joly's cohesion-tension theory: transpiration creates tension (negative pressure) at the leaf surface; water molecules are pulled up as a continuous column because cohesion (H-bonds) keeps them linked. Adhesion to lignified xylem walls prevents column breakage." },
    { q: "Translocation of sugars in phloem occurs by:", opts: ["Simple diffusion down a concentration gradient", "Active mass flow (Münch pressure flow) from source (high sugar conc.) to sink (low sugar conc.)", "Capillary action similar to xylem", "Root pressure generated at the base"], ans: 1, exp: "Münch's pressure-flow hypothesis: photosynthate loaded actively into phloem sieve tubes at source → osmotic water entry → turgor pressure → mass flow toward sink where sugar is unloaded, reducing turgor. Phloem is bidirectional unlike xylem." },
    { q: "Root pressure in plants is caused by:", opts: ["Transpiration creating suction", "Active secretion of mineral ions into xylem → water follows by osmosis → pushes water column upward", "Capillary action in xylem vessels", "Gravitational forces"], ans: 1, exp: "Root pressure: mineral ions are actively transported into the xylem vessels; water from the soil follows by osmosis (down the water potential gradient) into the xylem, generating a positive pressure that pushes water upward. Evident as guttation at leaf tips." },
    { q: "Guttation in plants is:", opts: ["Evaporation of water from stomata", "Exudation of water droplets (containing dissolved minerals) from hydathodes at leaf margins, driven by root pressure", "Loss of water vapour from the cuticle", "Secretion of salts from leaves"], ans: 1, exp: "Guttation occurs mainly at night/early morning when transpiration is low but root pressure is high. Water is forced out through hydathodes (modified stomata at leaf tips). Unlike transpiration, guttation involves liquid water with dissolved solutes." },
    { q: "In the human heart, the atrioventricular (AV) node is significant because:", opts: ["It generates the primary heartbeat pacemaker signal", "It delays the impulse from the SA node by about 0.1 s, allowing ventricles to fill before contracting", "It connects the right and left ventricles electrically", "It prevents backflow of blood between atria"], ans: 1, exp: "The SA node (sinoatrial node) generates the primary electrical impulse (~70/min). The AV node delays conduction by ~0.1 s (fibrous tissue blocks direct conduction) → atrial systole completes ventricular filling → then impulse travels via Bundle of His → Purkinje fibres → ventricular contraction." },
    { q: "Which statement correctly distinguishes arteries from veins?", opts: ["Arteries always carry oxygenated blood; veins always carry deoxygenated blood", "Arteries carry blood away from the heart under high pressure; veins return blood to the heart under low pressure with valves", "Arteries have valves to prevent backflow; veins have thick muscular walls", "Veins carry blood at higher pressure than arteries"], ans: 1, exp: "Arteries: thick muscular walls, no valves, carry blood from heart at high pressure. Veins: thin walls, have valves (prevent backflow), return blood to heart at low pressure. Exception: pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood; pulmonary vein carries oxygenated blood." },
    { q: "A patient has a haemoglobin level of 7 g/dL (normal: 12-16 g/dL). What is the DIRECT consequence on gas exchange?", opts: ["Reduced O₂ carrying capacity of blood → tissue hypoxia; CO₂ transport is also affected", "Only CO₂ transport is impaired", "Ventilation rate decreases to compensate", "No effect because plasma dissolves O₂"], ans: 0, exp: "Haemoglobin carries ~97% of O₂ in blood. Severe anaemia (low Hb) → greatly reduced O₂ delivery to tissues. Compensatory mechanisms: increased heart rate and respiratory rate. CO₂ (mainly as bicarbonate) is also partially carried by Hb as carbaminohaemoglobin, so CO₂ transport is also mildly impaired." },
    { q: "The Bohr effect describes how rising CO₂ and falling pH in active tissues:", opts: ["Increase haemoglobin's affinity for O₂", "Decrease haemoglobin's affinity for O₂ → O₂ is released more readily to tissues (right shift of O₂ dissociation curve)", "Prevent CO₂ from entering the blood", "Cause haemoglobin to release CO₂ instead of O₂"], ans: 1, exp: "Bohr effect: increased CO₂ → carbonic acid → H⁺ → binds to Hb → conformational change → lower O₂ affinity → O₂ released at active tissues. This right-shift of the O₂-Hb dissociation curve is physiologically crucial for tissue O₂ delivery." },
    { q: "Lymph differs from blood plasma primarily in that lymph:", opts: ["Contains RBCs and platelets", "Has a much higher protein concentration", "Lacks proteins (especially albumin) and RBCs, and contains more lymphocytes", "Has higher glucose concentration"], ans: 2, exp: "Lymph is formed when tissue fluid (plasma filtrate) enters lymph capillaries. It lacks RBCs and most plasma proteins (which are too large to leak from capillaries). Lymph is rich in lymphocytes. It drains fat from the intestinal lacteals (as chyle)." },
    { q: "In kidney function, if the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) is 125 mL/min, how much filtrate is produced per day (approximately)?", opts: ["1.5 L", "12 L", "180 L", "1800 L"], ans: 2, exp: "125 mL/min × 60 min/h × 24 h = 180,000 mL = 180 L of glomerular filtrate per day. Of this, ~178.5 L is reabsorbed (99%), producing only ~1.5 L of urine. This illustrates the enormous filtering and reabsorption capacity of the kidneys." },
    { q: "The countercurrent multiplier system in the loop of Henle achieves:", opts: ["Filtration of blood at high pressure", "Secretion of urea into the tubule", "Establishment of an increasing osmolarity gradient in the medulla, allowing urine concentration", "Reabsorption of glucose in the proximal tubule"], ans: 2, exp: "The loop of Henle operates as a countercurrent multiplier: descending limb is permeable to water (loses water → interstitium becomes hyperosmotic); ascending limb actively pumps NaCl (impermeable to water). This creates a medullary osmotic gradient (300→1200 mOsm/kg) used to concentrate urine in the collecting duct under ADH control." },
    { q: "A person with renal failure accumulates urea in the blood (uraemia). Which kidney function is MOST directly failing?", opts: ["Glucose reabsorption", "Selective reabsorption of amino acids", "Excretion of nitrogenous waste (urea) via filtration and tubular secretion", "Vitamin D activation"], ans: 2, exp: "Urea is freely filtered at the glomerulus and partially reabsorbed; some is also secreted in the collecting duct. In renal failure, the reduced GFR means less urea is filtered and excreted → blood urea nitrogen (BUN) rises → uraemia. Dialysis removes urea artificially." },
    { q: "In C4 plants (like maize and sugarcane), CO₂ is first fixed in mesophyll cells by PEP carboxylase into OAA. What is the advantage of this over RuBisCO?", opts: ["PEP carboxylase is slower but more accurate", "PEP carboxylase has a much higher affinity for CO₂ and does NOT fix O₂ (no photorespiration), making C4 plants more efficient in hot, dry conditions", "C4 plants produce more RuBP per cycle", "PEP carboxylase works only in the dark"], ans: 1, exp: "RuBisCO can fix O₂ instead of CO₂ (photorespiration), wasting energy. PEP carboxylase has ~60× higher CO₂ affinity and has no oxygenase activity. C4 plants concentrate CO₂ in bundle sheath cells for RuBisCO, suppressing photorespiration — crucial advantage in hot, high-light environments." },
    { q: "The oxygen evolved during photosynthesis comes from:", opts: ["CO₂ molecule", "Both CO₂ and H₂O equally", "Water (H₂O) only — confirmed by ¹⁸O isotope tracer experiments", "Ozone layer above the leaf"], ans: 2, exp: "Van Niel's hypothesis (confirmed by Ruben and Kamen with ¹⁸O₂): photosynthetic O₂ comes exclusively from water (photolysis: 2H₂O → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ + O₂). When ¹⁸O-labelled water was used, ¹⁸O₂ was released; when ¹⁸O-CO₂ was used, labelled O appeared in sugars, not in O₂." },
    { q: "A graph shows enzyme activity vs. substrate concentration. Initially the curve rises steeply, then plateaus (Vmax). This plateau is due to:", opts: ["Enzyme denaturation at high substrate", "All enzyme active sites being saturated — additional substrate cannot increase rate further until enzyme is freed", "Product inhibition at high substrate", "pH change due to high substrate"], ans: 1, exp: "Michaelis-Menten kinetics: at low [S], rate ∝ [S] (enzyme not saturated). At high [S], all enzyme active sites are occupied → rate becomes independent of [S] = Vmax. The half-maximum velocity occurs at Km (Michaelis constant), a measure of enzyme-substrate affinity." },
    { q: "The RQ (respiratory quotient) of a person on a fat-rich diet would be approximately:", opts: ["1.0", "0.7", "0.85", "1.3"], ans: 1, exp: "RQ = CO₂ produced / O₂ consumed. For carbohydrates (glucose): RQ = 1.0. For fats (e.g. palmitic acid): C₁₆H₃₂O₂ + 23O₂ → 16CO₂ + 16H₂O; RQ = 16/23 ≈ 0.7. For proteins: ~0.8. Fat metabolism requires more O₂ relative to CO₂ released." },
    { q: "During vigorous exercise, the oxygen debt that accumulates is later repaid by:", opts: ["Eating glucose to regenerate ATP", "Increased respiration after exercise to oxidise accumulated lactate back to pyruvate or glucose (Cori cycle)", "Hyperventilation before exercise", "Glycogen synthesis from O₂"], ans: 1, exp: "Oxygen debt (EPOC — excess post-exercise oxygen consumption): after intense exercise, elevated breathing/heart rate supplies extra O₂ to: (a) reconvert lactate → pyruvate → glucose (gluconeogenesis, Cori cycle) in the liver; (b) replenish ATP, creatine phosphate stores; (c) meet elevated metabolic demands of recovery." },
    { q: "Which of the following statements about phloem transport is INCORRECT?", opts: ["Phloem sap moves from source to sink", "Phloem transport is bidirectional in the same plant (upward to fruits, downward to roots)", "Phloem loading at source requires ATP expenditure", "Phloem transport occurs through dead sieve tube cells"], ans: 3, exp: "Phloem sieve tube cells are LIVING (unlike xylem vessels which are dead). Sieve tube cells have no nucleus but are metabolically active and connected by sieve plates. The other options are correct: phloem is bidirectional, loading is active, and sucrose moves from source to sink." },
    { q: "If a ring of bark (including phloem) is removed from a tree stem (ringing/girdling), what happens above and below the ring?", opts: ["Water transport stops immediately above the ring", "Sugar accumulates above the ring (phloem blocked); roots eventually starve since xylem (water) is intact but food supply is cut", "Both water and food transport stop", "Nothing — the tree heals instantly"], ans: 1, exp: "Ringing removes phloem (and cambium) while leaving xylem intact. Water and minerals still ascend through xylem. However, photosynthate cannot travel downward past the ring → sugar accumulates above (swelling/bulge) → roots are starved of food → plant eventually dies. Classic experiment proving phloem transports food." },
    { q: "The enzyme RuBisCO (ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase) is significant because:", opts: ["It is the fastest enzyme in nature", "It is the most abundant protein on Earth and catalyses the first step of carbon fixation in the Calvin cycle", "It only operates at temperatures above 40°C", "It is located in the thylakoid membrane"], ans: 1, exp: "RuBisCO (in the stroma of chloroplasts) catalyses CO₂ + RuBP → 2× 3-PGA. It is the most abundant protein on Earth (~0.7 billion tonnes), yet is catalytically slow and inefficient (can fix O₂ causing photorespiration). Its abundance compensates for its slow rate." },
    { q: "Which stage of aerobic respiration produces the most CO₂ per glucose molecule?", opts: ["Glycolysis", "Pyruvate decarboxylation + Krebs cycle (6 CO₂ total per glucose)", "Electron transport chain", "Oxidative phosphorylation"], ans: 1, exp: "Glycolysis: 0 CO₂. Pyruvate → Acetyl-CoA: 2 CO₂ (one per pyruvate, two per glucose). Krebs cycle: 4 CO₂ per glucose (2 turns × 2 CO₂). Total: 6 CO₂ per glucose. ETC and oxidative phosphorylation produce ATP but no CO₂." },
    { q: "The partial pressure of O₂ in the alveoli is approximately 100 mmHg and in pulmonary capillary blood is 40 mmHg. O₂ diffuses into blood because:", opts: ["Haemoglobin actively pumps O₂", "The concentration (partial pressure) gradient drives diffusion of O₂ from alveolus to blood", "Blood is at higher pressure than alveolar air", "O₂ is actively transported by ATPases in alveolar walls"], ans: 1, exp: "Gas exchange in lungs is entirely by passive diffusion, driven by the partial pressure gradient. O₂ moves from high pO₂ in alveolus (100 mmHg) to low pO₂ in blood (40 mmHg). Similarly, CO₂ diffuses from blood (pCO₂ 45 mmHg) to alveolus (pCO₂ 40 mmHg) and is expired." },
    { q: "Secretin, a hormone produced by the duodenal mucosa, stimulates:", opts: ["Gastric acid secretion", "Pancreatic secretion of bicarbonate-rich juice to neutralise acidic chyme", "Bile production in the liver", "Gallbladder contraction"], ans: 1, exp: "Secretin is released when acidic chyme enters the duodenum. It stimulates the pancreas to secrete bicarbonate-rich (alkaline) juice, neutralising HCl and creating the optimal pH for intestinal enzymes. CCK (cholecystokinin) stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion and gallbladder contraction." },
    { q: "In the hepatic portal circulation, blood passing from the intestines to the liver is richest in:", opts: ["O₂", "CO₂", "Absorbed nutrients (glucose, amino acids, fatty acids, vitamins)", "Urea"], ans: 2, exp: "After a meal, the hepatic portal vein delivers absorbed nutrients from the small intestine directly to the liver. The liver then: stores glucose as glycogen, deaminates excess amino acids → urea, processes lipids, and detoxifies compounds. This first-pass metabolism is clinically important for oral drugs." },
    { q: "The Bowman's capsule (glomerular capsule) collects filtrate. The filtrate at this point has the same composition as blood plasma EXCEPT it lacks:", opts: ["Glucose and amino acids", "Urea and creatinine", "Blood cells and large plasma proteins (albumin)", "Sodium and potassium ions"], ans: 2, exp: "Glomerular ultrafiltration: the filtration membrane has small pores. Blood cells (too large) and large proteins like albumin (MW ~69,000) cannot pass. Glucose, amino acids, salts, urea, creatinine all pass freely into the filtrate. Proteinuria (protein in urine) indicates glomerular damage." },
    { q: "Aldosterone (from adrenal cortex) acts on the collecting duct of the nephron to:", opts: ["Increase urine volume by decreasing water reabsorption", "Increase Na⁺ reabsorption (and K⁺ excretion) → water follows → higher blood volume and pressure", "Decrease glomerular filtration rate", "Increase glucose reabsorption"], ans: 1, exp: "Aldosterone (mineralocorticoid): upregulates Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase and ENaC (epithelial Na⁺ channels) in the collecting duct → more Na⁺ reabsorbed, K⁺ and H⁺ excreted. Water follows Na⁺ by osmosis → blood volume and BP increase. Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) regulates this." },
    { q: "Plants store excretory products differently from animals. Which of the following is a correct example?", opts: ["Plants excrete urea through specialised kidneys", "Tannins stored in bark and vacuoles, resins in resin canals, and essential oils in secretory cells serve as plant excretory products", "Plants release all waste as CO₂ through roots", "Plant excretion occurs only through flowers"], ans: 1, exp: "Plants lack specialised excretory organs. They store metabolic waste products in leaves (shed in autumn), bark, vacuoles (tannins, alkaloids), resin canals (resins, terpenes), and laticifers (latex). Some waste is eliminated by transpiration (CO₂, water vapour) or dissolves into soil through roots." },
    { q: "Which of the following statements about aerobic vs. anaerobic respiration is CORRECT?", opts: ["Anaerobic respiration always produces CO₂", "Aerobic respiration has a theoretical yield of ~38 ATP/glucose vs. 2 ATP/glucose for anaerobic respiration", "Both processes produce the same amount of ATP", "Anaerobic respiration requires mitochondria"], ans: 1, exp: "Aerobic: glycolysis (2 ATP) + Krebs (2 GTP) + oxidative phosphorylation (~34 ATP) ≈ 38 ATP total. Anaerobic: glycolysis only = 2 ATP (NADH reoxidised by fermentation). Note: anaerobic in yeast does produce CO₂, but anaerobic in muscle (lactic acid fermentation) does not produce CO₂." },
    { q: "Emulsification of fats by bile salts is important because:", opts: ["It chemically digests fat into fatty acids", "It increases the surface area of fat droplets for lipase action (bile salts are amphipathic detergents)", "It neutralises stomach acid", "It activates pepsin in the duodenum"], ans: 1, exp: "Bile salts have a hydrophilic head and hydrophobic tail (amphipathic). They coat large fat globules and disperse them into tiny droplets (emulsification) → greatly increases surface area → pancreatic lipase can hydrolyse fat (triglyceride → fatty acids + glycerol) much more efficiently." },
    { q: "A student measures the rate of photosynthesis at different light intensities. The rate initially increases linearly, then plateaus. What limits the rate at the plateau?", opts: ["CO₂ concentration and temperature (and enzyme activity) — light is no longer the limiting factor", "Lack of water in leaves", "Leaf pigment degradation at high light", "Night is approaching"], ans: 0, exp: "Blackman's law of limiting factors: when light intensity is high, photosynthesis is limited by other factors (CO₂ concentration, temperature, enzyme activity). The plateau in a light-intensity curve indicates CO₂ or temperature (dark reactions) is now limiting. Increasing CO₂ or temperature would raise the plateau." },
    { q: "The term \"oxygen debt\" after exercise refers to:", opts: ["Loss of O₂ from blood", "The extra O₂ consumed after exercise to metabolise accumulated lactate and restore pre-exercise conditions", "Reduced breathing rate post-exercise", "O₂ stored in myoglobin during exercise"], ans: 1, exp: "During intense exercise, anaerobic respiration produces lactate (and H⁺). Post-exercise, elevated respiration supplies O₂ to convert lactate → pyruvate → glucose (Cori cycle in liver), replenish ATP/creatine phosphate, and restore O₂ saturation of myoglobin. This extra O₂ consumption = oxygen debt or EPOC." },
    { q: "The net ionic equation for CO₂ transport in blood as bicarbonate is: CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻. Which enzyme catalyses this reaction in red blood cells?", opts: ["Haemoglobinase", "Carbonic anhydrase", "CO₂ synthetase", "Bicarbonate dehydrogenase"], ans: 1, exp: "Carbonic anhydrase (in RBCs) catalyses the rapid reversible hydration of CO₂ → H₂CO₃ → H⁺ + HCO₃⁻. HCO₃⁻ exits RBCs into plasma (chloride shift). At the lungs, the reverse occurs: CO₂ is released and expired. Without carbonic anhydrase, CO₂ transport would be too slow." },
    { q: "In the nephron, where does the maximum reabsorption of glucose and amino acids occur?", opts: ["Descending limb of loop of Henle", "Distal convoluted tubule (DCT)", "Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT)", "Collecting duct"], ans: 2, exp: "The proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) reabsorbs ~65% of filtered Na⁺ and water, virtually 100% of glucose (by Na⁺-glucose co-transporters, SGLT2) and amino acids, and most HCO₃⁻. The DCT and collecting duct fine-tune Na⁺ and water reabsorption under aldosterone and ADH control." },
    { q: "Guard cells in a fully open stoma are in which state, and why?", opts: ["Flaccid — water has left by osmosis due to low solute concentration", "Turgid — high osmotic solute concentration (K⁺, Cl⁻, malate) causes water entry by osmosis, bowing the cell wall outward and opening the pore", "Plasmolysed — the cytoplasm has shrunk away from the wall", "Isotonic with surrounding epidermal cells"], ans: 1, exp: "Open stoma: guard cells are turgid. In light, K⁺ and Cl⁻ are actively transported into guard cells, and malate²⁻ is synthesised from starch → high solute concentration → water enters by osmosis → turgor rises → kidney-shaped guard cells bow outward (due to differentially thickened walls) → pore opens." },
    { q: "Which of the following would NOT increase the rate of transpiration?", opts: ["Increased wind speed", "Increased humidity of surrounding air (reduces the water vapour gradient from leaf to air)", "Higher temperature", "Stomata opening wider"], ans: 1, exp: "Transpiration rate depends on the diffusion gradient of water vapour from inside the leaf to the surrounding air. High humidity reduces this gradient → less transpiration. Wind, high temperature, open stomata, and low humidity all increase transpiration rate." },
    { q: "What is the key difference between holozoic and saprotrophic (saprophytic) nutrition?", opts: ["Only holozoic organisms digest food externally", "Holozoic organisms ingest food then digest internally; saprotrophic organisms secrete digestive enzymes outside and absorb digested products (external digestion)", "Saprotrophic organisms only eat living plants", "Holozoic is a form of autotrophic nutrition"], ans: 1, exp: "Holozoic (animals): ingestion → internal digestion → absorption → assimilation. Saprotrophic (fungi, some bacteria): secrete enzymes extracellularly onto dead organic matter → absorb the small soluble products. Both are heterotrophic; neither makes their own food." },
    { q: "Chemosynthetic bacteria (e.g., Nitrosomonas) obtain energy by:", opts: ["Absorbing sunlight like plants", "Oxidising inorganic compounds (e.g., NH₃ → NO₂⁻) and using that chemical energy to fix CO₂ into organic molecules", "Consuming other bacteria holozoically", "Fermenting organic compounds in the absence of oxygen"], ans: 1, exp: "Chemosynthesis (chemoautotrophy): Nitrosomonas oxidises ammonia: NH₃ + O₂ → NO₂⁻ + energy. This energy drives CO₂ fixation (like photosynthesis but using chemical rather than light energy). Other examples: Nitrobacter (NO₂⁻ → NO₃⁻), Thiobacillus (S → SO₄²⁻). Important in the nitrogen cycle." },
    { q: "The FADH₂ produced in the Krebs cycle enters the electron transport chain at which complex?", opts: ["Complex I (NADH dehydrogenase)", "Complex II (succinate dehydrogenase)", "Complex III (cytochrome bc₁)", "Complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase)"], ans: 1, exp: "FADH₂ is produced at the succinate dehydrogenase step and is directly oxidised by Complex II, feeding electrons into the ETC at a lower energy point than NADH (which enters at Complex I). FADH₂ yields ~1.5 ATP vs ~2.5 ATP for NADH per molecule because fewer protons are pumped." },
    { q: "In a healthy adult human kidney, approximately what percentage of the glomerular filtrate is reabsorbed?", opts: ["50%", "75%", "99%", "100%"], ans: 2, exp: "Of the ~180 L of glomerular filtrate formed per day, approximately 178.5 L (99%) is reabsorbed by the tubules (PCT, loop of Henle, DCT, collecting duct). Only ~1.5 L is excreted as urine. This selective reabsorption is energy-intensive and hormonally regulated." },
    { q: "Insulin promotes glucose uptake in cells by causing:", opts: ["Breakdown of glycogen to glucose", "Translocation of GLUT4 glucose transporters to the cell membrane, increasing glucose entry into cells", "Increased gluconeogenesis in the liver", "Reduction in cell permeability to glucose"], ans: 1, exp: "Insulin binds to its receptor → signalling cascade → GLUT4-containing vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane of muscle and fat cells → more glucose transporters on surface → rapid glucose uptake. In the liver, insulin promotes glycolysis and glycogen synthesis, and inhibits gluconeogenesis." }
  ]
,

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════
     CH 6 — CONTROL AND COORDINATION
  ══════════════════════════════════════ */
  6: [
    { q: "Which of the following is the basic unit of the nervous system?", opts: ["Nephron", "Neuron", "Myocyte", "Glial cell"], ans: 1, exp: "Neuron (nerve cell) is the structural and functional unit of the nervous system. It consists of a cell body (soma), dendrites (receive signals), and axon (transmits signals)." },
    { q: "The gap between two neurons across which signals travel is called:", opts: ["Node of Ranvier", "Synapse", "Axon terminal", "Dendrite junction"], ans: 1, exp: "Synapse: the junction between two neurons. Electrical signal → chemical neurotransmitter released from axon terminal → crosses synaptic cleft → received by next neuron's dendrite." },
    { q: "A reflex arc bypasses which part of the CNS?", opts: ["Spinal cord", "Brain (cerebral cortex) — brain is informed later but does not initiate response", "Medulla oblongata", "Cerebellum"], ans: 1, exp: "Reflex arcs involve: receptor → sensory neuron → spinal cord (relay neuron) → motor neuron → effector. The brain is NOT involved in the reflex response (though it gets informed)." },
    { q: "The function of the cerebellum is:", opts: ["Thinking and intelligence", "Coordination of muscle movement and balance", "Regulation of heartbeat and breathing", "Vision processing"], ans: 1, exp: "Cerebellum (behind and below cerebrum): coordinates voluntary muscle movements, posture, and balance. Damage causes ataxia (loss of coordination)." },
    { q: "Which part of the brain controls involuntary actions like heartbeat, breathing, and swallowing?", opts: ["Cerebrum", "Cerebellum", "Medulla oblongata", "Thalamus"], ans: 2, exp: "Medulla oblongata (brain stem): controls vital involuntary actions — heartbeat rate, breathing, blood pressure, swallowing, coughing, vomiting." },
    { q: "The human nervous system is divided into:", opts: ["CNS and ANS only", "CNS and PNS (which includes ANS)", "Brain and spinal cord only", "Sensory and motor neurons"], ans: 1, exp: "CNS = brain + spinal cord. PNS = all nerves outside CNS. PNS includes somatic NS (voluntary) and autonomic NS (involuntary: sympathetic + parasympathetic)." },
    { q: "In a reflex action, the correct pathway is:", opts: ["Effector → spinal cord → brain → muscle", "Receptor → sensory neuron → spinal cord → motor neuron → effector", "Brain → motor neuron → receptor → effector", "Muscle → receptor → brain → muscle"], ans: 1, exp: "Reflex arc: stimulus → receptor → sensory neuron → relay neuron (in spinal cord) → motor neuron → effector (muscle/gland). Fast, automatic, protective responses." },
    { q: "Nastic movements in plants (like the touch-me-not Mimosa pudica) are caused by:", opts: ["Growth hormones", "Change in turgor pressure (seismonasty)", "Phototropism", "Gravitropism"], ans: 1, exp: "Mimosa pudica: touch triggers rapid loss of water from motor cells (pulvinus) at the leaf base → cells become flaccid → leaflets fold. Not a growth response, reversible." },
    { q: "Phototropism in plants is the growth response to:", opts: ["Water", "Gravity", "Light (stems grow toward light)", "Touch"], ans: 2, exp: "Phototropism: plant shoots bend toward light (positive phototropism). Auxin moves to the shaded side → more elongation on that side → shoot bends toward light." },
    { q: "The plant hormone responsible for cell elongation and phototropism is:", opts: ["Gibberellin", "Cytokinin", "Auxin (IAA)", "Abscisic acid"], ans: 2, exp: "Auxin (Indole-3-acetic acid, IAA): promotes cell elongation. In phototropism, auxin accumulates on the shaded side → greater elongation → shoot bends toward light." },
    { q: "Geotropism is the growth response to:", opts: ["Light", "Gravity (roots grow downward, shoots grow upward)", "Water", "Chemicals"], ans: 1, exp: "Geotropism (gravitropism): roots are positively geotropic (grow toward gravity/downward); shoots are negatively geotropic (grow away from gravity/upward)." },
    { q: "Which plant hormone promotes seed germination and stem elongation (internodal)?", opts: ["Auxin", "Gibberellin", "Cytokinin", "Ethylene"], ans: 1, exp: "Gibberellins: promote stem elongation, seed germination, and breaking dormancy. Applied gibberellin can make dwarf plants grow tall or cause seedless fruit development." },
    { q: "Cytokinin promotes:", opts: ["Root growth", "Seed dormancy", "Cell division (cytokinesis) and delays aging (senescence)", "Fruit ripening"], ans: 2, exp: "Cytokinins promote cell division and differentiation, delay leaf senescence (aging), and work with auxin to regulate growth. Discovered as kinetin from autoclaved DNA." },
    { q: "Abscisic acid (ABA) is called the \"stress hormone\" because it:", opts: ["Promotes growth under stress", "Causes wilting and inhibits growth (closes stomata in drought)", "Promotes flowering", "Speeds up fruit ripening"], ans: 1, exp: "ABA is the inhibitory hormone: causes stomatal closure during drought, promotes seed dormancy, inhibits growth. Antagonist of gibberellins. Helps plant survive water stress." },
    { q: "Ethylene (ethene) plant hormone is responsible for:", opts: ["Bending toward light", "Fruit ripening, leaf fall, and flower wilting", "Cell division", "Seed germination"], ans: 1, exp: "Ethylene is a gaseous hormone that promotes fruit ripening (commercially used to ripen bananas), induces leaf/flower abscission (fall), and promotes senescence." },
    { q: "A dendrite receives nerve impulses and sends them toward the:", opts: ["Axon terminal", "Cell body (soma)", "Next neuron's axon", "Myelin sheath"], ans: 1, exp: "Dendrites are short, branched projections that receive signals from other neurons and transmit them TOWARD the cell body. Axons transmit signals AWAY from the cell body." },
    { q: "The myelin sheath around axons is made of:", opts: ["Protein only", "Lipid (fatty substance) produced by Schwann cells", "Glycogen", "Collagen"], ans: 1, exp: "Myelin sheath: lipid-rich insulating layer around axons, formed by Schwann cells (PNS) or oligodendrocytes (CNS). Speeds up impulse transmission by saltatory conduction." },
    { q: "Multiple sclerosis is a disease where:", opts: ["Neurons die", "Myelin sheath is destroyed (demyelination) → slowed nerve impulse", "Synapses are blocked", "Dendrites overgrow"], ans: 1, exp: "Multiple sclerosis: autoimmune destruction of myelin sheath → disrupted/slowed nerve impulse transmission. Symptoms: muscle weakness, coordination problems, vision issues." },
    { q: "The part of the brain primarily responsible for intelligence, memory, and voluntary actions is the:", opts: ["Cerebellum", "Medulla", "Cerebrum (cerebral cortex)", "Pons"], ans: 2, exp: "Cerebrum is the largest part (2/3 of brain mass). Cerebral cortex processes sensory info, controls voluntary movement, language, memory, thinking, and personality." },
    { q: "The spinal cord is protected by:", opts: ["Skull", "Vertebral column (spine)", "Ribcage", "Pelvis"], ans: 1, exp: "The vertebral column (backbone/spine) protects the spinal cord. The brain is protected by the skull (cranium). Both are also protected by meninges and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)." },
    { q: "Which neurotransmitter is released at most excitatory synapses in the brain?", opts: ["GABA", "Acetylcholine", "Glutamate", "Dopamine"], ans: 2, exp: "Glutamate is the primary excitatory neurotransmitter in the CNS. GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter. Acetylcholine is used at neuromuscular junctions and in the PNS." },
    { q: "Which gland is called the \"master gland\" of the endocrine system?", opts: ["Thyroid", "Adrenal", "Pituitary (hypophysis)", "Pineal"], ans: 2, exp: "Pituitary gland (attached to hypothalamus) controls other endocrine glands by releasing tropic hormones (TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, GH). Hence called the master gland." },
    { q: "The hormone insulin is produced by which cells in the pancreas?", opts: ["Alpha cells (produce glucagon)", "Beta cells (produce insulin)", "Delta cells", "Acinar cells"], ans: 1, exp: "Beta cells of islets of Langerhans in the pancreas produce insulin. Insulin lowers blood glucose by promoting glucose uptake and glycogen synthesis. Alpha cells produce glucagon (raises glucose)." },
    { q: "Adrenaline (epinephrine) is secreted by the:", opts: ["Thyroid gland", "Pituitary gland", "Adrenal medulla (inner part of adrenal gland)", "Pancreas"], ans: 2, exp: "Adrenal medulla (inner part of adrenal gland above kidneys) secretes adrenaline. \"Fight or flight\" hormone: increases heart rate, blood pressure, blood glucose, dilates pupils." },
    { q: "Thyroxine (thyroid hormone) requires which element for its synthesis?", opts: ["Iron", "Calcium", "Iodine", "Phosphorus"], ans: 2, exp: "Thyroxine (T₄) contains 4 iodine atoms; T₃ contains 3. Iodine deficiency → goitre (enlarged thyroid). Thyroxine regulates metabolic rate, growth, and development." },
    { q: "Deficiency of growth hormone (GH) in children causes:", opts: ["Gigantism", "Dwarfism (pituitary dwarfism)", "Diabetes insipidus", "Cretinism"], ans: 1, exp: "GH deficiency in children → pituitary dwarfism (short stature with normal proportions). Excess GH in children → gigantism. Excess GH in adults → acromegaly." },
    { q: "Which hormone is responsible for secondary sexual characters in males (voice deepening, beard growth)?", opts: ["Progesterone", "Estrogen", "Testosterone (androgen)", "Oxytocin"], ans: 2, exp: "Testosterone (from testes) causes male secondary sexual characteristics: voice deepening, facial hair, muscle development, sperm production, increased height at puberty." },
    { q: "The \"fight or flight\" response is coordinated by which part of the ANS?", opts: ["Parasympathetic nervous system", "Sympathetic nervous system", "Central nervous system", "Somatic nervous system"], ans: 1, exp: "Sympathetic NS: \"fight or flight\" — increases heart rate, BP, glucose, dilates bronchioles, directs blood to muscles. Parasympathetic: \"rest and digest\" — opposite effects." },
    { q: "Hyposecretion of insulin causes:", opts: ["Hypoglycaemia", "Diabetes mellitus (hyperglycaemia)", "Diabetes insipidus", "Hypothyroidism"], ans: 1, exp: "Insulin deficiency → blood glucose stays high (hyperglycaemia) → diabetes mellitus. Symptoms: polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia, glycosuria. Managed by insulin injections or tablets." },
    { q: "Which plant movement is NOT a growth movement but a turgor movement?", opts: ["Phototropism", "Geotropism", "Thigmotropism in tendrils", "Nastic movement of Mimosa pudica"], ans: 3, exp: "Mimosa pudica's folding is a turgor movement (seismonasty) — reversible, due to water leaving motor cells. Tropisms (photo, geo, thigmo) involve irreversible growth toward/away from stimulus." },
    { q: "Hydrotropism is growth of roots in response to:", opts: ["Light", "Gravity", "Water (roots grow toward water)", "Touch"], ans: 2, exp: "Hydrotropism: roots grow toward water. Positive hydrotropism. This is why plant roots find water sources underground even from a distance." },
    { q: "The hormone oxytocin is responsible for:", opts: ["Milk production", "Uterine contraction during childbirth and milk ejection (let-down reflex)", "Ovulation", "Menstruation"], ans: 1, exp: "Oxytocin (posterior pituitary): stimulates uterine contractions during labour and milk ejection during breastfeeding. Also called the \"bonding hormone\" or \"love hormone.\"" },
    { q: "Which of the following is a chemical messenger that travels in blood to target organs?", opts: ["Nerve impulse", "Hormone", "Neurotransmitter", "Action potential"], ans: 1, exp: "Hormones are chemical messengers secreted by endocrine glands into the bloodstream and carried to distant target organs. They act slowly but for longer durations than nerve impulses." },
    { q: "The hypothalamus is connected to and controls the:", opts: ["Thyroid gland", "Pituitary gland (it is the link between nervous and endocrine systems)", "Adrenal gland directly", "Pancreas"], ans: 1, exp: "Hypothalamus produces releasing/inhibiting hormones that regulate pituitary secretions. It is the link between the nervous system (CNS) and the endocrine system." },
    { q: "Chemotropism in pollen tube growth is guided by:", opts: ["Light", "Gravity", "Chemical substances from the ovule", "Temperature"], ans: 2, exp: "Chemotropism: pollen tube grows toward the ovule guided by chemical substances (sugars, calcium gradients) secreted by the style and ovule. Positive chemotropism." },
    { q: "The brain and spinal cord are surrounded by protective membranes called:", opts: ["Periosteum", "Meninges (dura mater, arachnoid, pia mater)", "Endosteum", "Pericardium"], ans: 1, exp: "Meninges: 3 layers — dura mater (tough, outermost), arachnoid (middle), pia mater (thin, innermost). Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) fills the subarachnoid space — cushions the brain." },
    { q: "Parkinson's disease is caused by deficiency of which neurotransmitter?", opts: ["Acetylcholine", "Serotonin", "Dopamine (in substantia nigra)", "GABA"], ans: 2, exp: "Parkinson's: degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra → insufficient dopamine → tremors, rigidity, bradykinesia. Treated with L-DOPA (dopamine precursor)." },
    { q: "A tropism is defined as a growth movement that is:", opts: ["Random", "Directional, in response to an environmental stimulus", "Always toward light", "A non-growth movement"], ans: 1, exp: "Tropism: directional growth movement in response to a directional stimulus (light, gravity, water, chemicals). Can be positive (toward) or negative (away from) the stimulus." },
    { q: "Gibberellin was first isolated from which organism?", opts: ["Bacteria", "A fungus (Gibberella fujikuroi) infecting rice", "A virus", "Green algae"], ans: 1, exp: "Gibberellin was discovered in Japan from Gibberella fujikuroi (a fungus) that caused \"bakanae\" (foolish seedling) disease in rice — plants grew abnormally tall and died." },
    { q: "Which hormone causes the \"ripening\" of fruit and is commercially used to ripen bananas?", opts: ["Auxin", "Cytokinin", "Ethylene", "Gibberellin"], ans: 2, exp: "Ethylene (ethene) is a gaseous hormone promoting fruit ripening. Commercially, calcium carbide (which releases ethylene) or ethylene gas is used to ripen bananas and mangoes." },
    { q: "The knee-jerk reflex is an example of:", opts: ["Conditioned reflex", "Unconditioned (innate) spinal reflex", "Cranial reflex", "Learned reflex"], ans: 1, exp: "Knee-jerk (patellar reflex): tapping patellar tendon → muscle spindle stimulated → sensory neuron → spinal cord → motor neuron → quadriceps contracts. Unconditioned spinal reflex." },
    { q: "The forebrain is divided into which parts?", opts: ["Cerebrum, thalamus, and hypothalamus", "Cerebellum and medulla", "Pons and cerebellum", "Only cerebrum"], ans: 0, exp: "Forebrain: cerebrum (largest, conscious thought), thalamus (sensory relay), hypothalamus (homeostasis, hormone control). Midbrain + hindbrain (pons, cerebellum, medulla) form the rest." },
    { q: "Goitre is caused by:", opts: ["Excess iodine", "Iodine deficiency → thyroid enlarges trying to produce thyroxine", "Calcium deficiency", "Vitamin D deficiency"], ans: 1, exp: "Iodine deficiency → cannot synthesise thyroxine → pituitary releases more TSH → thyroid gland enlarges (goitre). Prevented by iodised salt." },
    { q: "Diabetes insipidus (not mellitus) is caused by deficiency of:", opts: ["Insulin", "ADH (antidiuretic hormone/vasopressin)", "Glucagon", "Cortisol"], ans: 1, exp: "ADH deficiency → kidneys cannot concentrate urine → polyuria (very large amounts of dilute urine) + polydipsia. Unrelated to insulin. ADH is secreted by posterior pituitary." },
    { q: "Which hormone prepares the uterus for implantation and maintains pregnancy?", opts: ["Estrogen", "Testosterone", "Progesterone", "FSH"], ans: 2, exp: "Progesterone (from corpus luteum, then placenta) maintains uterine lining, prevents menstruation during pregnancy, inhibits uterine contractions. \"Pro-gestation\" hormone." },
    { q: "The autonomic nervous system controls:", opts: ["Voluntary muscle movement", "Involuntary organs: heart, smooth muscle, glands", "Conscious thought", "Balance and coordination"], ans: 1, exp: "ANS controls involuntary functions: heart rate, digestive movements, glandular secretions, pupil size. Two divisions: sympathetic (arousal) and parasympathetic (rest/digest)." },
    { q: "Sensory neurons carry impulses:", opts: ["From CNS to effectors (muscles/glands)", "From receptors to CNS", "Within the brain only", "Bidirectionally"], ans: 1, exp: "Sensory (afferent) neurons: receptor → CNS. Motor (efferent) neurons: CNS → effector. Relay (interneurons): within CNS, connecting sensory and motor neurons." },
    { q: "The hormone melatonin (from pineal gland) regulates:", opts: ["Growth", "Blood glucose", "Sleep-wake cycle (circadian rhythm)", "Metabolism"], ans: 2, exp: "Melatonin is secreted by the pineal gland in response to darkness. It regulates the sleep-wake cycle (circadian rhythm). Light suppresses melatonin; darkness stimulates it." },
    { q: "What is the role of the thalamus in the brain?", opts: ["Controls breathing", "Sensory relay station (routes sensory signals to appropriate cortex areas)", "Controls hunger", "Coordinates movement"], ans: 1, exp: "Thalamus: the brain's \"relay station\" — receives all sensory signals (except smell) and routes them to appropriate areas of the cerebral cortex for processing." },
    { q: "Which cranial nerve controls the pupil reflex (pupil constriction in bright light)?", opts: ["Optic nerve (II)", "Oculomotor nerve (III)", "Trochlear nerve (IV)", "Trigeminal nerve (V)"], ans: 1, exp: "Pupil constriction: oculomotor nerve (III) carries parasympathetic fibres → constricts pupil (miosis) in bright light. Optic nerve (II) carries visual signals to brain." },
    { q: "Which of the following is a characteristic of hormonal coordination compared to nervous coordination?", opts: ["Faster", "Short duration effect", "Slow, long-lasting, wide-ranging effects through blood", "Uses electrical signals"], ans: 2, exp: "Hormonal: slow (seconds to days via blood), long-lasting, acts on distant target organs, general/widespread effects. Nervous: fast (milliseconds via nerves), short-duration, localised." },
    { q: "In a myelinated neuron, action potentials jump between nodes of Ranvier. This mode of conduction is called:", opts: ["Continuous conduction", "Saltatory conduction", "Decremental conduction", "Retrograde conduction"], ans: 1, exp: "Saltatory conduction (from Latin saltare = to jump): in myelinated neurons, the myelin sheath insulates the axon, preventing ion flow except at the unmyelinated nodes of Ranvier. The action potential jumps from node to node, greatly increasing conduction speed (up to 120 m/s in myelinated vs ~0.5 m/s in unmyelinated fibres)." },
    { q: "The resting membrane potential of a typical neuron is approximately −70 mV because:", opts: ["Na⁺ ions are more concentrated inside the cell", "K⁺ leaks out of the cell down its concentration gradient, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pumps 3 Na⁺ out for every 2 K⁺ in, creating a net negative interior", "Cl⁻ ions are actively pumped into the cell", "The membrane is impermeable to all ions at rest"], ans: 1, exp: "Resting potential: high intracellular K⁺ leaks out through open K⁺ channels; large intracellular proteins bear negative charges. The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase (electrogenic: 3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in) maintains the gradients and contributes −4 mV directly. Result: inside is ~−70 mV relative to outside." },
    { q: "During an action potential, the rapid depolarisation phase is caused by:", opts: ["K⁺ rushing into the cell", "Na⁺ rapidly entering through voltage-gated Na⁺ channels (activation gates open)", "Cl⁻ leaving the cell", "Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase activity"], ans: 1, exp: "Depolarisation: when the membrane potential reaches threshold (~−55 mV), voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open rapidly → Na⁺ floods in (down its electrochemical gradient) → membrane potential rises sharply from −70 mV to +30 mV. Repolarisation follows when K⁺ channels open and K⁺ rushes out." },
    { q: "At a chemical synapse, the synaptic cleft is approximately:", opts: ["1 mm wide", "1 μm wide", "10 cm wide", "20-40 nm wide (nanometres)"], ans: 3, exp: "The synaptic cleft is a narrow gap of ~20-40 nm (nanometres) between the presynaptic terminal and the postsynaptic membrane. Neurotransmitter molecules diffuse across this tiny gap in ~0.5 ms. The narrow width ensures fast, precise signalling." },
    { q: "Acetylcholine released at the neuromuscular junction (NMJ) binds to which type of receptor on the muscle cell?", opts: ["Dopamine D2 receptors", "Muscarinic receptors (G-protein coupled)", "Nicotinic receptors (ionotropic — ligand-gated ion channels)", "GABA-A receptors"], ans: 2, exp: "At the NMJ, ACh binds to nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (nAChR), which are ligand-gated Na⁺/K⁺ channels. Binding opens the channel → Na⁺ influx → end-plate potential → muscle action potential → contraction. In the heart and smooth muscle, ACh binds muscarinic receptors (G-protein coupled, slower)." },
    { q: "After a nerve impulse, acetylcholine is rapidly removed from the synaptic cleft by:", opts: ["Reuptake into the presynaptic neuron", "Degradation by acetylcholinesterase (AChE) into choline + acetate", "Binding to postsynaptic receptors permanently", "Diffusion out of the synapse only"], ans: 1, exp: "Acetylcholinesterase (AChE) on the postsynaptic membrane rapidly hydrolyses ACh → choline + acetic acid (within milliseconds). Choline is taken back up into the presynaptic terminal for ACh resynthesis. This rapid inactivation allows precise, high-frequency signalling. Organophosphate nerve agents inhibit AChE → continuous muscle stimulation → death." },
    { q: "The function of the hypothalamus as a link between the nervous and endocrine systems is best illustrated by:", opts: ["The hypothalamus producing insulin in response to glucose", "The hypothalamus secreting releasing/inhibiting hormones that regulate anterior pituitary secretions, and producing ADH/oxytocin stored in the posterior pituitary", "The hypothalamus directly innervating all endocrine glands", "The hypothalamus producing thyroxine in stress"], ans: 1, exp: "The hypothalamus receives neural inputs and responds by secreting hypophysiotropic hormones (CRH, TRH, GnRH, GHRH, etc.) that regulate anterior pituitary hormone release. The posterior pituitary stores and releases ADH and oxytocin, which are produced in hypothalamic nuclei. This neuroendocrine integration is the cornerstone of homeostatic control." },
    { q: "Negative feedback regulation of thyroxine (T₃/T₄) secretion works as follows:", opts: ["Low T₄ inhibits TSH from pituitary", "High T₄ stimulates more TRH from hypothalamus", "T₄ has no feedback effect on TSH", "High T₄ inhibits TRH from hypothalamus and TSH from pituitary → reduced T₄ production by thyroid"], ans: 3, exp: "Classic negative feedback: TRH (hypothalamus) → TSH (anterior pituitary) → T₃/T₄ (thyroid). When T₄ is high, it feeds back to inhibit both TRH and TSH secretion → thyroid reduces T₄ output → T₄ falls → inhibition lifted → cycle restores homeostasis. In hypothyroidism, low T₄ → high TSH (used diagnostically)." },
    { q: "In Parkinson's disease, the loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra leads to:", opts: ["Hyperactive cerebellum causing ataxia", "Excess acetylcholine only, without dopamine change", "Reduced dopamine in the striatum → loss of smooth movement initiation (basal ganglia circuit imbalanced) → tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia", "Increased dopamine in the striatum → excessive movement"], ans: 2, exp: "Substantia nigra dopaminergic neurons project to the striatum (nigrostriatal pathway), modulating the basal ganglia movement circuit. Loss of dopamine → imbalance between direct (Go) and indirect (NoGo) pathways → reduced thalamo-cortical drive to motor cortex → bradykinesia, tremor, rigidity. L-DOPA (dopamine precursor that crosses BBB) is the mainstay of treatment." },
    { q: "A patient's pupil does NOT constrict when a bright light is shone in it (afferent limb is intact). The defect is most likely in:", opts: ["Optic nerve (cranial nerve II)", "Oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve III) — carries parasympathetic fibres to the sphincter pupillae muscle", "Trochlear nerve (cranial nerve IV)", "Abducens nerve (cranial nerve VI)"], ans: 1, exp: "Pupillary light reflex arc: retina → optic nerve (CN II, afferent) → pretectal nucleus → Edinger-Westphal nucleus → oculomotor nerve (CN III, efferent, parasympathetic) → ciliary ganglion → short ciliary nerves → sphincter pupillae → pupil constricts. If CN III is damaged, efferent limb fails → pupil cannot constrict despite normal CN II." },
    { q: "The difference between cranial and spinal reflexes is:", opts: ["Cranial reflexes have their centre in the brain (brainstem); spinal reflexes have their integration centre in the spinal cord", "Cranial reflexes involve the spinal cord; spinal reflexes involve the brain", "Spinal reflexes are voluntary; cranial reflexes are involuntary", "There is no fundamental difference"], ans: 0, exp: "Cranial reflexes: reflex centre is in the brainstem (e.g., pupillary light reflex, gag reflex, corneal reflex — CN II-XII involved). Spinal reflexes: integration occurs in the spinal cord (e.g., knee-jerk/patellar reflex, withdrawal reflex). Both bypass conscious decision-making." },
    { q: "Synaptic plasticity (long-term potentiation, LTP) is most closely associated with:", opts: ["The neuromuscular junction", "Learning and memory — repeated stimulation strengthens synaptic connections (NMDA receptor-dependent mechanism)", "Reflex arc responses", "Autonomic nerve activity"], ans: 1, exp: "LTP: repeated high-frequency stimulation → NMDA receptors open (require both glutamate binding and membrane depolarisation) → Ca²⁺ entry → AMPA receptor insertion → strengthened synapse → lasting increase in synaptic strength. Hebb's rule: \"Neurons that fire together, wire together.\" Basis of learning and memory." },
    { q: "Which part of the autonomic nervous system would be active during the digestion of a meal (rest-and-digest state)?", opts: ["Sympathetic nervous system (increases heart rate, diverts blood from gut)", "Parasympathetic nervous system (increases gut motility, digestive secretions, relaxes sphincters)", "Somatic nervous system (controls skeletal muscles)", "Both sympathetic and parasympathetic equally"], ans: 1, exp: "Parasympathetic (rest and digest, craniosacral): via vagus nerve (CN X) → increases salivary and gastric secretions, gut motility and peristalsis, bile release, relaxes pyloric sphincter → promotes digestion and absorption. Sympathetic (fight or flight) does the opposite: reduces gut activity, diverts blood to muscles." },
    { q: "Auxin (IAA) at high concentrations inhibits root growth but promotes shoot growth. This is because:", opts: ["High auxin promotes cell division only in shoots", "Auxin is destroyed in root tissue by a specific enzyme", "Roots are insensitive to auxin at all concentrations", "Roots are far more sensitive to auxin than shoots; concentrations optimal for shoot elongation are supraoptimal/inhibitory for roots"], ans: 3, exp: "The dose-response curve for auxin differs by organ: roots respond to very low concentrations (optimal ~10⁻¹⁰ M), shoots to higher concentrations (~10⁻⁶ M). Auxin concentration in the shaded side of a phototropic shoot (~10⁻⁶ M) promotes shoot elongation but simultaneously inhibits root growth on the same side (concentration exceeds root optimum)." },
    { q: "Ethylene (a plant hormone) promotes fruit ripening. This occurs via:", opts: ["Inhibiting cell wall breakdown", "Activating cell wall-degrading enzymes (cellulase, pectinase), promoting starch → sugar conversion, chlorophyll degradation, and carotenoid/anthocyanin synthesis", "Blocking chlorophyll synthesis", "Increasing ABA levels which directly convert starch to sugar"], ans: 1, exp: "Ethylene activates a transcriptional cascade promoting: (a) cell wall-softening enzymes (pectinase, cellulase) → fruit softens; (b) amylase breaks down starch → sweetness; (c) chlorophyll degradation + carotenoid/anthocyanin synthesis → colour change. Ethylene also promotes its own synthesis (autocatalytic) — explains why one ripe fruit ripens others nearby." },
    { q: "Abscisic acid (ABA) causes stomatal closure during drought by which mechanism?", opts: ["ABA directly blocks K⁺ entry into guard cells via steric hindrance", "ABA binds PYR/RCAR receptors → inhibits PP2C phosphatases → SnRK2 kinases active → phosphorylate SLAC1 (anion channel) → Cl⁻/malate exits → K⁺ follows → guard cells lose turgor → stomata close", "ABA breaks down chlorophyll in guard cells", "ABA increases CO₂ fixation in guard cells"], ans: 1, exp: "ABA signalling in guard cells (detailed pathway): ABA → PYR/RCAR receptor → inhibit ABI1/ABI2 (PP2C phosphatases) → OST1/SnRK2 kinase activated → phosphorylates SLAC1 anion channel (opens) and RBOH (produces ROS) → anion efflux (Cl⁻, malate²⁻) → membrane depolarises → K⁺ efflux channels open → water exits by osmosis → guard cells flaccid → stomata close." },
    { q: "Cytokinin levels are highest in which part of a plant, and what is their main site of synthesis?", opts: ["Seeds only after germination — synthesised by the embryo", "Heartwood — synthesised in dead xylem cells", "Actively dividing regions (root tips, shoot apical meristems, developing fruits) — primarily synthesised in root tips and transported upward", "Mature leaves — synthesised by chloroplasts only"], ans: 2, exp: "Cytokinins are adenine derivatives (e.g., zeatin) synthesised mainly in root tips (and also in developing seeds and young leaves). They are transported upward in xylem to shoot meristems and leaves. They promote cell division, delay leaf senescence, and antagonise auxin in apical dominance." },
    { q: "Gibberellin treatment of dwarf (rosette) plants causes them to bolt (elongate rapidly). The molecular reason is:", opts: ["Gibberellin activates chloroplast division → more photosynthesis → more energy for growth", "Gibberellin directly binds DNA and activates growth genes without a receptor", "Gibberellin mimics the action of ABA in the stem at internode cells", "Gibberellin promotes the destruction of DELLA repressor proteins → transcription factors for cell elongation genes become active → internode elongation"], ans: 3, exp: "DELLA proteins are transcriptional repressors of growth. In the absence of gibberellin, DELLAs suppress growth gene transcription. Gibberellin binds GID1 receptor → GID1-GA-DELLA complex targeted for proteasomal degradation → DELLAs removed → growth genes expressed → rapid internode elongation. Dwarf plants often have mutations in DELLA degradation." },
    { q: "The hormone responsible for \"apical dominance\" (suppression of lateral bud growth by the apical bud) is primarily:", opts: ["Gibberellin", "Cytokinin", "Auxin (produced by the apical bud and transported downward, inhibiting lateral buds)", "Ethylene"], ans: 2, exp: "Apical dominance: the apical bud produces high concentrations of auxin which is transported downward (polar auxin transport). High auxin in lateral buds suppresses their outgrowth. Removing the apical bud removes the auxin source → lateral buds grow. Cytokinin (from roots) promotes lateral bud growth and antagonises auxin — the ratio of auxin to cytokinin determines lateral branching." },
    { q: "Which of the following best explains why the knee-jerk reflex occurs even in an unconscious patient?", opts: ["The brain must be active for reflexes to occur", "Spinal reflexes only require an intact spinal cord segment (L3-L4) and do not need the brain — integration occurs within the spinal cord", "The cerebellum controls the knee-jerk", "The pituitary gland initiates reflexes during unconsciousness"], ans: 1, exp: "The patellar (knee-jerk) reflex is a monosynaptic spinal reflex: muscle spindle → Ia afferent → spinal cord L3-L4 → alpha motor neuron → quadriceps contracts. The pathway does not pass through the brain. Clinically, the reflex is used to assess spinal cord integrity, not brain function. Brain damage does not eliminate spinal reflexes." },
    { q: "GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is the principal inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS. It acts by:", opts: ["Opening Cl⁻ channels (GABA-A receptor) → hyperpolarisation of the postsynaptic membrane → reduced likelihood of action potential", "Stimulating adenylyl cyclase to produce cAMP → protein kinase A activation", "Blocking dopamine receptors competitively at the synapse", "Increasing intracellular Ca²⁺ in the postsynaptic neuron → activation"], ans: 0, exp: "GABA-A receptor: ligand-gated Cl⁻ channel. GABA binding → Cl⁻ influx → membrane hyperpolarises (becomes more negative than resting potential) → inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) → harder to generate an action potential. Benzodiazepines (e.g., diazepam) and barbiturates enhance GABA-A activity → sedation, anxiolysis." },
    { q: "Serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine) is synthesised from which amino acid?", opts: ["Phenylalanine", "Tyrosine (same as dopamine/adrenaline pathway)", "Tryptophan (via 5-hydroxytryptophan by tryptophan hydroxylase)", "Glutamate"], ans: 2, exp: "Serotonin synthesis: tryptophan → 5-hydroxytryptophan (tryptophan hydroxylase, rate-limiting) → serotonin (5-HTP decarboxylase). Serotonin regulates mood, sleep, appetite, and gut motility. SSRIs (selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors like fluoxetine) block serotonin reuptake → more serotonin in synapse → antidepressant effect." },
    { q: "In the endocrine system, the lag time between stimulus and response is much longer than in neural signalling. Which statement best explains this?", opts: ["Hormones travel faster than nerve impulses", "Hormones are transported by blood (slower, ~minutes to hours), act via second messenger cascades requiring gene transcription, compared to direct neural electrical signalling (milliseconds)", "Endocrine glands are far from target organs", "Hormones have stronger effects so less precision is needed"], ans: 1, exp: "Neural response: electrical impulse at ~120 m/s → response in milliseconds; localised. Hormonal response: hormone secreted into blood → transported (~minutes), binds receptor → intracellular signalling (protein kinases, gene expression changes) → response in minutes to hours; widespread, long-lasting. The trade-off: hormones are slower but create prolonged, whole-body effects." },
    { q: "The pituitary gland (hypophysis) is anatomically divided into two distinct parts. The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) differs from the anterior pituitary because:", opts: ["Posterior pituitary synthesises its own hormones (insulin and glucagon)", "Posterior pituitary stores and releases hormones (ADH and oxytocin) synthesised in the hypothalamus; anterior pituitary synthesises its own hormones under hypothalamic regulation", "Anterior pituitary receives direct neural inputs from the brain", "Both parts are functionally identical"], ans: 1, exp: "Posterior pituitary: a downgrowth of neural tissue from the hypothalamus. It stores and releases ADH (vasopressin) and oxytocin, which are actually produced in hypothalamic nuclei (supraoptic and paraventricular) and transported down axons. Anterior pituitary: a true glandular tissue (adenohypophysis) that synthesises GH, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, and prolactin in response to hypothalamic releasing/inhibiting hormones." },
    { q: "Phototropism in plants: when a unilateral light stimulus is applied to a shoot tip, auxin moves to the shaded side. The mechanism of this lateral transport is thought to involve:", opts: ["Photodegradation of auxin on the lit side", "Differential expression of PIN auxin efflux carriers — light causes redistribution of PIN proteins, pumping auxin toward the shaded side", "Auxin synthesis increases specifically on the shaded side", "Cytokinin is the actual mediator in phototropism"], ans: 1, exp: "Phototropism mechanism: blue light (440-480 nm) activates phototropin receptors (PHOT1/PHOT2) at the shoot tip → signalling cascade → PIN auxin efflux carriers relocate to the lateral membrane on the shaded side → auxin is preferentially pumped toward the shaded side → higher auxin concentration → greater cell elongation → bending toward light." },
    { q: "In geotropism, roots grow downward (positive) despite having the same auxin distribution as shoots. The reason is:", opts: ["Roots produce more auxin than shoots", "Roots are hypersensitive to auxin — the concentration that promotes shoot elongation is supraoptimal and inhibitory for roots", "Gravity directly stimulates root cell division", "Roots respond to gravity by producing ethylene that promotes downward growth"], ans: 1, exp: "In a horizontal root, auxin accumulates on the lower side (gravity-induced, via amyloplast sedimentation detected by columella cells in root cap → PIN redistribution → auxin moves to lower side). The concentration on the lower side inhibits lower-root elongation; upper side grows more → root bends downward. Same auxin redistribution as shoots, but roots respond inhibitorily because of hypersensitivity." },
    { q: "The \"master clock\" controlling circadian rhythms in mammals is located in:", opts: ["Pineal gland", "Cerebellum", "Suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus", "Hippocampus"], ans: 2, exp: "The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) in the hypothalamus receives direct photic input from the retina (via retinohypothalamic tract) and entrains the body's ~24-hour circadian clock. The SCN regulates melatonin secretion from the pineal gland. Lesioning the SCN abolishes circadian rhythms in animals." },
    { q: "Diabetes mellitus type 1 and type 2 differ primarily in:", opts: ["Type 1: insulin resistance; Type 2: no insulin secretion", "Type 1: autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells → no insulin; Type 2: insulin resistance in target cells (initially normal/high insulin secretion)", "Type 1 only affects adults; Type 2 only affects children", "Both result in identical blood glucose regulation failures"], ans: 1, exp: "Type 1 DM: autoimmune destruction of islet beta cells → absolute insulin deficiency → requires exogenous insulin. Type 2 DM: peripheral insulin resistance (muscle, liver, fat cells fail to respond adequately to insulin) + progressive beta cell dysfunction; initially hyperinsulinaemia, later insufficient insulin. Managed by lifestyle, metformin, and eventually insulin." },
    { q: "Cortisol (glucocorticoid from adrenal cortex) has which of the following metabolic effects?", opts: ["Promotes fat storage and decreases protein catabolism", "Has no metabolic effects — only immune suppression", "Decreases blood glucose by promoting glycogen synthesis (insulin-like effect)", "Increases blood glucose (promotes gluconeogenesis from protein breakdown, inhibits peripheral glucose uptake) — acts as a stress hyperglycaemic hormone"], ans: 3, exp: "Cortisol (released in stress, regulated by CRH-ACTH axis): (1) stimulates gluconeogenesis in liver; (2) promotes proteolysis of muscle → amino acids for gluconeogenesis; (3) promotes lipolysis; (4) inhibits glucose uptake in peripheral tissues (anti-insulin effect) → hyperglycaemia. Also: powerful anti-inflammatory/immunosuppressive. Cushing's syndrome: excess cortisol → hyperglycaemia, muscle wasting, central obesity." },
    { q: "The hormone glucagon (from alpha cells of pancreas) counters insulin by:", opts: ["Promoting glycogen synthesis from glucose", "Stimulating glycogenolysis (glycogen → glucose) and gluconeogenesis in the liver → raising blood glucose", "Inhibiting gluconeogenesis", "Promoting glucose uptake in muscle cells"], ans: 1, exp: "Glucagon (secreted when blood glucose falls): binds liver glucagon receptor → cAMP-PKA cascade → activates glycogen phosphorylase (glycogenolysis) → inhibits glycogen synthase → activates gluconeogenesis pathway. Net: liver releases glucose into blood. The insulin/glucagon ratio is the key determinant of hepatic glucose output." },
    { q: "Oxytocin acts as a positive feedback hormone during childbirth. This means:", opts: ["Oxytocin is a negative feedback hormone during labour — it reduces contractions once the foetus is delivered", "Oxytocin production stops once uterine contractions begin", "Uterine contractions stimulate more oxytocin release → stronger contractions → more oxytocin → culminating in delivery (Ferguson's reflex)", "Oxytocin inhibits uterine contractions to protect the foetus from hypoxia"], ans: 2, exp: "Ferguson's reflex: foetal head presses on cervix → stretch receptors → neural signals to hypothalamus → more oxytocin released from posterior pituitary → stronger uterine contractions → more pressure on cervix → more oxytocin → escalating cycle until delivery. This is a classic physiological positive feedback loop (self-amplifying)." },
    { q: "Which statement about the autonomic nervous system is CORRECT?", opts: ["The parasympathetic system is active only during emergencies", "The autonomic system requires voluntary control to function properly", "The sympathetic nervous system always inhibits organ function regardless of context", "Most organs receive dual innervation (sympathetic AND parasympathetic) with opposing effects; the balance determines organ activity"], ans: 3, exp: "Dual autonomic innervation: most visceral organs (heart, lungs, gut, bladder) receive both sympathetic and parasympathetic fibres with opposing actions. Heart: sympathetic increases rate; parasympathetic (vagus) decreases it. The ANS is tonically active, and the balance of sympathetic vs. parasympathetic tone determines baseline organ function. It is entirely involuntary." },
    { q: "Nociceptors (pain receptors) are classified as which type of receptor based on their stimulus?", opts: ["Thermoreceptors — respond to heat only", "Chemoreceptors — respond to blood chemistry", "Polymodal nociceptors — respond to tissue-damaging mechanical, thermal, and chemical stimuli", "Mechanoreceptors — respond only to pressure"], ans: 2, exp: "Nociceptors (free nerve endings, Aδ and C fibres) detect noxious stimuli that signal actual or potential tissue damage. Most are polymodal — responding to intense mechanical pressure, extreme temperatures, and inflammatory chemicals (bradykinin, prostaglandins, H⁺). Aδ fibres: fast, sharp pain. C fibres: slow, burning/aching pain." },
    { q: "In which lobe of the cerebrum is the primary somatosensory cortex located?", opts: ["Frontal lobe", "Temporal lobe", "Parietal lobe (postcentral gyrus)", "Occipital lobe"], ans: 2, exp: "Primary somatosensory cortex: postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe. Receives sensory information from the contralateral body (touch, pressure, temperature, pain, proprioception). Primary motor cortex: precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe. Broca's (speech production): inferior frontal. Wernicke's (speech comprehension): superior temporal." },
    { q: "The blood-brain barrier (BBB) is formed by which cells and what is its primary function?", opts: ["Tight junctions between cerebral capillary endothelial cells (supported by astrocyte foot processes) — creates a selective barrier protecting the brain from toxins, pathogens, and maintaining CNS homeostasis", "Neurons — to enhance neural communication speed", "Meninges — purely mechanical protection without selectivity", "Microglia — immune surveillance only, no barrier function"], ans: 0, exp: "BBB: tight junctions between brain capillary endothelial cells (no fenestrations) + astrocyte end-feet + pericytes. Highly selective: allows O₂, CO₂, glucose, lipid-soluble molecules to pass; blocks large proteins, most pathogens, many drugs. Clinically important: why most antibiotics/anti-cancer drugs cannot easily treat brain infections/tumours. L-DOPA (not dopamine) crosses because it is lipophilic." },
    { q: "Thigmotropism in climbing plants (tendrils) involves auxin redistribution in response to contact. The side that touches the support:", opts: ["Grows faster due to more auxin", "Grows slower (less auxin on contact side → less elongation) while the non-contact side elongates more → tendril coils around the support", "Produces ethylene that causes curling", "Is unaffected; the entire tendril coils randomly"], ans: 1, exp: "In thigmotropism: contact mechanically stimulates one side of the tendril → auxin redistributes to the non-contact side → greater elongation on the non-contact side → tendril curves toward and coils around the support. This rapid response (minutes) also involves action potential-like electrical signals in some plants." },
    { q: "A child is given a drug that blocks all dopamine receptors. Which of the following effects would NOT be expected?", opts: ["Movement disorders (Parkinson-like symptoms)", "Emotional blunting (dopamine is involved in reward/motivation pathways)", "Increased heart rate via sympathetic stimulation", "Disruption of prolactin regulation (dopamine normally inhibits prolactin secretion)"], ans: 2, exp: "Blocking dopamine receptors would impair: (a) basal ganglia motor control → drug-induced Parkinsonism; (b) mesolimbic reward pathway → reduced motivation/pleasure; (c) hypothalamo-pituitary axis → increased prolactin (galactorrhoea). Dopamine does NOT directly control heart rate via the sympathetic system (that is mainly adrenaline/noradrenaline). Option C would not be a direct effect of dopamine blockade." },
    { q: "In plants, chemotropism of the pollen tube toward the ovule is guided by a gradient of:", opts: ["Light intensity", "Gravity", "γ-aminobutyric acid (GABA) and other chemical signals (Ca²⁺ gradients, LURE peptides) from the style and ovule", "Temperature gradient"], ans: 2, exp: "Pollen tube growth is directed by chemotropic signals including: GABA gradient (synthesised by the style, acts as directional cue), Ca²⁺ gradient at the pollen tube tip, and LURE peptides secreted by synergid cells of the ovule. This chemical guidance ensures the pollen tube reaches the egg cell for fertilisation." },
    { q: "Compared to steroid hormones, peptide hormones differ in that they:", opts: ["Are lipid-soluble and cross the cell membrane freely", "Cannot cross the cell membrane → bind surface receptors → activate intracellular second messengers (cAMP, IP₃, DAG, Ca²⁺)", "Act by directly binding DNA in the nucleus", "Have longer lasting effects and are stored in the nucleus"], ans: 1, exp: "Peptide hormones (insulin, glucagon, ADH, GH, PTH): water-soluble, cannot cross the lipid bilayer → bind membrane receptors → activate second messenger cascades (cAMP via Gs, IP₃/DAG via Gq, tyrosine kinase). Steroid hormones (cortisol, testosterone, oestrogen, aldosterone): lipid-soluble, cross membrane freely → bind intracellular/nuclear receptors → directly regulate gene transcription. Steroids act more slowly but with longer-lasting effects." },
    { q: "Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease characterised by:", opts: ["Deficiency of acetylcholinesterase → persistent muscle contraction", "Degeneration of motor neurons in the anterior horn of spinal cord", "Autoantibodies against nicotinic ACh receptors (nAChR) at the NMJ → reduced ACh receptor number → muscle weakness and fatiguability", "Autoantibodies against voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels at the presynaptic terminal (Lambert-Eaton)"], ans: 2, exp: "Myasthenia gravis: antibodies bind and destroy nAChRs at the NMJ → fewer functional receptors → less response to ACh → progressive muscle weakness worsened by activity (fatigability). Treated by: AChE inhibitors (neostigmine — increases ACh in cleft), immunosuppressants, and thymectomy. Distinguished from Lambert-Eaton syndrome (presynaptic Ca²⁺ channel antibodies)." },
    { q: "In the menstrual cycle, the LH surge that triggers ovulation is an example of:", opts: ["Negative feedback — high oestrogen inhibits LH", "Positive feedback — high oestrogen (from mature follicle) stimulates GnRH/LH surge → ovulation", "Absence of feedback — LH is constitutively secreted", "Negative feedback by progesterone triggering ovulation"], ans: 1, exp: "Unique positive feedback: rising oestrogen from the dominant follicle (>200 pg/mL for >48h) switches from negative to positive feedback at the hypothalamus and pituitary → massive GnRH/LH surge → ovulation (follicle ruptures on day 14). After ovulation, the corpus luteum secretes progesterone + oestrogen → negative feedback → suppresses LH/FSH → menstrual cycle phases." },
    { q: "Prolactin (from anterior pituitary) is unusual among pituitary hormones because its secretion is under primarily _____ control by the hypothalamus:", opts: ["No hypothalamic control — prolactin is self-regulated", "Stimulatory only via TRH and PRH (prolactin-releasing hormone)", "Stimulatory — via a dominant prolactin-releasing hormone (PRH) that outweighs inhibition", "Inhibitory — dopamine (from hypothalamus) tonically inhibits prolactin secretion; cutting the pituitary stalk raises prolactin"], ans: 3, exp: "Unlike most anterior pituitary hormones (where hypothalamic releasing hormones predominate), prolactin secretion is tonically inhibited by hypothalamic dopamine (via the tuberoinfundibular pathway). Destroying the hypothalamo-pituitary portal connection raises prolactin. Dopamine agonists (bromocriptine) lower prolactin; dopamine antagonists (antipsychotics) raise it (causing galactorrhoea). TRH also stimulates prolactin mildly." },
    { q: "The hormone calcitonin (from thyroid C cells) and parathyroid hormone (PTH, from parathyroid glands) have antagonistic effects on blood calcium. If blood Ca²⁺ rises, which hormone is released and what does it do?", opts: ["PTH is released → increases osteoclast activity → more Ca²⁺ released from bone", "Calcitonin is released → inhibits osteoclast activity (reduces bone resorption) → lowers blood Ca²⁺ back to normal", "Aldosterone is released → promotes Ca²⁺ excretion in kidney", "Insulin is released → promotes Ca²⁺ uptake by muscle cells"], ans: 1, exp: "When blood Ca²⁺ is high, calcitonin (C cells of thyroid, not thyroid follicular cells) is secreted → inhibits osteoclasts (reduces bone resorption) → promotes renal Ca²⁺ excretion → blood Ca²⁺ falls. When Ca²⁺ is low, PTH is secreted → activates osteoclasts, increases renal Ca²⁺ reabsorption, promotes vitamin D activation → blood Ca²⁺ rises. PTH is far more potent than calcitonin in humans." },
    { q: "In the spinal cord, the dorsal horn primarily contains neurons associated with:", opts: ["Sensory processing (receives sensory afferents from the body via dorsal root ganglia)", "Motor output to skeletal muscles (alpha motor neurons)", "Sympathetic ganglia neurons only", "Autonomic preganglionic neurons (which are only in the lateral horn)"], ans: 0, exp: "Spinal cord organisation: dorsal (posterior) horn — sensory neurons (receive and process afferent sensory signals from the periphery via dorsal root ganglia). Ventral (anterior) horn — motor neurons (alpha motor neurons that innervate skeletal muscles). Lateral horn (thoracic/lumbar) — sympathetic preganglionic neurons. The law of Bell and Magendie: dorsal roots are sensory, ventral roots are motor." },
    { q: "A patient with damage to the cerebellum would show which specific symptom?", opts: ["Loss of sensation on one side of the body", "Paralysis of voluntary muscles", "Ataxia — loss of coordination, intention tremor, dysmetria (overshooting movements)", "Loss of consciousness and memory"], ans: 2, exp: "Cerebellar damage: ataxia (unsteady gait), intention tremor (tremor when reaching for a target), dysmetria (misjudging distance), dysdiadochokinesia (inability to perform rapid alternating movements). The cerebellum compares intended movements (from cerebral cortex) with actual movements (proprioceptive feedback) and corrects errors in real time. Consciousness and sensation are not affected." },
    { q: "Which of the following plant hormones has been shown to delay leaf senescence (ageing) by promoting protein synthesis and retarding chlorophyll degradation?", opts: ["Abscisic acid (ABA)", "Ethylene", "Cytokinin (promotes cell division and protein synthesis, maintains chlorophyll)", "Auxin"], ans: 2, exp: "Cytokinin application to detached leaves delays yellowing (senescence): promotes protein synthesis, mobilises nutrients toward the treated area (Richmond-Lang effect), retards chlorophyll breakdown. ABA and ethylene promote senescence. Auxin has minimal direct anti-senescent effect on leaves. This is why cytokinins are commercially used to keep cut flowers fresh." },
    { q: "The concept of \"tropism vs. nastic movement\" in plants can be distinguished by the fact that:", opts: ["Tropisms are irreversible; nastic movements are always reversible AND direction of response is determined by direction of stimulus in tropisms, but by plant anatomy in nastic movements", "Both tropisms and nasties are growth-based and irreversible", "Nastic movements require auxin; tropisms do not", "Tropisms occur only in roots; nastic movements only in leaves"], ans: 0, exp: "Tropism: directional growth response to a directional stimulus (direction of bending determined by where the stimulus comes from — phototropism bends toward light). Nastic movement: non-directional response to a non-directional stimulus (e.g., thermonasty: flower opening with warmth regardless of direction). Nastic movements are often reversible (turgor changes); tropisms are irreversible growth changes. Some nastic movements (nyctinasty) also involve turgor." },
    { q: "In the context of plant hormones, the ratio of auxin to cytokinin determines cell fate in tissue culture. A HIGH auxin:cytokinin ratio promotes:", opts: ["Shoot (bud) formation", "Root formation (callus differentiates into roots)", "Fruit development", "Chlorophyll synthesis"], ans: 1, exp: "Skoog and Miller's experiment: high auxin:cytokinin → root formation; low auxin:cytokinin (high cytokinin) → shoot/bud formation. Equal ratio: undifferentiated callus growth. This principle underlies plant tissue culture and micropropagation techniques, allowing controlled regeneration of entire plants from single cells." },
    { q: "The hippocampus, a structure in the medial temporal lobe, is critically important for:", opts: ["Regulating heart rate and blood pressure", "Consolidation of new declarative memories (explicit memory formation) and spatial navigation", "Controlling voluntary movement of the hands", "Processing visual information from the retina"], ans: 1, exp: "The hippocampus (part of the limbic system) is essential for converting short-term memory into long-term declarative memory (episodic and semantic). Bilateral hippocampal damage (as in H.M. — famous patient with bilateral hippocampectomy) causes anterograde amnesia (inability to form new explicit memories) while procedural/implicit memories are preserved." }
  ]
,

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════
     CH 7 — HOW DO ORGANISMS REPRODUCE?
  ══════════════════════════════════════ */
  7: [
    { q: "Which type of reproduction does NOT involve gametes (sex cells)?", opts: ["Sexual reproduction", "Asexual reproduction", "Fusion reproduction", "Cross-fertilisation"], ans: 1, exp: "Asexual reproduction: a single parent produces offspring without formation or fusion of gametes. Offspring are genetically identical to parent (clones). Examples: budding, binary fission." },
    { q: "Binary fission in Amoeba occurs by:", opts: ["Budding", "Division of one cell into two equal daughter cells", "Spore formation", "Fragmentation"], ans: 1, exp: "Binary fission: nucleus divides by mitosis, then cytoplasm divides → 2 identical daughter cells. Amoeba, Paramecium, bacteria use binary fission." },
    { q: "Budding is a mode of asexual reproduction seen in:", opts: ["Amoeba", "Yeast and Hydra", "Fern", "Bryophyllum"], ans: 1, exp: "Budding: a small outgrowth (bud) develops from parent → detaches and grows into new organism. Yeast (unicellular fungus) and Hydra (animal) reproduce by budding." },
    { q: "Vegetative propagation in Bryophyllum occurs through:", opts: ["Seeds", "Pollen grains", "Leaf margins (adventitious buds on leaves)", "Roots only"], ans: 2, exp: "Bryophyllum (Kalanchoe) has adventitious buds on leaf margins. Each bud can grow into a new plant. This is an example of vegetative propagation by leaves." },
    { q: "Spore formation (sporulation) in Rhizopus (bread mould) involves:", opts: ["Sexual fusion of gametes", "Asexual reproduction by producing spores in sporangia", "Budding", "Fragmentation"], ans: 1, exp: "Rhizopus: sporangia (spore cases) form on hyphae. Mature sporangia burst → release thousands of lightweight spores that are dispersed by air → germinate when conditions are suitable." },
    { q: "Regeneration in Planaria (flatworm) means:", opts: ["Only tail regrows", "Any cut fragment can develop into a complete organism", "Only the head part survives", "Regeneration is impossible in Planaria"], ans: 1, exp: "Planaria: even a tiny cut fragment can regenerate into a complete organism (due to totipotent neoblast stem cells). A 1/279th piece can regrow — remarkable regenerative capacity." },
    { q: "The main advantage of sexual reproduction over asexual is:", opts: ["It is faster", "It is simpler", "It creates genetic variation (important for evolution and adaptation)", "No energy needed"], ans: 2, exp: "Sexual reproduction: mixing of genes from two parents creates genetic variation → different offspring → some may be better adapted to changing environments → natural selection can act." },
    { q: "Fragmentation as a mode of reproduction is seen in:", opts: ["Bacteria", "Spirogyra (filamentous alga) and some animals", "Yeast", "Amoeba"], ans: 1, exp: "Fragmentation: organism breaks into fragments, each growing into a new individual. Seen in Spirogyra (alga), Planaria, sea stars, and various simple organisms." },
    { q: "In plants, the male reproductive organ (producing pollen) is called the:", opts: ["Pistil", "Sepal", "Stamen (anther)", "Petal"], ans: 2, exp: "Stamen: male part = anther (pollen production) + filament (stalk). Pistil: female part = stigma (pollen lands) + style + ovary (contains ovules)." },
    { q: "Self-pollination means:", opts: ["Pollen from one flower to another flower on the same plant or to itself", "Only pollen from the same flower", "Pollen transferred to a different plant", "Wind pollination only"], ans: 0, exp: "Self-pollination: pollen from a flower reaches the stigma of the same flower or another flower on the same plant. Reduces genetic variation but ensures reproduction when pollinators are absent." },
    { q: "Cross-pollination is advantageous because it:", opts: ["Is simpler and uses less energy", "Produces more seeds always", "Introduces genetic variation (mixing genes from two plants)", "Is faster than self-pollination"], ans: 2, exp: "Cross-pollination: pollen from one plant → stigma of another plant of same species. Mixes genes → genetic variation → better adaptation. Facilitated by wind, insects, water, birds." },
    { q: "Double fertilisation in flowering plants involves:", opts: ["Two sperm fusing with two eggs", "One sperm fuses with egg (zygote) + one sperm fuses with polar nuclei (endosperm)", "Two eggs fusing together", "No fertilisation"], ans: 1, exp: "Double fertilisation (unique to angiosperms): 1st sperm + egg → zygote (embryo). 2nd sperm + 2 polar nuclei → triploid (3n) endosperm (food for seed). Both processes occur simultaneously." },
    { q: "After fertilisation, the ovule develops into a:", opts: ["Fruit", "Seed", "Flower", "Pollen grain"], ans: 1, exp: "Ovule → seed (after fertilisation). Ovary → fruit. Zygote → embryo (inside seed). The seed contains the embryo and food reserve (endosperm or cotyledons)." },
    { q: "In humans, which chromosome combination determines male sex?", opts: ["XX", "XY (X from mother, Y from father)", "XO", "YY"], ans: 1, exp: "Human sex: XX = female, XY = male. The Y chromosome determines maleness (SRY gene). Father determines sex of child — contributes either X or Y sperm; mother always contributes X." },
    { q: "The onset of menstrual cycle (menarche) in girls is a sign of:", opts: ["Ovulation starting", "Puberty and reproductive maturity", "Menopause", "Conception"], ans: 1, exp: "Menarche (first menstruation): occurs at puberty (~10-15 years), indicates the reproductive system is maturing and monthly ovulation cycles are beginning." },
    { q: "The menstrual cycle in humans is approximately:", opts: ["7 days", "14 days", "28 days", "365 days"], ans: 2, exp: "Menstrual cycle: ~28 days. Day 1-5: menstruation. Day 6-13: follicular phase (estrogen rises, lining rebuilds). Day 14: ovulation. Day 15-28: luteal phase (progesterone from corpus luteum)." },
    { q: "Ovulation occurs on approximately which day of the menstrual cycle?", opts: ["Day 1", "Day 7", "Day 14 (mid-cycle)", "Day 28"], ans: 2, exp: "Ovulation: LH surge on ~day 14 → mature Graafian follicle releases ovum from ovary. The ovum travels down the Fallopian tube toward the uterus. Most fertile time." },
    { q: "Fertilisation of egg by sperm normally occurs in the:", opts: ["Uterus", "Ovary", "Fallopian tube (oviduct)", "Cervix"], ans: 2, exp: "Fertilisation occurs in the Fallopian tube (ampulla region). Sperm travel from vagina → cervix → uterus → Fallopian tube. Zygote then implants in the uterine wall ~6-10 days later." },
    { q: "The placenta provides the developing foetus with:", opts: ["Only oxygen", "Only food", "Oxygen and nutrients (from mother) and removes CO₂ and waste", "Blood cells"], ans: 2, exp: "Placenta: intimate contact between foetal and maternal blood (no direct mixing). Diffusion of O₂, glucose, amino acids TO foetus; CO₂, urea FROM foetus. Also produces hormones (hCG, progesterone)." },
    { q: "Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) is the hormone detected in pregnancy tests. It is produced by the:", opts: ["Pituitary gland", "Ovary", "Placenta/trophoblast", "Foetal kidney"], ans: 2, exp: "hCG is produced by the trophoblast (outer cell layer of blastocyst, early placenta) from implantation. hCG maintains corpus luteum → keeps progesterone levels high. Detected in urine by day 10." },
    { q: "The duration of human pregnancy (gestation period) is approximately:", opts: ["6 months", "9 months (38-40 weeks)", "12 months", "15 months"], ans: 1, exp: "Human gestation: ~38-40 weeks (about 9 months). Counted from last menstrual period (LMP) = 40 weeks; from fertilisation = 38 weeks." },
    { q: "Which method of contraception works by preventing ovulation?", opts: ["Condom", "Copper-T (IUD)", "Oral contraceptive pills (OCP)", "Tubectomy"], ans: 2, exp: "Oral contraceptive pills contain synthetic estrogen/progesterone → suppress LH surge → prevent ovulation. Also thicken cervical mucus and alter endometrium. Failure rate < 1% if taken correctly." },
    { q: "Sexually transmitted infections (STIs) like gonorrhoea and syphilis are caused by:", opts: ["Viruses only", "Bacteria (Neisseria gonorrhoeae; Treponema pallidum)", "Fungi", "Parasitic worms"], ans: 1, exp: "Gonorrhoea: Neisseria gonorrhoeae (bacterium). Syphilis: Treponema pallidum (bacterium). HIV/AIDS is viral. Both gonorrhoea and syphilis are treatable with antibiotics." },
    { q: "HIV attacks which specific immune cells?", opts: ["B lymphocytes", "Red blood cells", "CD4+ T helper cells", "Platelets"], ans: 2, exp: "HIV (Human Immunodeficiency Virus) specifically attacks CD4+ T helper lymphocytes, destroying the immune system → AIDS (Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome). No cure; managed with ART." },
    { q: "Cloning produces organisms that are:", opts: ["Genetically diverse", "Genetically identical to the parent (nuclear DNA)", "Hybrid organisms", "Weaker than parents"], ans: 1, exp: "Cloning: somatic cell nuclear transfer (SCNT) or other methods produce organisms with identical nuclear DNA to the donor. First mammal cloned: Dolly the sheep (1996, Roslin Institute)." },
    { q: "The process of vegetative propagation by stem cuttings relies on the ability of stems to form:", opts: ["Gametes", "Adventitious roots", "Flowers directly", "Seeds"], ans: 1, exp: "Stem cuttings: cut stem placed in soil → adventitious roots develop at cut end → new plant grows. Used in rose, sugarcane, hibiscus. Rooting hormone (auxin) can speed root formation." },
    { q: "In tissue culture, plants are grown from:", opts: ["Seeds only", "Small pieces of plant tissue in sterile nutrient medium", "Pollinated flowers", "Only leaves"], ans: 1, exp: "Tissue culture (micropropagation): small explant (leaf, stem, root cells) on sterile nutrient agar medium → callus (undifferentiated mass) → shoots and roots → plantlets → transplanted." },
    { q: "Parthenogenesis is development of an unfertilised egg into a new organism. It occurs in:", opts: ["Humans", "Bacteria", "Some insects (bees, wasps), fish, and plants (seedless fruits)", "Mammals only"], ans: 2, exp: "Parthenogenesis: egg develops without fertilisation. Bees: unfertilised eggs → male drones (haploid); fertilised eggs → female workers/queens. Seedless fruits in plants." },
    { q: "Which is an example of internal fertilisation?", opts: ["Fish spawning in water", "Frog releasing eggs in pond", "Human reproduction", "Most aquatic invertebrate reproduction"], ans: 2, exp: "Internal fertilisation: sperm meets egg inside the female body. Mammals, birds, reptiles, most insects. External fertilisation (fish, frogs): gametes released in water and fuse externally." },
    { q: "The term \"viviparous\" refers to organisms that:", opts: ["Lay eggs that hatch outside", "Give birth to live young (developed inside mother)", "Reproduce asexually only", "Reproduce in water"], ans: 1, exp: "Viviparous: give birth to live young (most mammals, some fish/reptiles). Oviparous: lay eggs (birds, reptiles, most fish). Ovoviviparous: eggs hatch inside mother (some sharks)." },
    { q: "In the flower, the transfer of pollen from anther to stigma is called:", opts: ["Fertilisation", "Germination", "Pollination", "Dispersal"], ans: 2, exp: "Pollination: transfer of pollen grains from anther (male) to stigma (female) — within same flower (self-pollination) or to another flower (cross-pollination). Precedes fertilisation." },
    { q: "Unisexual flowers have:", opts: ["Both stamens and pistil in the same flower", "Either stamens (male) OR pistil (female) but not both", "No reproductive parts", "Only petals"], ans: 1, exp: "Unisexual (imperfect) flowers: only male (staminate) or only female (pistillate) flowers. Examples: maize (corn), papaya, cucumber. Bisexual (perfect): both in same flower (rose, mustard)." },
    { q: "The primary function of the fruit in flowering plants is:", opts: ["Provide food for humans", "Photosynthesis", "Seed dispersal and protection of seeds", "Pollination"], ans: 2, exp: "Fruit develops from the ripened ovary wall (pericarp), encloses and protects seeds. Attracts animals (with colour/smell) → animals eat fruit → disperse seeds away from parent plant." },
    { q: "Apomixis in plants refers to:", opts: ["Vegetative reproduction", "Production of seeds without fertilisation (asexual seed formation)", "Formation of pollen", "Fruit development"], ans: 1, exp: "Apomixis: seed formation without fertilisation. Embryo develops from unfertilised egg or from diploid cells of ovule. Common in grasses, dandelions. Produces clonal offspring via seeds." },
    { q: "The foetal heartbeat can first be detected at approximately:", opts: ["2 weeks", "6 weeks", "Week 5-6 of pregnancy", "4 months"], ans: 2, exp: "Foetal heartbeat first detectable by ultrasound at ~5-6 weeks of gestation. The heart is one of the first organs to develop and function in the embryo." },
    { q: "Condoms prevent pregnancy primarily by:", opts: ["Killing sperm", "Preventing ovulation", "Acting as a physical barrier preventing sperm from reaching the egg", "Changing uterine lining"], ans: 2, exp: "Condom: physical barrier that prevents sperm from entering the vagina/reaching the egg. Also prevents STIs (unlike other contraceptive methods). Male or female condoms available." },
    { q: "Testicular torsion is dangerous because:", opts: ["It causes infertility always", "Twisting cuts off blood supply to testis → ischaemia/necrosis if not treated quickly", "It affects hormone production only", "It is very common and harmless"], ans: 1, exp: "Testicular torsion: twisting of spermatic cord cuts off blood supply → ischaemia → testicular damage within 6 hours. Medical emergency requiring immediate surgery (orchiopexy)." },
    { q: "Hermaphrodite organisms have:", opts: ["No reproductive organs", "Only male organs", "Both male and female reproductive organs in the same organism", "Only female organs"], ans: 2, exp: "Hermaphrodites: both male and female organs in same organism. Examples: earthworm, snail, many flowering plants. Usually cross-fertilise (not self-fertilise) to prevent inbreeding." },
    { q: "The corpus luteum (yellow body) in the ovary produces:", opts: ["Estrogen only", "Progesterone (and some estrogen) to maintain uterine lining", "FSH", "Testosterone"], ans: 1, exp: "After ovulation, the ruptured Graafian follicle becomes the corpus luteum. It secretes progesterone → maintains uterine lining for implantation. If no pregnancy, corpus luteum degenerates → menstruation." },
    { q: "Menopause occurs when:", opts: ["Menarche begins", "Menstruation permanently ceases (~45-55 years) due to declining ovarian function", "Ovulation increases", "Estrogen levels rise sharply"], ans: 1, exp: "Menopause: permanent cessation of menstrual cycles (~45-55 years). Ovarian follicles depleted → estrogen/progesterone fall → FSH/LH rise (feedback). Symptoms: hot flushes, mood changes." },
    { q: "Which structure in the male testes actually produces sperm?", opts: ["Leydig cells", "Sertoli cells", "Seminiferous tubules", "Epididymis"], ans: 2, exp: "Seminiferous tubules (tightly coiled tubes inside testes): site of spermatogenesis (sperm production). Sertoli cells nourish developing sperm; Leydig cells produce testosterone." },
    { q: "The acrosome of a sperm cell contains:", opts: ["Mitochondria", "DNA", "Hydrolytic enzymes to penetrate the egg", "Flagellum motor proteins"], ans: 2, exp: "Acrosome (cap over sperm head): contains hydrolytic enzymes (hyaluronidase, acrosin) released during acrosome reaction → digest zona pellucida of egg → allows sperm entry." },
    { q: "In humans, the egg (ovum) is haploid, containing:", opts: ["46 chromosomes", "23 chromosomes (n)", "92 chromosomes", "48 chromosomes"], ans: 1, exp: "Human egg: 23 chromosomes (haploid, n=23). Human sperm: 23 chromosomes. After fertilisation: zygote has 46 chromosomes (diploid, 2n=46). 22 autosomes + 1 sex chromosome (X or Y) in gametes." },
    { q: "Which of the following is a sexually transmitted viral disease?", opts: ["Syphilis", "Gonorrhoea", "Genital herpes (HSV-2)", "Chlamydia"], ans: 2, exp: "Genital herpes: caused by Herpes Simplex Virus type 2 (HSV-2), a viral STI — incurable (virus persists). Syphilis, gonorrhoea, chlamydia are bacterial STIs — treatable with antibiotics." },
    { q: "Emergency contraception (morning after pill) works by:", opts: ["Causing abortion of implanted embryo", "Preventing ovulation or fertilisation if taken within 72 hours", "Killing sperm in uterus", "Blocking estrogen permanently"], ans: 1, exp: "Emergency contraception (Plan B, levonorgestrel): high-dose progesterone taken within 72 hours of unprotected sex → delays/prevents ovulation, may affect fertilisation. Does NOT cause abortion of implanted embryo." },
    { q: "Test-tube baby (IVF) involves:", opts: ["Baby growing completely in lab", "Fertilisation occurring in vitro (lab), then embryo transferred to uterus", "Only artificial insemination", "Cloning"], ans: 1, exp: "IVF (In Vitro Fertilisation): eggs retrieved from ovary, fertilised with sperm in lab dish → embryo cultured 3-5 days → transferred to uterus for implantation. Louise Brown: first IVF baby (1978)." },
    { q: "The embryo at the 8-cell stage is called a:", opts: ["Zygote", "Blastocyst", "Morula", "Gastrula"], ans: 2, exp: "Morula: solid ball of 8-16 cells formed by repeated cleavage of the zygote (before fluid-filled cavity develops). Blastocyst (32+ cells with cavity) implants in the uterus." },
    { q: "Which hormone triggers ovulation in the female cycle?", opts: ["FSH", "LH surge on day 14", "Progesterone", "Estrogen only"], ans: 1, exp: "LH (Luteinising Hormone) surge from the anterior pituitary on ~day 14 triggers ovulation — release of the mature ovum from the Graafian follicle in the ovary." },
    { q: "Seminal vesicles and prostate gland in males produce:", opts: ["Sperm", "Testosterone", "Seminal fluid (nourishes and transports sperm)", "Estrogen"], ans: 2, exp: "Seminal vesicles: secrete fructose-rich fluid (~60% of semen). Prostate: secretes alkaline fluid (neutralises vaginal acidity). Together they form seminal fluid that nourishes/transports sperm." },
    { q: "Binary fission in Amoeba is an example of:", opts: ["Sexual reproduction", "Asexual reproduction (mitosis)", "Budding", "Fragmentation"], ans: 1, exp: "Binary fission in Amoeba: a single parent cell divides into two identical daughter cells by mitosis. This is asexual reproduction — no gametes involved, offspring are genetically identical." },
    { q: "Leishmania, a parasitic protozoan, reproduces by binary fission but the plane of division is longitudinal. In Plasmodium (malaria parasite), asexual reproduction inside red blood cells occurs by:", opts: ["Binary fission (longitudinal)", "Multiple fission (schizogony)", "Budding", "Sporulation in a sporangium"], ans: 1, exp: "Plasmodium undergoes multiple fission (schizogony) inside RBCs — the nucleus divides repeatedly, then cytoplasm divides around each nucleus producing many merozoites simultaneously. This is why so many RBCs lyse at once causing periodic fever." },
    { q: "In Hydra, a bud begins as a small outgrowth. Which cell type in Hydra is primarily responsible for initiating budding?", opts: ["Nematocysts", "Interstitial (I) cells — multipotent stem cells", "Gastrodermal cells only", "Cnidocytes"], ans: 1, exp: "Interstitial (I) cells are multipotent stem cells interspersed among ectodermal cells of Hydra. They proliferate to form buds and can differentiate into all cell types. Nematocysts (stinging cells) are defence/capture structures, not reproductive cells." },
    { q: "Vegetative propagation in potato occurs through eyes (buds) on tubers. A potato tuber is morphologically a:", opts: ["Root modified for storage", "Underground stem (stem tuber) bearing nodes with axillary buds", "Swollen leaf petiole", "Modified flower bud"], ans: 1, exp: "Potato (Solanum tuberosum) tubers are modified underground stems (stem tubers). Evidence: they have nodes (eyes are axillary buds), internodes, and scale leaves. Roots lack nodes and buds. Sweet potato is a root tuber (different structure)." },
    { q: "During spore formation in Rhizopus, the sporangiospores are enclosed in a sporangium at the tip of an erect hypha called a:", opts: ["Rhizoid", "Stolon", "Sporangiophore", "Haustorium"], ans: 2, exp: "The erect hyphae bearing sporangia are sporangiophores. Rhizoids are root-like hyphae anchoring Rhizopus to substrate. Stolons are horizontal hyphae connecting upright sporangiophores. Haustoria are nutrient-absorbing hyphae in parasitic fungi." },
    { q: "Fragmentation in Spirogyra is typically triggered by:", opts: ["Increase in light intensity only", "Unfavourable conditions (drought, temperature extremes) causing filaments to break at cell walls", "Sexual reproduction followed by fragmentation", "Enzyme secretion by predators"], ans: 1, exp: "Spirogyra filaments fragment mainly under adverse conditions (nutrient depletion, desiccation, mechanical stress). Each fragment can grow into a new filament under favourable conditions. This is a form of asexual reproduction distinct from conjugation (sexual)." },
    { q: "If a flatworm (Planaria) is cut transversely into three pieces — head, middle, and tail — what is the most accurate prediction?", opts: ["Only the head piece survives", "All three regenerate into complete organisms (head piece fastest, tail piece slowest)", "Only head and middle regenerate", "None regenerate — flatworms need their nervous system intact"], ans: 1, exp: "Planaria's neoblast (totipotent stem cells) allow any fragment to regenerate a complete organism. The head end regenerates fastest (the Wnt/β-catenin gradient guides polarity). Even a 1/279th fragment can regenerate. Tail fragment takes longer but does regenerate." },
    { q: "In a bisexual flower, which part is the receptacle?", opts: ["The stalk of the flower", "The swollen end of the peduncle bearing all floral whorls", "The basal part of the ovary", "The place where pollen is deposited"], ans: 1, exp: "The receptacle is the swollen tip of the pedicel (flower stalk) from which all four floral whorls arise: calyx (sepals), corolla (petals), androecium (stamens), and gynoecium (pistil). In some plants (e.g., rose), it develops into a false fruit (accessory fruit)." },
    { q: "A pollen grain that lands on the stigma germinates to produce a pollen tube. The pollen tube grows through the:", opts: ["Petal wall", "Style, carrying two male gametes (sperm cells) to the ovule", "Directly through the ovary wall", "Sepal tissue"], ans: 1, exp: "The pollen tube grows down through the style (often through a transmitting tract tissue) carrying the tube nucleus and two male gametes (sperm cells). It enters the ovule through the micropyle and delivers the gametes for double fertilisation." },
    { q: "In angiosperms, after double fertilisation, the primary endosperm nucleus (3n) undergoes repeated mitotic divisions to form the endosperm. In most monocots, the mature seed endosperm is:", opts: ["Completely consumed before seed maturity, stored in cotyledons", "Retained as a persistent food-storage tissue in the mature seed", "Converted to fruit flesh", "Triploid but becomes diploid at maturity"], ans: 1, exp: "In monocots (e.g., maize, wheat, rice), the endosperm persists as a major food-storage tissue in the mature seed. In most dicots (e.g., pea, bean), the endosperm is absorbed by the cotyledons during seed development, so mature seeds have large cotyledons and little/no endosperm." },
    { q: "Wind-pollinated (anemophilous) flowers show which combination of features?", opts: ["Large, colourful petals; large, sticky stigma; scented; abundant nectar", "Small or absent petals; large feathery stigma; light dry pollen produced in large quantities; no nectar", "Enclosed anthers; small stigma; large flowers", "Tubular flowers; sticky pollen; prominent anthers projecting outward"], ans: 1, exp: "Wind-pollinated flowers: petals small or absent (no need to attract insects); stigma large and feathery to catch airborne pollen; pollen is light, smooth, dry, and produced in enormous quantities; no nectar or scent needed. Examples: grasses, maize, wheat, date palm." },
    { q: "In the human male reproductive system, the correct pathway of sperm from production to ejaculation is:", opts: ["Seminiferous tubules → Epididymis → Vas deferens → Ejaculatory duct → Urethra", "Leydig cells → Epididymis → Vas deferens → Urethra", "Seminiferous tubules → Vas deferens → Epididymis → Urethra", "Testes → Prostate → Vas deferens → Epididymis → Urethra"], ans: 0, exp: "Spermatogenesis occurs in seminiferous tubules. Immature sperm mature in the epididymis (takes ~2-3 weeks). During ejaculation: epididymis → vas deferens → ejaculatory duct (joined by seminal vesicle duct) → urethra → out. Prostate and bulbourethral glands secrete fluids along the way." },
    { q: "Spermatogenesis requires that the testes be at approximately 2–3°C below core body temperature. This is why testes are located in the scrotum. If the testes fail to descend (cryptorchidism), the primary consequence is:", opts: ["Excess testosterone production", "Infertility due to failure of spermatogenesis at core body temperature", "Complete loss of testosterone", "Increased sperm production"], ans: 1, exp: "Spermatogenesis is highly temperature sensitive. At core body temperature (~37°C), sperm production fails. Cryptorchidism results in infertility if untreated. Testosterone production by Leydig cells is less temperature sensitive, so hormonal function is largely preserved." },
    { q: "The acrosome reaction of a sperm is triggered by:", opts: ["Contact with the zona pellucida of the egg, releasing hydrolytic enzymes", "The acidic environment of the vagina", "LH from the pituitary", "Contact with the uterine wall"], ans: 0, exp: "When a sperm binds to the zona pellucida (glycoprotein coat around the egg), the acrosome reaction is triggered: the acrosomal membrane fuses with the plasma membrane, releasing hydrolytic enzymes (hyaluronidase, acrosin) that digest the zona pellucida, allowing sperm penetration." },
    { q: "After fertilisation, a zygote undergoes cleavage. Unlike normal cell division, cleavage results in:", opts: ["Cells that grow larger with each division", "Successively smaller cells (blastomeres) with no increase in total cytoplasmic volume", "Only 2-4 cells total before implantation", "Immediate differentiation into specific tissue types"], ans: 1, exp: "Cleavage divisions are rapid mitoses without growth between divisions. The large zygote is subdivided into smaller and smaller blastomeres. Total cytoplasmic volume remains roughly constant — cell number increases while cell size decreases. This continues from 2-cell to morula (~16 cells) to blastocyst." },
    { q: "The corpus luteum degenerates at the end of a non-conception menstrual cycle because:", opts: ["FSH levels rise sharply", "LH support (from pituitary) is withdrawn; without hCG from an embryo, corpus luteum regresses", "Estrogen permanently suppresses it", "The uterine lining absorbs it"], ans: 1, exp: "Corpus luteum requires LH for maintenance. After ovulation, LH levels drop. If no embryo is present (no hCG), the corpus luteum regresses after ~14 days → progesterone and estrogen fall → uterine lining sheds (menstruation). If pregnancy occurs, embryonic hCG maintains corpus luteum until placenta takes over." },
    { q: "Copper-T (intrauterine device) prevents pregnancy primarily by:", opts: ["Releasing copper ions that inhibit ovulation", "Copper ions impair sperm motility and viability, and may prevent implantation by causing local inflammation", "Acting as a physical barrier at the cervix", "Releasing progesterone to suppress LH"], ans: 1, exp: "Copper IUD works mainly by the spermicidal effect of copper ions (Cu²⁺ impair sperm motility and acrosome reaction) and the local inflammatory reaction in the uterus that is hostile to sperm and fertilised eggs. It does NOT primarily prevent ovulation (unlike hormonal IUDs)." },
    { q: "HIV is classified as a retrovirus. The key feature that makes retroviruses distinct from other RNA viruses is:", opts: ["They are always lethal", "They carry reverse transcriptase, converting their RNA genome into DNA that integrates into the host genome", "They replicate in the nucleus only", "They produce DNA from the host cell's own DNA"], ans: 1, exp: "Retroviruses (HIV, HTLV) carry the enzyme reverse transcriptase, which transcribes their RNA genome into complementary DNA (cDNA). This DNA is then integrated into the host cell's chromosomal DNA (as a provirus) by integrase. Standard RNA viruses do not use reverse transcription." },
    { q: "Which contraceptive method provides protection against sexually transmitted infections (STIs) AND pregnancy?", opts: ["Oral contraceptive pills", "Copper-T IUD", "Male condom used correctly and consistently", "Tubectomy"], ans: 2, exp: "Only barrier methods (condoms) provide dual protection: against STIs (by preventing exchange of body fluids) AND against pregnancy (physical barrier to sperm). Pills, IUDs, and tubectomy prevent pregnancy but offer NO protection against STIs." },
    { q: "Apomixis in plants such as dandelion produces offspring that are:", opts: ["Genetically diverse due to meiotic recombination", "Genetically identical to the mother plant (clones) because no fertilisation occurs", "Always triploid", "Produced from pollen of another plant"], ans: 1, exp: "Apomixis (agamospermy): seeds develop without fertilisation, from diploid cells of the ovule or from an unreduced embryo sac. Offspring are genetically identical to the mother (maternal clones). Agronomy advantage: preserving hybrid vigour in seeds without re-crossing." },
    { q: "In tissue culture (micropropagation), a callus is:", opts: ["A type of hormone used in the medium", "An undifferentiated mass of totipotent dividing cells formed from an explant on nutrient agar", "A mature plantlet ready for transplanting", "Dead tissue from the cut explant"], ans: 1, exp: "A callus is an undifferentiated mass of dividing parenchyma-like cells that forms when an explant is placed on a nutrient medium containing auxins and cytokinins. By adjusting hormone ratios, the callus can then be induced to differentiate into shoots and roots (organogenesis)." },
    { q: "Sexual reproduction in flowering plants requires meiosis to produce haploid gametes. In which specific structure does meiosis occur to produce pollen grains?", opts: ["The pollen grain itself divides by meiosis", "The pollen mother cells (PMC) inside the anther undergo meiosis → 4 haploid microspores → each becomes a pollen grain", "The stigma undergoes meiosis", "Meiosis occurs in the style"], ans: 1, exp: "Microsporogenesis: pollen mother cells (2n) in the anther's microsporangia undergo meiosis → tetrad of 4 haploid microspores. Each microspore undergoes mitosis → mature pollen grain (with tube cell nucleus and generative cell). The generative cell will divide into 2 sperm." },
    { q: "The significance of DNA replication before cell division in reproduction is best explained as:", opts: ["To increase cell size before division", "To ensure each daughter cell receives a complete, identical copy of the genetic information, maintaining continuity", "To repair mutations in DNA", "To produce RNA for protein synthesis"], ans: 1, exp: "Before each mitotic division (and in the S-phase before meiosis), DNA is precisely replicated so that every daughter cell inherits a complete, accurate copy of the genome. This ensures genetic continuity across generations of cells and is the molecular basis of heredity in asexual reproduction." },
    { q: "Variation in sexually reproducing organisms arises from all of the following EXCEPT:", opts: ["Crossing over during meiosis I", "Independent assortment of chromosomes", "Random fertilisation between egg and sperm", "DNA replication of a single parent cell in binary fission"], ans: 3, exp: "Binary fission produces genetically identical cells — it is NOT a source of variation. Sexual reproduction generates variation through: crossing over (new allele combinations), independent assortment (random chromosome segregation), and random fertilisation (one of millions of possible gamete combinations)." },
    { q: "The advantage of variations in a sexually reproducing population under environmental change is best described as:", opts: ["Variations cause all individuals to adapt equally", "Some variants may be pre-adapted to new conditions, giving them survival advantage — the population avoids extinction", "Variations always make organisms stronger", "Variations reduce reproductive rate"], ans: 1, exp: "Variation is the raw material for natural selection. When conditions change (new pathogen, climate shift), variants pre-adapted to the new conditions survive and reproduce while others may die. Without variation, a new challenge could wipe out the entire population. This is Darwin's insight." },
    { q: "Genital herpes (HSV-2) is an STI that cannot be cured because:", opts: ["Antibiotics are not effective on it, but antiviral drugs can eradicate it", "HSV-2 establishes latent infection in sensory nerve ganglia; the virus persists for life despite antiviral treatment", "The immune system destroys the drug", "HSV-2 lives only in blood cells"], ans: 1, exp: "HSV-2 establishes lifelong latency in sacral (dorsal root) sensory ganglia. Antiviral drugs (acyclovir) suppress replication and reduce outbreaks but cannot eliminate the latent virus. Periodic reactivation causes recurrent lesions. This distinguishes viral (incurable) from bacterial STIs (curable with antibiotics)." },
    { q: "A child is born with cystic fibrosis, a condition due to a defect in a gene whose product is required for chloride ion transport. The parents appear normal. This is consistent with:", opts: ["Autosomal dominant inheritance", "X-linked recessive inheritance (mother is carrier)", "Autosomal recessive inheritance (both parents are carriers)", "A new mutation in the germ cell"], ans: 2, exp: "Autosomal recessive: both parents carry one defective allele (Cc × Cc) and are phenotypically normal. 25% of children are affected (cc). Cystic fibrosis (CFTR gene, chromosome 7) is the most common autosomal recessive disorder in Caucasians. X-linked disorders affect sons more often and mothers are carriers." },
    { q: "In the female reproductive cycle, which hormone is responsible for the proliferative (follicular) phase, causing the uterine lining to thicken?", opts: ["Progesterone", "Estrogen (secreted by the growing follicle)", "FSH directly", "LH directly"], ans: 1, exp: "Estrogen secreted by the developing ovarian follicle (days 6-13) acts on the uterine endometrium causing cell proliferation (thickening). Progesterone (from corpus luteum, days 15-28) causes further changes (secretory phase). FSH and LH are pituitary hormones that regulate the ovary." },
    { q: "A woman wishes to use the rhythm (calendar) method of contraception. If her cycle is consistently 28 days, the MOST fertile period to avoid unprotected sex is approximately:", opts: ["Days 1-5 (menstruation)", "Days 10-17 (around ovulation on day 14)", "Days 22-28 (late luteal phase)", "There is no fertile period — the entire cycle is risky"], ans: 1, exp: "The ovum survives ~12-24 hours after ovulation. Sperm survive up to 5 days. Ovulation in a 28-day cycle is ~day 14. The fertile window is days 10-17 (sperm can be present when ovulation occurs). The calendar method avoids intercourse during this window. It is unreliable in women with irregular cycles." },
    { q: "The placenta acts as a selective barrier. Which of the following can cross the placenta and potentially harm the foetus?", opts: ["Maternal red blood cells", "Maternal IgG antibodies, some viruses (HIV, rubella), alcohol, and certain drugs", "Maternal platelets", "Maternal white blood cells (in normal pregnancy)"], ans: 1, exp: "The placenta allows passive transport of maternal IgG (protective immunity) and small molecules (O₂, glucose, alcohol, many drugs, nicotine). Viruses like HIV and rubella can cross via trophoblast cells. Maternal blood cells do not cross in a normal pregnancy (separate circulations), though foetal RBCs can leak across during trauma/delivery." },
    { q: "Parthenogenesis in honey bees determines the caste/sex of offspring. Unfertilised eggs develop into:", opts: ["Queens (diploid)", "Workers (diploid)", "Drones — haploid males (n)", "Infertile females"], ans: 2, exp: "Bee sex determination: unfertilised eggs → haploid drones (males, n). Fertilised eggs → diploid females (queen or workers depending on larval nutrition). Drones develop by parthenogenesis and have only mothers (no fathers). Queens produce fertilised and unfertilised eggs." },
    { q: "Vasectomy (male sterilisation) involves cutting and sealing the:", opts: ["Seminal vesicles", "Vas deferens (sperm duct) — prevents sperm from reaching semen", "Epididymis", "Urethra"], ans: 1, exp: "Vasectomy: the vas deferens is cut, tied, or sealed (bilateral), preventing sperm from being ejaculated. Testosterone production and libido are unaffected. The man still ejaculates (semen without sperm). It is highly effective (>99.9%) and largely irreversible. Tubectomy is the female equivalent (fallopian tubes blocked)." },
    { q: "In angiosperm reproduction, the \"embryo sac\" inside the ovule typically contains how many cells/nuclei at maturity?", opts: ["4 cells", "7 cells and 8 nuclei (Polygonum type)", "2 cells", "16 cells"], ans: 1, exp: "The mature female gametophyte (embryo sac) of most angiosperms (Polygonum type) contains 7 cells and 8 nuclei: 3 antipodal cells, 2 synergid cells, 1 egg cell, and 1 central cell with 2 polar nuclei. The egg cell is fertilised by one sperm; the central cell (with 2 polar nuclei) is fertilised by the other sperm → triploid endosperm." },
    { q: "Why must sexually reproducing organisms maintain alternation of generations (meiosis) to keep chromosome number constant?", opts: ["Because mitosis doubles chromosome number each generation", "Without meiosis to halve the chromosome number in gametes, fusion of two gametes would double the chromosome number each generation, causing geometric increase", "Because meiosis repairs damaged chromosomes", "Organisms do not need meiosis if they have repair mechanisms"], ans: 1, exp: "Each gamete contributes the haploid (n) set. Fertilisation (n + n = 2n) restores the diploid number. Without meiosis: gametes would be diploid (2n); fertilisation would give 4n; next generation 8n, etc. Meiosis is the essential compensatory halving that maintains ploidy across generations." },
    { q: "Cross-pollination between two different plant species (interspecific pollination) usually does NOT result in fertile offspring because:", opts: ["Pollen grain is too large", "Post-pollination barriers: incompatible pollen tube growth, failure of fertilisation, or hybrid sterility (reproductive isolation)", "Cross-species pollen has no germination capacity at all", "The stigma destroys all foreign pollen"], ans: 1, exp: "Post-pollination barriers prevent interspecific fertilisation: (1) pollen tube may fail to grow through the style (incompatible signals); (2) if fertilisation occurs, the hybrid embryo may abort; (3) if the hybrid is born, it may be sterile (like a mule). These barriers contribute to reproductive isolation — the basis of speciation." },
    { q: "A student compares asexual reproduction in yeast and Hydra. One key distinction is:", opts: ["Yeast buds are formed by meiosis; Hydra buds by mitosis", "In yeast, the bud separates while still small; in Hydra, the bud usually detaches only after forming a complete individual with tentacles and mouth", "Hydra buds form inside the parent body; yeast buds form externally", "Both processes produce genetically diverse offspring"], ans: 1, exp: "In Hydra budding, the bud grows into a miniature adult — developing a mouth and tentacles — before detaching. Yeast buds pinch off while much smaller (the daughter cell grows after separation). Both use mitosis, producing genetically identical offspring. Neither involves meiosis." },
    { q: "Seed dormancy is an adaptive feature because:", opts: ["It prevents seeds from being eaten by animals", "It delays germination until environmental conditions (temperature, moisture, light) are suitable, improving seedling survival", "It prevents cross-pollination", "It reduces competition between seeds and pollen"], ans: 1, exp: "Seed dormancy is controlled by abscisic acid (ABA). It ensures seeds do not germinate during unfavourable seasons (winter, drought). Germination is triggered by specific cues (stratification by cold, scarification, water). This is an evolutionary adaptation maximising seedling survival rate." },
    { q: "A woman with irregular menstrual cycles is planning pregnancy. Her doctor recommends using basal body temperature (BBT) to identify ovulation. BBT rises by ~0.2-0.5°C after ovulation because of:", opts: ["Estrogen surge at ovulation", "Progesterone secreted by the corpus luteum has a thermogenic effect", "LH itself raises body temperature", "The uterine lining generates heat during the luteal phase"], ans: 1, exp: "Progesterone, secreted by the corpus luteum after ovulation, acts on the hypothalamus and has a thermogenic (heat-raising) effect — BBT rises by 0.2-0.5°C and stays elevated throughout the luteal phase. A sustained rise in BBT confirms ovulation has occurred. The fall in BBT signals impending menstruation." },
    { q: "Which of the following represents the correct order of events after pollen lands on the stigma?", opts: ["Pollen tube forms → double fertilisation → pollination complete", "Pollen hydrates → pollen tube germinates → tube grows through style → two sperm released in ovule → double fertilisation", "Sperm are released on stigma → swim to ovule → fertilisation", "Stigma secretes enzymes that fertilise the egg directly"], ans: 1, exp: "Correct sequence: pollen grain hydrates (absorbs water from stigma) → pollen tube germinates (grows from germination pore) → tube grows through style, guided by chemical signals → enters ovule via micropyle → tube bursts → 2 sperm released → double fertilisation (1 sperm + egg = zygote; 1 sperm + 2 polar nuclei = endosperm)." },
    { q: "Semen analysis revealed a man has spermatozoa with normal morphology and count, but very low motility (asthenozoospermia). Which organelle defect most likely explains this?", opts: ["Defective acrosome (missing enzymes)", "Defective mitochondria in the sperm midpiece (insufficient ATP for flagellar movement)", "Missing nucleus (no DNA)", "Absent plasma membrane"], ans: 1, exp: "Sperm flagellar movement (via dynein arms on axoneme) requires ATP. The midpiece of the sperm is packed with mitochondria that generate ATP by oxidative phosphorylation. Defective mitochondria → insufficient ATP → immotile sperm (asthenozoospermia), a major cause of male infertility." },
    { q: "The term \"totipotency\" as used in plant tissue culture means:", opts: ["The ability of a cell to grow indefinitely in culture", "The ability of a single plant cell to develop into a complete organism — expressing the full genetic potential", "The ability of a cell to resist disease", "The ability to produce all types of pollen"], ans: 1, exp: "Totipotency: every living plant cell retains the complete genome and, under appropriate conditions, can develop into a complete organism. This was proposed by Haberlandt (1902) and demonstrated by Steward (1958) with carrot cells. It is the scientific basis of plant tissue culture and micropropagation." },
    { q: "Amenorrhoea (absence of menstruation) during pregnancy occurs because:", opts: ["The uterus is stretched", "High levels of hCG, progesterone, and estrogen suppress FSH/LH, preventing follicle development and menstruation", "The foetus consumes all available hormones", "Estrogen alone suppresses ovulation permanently"], ans: 1, exp: "Pregnancy: hCG maintains corpus luteum → high progesterone and estrogen levels → negative feedback on pituitary → FSH and LH suppressed → no new follicle develops → no ovulation → no endometrial shedding (amenorrhoea). Progesterone also prevents uterine contractions to maintain pregnancy." },
    { q: "Microbial diseases can be transmitted sexually. Chlamydia trachomatis is particularly dangerous because:", opts: ["It is always symptomatic and easy to detect", "It is often asymptomatic, leading to silent spread and complications such as pelvic inflammatory disease and infertility", "It can only infect males", "It is a viral infection unresponsive to antibiotics"], ans: 1, exp: "Chlamydia (Chlamydia trachomatis — obligate intracellular bacterium) is the most common bacterial STI worldwide. It is often asymptomatic (especially in women), allowing silent transmission. Untreated, it causes pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), ectopic pregnancy, and infertility. It is treatable with antibiotics (azithromycin)." },
    { q: "The vas deferens runs from the epididymis through the inguinal canal into the pelvic cavity and is joined by the duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct. The ejaculatory duct then passes through the:", opts: ["Prostate gland and opens into the urethra", "Bladder and opens into the ureter", "Testis and loops back", "Directly to the exterior"], ans: 0, exp: "The ejaculatory duct (formed by vas deferens + seminal vesicle duct) passes through the prostate gland and opens into the prostatic urethra. The prostate also adds its secretion to semen at this point. The urethra then carries semen (and urine, separately) to the exterior through the penis." },
    { q: "In plant asexual reproduction by runners (stolons), as seen in strawberry, the new plant forms at the:", opts: ["Tip of every internode on the runner", "Node of the runner, where adventitious roots and shoots develop", "Base of the parent plant only", "Leaf axils of the original plant"], ans: 1, exp: "Stolons (runners) are horizontal stems that grow along the soil surface. At alternate nodes, adventitious roots grow downward and shoots grow upward → new plantlet. The runner then withers, leaving independent plants. Grass, strawberry, and spider plant reproduce this way." },
    { q: "The sequence of events in the human menstrual cycle from Days 1-28 is governed by hormonal interactions. The CORRECT hormonal sequence is:", opts: ["FSH → Estrogen → LH surge → Ovulation → Progesterone", "LH → FSH → Progesterone → Estrogen → Ovulation", "Estrogen → LH → FSH → Ovulation → Progesterone", "Progesterone → FSH → Estrogen → LH → Ovulation"], ans: 0, exp: "FSH (from pituitary) stimulates follicle growth and estrogen production. Rising estrogen (positive feedback) triggers the LH surge at ~day 13-14. LH surge causes ovulation (day 14). Post-ovulation: corpus luteum secretes progesterone (and estrogen), maintaining the endometrium (days 15-28). If no pregnancy, corpus luteum degenerates → progesterone falls → menstruation." },
    { q: "A 14-year-old boy is concerned that he is producing semen but not fertile sperm yet. In normal puberty, the hormone that initiates spermatogenesis is:", opts: ["Growth hormone", "Testosterone (rising under LH stimulation at puberty, combined with FSH acting on Sertoli cells)", "Estrogen", "Cortisol"], ans: 1, exp: "Puberty: the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis activates. LH stimulates Leydig cells to produce testosterone. FSH acts on Sertoli cells to support spermatogenesis. Rising testosterone causes secondary sexual characteristics AND initiates spermatogenesis in seminiferous tubules. Full spermatogenic maturation takes weeks-months after puberty onset." },
    { q: "Which of the following correctly distinguishes between geitonogamy and xenogamy?", opts: ["Geitonogamy: pollen from same flower. Xenogamy: pollen from a different species", "Geitonogamy: cross-pollination between different flowers on the SAME plant (genetically = self-pollination). Xenogamy: cross-pollination between different plants of the same species", "Geitonogamy: pollination by insects. Xenogamy: pollination by wind", "Geitonogamy: occurs only in monoecious plants. Xenogamy: occurs only in dioecious plants"], ans: 1, exp: "Autogamy: same flower (self-pollination). Geitonogamy: pollen from a different flower on the SAME plant — mechanically cross-pollination but genetically self-pollination (same genome). Xenogamy: pollen from a genetically different individual of the same species — true cross-pollination, introducing new allele combinations." },
    { q: "Emergency contraception using mifepristone (RU-486) works differently from levonorgestrel. Mifepristone is a:", opts: ["Progestogen agonist that prevents ovulation", "Progesterone receptor antagonist that blocks progesterone action, used within 72h to prevent or interrupt early pregnancy", "Estrogen blocker that prevents fertilisation", "Gonadotropin that triggers ovulation at a wrong time"], ans: 1, exp: "Mifepristone (RU-486) is a progesterone receptor antagonist — it blocks progesterone's action. Without progesterone signalling, the endometrium cannot support implantation, and the uterine lining sheds. Used within 72 hours post-coital (emergency contraception) or up to 7 weeks for medical abortion (combined with misoprostol)." },
    { q: "Organisms that reproduce only asexually in stable environments are at a long-term evolutionary disadvantage compared to sexually reproducing organisms because:", opts: ["Asexual reproduction is slower", "Asexual clones lack variation; any new pathogen or environmental change could wipe out the entire genetically identical population", "Asexual reproduction uses more energy", "Sexual organisms can live longer"], ans: 1, exp: "The \"Red Queen hypothesis\" describes the evolutionary advantage of sexual reproduction: pathogens rapidly evolve to infect a genetically uniform host population. Sexual reproduction continuously generates new allele combinations, making it much harder for parasites/pathogens to adapt. A clonal population could be devastated by a single new pathogen to which none have resistance." },
    { q: "In IVF, the number of embryos transferred to the uterus is typically limited (1-2). The main reason for this limitation is:", opts: ["Only 1-2 embryos can be created in the lab", "To reduce the risk of multiple pregnancy (twins, triplets), which carries higher maternal and foetal risks", "The uterus can only hold 1-2 blastocysts genetically", "More embryos reduce the chance of success"], ans: 1, exp: "Multiple embryo transfer increases IVF success rate per cycle but also greatly increases risk of multiple pregnancies (twins, triplets). Multiple pregnancies have higher risks: premature delivery, low birth weight, maternal complications. Current guidelines recommend single embryo transfer (SET) in most cases to minimize these risks." }
  ]
,

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════
     CH 8 — HEREDITY AND EVOLUTION
  ══════════════════════════════════════ */
  8: [
    { q: "The physical appearance of an organism (its observable traits) is called its:", opts: ["Genotype", "Phenotype", "Allele", "Karyotype"], ans: 1, exp: "Phenotype: observable characteristics (height, colour, blood group). Genotype: genetic makeup (alleles). Same genotype can give same phenotype; different genotypes can give same phenotype." },
    { q: "In Mendel's experiment, tall plant crossed with short plant gave all tall offspring (F1). This means:", opts: ["Short is dominant", "Tall trait is dominant over short", "Both traits blend", "Neither is dominant"], ans: 1, exp: "All F1 plants were tall → tallness is dominant (expressed in heterozygous state). Shortness is recessive (only expressed when homozygous). Mendel's Law of Dominance." },
    { q: "If TT (tall) × tt (short) → F1: all Tt. F1 × F1 → F2 ratio is:", opts: ["3 tall : 1 short", "1 tall : 1 short", "2 tall : 1 short", "All short"], ans: 0, exp: "F1 Tt × Tt → TT : Tt : tt = 1:2:1 (genotypic). Phenotypic: 3 tall (TT + Tt) : 1 short (tt). 3:1 ratio. Mendel's Law of Segregation result." },
    { q: "Mendel's Law of Segregation states that:", opts: ["Alleles mix permanently", "Alleles of a gene pair separate (segregate) during gamete formation", "Genes on same chromosome assort independently", "Dominant allele doubles in frequency"], ans: 1, exp: "Law of Segregation: the two alleles of a gene pair separate from each other during meiosis (gamete formation) and only one allele passes to each gamete." },
    { q: "Mendel's Law of Independent Assortment states:", opts: ["One gene affects multiple traits", "Alleles of different genes assort independently of each other during gamete formation", "Dominant traits always mask recessive ones", "Genes on same chromosome segregate together"], ans: 1, exp: "Law of Independent Assortment: genes for different traits assort independently in gametes (IF on different chromosomes). Dihybrid cross: RrYy × RrYy → 9:3:3:1 ratio." },
    { q: "A dihybrid cross (RrYy × RrYy) gives phenotypic ratio:", opts: ["3:1", "1:2:1", "9:3:3:1", "1:1:1:1"], ans: 2, exp: "9:3:3:1 phenotypic ratio in F2 dihybrid cross: 9 round yellow : 3 round green : 3 wrinkled yellow : 1 wrinkled green. (9 R_Y_ : 3 R_yy : 3 rrY_ : 1 rryy)." },
    { q: "An organism with two identical alleles for a trait is called:", opts: ["Heterozygous", "Homozygous", "Hemizygous", "Monozygous"], ans: 1, exp: "Homozygous: two identical alleles (TT or tt). Heterozygous: two different alleles (Tt). Homozygous dominant (TT) and homozygous recessive (tt) breed true." },
    { q: "Haemophilia is an X-linked recessive disorder. A carrier mother (X^H X^h) × normal father (X^H Y) gives probability of affected son:", opts: ["0%", "25%", "50% of sons", "100% of sons"], ans: 2, exp: "Carrier mother: X^H X^h. Father: X^H Y. Sons receive Y from father + X from mother. Probability of son getting X^h = 50% → 50% of sons are affected. Daughters can be carriers." },
    { q: "DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is found mainly in the:", opts: ["Cytoplasm", "Nucleus (chromosomes) + mitochondria and chloroplasts", "Ribosome", "Cell membrane"], ans: 1, exp: "Nuclear DNA: main genetic material in chromosomes. Also in mitochondria (maternal inheritance) and chloroplasts. Ribosomes contain RNA. Cell membrane contains no DNA." },
    { q: "A gene is best defined as:", opts: ["A chromosome segment", "A segment of DNA that codes for a specific protein/trait", "A type of protein", "An RNA molecule"], ans: 1, exp: "Gene: a specific sequence of DNA (nucleotide bases) that encodes information for making a specific protein or RNA, determining one or more traits. \"One gene – one enzyme\" hypothesis (Beadle & Tatum)." },
    { q: "Chromosomes are composed of:", opts: ["Only DNA", "Only protein", "DNA + histone proteins (chromatin)", "RNA + lipids"], ans: 2, exp: "Chromosomes: DNA double helix wound around histone proteins → nucleosomes → chromatin fibre → chromosome. During cell division, chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes." },
    { q: "Down syndrome (Trisomy 21) is caused by:", opts: ["Deletion of chromosome 21", "Extra copy of chromosome 21 (47 chromosomes total)", "Inversion of chromosome 21", "Loss of Y chromosome"], ans: 1, exp: "Down syndrome: non-disjunction of chromosome 21 during meiosis → gamete with 2 copies of ch21 → zygote with 3 copies (trisomy 21) → 47 chromosomes. Features: intellectual disability, flat face." },
    { q: "What is the full form of DNA?", opts: ["Deoxyribonucleic Acid", "Deoxyribose Nucleic Acid", "Di-nitrogen Acid", "Deoxyribose Nucleotide Acid"], ans: 0, exp: "DNA = Deoxyribonucleic Acid. Contains: deoxyribose sugar, phosphate, nitrogenous bases (A, T, G, C). Double helix structure discovered by Watson and Crick (1953), based on X-ray data of Franklin." },
    { q: "According to Darwin, evolution occurs through:", opts: ["Use and disuse of organs", "Natural selection acting on inherited variations", "Genetic mutations only", "Directed mutation"], ans: 1, exp: "Darwin's natural selection: random heritable variations → some give better survival/reproduction (fitness) → those individuals survive and reproduce more → traits spread through population over generations." },
    { q: "Lamarck's theory of evolution was based on:", opts: ["Natural selection", "Inheritance of acquired characters (use and disuse)", "Genetic mutations", "Isolation"], ans: 1, exp: "Lamarckism: \"use and disuse\" — organs used more develop, unused shrink; acquired traits pass to offspring. Example: giraffe stretching neck → longer neck inherited. Now disproven (can't inherit acquired traits)." },
    { q: "Analogous organs are those that:", opts: ["Have same structure and origin (common ancestor)", "Have different structure but similar function (convergent evolution)", "Are vestigial remains", "Are found only in plants"], ans: 1, exp: "Analogous organs: similar function, different evolutionary origin. Wing of butterfly (modified cuticle) vs wing of bird (modified forelimb). Show convergent evolution (similar environments → similar adaptations)." },
    { q: "Homologous organs are those that:", opts: ["Look identical and do same function", "Have same basic structure and origin but may serve different functions (divergent evolution)", "Are found only in primitive animals", "Are no longer functional"], ans: 1, exp: "Homologous organs: same basic structure/embryonic origin, different functions. Forelimbs of horse, whale, bat, human — all modified from same ancestral tetrapod limb. Show divergent evolution." },
    { q: "Vestigial organs are:", opts: ["Organs in early development", "Reduced, non-functional organs inherited from ancestors (evidence of evolution)", "Organs in embryos only", "Organs that are extra-functional"], ans: 1, exp: "Vestigial organs: functionless remnants of organs that were functional in ancestors. Human examples: appendix, coccyx (tailbone), wisdom teeth, arrector pili muscles (goosebumps). Evidence for evolution." },
    { q: "The concept of \"survival of the fittest\" was coined by:", opts: ["Charles Darwin", "Herbert Spencer (later used by Darwin)", "Jean-Baptiste Lamarck", "Gregor Mendel"], ans: 1, exp: "\"Survival of the fittest\" was coined by Herbert Spencer after reading Darwin's On the Origin of Species (1859). Darwin himself used \"natural selection.\" Spencer applied it to social theory." },
    { q: "Speciation is the process of:", opts: ["One species dying out", "Formation of new species from existing populations (usually via reproductive isolation)", "Individuals migrating", "Genetic recombination only"], ans: 1, exp: "Speciation: new species formed when populations become reproductively isolated → accumulate different mutations/adaptations → eventually cannot interbreed. Geographical isolation often starts the process." },
    { q: "The fossil record provides evidence for evolution because:", opts: ["Fossils show no change over time", "Fossils show gradual changes in organisms over geological time, with older fossils being more primitive", "All fossils look the same", "Fossils only prove that species don't change"], ans: 1, exp: "Fossil record: older (deeper) rock layers have simpler/more primitive organisms; newer layers show more complex, modern forms. Transitional fossils show gradual anatomical changes over millions of years." },
    { q: "Carbon-14 (radiocarbon) dating is used to date fossils that are:", opts: ["Over 1 billion years old", "Up to ~50,000 years old", "Only dinosaur fossils", "Rock layers only"], ans: 1, exp: "C-14 (half-life ~5,730 years) is useful for dating organic material up to ~50,000 years. For older fossils: uranium-lead (4.5 billion years half-life) or potassium-argon dating." },
    { q: "Evolution is a change in the _____ of a population over generations.", opts: ["Phenotype", "Allele/gene frequencies (heritable genetic composition)", "Environment", "Body size only"], ans: 1, exp: "Evolution is defined as a change in allele frequencies in a population over time across generations. It acts on populations, not individuals. Individual organisms do not evolve." },
    { q: "Which type of mutation is likely to have the MOST significant effect on natural selection?", opts: ["Somatic mutations (in body cells)", "Germline mutations (in gametes — heritable)", "Point mutations (single base change)", "Silent mutations"], ans: 1, exp: "Germline mutations occur in gametes (egg/sperm) → passed to offspring → heritable → can be subject to natural selection. Somatic mutations affect only that individual and are not inherited." },
    { q: "Sex-linked traits in humans are carried on the:", opts: ["Autosomes (ch 1-22)", "X or Y chromosome", "Both X and Y equally", "Mitochondrial DNA only"], ans: 1, exp: "Sex-linked traits are on X or Y chromosome. Most sex-linked traits are X-linked (e.g., haemophilia, colour blindness). Y-linked traits (holandric): passed only from father to all sons." },
    { q: "Colour blindness (red-green) is an X-linked recessive condition. An affected father (X^b Y) × normal mother (X^B X^B) → children:", opts: ["All affected", "All daughters are carriers, sons are unaffected", "All sons are affected", "No one is affected"], ans: 1, exp: "Affected father X^b Y × normal mother X^B X^B: daughters get X^b from dad + X^B from mom = X^B X^b (all daughters are carriers). Sons get Y from dad + X^B from mom = X^B Y (all normal)." },
    { q: "Which famous experiment showed that genes (DNA) could transform bacteria (proving DNA is the genetic material)?", opts: ["Miller-Urey experiment", "Griffith's transformation experiment (and Avery-MacLeod-McCarty)", "Mendel's pea experiments", "Hershey-Chase experiment"], ans: 1, exp: "Griffith (1928): heat-killed virulent bacteria + live non-virulent bacteria → live virulent bacteria (transformation). Avery et al. (1944) showed DNA was the transforming principle. Hershey-Chase (1952) confirmed DNA." },
    { q: "The theory of evolution by common descent suggests:", opts: ["Each species was created separately", "All life shares a common ancestor and is related through evolutionary history", "Simple organisms cannot evolve into complex ones", "Evolution is random with no pattern"], ans: 1, exp: "Common descent: all life on Earth shares a universal common ancestor (LUCA — Last Universal Common Ancestor) ~3.8 billion years ago. Supported by shared genetic code, biochemistry, anatomy." },
    { q: "Geographic isolation leads to speciation by:", opts: ["Directly causing mutations", "Separating populations → independent evolution → reproductive incompatibility over time", "Reducing genetic diversity", "Causing extinction"], ans: 1, exp: "Allopatric speciation: geographic barrier (river, mountain) separates population → two populations evolve independently → accumulate different mutations/adaptations → no longer able to interbreed = new species." },
    { q: "The Hardy-Weinberg principle states that allele frequencies in a population remain constant if:", opts: ["Mutations occur frequently", "There is random mating, no mutation, no selection, no migration, large population size", "Natural selection is acting", "The population is small"], ans: 1, exp: "Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium: no evolution occurs (allele frequencies stable) when: no mutation, random mating, no selection, no migration, large population. Violations = evolutionary forces acting." },
    { q: "Which of the following is evidence of molecular evolution?", opts: ["Similar bone structure in vertebrates", "Nearly identical cytochrome c protein sequence across many species", "Same habitat preferences", "Similar body size"], ans: 1, exp: "Molecular evidence: conserved proteins like cytochrome c are nearly identical across vastly different species (human, yeast, bacteria) → all life shares a common ancestor. The more similar, the more closely related." },
    { q: "A mutation that changes a codon from one amino acid to the SAME amino acid is called:", opts: ["Missense mutation", "Nonsense mutation", "Silent (synonymous) mutation", "Frameshift mutation"], ans: 2, exp: "Silent mutation: nucleotide change → different codon but codes for same amino acid (due to genetic code degeneracy). No change in protein sequence → usually no phenotypic effect." },
    { q: "The ABO blood group system is an example of:", opts: ["Complete dominance", "Codominance (A and B are codominant; O is recessive)", "Incomplete dominance", "Epistasis"], ans: 1, exp: "ABO blood groups: I^A and I^B alleles are codominant (both expressed in type AB). I^O is recessive. Blood type AB shows both A and B antigens on RBCs — codominance." },
    { q: "In incomplete dominance, heterozygous (Rr) offspring show:", opts: ["Same as dominant parent (RR)", "Same as recessive parent (rr)", "Intermediate phenotype between both parents", "No phenotype at all"], ans: 2, exp: "Incomplete dominance: Rr = intermediate phenotype (e.g., red × white snapdragons → pink Rr offspring). Neither allele fully dominates. F2 ratio: 1 red : 2 pink : 1 white." },
    { q: "Genetic engineering uses which enzyme to cut DNA at specific sequences?", opts: ["DNA polymerase", "Ligase", "Restriction endonuclease", "Helicase"], ans: 2, exp: "Restriction endonucleases (restriction enzymes): bacterial enzymes that cut double-stranded DNA at specific palindromic sequences. Used in recombinant DNA technology, PCR, gene cloning." },
    { q: "Pleiotropy means one gene:", opts: ["Has two alleles", "Controls two different genes", "Affects multiple phenotypic traits", "Is located on sex chromosome"], ans: 2, exp: "Pleiotropy: a single gene affects multiple, apparently unrelated traits. Example: sickle cell anaemia gene (HBB) → abnormal haemoglobin → anaemia, heart failure, kidney damage, bone pain." },
    { q: "Who proposed the Central Dogma of molecular biology?", opts: ["Gregor Mendel", "Charles Darwin", "Francis Crick", "James Watson"], ans: 2, exp: "Francis Crick (1958) proposed the Central Dogma: DNA → RNA → Protein (replication → transcription → translation). Information flows one way from DNA to protein." },
    { q: "What is a test cross used for?", opts: ["Determining if an organism is diploid or haploid", "Determining the unknown genotype of a dominant phenotype organism (cross with homozygous recessive)", "Producing F2 generation", "Counting chromosomes"], ans: 1, exp: "Test cross: unknown dominant phenotype × homozygous recessive (tt). If all offspring are dominant → unknown is TT. If 50% dominant : 50% recessive → unknown is Tt." },
    { q: "Which of the following is NOT a source of genetic variation?", opts: ["Mutation", "Meiotic recombination (crossing over)", "Mitosis", "Random fertilisation"], ans: 2, exp: "Mitosis produces genetically identical cells (no variation). Sources of variation: mutation (new alleles), crossing over in meiosis (new combinations), independent assortment, random fertilisation." },
    { q: "Turner syndrome (45,X) affects females because they have:", opts: ["Extra X chromosome", "Only one X chromosome (no second sex chromosome)", "Two Y chromosomes", "Three X chromosomes"], ans: 1, exp: "Turner syndrome (45,X0): female with only one X chromosome (non-disjunction). Features: short stature, infertile (streak ovaries), no secondary sexual characteristics. 1 in 2,500 female births." },
    { q: "Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) affects males because they have:", opts: ["Missing Y chromosome", "Extra X chromosome (XXY) in addition to Y", "Two Y chromosomes", "Missing autosomes"], ans: 1, exp: "Klinefelter (47,XXY): male with extra X. Features: tall, small testes, infertile, gynecomastia (breast development). 1 in 1,000 males. Non-disjunction during meiosis." },
    { q: "Genetic drift causes changes in allele frequency in:", opts: ["Large populations", "Small isolated populations (by random chance, not natural selection)", "Only sexually reproducing populations", "Populations under selection pressure"], ans: 1, exp: "Genetic drift: random change in allele frequencies due to chance events in small populations. Can cause rare alleles to be lost or fixed. Two types: founder effect and bottleneck effect." },
    { q: "The founder effect is a type of genetic drift that occurs when:", opts: ["A large population splits in half", "A small group establishes a new population (carries only a subset of the original gene pool)", "A population undergoes mass extinction", "Multiple populations merge"], ans: 1, exp: "Founder effect: a small group (founders) colonises a new area. Their limited gene pool may have high frequency of rare alleles. Example: high rate of certain genetic diseases in Amish community (descended from few founders)." },
    { q: "The Miller-Urey experiment (1953) showed:", opts: ["DNA structure", "Evolution of multicellular life", "Simple organic molecules (amino acids) can form abiotically from inorganic chemicals", "Photosynthesis mechanism"], ans: 2, exp: "Miller-Urey: simulated early Earth conditions (CH₄, NH₃, H₂, H₂O, electrical sparks as lightning) → produced amino acids and other organic molecules. Supports chemical origin of life theory." },
    { q: "Convergent evolution results in organisms that:", opts: ["Share a recent common ancestor", "Look alike and have similar functions despite different ancestry (analogous structures)", "Have identical DNA sequences", "Are always the same species"], ans: 1, exp: "Convergent evolution: unrelated organisms develop similar traits (analogous structures) because they adapted to similar environments. Dolphin (mammal) and shark (fish) have similar body shapes." },
    { q: "Which of the following is the most recent common ancestor of humans and chimpanzees?", opts: ["Gorillas", "An ape-like ancestor approximately 6-7 million years ago", "Australopithecus", "Homo erectus"], ans: 1, exp: "Humans (Homo sapiens) and chimpanzees (Pan troglodytes) share a common ancestor ~6-7 million years ago. DNA similarity: ~98.7%. This confirms close evolutionary relationship." },
    { q: "In a cross between two parents, if the probability of each gamete type is equal, which Mendelian law predicts the gamete combinations?", opts: ["Law of Dominance", "Law of Segregation", "Law of Independent Assortment", "Law of Probability"], ans: 2, exp: "Law of Independent Assortment predicts that gametes from dihybrid (AaBb) will be AB, Ab, aB, ab in equal proportions (1:1:1:1). Combined with Punnett square analysis predicts offspring ratios." },
    { q: "The theory of abiogenesis (chemical evolution) proposes that life originated from:", opts: ["Living cells only", "Simple inorganic molecules forming organic molecules → complex molecules → protocells", "Extraterrestrial sources (panspermia)", "Spontaneous generation in any era"], ans: 1, exp: "Abiogenesis: chemical evolution theory. Inorganic molecules → simple organics (amino acids, nucleotides) → polymers → self-replicating molecules → protocells → primitive life. Miller-Urey supports step 1." },
    { q: "Which of the following is a polygenic trait in humans?", opts: ["ABO blood group (single gene)", "Skin colour (multiple genes contribute)", "Haemophilia (X-linked)", "Sickle cell anaemia (single gene)"], ans: 1, exp: "Polygenic traits: controlled by multiple genes, each contributing a small effect → continuous variation. Examples: human height, skin colour, intelligence, weight. Distribution is bell-shaped (normal distribution)." },
    { q: "An example of co-evolution is:", opts: ["Two unrelated species becoming similar", "Flowers and their pollinators evolving together (mutualistic evolutionary relationship)", "A single species evolving over time", "Geographic isolation causing speciation"], ans: 1, exp: "Co-evolution: two or more species exert selective pressure on each other → evolve together. Flowering plants and pollinators: flower shape/colour evolves to attract specific pollinators; pollinators evolve to access specific flowers." },
    { q: "In Mendel's monohybrid cross (Tt × Tt), the genotypic ratio in F2 is 1:2:1. If TT and Tt both show the tall phenotype, but a researcher doing a test cross (F2 tall × tt) finds that 1/3 of the \"tall\" plants give ONLY tall offspring, this result identifies those plants as:", opts: ["Tt (heterozygous)", "TT (homozygous dominant) — all offspring from TT × tt are Tt (tall)", "tt (homozygous recessive)", "A new mutation"], ans: 1, exp: "Test cross of tall F2 plants × tt: TT × tt → all Tt (100% tall offspring). Tt × tt → 50% Tt (tall) + 50% tt (short). Since TT makes up 1/3 of tall plants (ratio TT:Tt = 1:2), 1/3 give only tall offspring (TT). The test cross is the definitive tool for determining unknown genotype." },
    { q: "In a dihybrid cross RrYy × rryy (test cross), which phenotypic ratio is expected?", opts: ["9:3:3:1", "3:1", "1:1:1:1 (round yellow : round green : wrinkled yellow : wrinkled green)", "1:2:1"], ans: 2, exp: "RrYy × rryy: RrYy produces 4 gamete types (RY, Ry, rY, ry) in equal proportions. rryy produces only ry. The 4 offspring types are RrYy (round yellow) : Rryy (round green) : rrYy (wrinkled yellow) : rryy (wrinkled green) in 1:1:1:1 ratio. This is the standard dihybrid test cross outcome, demonstrating independent assortment." },
    { q: "Mendel chose pea plants (Pisum sativum) for his experiments. One key reason was that pea plants have contrasting, clearly distinguishable traits. An equally important practical reason was:", opts: ["Pea plants reproduce very slowly", "Peas are naturally self-fertilising (cleistogamy), allowing Mendel to maintain pure-breeding lines, and also cross-pollinate them manually when desired", "Pea plants have only 2 chromosomes", "Peas have no flowers, simplifying observation"], ans: 1, exp: "Pea plants are normally self-fertilising (cleistogamous — flowers fertilise before opening), making it easy to maintain true-breeding lines. Mendel could remove anthers from flowers before they opened and manually cross-pollinate (emasculation + artificial cross), giving him complete experimental control over mating." },
    { q: "A couple both have normal vision. They have a son with red-green colour blindness (X-linked recessive). What is the probability that their next child will be a colour-blind daughter?", opts: ["25%", "0% — daughters cannot be colour blind if father is normal", "12.5% (1 in 8)", "50%"], ans: 1, exp: "Mother is a carrier (X^B X^b); father is normal (X^B Y). Sons: 50% colour blind (X^b Y). Daughters: X^B X^B (normal) or X^B X^b (carrier), none affected (X^b X^b would require affected father). Since father is normal (X^B Y), daughters get X^B from father → cannot be X^b X^b. P(colour-blind daughter) = 0%." },
    { q: "If a woman is colour blind (X^b X^b) and she has children with a normal-visioned man (X^B Y), what fraction of her DAUGHTERS will be colour blind?", opts: ["None", "50% of daughters", "All daughters (100%)", "25% of daughters"], ans: 0, exp: "Colour-blind mother X^b X^b × normal father X^B Y: daughters receive X^b from mother + X^B from father = X^B X^b (all daughters are carriers, NOT colour blind). Sons receive X^b from mother + Y from father = X^b Y (all sons are colour blind). Zero daughters are colour blind." },
    { q: "A man with blood type AB (I^A I^B) and a woman with blood type O (ii) have children. What blood types are possible in their children?", opts: ["AB and O only", "A and B only (50% type A, 50% type B)", "A, B, AB, and O", "AB only"], ans: 1, exp: "I^A I^B × ii: gametes from father are I^A or I^B; gametes from mother are i only. Possible offspring: I^A i (blood type A) and I^B i (blood type B) in 1:1 ratio. AB (I^A I^B) and O (ii) are NOT possible from this cross." },
    { q: "In a pedigree, a trait appears in every generation (grandfather, mother, and her son), with both males and females affected. Unaffected parents never have affected children. This inheritance pattern is most consistent with:", opts: ["Autosomal recessive", "X-linked recessive", "Autosomal dominant", "Y-linked (holandric)"], ans: 2, exp: "Autosomal dominant: trait appears in every generation (vertical transmission), affects both sexes equally, and unaffected individuals (homozygous recessive) do NOT pass the trait. If both males and females are affected across generations, Y-linkage (males only) and X-linkage (patterns differ by sex) are excluded." },
    { q: "A pedigree shows a trait that appears ONLY in males, and ALL sons of affected fathers are affected, but NO daughters are affected. This suggests:", opts: ["X-linked recessive", "Autosomal dominant (sex-limited expression)", "Y-linked (holandric) inheritance", "Mitochondrial inheritance"], ans: 2, exp: "Y-linked (holandric) traits: Y chromosome carries the gene → father passes Y to all sons → 100% of sons affected. Daughters get X from father (not Y) → never affected. Hairy pinna (outer ear hair) was a classic example (now disputed). Absolute father-to-son transmission distinguishes Y-linkage from all other patterns." },
    { q: "In incomplete dominance, a cross between pink (Rr) × pink (Rr) snapdragons produces what phenotypic ratio?", opts: ["3 red : 1 white", "1 red : 2 pink : 1 white", "All pink", "1 red : 1 white"], ans: 1, exp: "Incomplete dominance: RR = red, Rr = pink (intermediate), rr = white. Rr × Rr → 1 RR : 2 Rr : 1 rr = 1 red : 2 pink : 1 white. The phenotypic and genotypic ratios are both 1:2:1 (unlike complete dominance where phenotypic is 3:1)." },
    { q: "Codominance differs from incomplete dominance in that in codominance:", opts: ["A blend of both traits appears in the heterozygote", "BOTH alleles are fully and separately expressed in the heterozygote (no blending)", "One allele is completely dominant", "The heterozygote shows neither parental phenotype"], ans: 1, exp: "Incomplete dominance: blended phenotype (e.g., red + white = pink). Codominance: both alleles expressed simultaneously and distinctly (e.g., type AB blood: both A and B antigens present on RBCs; roan cattle: both red and white hairs present — no blending, just both together)." },
    { q: "Epistasis is a phenomenon where one gene MASKS or modifies the expression of another gene. In a 9:3:3:1 dihybrid cross, if one dominant allele (A_) masks expression of the B gene, the ratio becomes:", opts: ["9:3:3:1", "12:3:1 (dominant epistasis)", "9:7", "15:1"], ans: 1, exp: "Dominant epistasis (e.g., gene A epistatic to gene B): A_ suppresses expression of B gene. 9 A_B_ + 3 A_bb = 12 (show A phenotype, B masked). 3 aaB_ = 3 (B expressed when A absent). 1 aabb = 1 (recessive). Ratio 12:3:1. Other epistasis types give 9:3:4, 9:7, 15:1, etc." },
    { q: "Sickle cell anaemia is caused by a SINGLE nucleotide change in the β-globin gene (HBB), converting glutamic acid → valine at position 6. The inheritance pattern is:", opts: ["Autosomal dominant (one copy causes disease)", "Autosomal recessive (two copies needed for full anaemia, but carriers show sickling under low oxygen — codominant at molecular level)", "X-linked dominant", "Mitochondrial"], ans: 1, exp: "Sickle cell anaemia: classically autosomal recessive (two HbS alleles → severe anaemia). However, at the molecular/cellular level it shows codominance: HbA/HbS carriers (sickle cell trait) have BOTH normal and sickle haemoglobin in their RBCs, showing mild sickling under extreme low-oxygen conditions. Clinically recessive, molecularly codominant." },
    { q: "The Hardy-Weinberg equation is p² + 2pq + q² = 1. In a population of 1000, if 360 individuals are homozygous dominant (AA), 480 are heterozygous (Aa), and 160 are homozygous recessive (aa), calculate the allele frequency of the recessive allele (q):", opts: ["0.4", "0.6", "0.16", "0.32"], ans: 0, exp: "Total alleles = 2000. Recessive alleles (q): from aa: 160×2 = 320; from Aa: 480×1 = 480. Total q alleles = 800. q = 800/2000 = 0.4. Check: q² = 0.16 = 160/1000 ✓. p = 0.6. p² = 0.36 = 360/1000 ✓. 2pq = 0.48 = 480/1000 ✓. Population is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium." },
    { q: "If the frequency of a recessive allele (q) in a Hardy-Weinberg population is 0.2, what is the frequency of carriers (heterozygotes)?", opts: ["0.04", "0.32", "0.16", "0.64"], ans: 1, exp: "p = 1 − q = 0.8. Frequency of carriers (Aa) = 2pq = 2 × 0.8 × 0.2 = 0.32 (32% of population). Note: q² = 0.04 (frequency of affected, homozygous recessive). 2pq is ALWAYS higher than q² when q < 0.5 — most recessive alleles in a population are \"hidden\" in carriers." },
    { q: "Which of the following situations would VIOLATE Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium and cause evolutionary change?", opts: ["Large random-mating population with no external selective pressure", "A natural disaster kills 99% of the population, leaving a few survivors (bottleneck effect)", "Equal sex ratio and random mating", "Absence of migration and mutation"], ans: 1, exp: "A population bottleneck drastically reduces population size → genetic drift becomes powerful → allele frequencies change by chance, not selection → departure from H-W equilibrium. The 5 conditions for H-W equilibrium are: large population, random mating, no mutation, no migration, no natural selection." },
    { q: "In the Hershey-Chase experiment (1952), bacteriophage T4 was grown with radioactive ³²P (labels DNA) and ³⁵S (labels protein). After infection of bacteria, the ³²P was found INSIDE the bacteria. This proved:", opts: ["Protein is the genetic material", "DNA is the genetic material (DNA, not protein, enters the cell and directs new phage production)", "RNA is the genetic material", "Both DNA and protein enter the cell"], ans: 1, exp: "In the Hershey-Chase experiment, radioactive ³²P (DNA) was found inside infected bacteria (and in new phage progeny), while ³⁵S (protein) remained outside in the viral coat. Since ³²P directed new phage production, DNA was conclusively proven to be the genetic material, not protein." },
    { q: "A human genetic disease shows the following pedigree pattern: only females are affected; affected females always have affected mothers; the trait appears in every generation on the maternal line; affected fathers do NOT pass the trait to children. This pattern strongly suggests:", opts: ["Autosomal dominant", "X-linked dominant", "Mitochondrial (maternal) inheritance", "Y-linked"], ans: 2, exp: "Mitochondrial inheritance: mitochondria are inherited exclusively from the mother (cytoplasmic inheritance). Affected mothers pass the disease to ALL children (sons and daughters), but affected fathers NEVER pass it to any child. Leber's hereditary optic neuropathy (LHON) and MELAS syndrome follow mitochondrial inheritance." },
    { q: "Homologous chromosomes pair during meiosis I (synapsis). Crossing over (chiasmata formation) during prophase I results in:", opts: ["Identical sister chromatids", "New combinations of alleles on chromosomes (recombinant chromosomes), increasing genetic diversity", "Doubling of chromosome number", "Chromosomal deletion only"], ans: 1, exp: "Crossing over: homologous chromosomes exchange segments at chiasmata during prophase I of meiosis. This creates recombinant chromosomes with new combinations of alleles not present in either parent. It is a major source of genetic variation (along with independent assortment and random fertilisation)." },
    { q: "Darwin observed that finches on the Galápagos Islands had different beak shapes adapted to different food sources. This is an example of:", opts: ["Convergent evolution (similar environments, different species)", "Adaptive radiation (divergent evolution from a common ancestor into different ecological niches)", "Co-evolution", "Genetic drift in isolated populations"], ans: 1, exp: "Adaptive radiation: a single ancestral finch colonised the Galápagos. In the absence of competition, different populations adapted to different food sources (seeds, insects, cactus) → natural selection shaped different beak morphologies. This divergent evolution from one ancestor into multiple ecological niches is adaptive radiation (also seen in Australian marsupials, Hawaiian honeycreepers)." },
    { q: "Lamarck proposed that the giraffe's long neck evolved because ancestral giraffes stretched their necks and passed this acquired trait to offspring. The FUNDAMENTAL error in Lamarck's theory is:", opts: ["Giraffes never ate from tall trees", "Acquired (somatic) characteristics cannot be inherited — changes to body (muscle, structure) do not alter germline DNA", "Natural selection does not exist", "Lamarck did not know about DNA, so his theory is entirely wrong"], ans: 1, exp: "Lamarck's fatal flaw: the Weismann barrier. Germline cells (gametes) are separate from somatic (body) cells. Changes to muscles, bones, or organs during an organism's lifetime do NOT alter the DNA in gametes and cannot be passed to offspring. Modern genetics confirms this (epigenetics has nuanced exceptions but does not rescue Lamarckism). Weismann's experiments cutting rat tails for 22 generations showed no shortening." },
    { q: "The forelimbs of a whale (flipper), a bat (wing), a human (arm), and a horse (leg) all have the same basic skeletal elements (humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, digits). These are:", opts: ["Analogous organs (same function, different origin)", "Homologous organs (same basic structure and common evolutionary origin, different functions)", "Vestigial organs", "Convergent structures"], ans: 1, exp: "Homologous organs share the same basic bone structure (pentadactyl limb) inherited from a common tetrapod ancestor (~370 MYA), even though they perform different functions (swimming, flying, grasping, walking). They are evidence for divergent evolution and common descent. Analogous organs have similar functions but different origins (e.g., wing of bat vs wing of butterfly)." },
    { q: "The human appendix is considered a vestigial organ. This means:", opts: ["It is non-functional in all organisms", "It is a reduced remnant of a larger, functional organ in our ancestors (caecum for cellulose digestion), retained without its original major function", "It was added recently by evolution", "It will disappear within one generation"], ans: 1, exp: "The appendix is a vestige of the enlarged caecum of herbivorous ancestors that housed bacteria for cellulose fermentation. In humans, the caecum is reduced and the appendix (vermiform) is a tiny remnant. It retains some lymphoid tissue (minor immune function) but has lost its primary ancestral digestive function. Vestigial organs are evidence of evolutionary ancestry." },
    { q: "Fossils in which geological time period would most likely represent the earliest fish-to-tetrapod transition (vertebrates moving from water to land)?", opts: ["Cambrian (~540 MYA)", "Devonian (~375 MYA) — transitional fossils like Tiktaalik", "Cretaceous (~66-145 MYA)", "Permian (~252-299 MYA)"], ans: 1, exp: "Tiktaalik roseae (Devonian, ~375 MYA) is a classic transitional fossil showing fish-to-tetrapod evolution: it had gills AND primitive lung-like organs, fish-like scales AND proto-limb-like fins with wrist joints capable of supporting weight. The Devonian period is the key era for fish diversification and the first vertebrate land colonisation." },
    { q: "The molecular clock hypothesis, used to estimate divergence times between species, is based on the observation that:", opts: ["DNA mutations accumulate rapidly in highly variable genes", "Certain genes accumulate mutations at an approximately constant rate over time, allowing divergence time estimation from molecular differences", "All genes mutate at the same rate", "Fossils always provide accurate dating"], ans: 1, exp: "Molecular clock: neutral mutations in conserved genes (like cytochrome c or rRNA) accumulate at a roughly constant rate over geological time. By comparing the degree of molecular difference between two species and knowing the mutation rate, scientists can estimate WHEN they diverged (calibrated with fossil dates). Used in human evolution studies (mitochondrial Eve, ~150,000 years ago)." },
    { q: "Gene flow between populations tends to:", opts: ["Increase genetic differences between populations", "Decrease genetic differences between populations by introducing alleles from one population into another", "Cause speciation directly", "Always reduce genetic diversity in recipient population"], ans: 1, exp: "Gene flow (migration): individuals move between populations and reproduce, transferring alleles. This homogenises allele frequencies between populations — reducing genetic divergence. Gene flow opposes speciation. In contrast, geographic isolation prevents gene flow → populations diverge → speciation. Extensive gene flow between two groups maintains them as one genetic unit." },
    { q: "Natural selection acts on:", opts: ["Individual genotype directly", "Individual phenotype — individuals with favourable phenotypes survive/reproduce more, causing allele frequency changes at the population level", "The entire species at once", "DNA sequences directly without phenotypic expression"], ans: 1, exp: "Natural selection \"sees\" phenotype (observable traits), not genotype directly. A favourable phenotype (caused by advantageous alleles) leads to greater survival and reproduction → those alleles become more frequent in the next generation. Selection cannot act on recessive alleles hidden in heterozygotes — they are invisible to selection." },
    { q: "The bottleneck effect is a type of genetic drift that occurs when:", opts: ["A small founder group colonises a new area", "A population is drastically reduced in size by a catastrophic event, losing most genetic diversity", "Two populations merge", "A single individual has many offspring"], ans: 1, exp: "Population bottleneck: a catastrophe (disease, disaster) drastically reduces population size. The surviving gene pool is a random, unrepresentative sample of the original → some alleles are lost forever, others become fixed by chance (not selection). Example: cheetah genetic uniformity (past bottleneck ~10,000 years ago). Distinguished from founder effect (voluntary colonisation of new habitat)." },
    { q: "Speciation by polyploidy (increase in chromosome sets) is most common in:", opts: ["Animals (especially vertebrates)", "Plants — many crop species (wheat, cotton, banana) are ancient polyploids", "Bacteria", "Fungi only"], ans: 1, exp: "Polyploidy speciation: common in plants, rare in animals. Example: bread wheat (Triticum aestivum) is hexaploid (6n = 42 chromosomes), formed by hybridisation of 3 different diploid grass species. Polyploids are reproductively isolated from their parent species (different chromosome numbers → sterile hybrids if mated) — instant speciation." },
    { q: "Which form of natural selection increases the frequency of individuals with EXTREME phenotypes and reduces those with intermediate phenotypes?", opts: ["Stabilising selection", "Directional selection", "Disruptive (diversifying) selection", "Sexual selection"], ans: 2, exp: "Disruptive (diversifying) selection: favours two extreme phenotypes over the intermediate. Example: black and white oyster catchers survive better than intermediate-coloured ones in a patchwork habitat. Over time, this can split one population into two distinct groups, potentially leading to speciation. Stabilising: favours intermediate. Directional: favours one extreme." },
    { q: "Australopithecus afarensis (\"Lucy\") is significant in human evolution because:", opts: ["It was the first Homo species with a large brain", "It demonstrated bipedalism (upright walking) while retaining small brain size (~400-550 cc), showing bipedality preceded brain expansion in human evolution", "It used complex stone tools", "It was the first hominid to use fire"], ans: 1, exp: "Lucy (A. afarensis, ~3.2 MYA) had a chimp-sized brain (~400-550 cc) but fossilised pelvis and knee joints confirm bipedal locomotion. This overturned earlier assumptions that large brain evolved first. The sequence was: bipedalism → free hands → tool use → further brain expansion (in Homo). Fire use: Homo erectus (~1 MYA)." },
    { q: "Darwin's theory of evolution requires THREE conditions to hold for natural selection to operate. Which set correctly states all three?", opts: ["Variation, Heritability, Differential survival/reproduction (fitness differences)", "Variation, Mutation, Large population size", "Heritability, Random mating, Geographic isolation", "Mutation, Selection pressure, Time"], ans: 0, exp: "For natural selection: (1) Variation — individuals differ in traits. (2) Heritability — traits are inherited by offspring. (3) Differential reproduction — variation affects survival/reproduction (fitness). If all three conditions are met, natural selection INEVITABLY occurs. Darwin did not know about DNA/genes but correctly identified these logical requirements." },
    { q: "In a dominant lethal condition where the heterozygous state is lethal before reproductive age, what is the long-term fate of that dominant allele in the population?", opts: ["It increases because dominant alleles spread faster", "It is rapidly eliminated because affected individuals die before reproducing, preventing the allele from being passed on", "It remains at a constant level through mutation-selection balance", "It becomes recessive over time"], ans: 1, exp: "A dominant lethal allele (kills heterozygotes before reproduction) is under intense negative selection pressure. Affected individuals (Aa) never reproduce → the allele is not transmitted. The only source maintaining it is NEW mutations (de novo). It is rapidly eliminated each generation. Example: dominant lethal mutations causing early embryonic death." },
    { q: "In a cross AaBb × AaBb, what is the probability of an offspring with phenotype A_bb (dominant A, recessive bb)?", opts: ["1/16", "3/16", "9/16", "1/4"], ans: 1, exp: "Apply independent assortment. P(A_) = 3/4. P(bb) = 1/4. If genes are on different chromosomes: P(A_bb) = 3/4 × 1/4 = 3/16. This corresponds to the 9:3:3:1 ratio where A_bb is one of the 3/16 classes (round green in Mendel's peas, if A = round, B = yellow)." },
    { q: "Two parents both with normal hearing (autosomal recessive deafness) have a deaf child. If they decide to have 4 more children, what is the probability that exactly 2 of the next 4 children will be deaf?", opts: ["25%", "21.1%", "50%", "6.25%"], ans: 1, exp: "Both parents are carriers (Dd × Dd): P(deaf, dd) = 1/4. Use binomial probability: P(exactly 2 deaf in 4 children) = C(4,2) × (1/4)² × (3/4)² = 6 × 1/16 × 9/16 = 54/256 ≈ 0.211 = 21.1%. This requires both the independent probability for each child AND combinatorial counting." },
    { q: "A woman is heterozygous for two X-linked genes A and B (X^{AB} / X^{ab}), meaning the alleles are in coupling (cis). If there is NO crossing over, what gametes does she produce?", opts: ["X^{AB}, X^{Ab}, X^{aB}, X^{ab} in equal proportions", "X^{AB} and X^{ab} in equal proportions (parental types only)", "X^{Ab} and X^{aB} only (recombinant types)", "All four types in 9:3:3:1 ratio"], ans: 1, exp: "If genes A and B are linked in coupling (AB/ab) on the X chromosome, and no crossing over occurs, only parental gametes are produced: X^{AB} and X^{ab} in 1:1 ratio. Recombinant gametes (X^{Ab} and X^{aB}) are produced only through crossing over between the two loci. The frequency of recombinants = recombination frequency (map distance in cM)." },
    { q: "The term \"gene pool\" refers to:", opts: ["The total genetic information in a single individual", "The total collection of all alleles of all genes present in all individuals of a population at a given time", "A laboratory collection of genes", "The gametes of one generation only"], ans: 1, exp: "Gene pool: the sum total of all alleles (for all loci) in all individuals of a population at a given time. Evolution is the change in the gene pool over time. A large, diverse gene pool provides more raw material for natural selection and increases a population's ability to adapt." },
    { q: "Analogous structures provide evidence for:", opts: ["Divergent evolution and common descent", "Convergent evolution — similar selective pressures drive similar adaptations in unrelated lineages", "The validity of Lamarckism", "Genetic drift in isolated populations"], ans: 1, exp: "Analogous structures (e.g., wings of birds vs insects, eyes of vertebrates vs octopus) arise independently in unrelated lineages — convergent evolution. Similar environments exert similar selection pressures, independently driving similar solutions. They do NOT indicate common ancestry. Homologous structures (same origin, different functions) indicate common ancestry and divergent evolution." },
    { q: "Which of the following provides the STRONGEST direct evidence for evolution?", opts: ["Biogeography (species distribution patterns)", "Comparative anatomy (homologous structures)", "Fossil record showing transitional forms", "Molecular genetics (shared DNA sequences)"], ans: 2, exp: "Transitional fossils provide the most direct, tangible evidence of evolutionary change over time — actual physical specimens showing intermediate forms between ancestor and descendant. Examples: Archaeopteryx (dinosaur-to-bird), Tiktaalik (fish-to-tetrapod), whale ancestors (Pakicetus, Ambulocetus). All other lines of evidence are circumstantial (though highly compelling and consistent)." },
    { q: "Genetic drift is most powerful (has the strongest evolutionary effect) in:", opts: ["Very large populations with high gene flow", "Small, isolated populations where random sampling errors cause large fluctuations in allele frequency", "Populations under intense natural selection", "Populations with high mutation rates"], ans: 1, exp: "Genetic drift: random change in allele frequency due to chance. In a large population, random sampling errors are small relative to total allele pool. In a small population (e.g., 10 individuals), losing one individual can eliminate alleles. Drift is proportional to 1/2N (N = population size) — the smaller N, the greater the drift effect." },
    { q: "In Mendel's experiment, when he crossed F1 dihybrids (RrYy × RrYy), he obtained a 9:3:3:1 ratio. If the two genes were completely LINKED (on the same chromosome, no crossing over), the expected F2 ratio would instead be:", opts: ["9:3:3:1 (unchanged)", "3:1 (only round yellow : wrinkled green)", "1:2:1", "1:1:1:1"], ans: 1, exp: "If two genes are completely linked (no crossing over), the RrYy parent produces only two gamete types: RY and ry (parental types). RY and ry gametes in 1:1 ratio → offspring are RRYY (round yellow) : RrYy (round yellow) : rryy (wrinkled green) in 1:2:1 → phenotypic ratio: 3 round yellow : 1 wrinkled green. No round green or wrinkled yellow appears. Mendel's 9:3:3:1 ratio depends on INDEPENDENT assortment (genes on different chromosomes)." },
    { q: "The \"Out of Africa\" theory of modern human origins proposes that:", opts: ["Homo sapiens evolved independently on each continent from local Homo erectus populations (multiregional hypothesis)", "All modern humans descend from a single African population of Homo sapiens that migrated out of Africa ~60,000-70,000 years ago, replacing other archaic hominids", "Humans originated in Asia and colonised Africa later", "Modern humans and Neanderthals have no shared ancestry"], ans: 1, exp: "The \"Out of Africa\" (single-origin) model is strongly supported by mitochondrial DNA analysis (mitochondrial Eve, ~150,000 years ago in Africa), Y-chromosome data, and genomic studies. Modern Homo sapiens emerged in Africa and migrated globally, with some interbreeding with Neanderthals and Denisovans. The multiregional hypothesis (parallel evolution) is now largely rejected." },
    { q: "A new antibiotic kills 99.9% of bacteria in a culture. The 0.1% that survive are likely to be:", opts: ["Weakened mutants that developed immunity through exposure", "Pre-existing resistant variants whose resistance was not caused by the antibiotic — natural selection amplified them", "Bacteria that \"learned\" resistance", "Bacteria that used Lamarckian mechanisms to adapt"], ans: 1, exp: "Antibiotic resistance illustrates Darwinian natural selection (not Lamarckism). Rare resistant mutants exist in the population BEFORE antibiotic exposure (random mutation). The antibiotic kills sensitive bacteria, leaving resistant ones to reproduce → rapid selection. Antibiotics do not CAUSE mutations; they SELECT for pre-existing resistant variants. This is direct evidence for evolution by natural selection." },
    { q: "In a cross between two true-breeding varieties, if F1 shows a phenotype BEYOND the range of both parents (transgressive segregation), this is evidence of:", opts: ["X-linkage of the gene", "Polygenic inheritance — multiple genes with additive effects, and recombination in F2 creates extreme combinations", "Dominance of one allele", "Epistasis always producing extreme phenotypes"], ans: 1, exp: "Transgressive segregation: F2 individuals show phenotypes more extreme than either parent. This occurs with polygenic traits: each parent is homozygous for different alleles at different loci. Recombination in F1 × F1 produces offspring that combine extreme alleles from both parents, exceeding parental range. E.g., if parents are AAbb and aaBB, some F2 offspring can be AABB or aabb (more extreme than either parent)." },
    { q: "The sex of offspring in humans is determined by the father because:", opts: ["The mother always contributes X chromosome; only the father contributes X or Y", "The father's somatic cells determine sex", "The X chromosome is always from the father", "Sex is determined after fertilisation by maternal hormones"], ans: 0, exp: "Every human egg carries exactly one X chromosome (mother is XX — always contributes X). Father (XY) produces two types of sperm: X-bearing (→ daughter) and Y-bearing (→ son) in ~50:50 ratio. The sperm type that fertilises the egg determines the sex. The SRY gene on the Y chromosome triggers male development. Therefore, the father's sperm type is the determining factor." },
    { q: "Evidence from comparative embryology supports evolution because:", opts: ["All embryos look identical throughout development", "Distantly related vertebrates (fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds, mammals) share strikingly similar embryonic structures (gill slits, notochord, tail) that diverge later — reflecting shared ancestry", "Embryos are simpler organisms that can be studied more easily", "Embryology only shows similarities in adult organisms"], ans: 1, exp: "Ernst Haeckel's observation (though his drawings were exaggerated) that vertebrate embryos pass through similar developmental stages reflects shared evolutionary heritage (conserved developmental genes, Hox genes). Early embryos of fish, chickens, and humans all show gill pouches, notochords, and tails, reflecting their common vertebrate ancestor. These disappear as development proceeds along species-specific paths." },
    { q: "A population of moths in an industrial area shifted from predominantly light-coloured to predominantly dark-coloured after industrialisation darkened tree bark with soot. This is a real example of:", opts: ["Genetic drift in a large population", "Directional natural selection (industrial melanism) — peppered moth (Biston betularia)", "Founder effect", "Disruptive selection"], ans: 1, exp: "Industrial melanism in the peppered moth: before industrialisation, light moths were camouflaged on lichen-covered bark; dark (melanic) mutants were easily seen and eaten by birds. After industrialisation blackened bark, dark moths were camouflaged and light moths were preyed upon. Directional selection rapidly shifted the population toward dark phenotype. Classic demonstration of observable, rapid natural selection." },
    { q: "In Mendel's original 7 traits in pea plants, he was lucky that the genes assorted independently. We now know this is because:", opts: ["All 7 genes are on different chromosomes", "Some genes are on the same chromosomes but far enough apart (or on different chromosomes) that they appear to assort independently due to crossing over", "Peas have 7 chromosomes, one gene per chromosome", "Mendel selected traits specifically located on different chromosomes"], ans: 1, exp: "Pea plants have 7 pairs of chromosomes. Of Mendel's 7 traits, most happen to be on different chromosomes OR are far enough apart on the same chromosome that crossing over makes them appear to assort independently. Modern mapping has confirmed this fortunate coincidence — some of his gene pairs ARE on the same chromosome but unlinked due to distance. If he had chosen closely linked genes, he would not have observed 9:3:3:1 ratios." },
    { q: "A woman's karyotype shows 47 chromosomes: 47,XXX (Triple X syndrome). This condition most commonly arises from:", opts: ["Crossing over during meiosis", "Non-disjunction of sex chromosomes during meiosis in the mother — both X chromosomes fail to separate", "Non-disjunction in the father's meiosis", "A somatic mutation after fertilisation"], ans: 1, exp: "47,XXX arises when an egg receives two X chromosomes instead of one (non-disjunction in maternal meiosis I or II) and is fertilised by a normal X-bearing sperm. Most cases (>90%) result from maternal non-disjunction. The condition is usually mild, with most women being fertile. Maternal age is a risk factor." },
    { q: "The concept of \"punctuated equilibrium\" proposed by Gould and Eldredge challenges the classical Darwinian view by suggesting that:", opts: ["Evolution occurs at a completely constant, slow rate", "Long periods of stasis (little change) in species are punctuated by rapid bursts of evolutionary change (speciation events), often not gradual", "Evolution is directed toward a goal (orthogenesis)", "Genetic drift is more important than natural selection in all contexts"], ans: 1, exp: "Punctuated equilibrium (1972): the fossil record often shows species remaining unchanged (stasis) for millions of years, then rapidly changing (in geological time scales) at speciation events. This contrasts with gradualism (slow, continuous change). Rapid change during speciation may be driven by small, isolated populations undergoing strong selection. Both views (gradualism and punctuated equilibrium) may apply in different contexts." },
    { q: "What is the correct interpretation of \"survival of the fittest\" in evolutionary biology?", opts: ["The physically strongest organism always survives", "Fitness refers to reproductive success (leaving more offspring) — the \"fittest\" organism is the one best adapted to reproduce in its current environment", "Faster organisms always outcompete slower ones", "Fit organisms never die young"], ans: 1, exp: "\"Fitness\" in evolutionary biology = reproductive success = how many offspring an organism leaves in the next generation. A slow, small organism may be \"fitter\" than a large, fast one if it reproduces more successfully in its environment. Fitness is always relative to the specific environment. This is often misunderstood as \"strongest\" (social Darwinism fallacy)." }
  ]
,

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════
     CH 9 — LIGHT — REFLECTION AND REFRACTION
  ══════════════════════════════════════ */
  9: [
    { q: "The law of reflection states that the angle of incidence equals:", opts: ["The angle of refraction", "The angle of reflection", "90°", "Half the angle of incidence"], ans: 1, exp: "Law of reflection: ∠i = ∠r (angle of incidence = angle of reflection). Both angles measured from the normal at the point of incidence. Incident ray, reflected ray, and normal are coplanar." },
    { q: "A concave mirror is also called a:", opts: ["Diverging mirror", "Convex mirror", "Converging mirror", "Plane mirror"], ans: 2, exp: "Concave mirror: reflecting surface curves inward (like the inside of a bowl). It is a converging mirror — parallel rays reflect and converge at the focus. Used in torches, telescopes, shaving mirrors." },
    { q: "The focal length of a concave mirror of radius of curvature 30 cm is:", opts: ["30 cm", "60 cm", "15 cm", "10 cm"], ans: 2, exp: "f = R/2 = 30/2 = 15 cm. The focal point (F) is exactly halfway between the centre of curvature (C) and the pole (P) of the mirror." },
    { q: "A concave mirror forms a virtual, erect, and magnified image when the object is placed:", opts: ["At C", "Between F and pole (P)", "Beyond C", "At infinity"], ans: 1, exp: "Object between F and P: image is virtual, erect, magnified, behind the mirror. This is why concave mirrors are used as shaving/makeup mirrors (magnified upright image of face)." },
    { q: "Which mirror always forms a virtual, erect, and diminished image regardless of object position?", opts: ["Concave mirror", "Plane mirror", "Convex mirror", "Plano-convex mirror"], ans: 2, exp: "Convex mirror: diverging mirror. Always forms virtual, erect, diminished image (between P and F, behind mirror) for any object position. Wide field of view → used in rear-view mirrors and security mirrors." },
    { q: "The mirror formula is: 1/v + 1/u = 1/f. If object is at −30 cm, f = −15 cm (concave), image distance is:", opts: ["−30 cm", "+30 cm", "−10 cm", "+10 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/(−15) − 1/(−30) = −1/15 + 1/30 = −2/30 + 1/30 = −1/30. So v = −30 cm. Real, inverted image at same position as object (at C for concave mirror)." },
    { q: "When an object is placed at the focus (F) of a concave mirror, the image is formed:", opts: ["At the focus", "At C (radius of curvature)", "At infinity", "Between F and mirror"], ans: 2, exp: "Object at F of concave mirror → reflected rays are parallel → image at infinity. Used in torches/searchlights: bulb placed at focus → parallel beam of light emerges." },
    { q: "Magnification (m) of a mirror is defined as:", opts: ["m = f/v", "m = −v/u (ratio of image height to object height)", "m = u/f", "m = v/f"], ans: 1, exp: "m = h_i/h_o = −v/u. Negative m: real, inverted image. Positive m: virtual, erect image. |m| > 1: magnified. |m| < 1: diminished. |m| = 1: same size." },
    { q: "The speed of light in a medium is related to the refractive index by:", opts: ["n = c × v", "n = c/v (refractive index = speed in vacuum / speed in medium)", "n = v/c", "n = v + c"], ans: 1, exp: "Refractive index n = c/v, where c = speed of light in vacuum (3×10⁸ m/s), v = speed of light in the medium. Higher n → slower speed → more bending (greater refraction)." },
    { q: "When light goes from a rarer medium to a denser medium, it bends:", opts: ["Away from the normal", "Toward the normal", "Along the normal", "Does not bend"], ans: 1, exp: "Rarer → denser (e.g., air → glass): light slows down → bends toward the normal (∠r < ∠i). Denser → rarer (glass → air): light speeds up → bends away from normal (∠r > ∠i)." },
    { q: "Snell's law of refraction states:", opts: ["n₁/n₂ = sin r / sin i", "n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r", "sin i = sin r always", "n₁ + n₂ = constant"], ans: 1, exp: "Snell's law: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂. The product of refractive index and sine of angle of incidence is constant. Allows calculation of bending of light at interface." },
    { q: "A convex lens is also called a:", opts: ["Diverging lens", "Concave lens", "Converging lens", "Biconcave lens"], ans: 2, exp: "Convex (converging) lens: thicker at centre, thinner at edges. Parallel rays converge at the principal focus on the other side. Used in magnifying glasses, cameras, human eye." },
    { q: "The power of a lens of focal length f (in metres) is P = 1/f. A lens with f = +50 cm has power:", opts: ["+0.5 D", "+2 D", "−2 D", "+5 D"], ans: 1, exp: "P = 1/f(m) = 1/0.5 = +2 dioptre (D). Positive for converging (convex) lens. Negative for diverging (concave) lens. The dioptre is the SI unit of lens power." },
    { q: "A concave lens always forms an image that is:", opts: ["Real, inverted, magnified", "Virtual, erect, diminished on the same side as object", "Real and at infinity", "Virtual and magnified"], ans: 1, exp: "Concave (diverging) lens: always forms virtual, erect, diminished image between the lens and the object (same side as object). Used in spectacles for myopia (short-sightedness)." },
    { q: "The lens formula is 1/v − 1/u = 1/f. For a convex lens of f = +20 cm with object at u = −30 cm (real object), image distance is:", opts: ["+60 cm", "−60 cm", "+12 cm", "−12 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "1/v = 1/f + 1/u = 1/20 + 1/(−30) = 3/60 − 2/60 = 1/60. So v = +60 cm (real image on other side of lens)." },
    { q: "Total internal reflection (TIR) occurs when:", opts: ["Light goes from rarer to denser medium", "Light hits a mirror", "Light goes from denser to rarer medium at angle ≥ critical angle", "Light is absorbed"], ans: 2, exp: "TIR: light in a denser medium hitting interface with rarer medium at angle ≥ critical angle → completely reflected (no refraction). Used in optical fibres, diamonds, prisms." },
    { q: "Dispersion of white light through a prism occurs because:", opts: ["Prism absorbs some colours", "Different colours have different wavelengths → different refractive indices → bend by different amounts", "Reflection occurs", "Prism is triangular"], ans: 1, exp: "Dispersion: white light = mixture of 7 colours. Each colour has different wavelength → different n in glass → bends differently. Violet bends most (highest n, shortest λ); red bends least. VIBGYOR." },
    { q: "In the colour spectrum (VIBGYOR), which colour has the longest wavelength?", opts: ["Violet", "Blue", "Green", "Red"], ans: 3, exp: "Red has the longest wavelength (~700 nm) and lowest frequency in visible spectrum. Violet has shortest wavelength (~400 nm) and highest frequency. Wavelength order: R > O > Y > G > B > I > V." },
    { q: "An object is placed at 2F of a convex lens. The image is:", opts: ["At infinity", "At F on other side", "At 2F on other side: real, inverted, same size", "Virtual and erect"], ans: 2, exp: "Object at 2F (= 2× focal length): image forms at 2F on other side. Real, inverted, same size (m = −1). One of standard ray diagram cases." },
    { q: "Optical fibre works on the principle of:", opts: ["Refraction", "Diffraction", "Total internal reflection", "Dispersion"], ans: 2, exp: "Optical fibres: light signal enters glass fibre → hits core-cladding boundary at > critical angle → TIR → propagates along fibre without loss. Used in telecommunications and endoscopy." },
    { q: "The critical angle for glass-air interface (n_glass ≈ 1.5) is approximately:", opts: ["30°", "42°", "45°", "60°"], ans: 1, exp: "sin(C) = 1/n = 1/1.5 = 0.667 → C ≈ 41.8° ≈ 42°. At angle > 42°, TIR occurs in glass. Diamond has very high n (~2.4) → very small critical angle → exceptional brilliance (lots of TIR)." },
    { q: "A periscope uses which optical devices?", opts: ["Two convex lenses", "Two concave mirrors", "Two plane mirrors at 45°", "A prism and a lens"], ans: 2, exp: "Periscope: two plane mirrors set at 45° to the horizontal, one at each end of a tube. Light bounces off upper mirror, travels down, reflects off lower mirror → viewer sees above obstacles." },
    { q: "Which mirror is used in a solar furnace (solar cooker)?", opts: ["Plane mirror", "Convex mirror", "Concave mirror (parabolic)", "Cylindrical mirror"], ans: 2, exp: "Concave (parabolic) mirrors focus all parallel solar rays to a single focal point → very high temperature at focus. Used in solar furnaces, solar cookers, solar power plants." },
    { q: "The image formed by a plane mirror is:", opts: ["Real, inverted, same size", "Virtual, erect, same size, as far behind mirror as object is in front (laterally inverted)", "Virtual, diminished, closer to mirror", "Real, magnified"], ans: 1, exp: "Plane mirror image: virtual (behind mirror), erect, same size, laterally inverted (left-right reversed), distance behind mirror = object distance in front. Cannot be caught on screen." },
    { q: "Rear-view mirrors in vehicles are:", opts: ["Plane mirrors", "Concave mirrors", "Convex mirrors (wider field of view)", "Both plane and convex"], ans: 2, exp: "Convex mirrors used as rear-view mirrors: smaller, upright images over a wide field of view → driver sees more of the road behind. Disadvantage: objects appear closer than they are (\"objects in mirror are closer than they appear\")." },
    { q: "The refractive index of water is 1.33. This means light travels in water at speed:", opts: ["3×10⁸ m/s", "1.33×10⁸ m/s", "(3/1.33)×10⁸ ≈ 2.26×10⁸ m/s", "4×10⁸ m/s"], ans: 2, exp: "n = c/v → v = c/n = 3×10⁸/1.33 ≈ 2.26×10⁸ m/s. Light travels slower in water than in vacuum. Diamond: n ≈ 2.4 → v ≈ 1.25×10⁸ m/s." },
    { q: "Which of the following lenses could be used to start a fire using sunlight?", opts: ["Concave lens (biconcave)", "Plane glass", "Convex lens (converging)", "Frosted glass"], ans: 2, exp: "Convex (converging) lens focuses parallel sunlight to a point (principal focus) → intense heat at focal point → can ignite paper/leaves. Used in solar cookers as lens/fresnel lens." },
    { q: "Lateral displacement of a light ray by a glass slab occurs because:", opts: ["Glass absorbs some light", "Refraction at entry and exit surfaces shifts the ray sideways but keeps it parallel to original direction", "The slab creates TIR", "Reflection inside the slab"], ans: 1, exp: "Glass slab: ray bends toward normal entering (rarer→denser), then away from normal exiting (denser→rarer). Net result: ray exits parallel to original but displaced sideways (lateral displacement)." },
    { q: "A convex mirror of focal length 15 cm: an object placed 30 cm in front. Image distance:", opts: ["+10 cm (virtual)", "−10 cm", "+30 cm", "−30 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "Mirror formula: 1/v + 1/u = 1/f. f = +15 cm (convex), u = −30 cm. 1/v = 1/15 − 1/(−30) = 1/15 + 1/30 = 2/30 + 1/30 = 3/30 = 1/10. v = +10 cm (virtual, behind mirror)." },
    { q: "When light enters a prism, the emergent beam is deviated because:", opts: ["Reflection occurs", "Both refraction at entry and exit surfaces bend light away from the base", "Light is absorbed", "The prism is opaque"], ans: 1, exp: "Prism: light refracts toward base at entry face; refracts further toward base at exit face. Net deviation is toward the base. Angle of deviation depends on prism angle and n of glass." },
    { q: "The new Cartesian sign convention for mirrors: incident light travels in the:", opts: ["Negative direction", "Positive direction (left to right)", "Upward direction", "Any direction"], ans: 1, exp: "New Cartesian convention: principal axis is x-axis. Incident light travels left to right (positive direction). Distances measured from pole (P). Distances in direction of light (+ve); against light (−ve)." },
    { q: "A concave mirror is used in car headlights because:", opts: ["It creates a wide beam", "Bulb at focus → reflected rays emerge as parallel beam (strong, focused light)", "It magnifies the road", "It disperses light"], ans: 1, exp: "Car headlights/torches: bulb placed at focal point of concave mirror → all light reflects as a parallel beam → projects far ahead. Concave mirrors also used in searchlights and spotlights." },
    { q: "The focal length of a convex lens and concave lens of same focal length placed together:", opts: ["P_total = P_convex + P_concave → cancels to 0 (plane glass)", "P_total = P_convex − P_concave", "P_total = P_convex only", "P_total = P_concave only"], ans: 0, exp: "Thin lenses in contact: P_total = P₁ + P₂. If convex (+P) and concave (−P) have equal magnitude: P_total = 0 → f = ∞ → acts like a plane glass slab." },
    { q: "Which of the following phenomena shows that light is a transverse wave?", opts: ["Refraction", "Dispersion", "Polarisation", "Diffraction"], ans: 2, exp: "Polarisation: only transverse waves can be polarised (oscillations restricted to one plane). Light can be polarised by polaroid filters → proves light is a transverse electromagnetic wave." },
    { q: "The speed of light in vacuum (c) is approximately:", opts: ["3×10⁶ m/s", "3×10⁸ m/s", "3×10¹⁰ m/s", "3×10⁴ m/s"], ans: 1, exp: "Speed of light in vacuum: c ≈ 3×10⁸ m/s = 300,000 km/s. This is the maximum possible speed in the universe (Einstein's special relativity). Light takes ~8 minutes from Sun to Earth." },
    { q: "If a concave mirror has a focal length of 20 cm, its radius of curvature is:", opts: ["10 cm", "20 cm", "40 cm", "5 cm"], ans: 2, exp: "R = 2f = 2 × 20 = 40 cm. The centre of curvature C is always twice as far from the pole as the focus F (R = 2f)." },
    { q: "Which lens is used to correct hypermetropia (long-sightedness)?", opts: ["Concave lens", "Convex (converging) lens", "Cylindrical lens", "Prism"], ans: 1, exp: "Hypermetropia: eyeball too short/cornea too flat → parallel rays focus behind retina. Corrected with convex lens: it converges rays before they enter eye → image falls on retina." },
    { q: "Myopia (short-sightedness) occurs because the image of distant objects forms:", opts: ["Behind the retina", "In front of the retina", "On the retina", "On the cornea"], ans: 1, exp: "Myopia: eyeball too long/cornea too curved → parallel rays from far objects converge in front of retina. Nearby objects OK. Corrected with concave (diverging) lens." },
    { q: "Coloured shadows can be produced by:", opts: ["A single white light source", "Mixing pigments", "Multiple different coloured light sources illuminating an object", "Monochromatic light only"], ans: 2, exp: "Coloured shadows: with multiple differently-coloured light sources, each source creates a shadow not illuminated by it. The other sources illuminate the shadow region with their colours → coloured shadows." },
    { q: "The phenomenon of reflection from a very smooth surface where the angle is uniform is called:", opts: ["Diffuse reflection", "Scattering", "Specular (regular) reflection", "Total internal reflection"], ans: 2, exp: "Specular reflection: parallel incident rays reflect as parallel rays (smooth mirror). Diffuse reflection: rough surface → parallel incident rays scattered in different directions (matte surfaces, paper, walls)." },
    { q: "A student finds the focal length of a concave mirror by obtaining a sharp image of a distant tree on a screen. The distance between mirror and screen measures:", opts: ["The radius of curvature", "The focal length", "The image distance", "Both b and c — the focal length and image distance (distant object → image at F)"], ans: 3, exp: "For a very distant object (effectively at infinity), image forms at the focus. So screen-mirror distance = focal length (f). Both statements b and c are the same here — image distance = focal length." },
    { q: "The image formed when object is beyond C in a concave mirror is:", opts: ["Virtual, erect, magnified", "Real, inverted, between F and C (diminished)", "At infinity", "Real, inverted, magnified, between F and P"], ans: 1, exp: "Object beyond C: image is between F and C, real, inverted, and diminished (smaller than object). Example: for f=20cm, C=40cm, object at u=−60cm → image at v=−30cm (between F and C)." },
    { q: "The lens formula uses which sign convention for a real object (on the same side as incoming light)?", opts: ["Positive u", "Negative u (object distance is negative for real objects)", "u = 0", "u is not used"], ans: 1, exp: "New Cartesian convention: real objects are placed to the left of lens (against direction of light), so u is always negative for real objects. v is positive for real images (right side of convex lens)." },
    { q: "A concave mirror of focal length 10 cm has an object at 15 cm. The image position (using mirror formula) is at:", opts: ["−30 cm (real, inverted)", "+30 cm (virtual)", "−10 cm", "+10 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "1/v + 1/u = 1/f. f = −10 cm (concave), u = −15 cm. 1/v = 1/(−10) − 1/(−15) = −1/10 + 1/15 = −3/30 + 2/30 = −1/30. v = −30 cm. Real, inverted, magnified image." },
    { q: "When a ray passes from air (n=1) into a medium at 30° incidence and refracts at 20°, the medium's refractive index is approximately:", opts: ["0.67", "1.46", "2.0", "0.5"], ans: 1, exp: "Snell's law: n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r → 1 × sin 30° = n₂ × sin 20°. n₂ = sin 30°/sin 20° = 0.5/0.342 ≈ 1.46. This is approximately the refractive index of glass." },
    { q: "The power of a concave lens with focal length −25 cm is:", opts: ["+4 D", "−4 D", "+25 D", "−25 D"], ans: 1, exp: "P = 1/f(m) = 1/(−0.25) = −4 D. Negative power = diverging (concave) lens. Positive power = converging (convex) lens." },
    { q: "The image formed by a convex lens when the object is between F and the lens is:", opts: ["Real, inverted, on the other side", "Virtual, erect, magnified — on the same side as object", "At infinity", "Real and diminished"], ans: 1, exp: "Object between F and convex lens: image is virtual, erect, and magnified on the same side as the object. This is how a magnifying glass works — object closer than F → magnified virtual image." },
    { q: "Rainbows are formed by:", opts: ["Reflection only", "Dispersion and total internal reflection inside raindrops", "Diffraction of sunlight", "Polarisation of light"], ans: 1, exp: "Rainbow: sunlight enters a raindrop → refracts (dispersing colours) → undergoes TIR inside the drop → refracts again on exit. Different colours exit at different angles → VIBGYOR arc (red at top, violet at bottom)." },
    { q: "Glass prism produces a spectrum because:", opts: ["It absorbs some colours", "Each colour of light has a different refractive index in glass (and hence bends differently)", "It reflects light", "It emits light"], ans: 1, exp: "Dispersion by prism: violet light refracts most (highest n in glass), red refracts least (lowest n). The prism separates white light into constituent colours VIBGYOR." },
    { q: "The phenomenon where a stick appears broken when inserted diagonally into water is due to:", opts: ["Reflection at the water surface", "Refraction — light changes speed at water-air boundary causing apparent bending of the stick", "Dispersion of light", "Total internal reflection"], ans: 1, exp: "Apparent bending of a stick in water: light from the submerged part refracts at the water surface → apparent image is displaced → stick appears bent/broken at the interface." },
    { q: "An object is placed 30 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 20 cm. Using the mirror formula, the image distance v is:", opts: ["−60 cm", "+60 cm", "−12 cm", "+12 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "Using 1/v + 1/u = 1/f with u = −30 cm, f = −20 cm: 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = −1/20 + 1/30 = −3/60 + 2/60 = −1/60. So v = −60 cm (real, same side as object)." },
    { q: "A concave mirror produces a virtual, erect image 3 times the size of the object. If the object is 10 cm from the mirror, the focal length is:", opts: ["15 cm", "30 cm", "20 cm", "12 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "Magnification m = +3 (virtual, erect) → m = −v/u → +3 = −v/(−10) → v = −30 cm. Mirror formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u = 1/(−30) + 1/(−10) = −1/30 − 3/30 = −4/30, so f = −7.5 cm... Re-check: virtual image means v is positive for mirror: v = +30, u = −10. 1/f = 1/30 + 1/(−10) = 1/30 − 3/30 = −2/30. f = −15 cm. Focal length magnitude is 15 cm." },
    { q: "A ray of light travels from glass (n = 1.5) to air. The critical angle is approximately:", opts: ["41.8°", "48.6°", "33.7°", "60.0°"], ans: 0, exp: "sin C = n₂/n₁ = 1/1.5 = 0.667. C = sin⁻¹(0.667) ≈ 41.8°. Above this angle, total internal reflection occurs and no refracted ray exits into air." },
    { q: "An object is placed at the centre of curvature of a concave mirror (u = −2f). The magnification of the image is:", opts: ["−1", "+1", "−2", "+0.5"], ans: 0, exp: "m = −v/u. At u = −2f, mirror formula gives v = −2f (image also at C). So m = −(−2f)/(−2f) = −1. The image is real, inverted, and same size as the object." },
    { q: "Light passes from water (n = 1.33) into a glass slab (n = 1.5). If the angle of incidence is 45°, the angle of refraction is closest to:", opts: ["39.1°", "50.8°", "45.0°", "30.0°"], ans: 0, exp: "Snell's law: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂ → 1.33 × sin 45° = 1.5 × sin θ₂ → sin θ₂ = (1.33 × 0.707)/1.5 = 0.627 → θ₂ ≈ 38.8° ≈ 39.1°. Light bends toward normal when entering denser medium." },
    { q: "A convex mirror of focal length 25 cm forms an image of an object placed 75 cm away. The image distance is:", opts: ["18.75 cm behind mirror", "−18.75 cm", "−37.5 cm", "+37.5 cm behind mirror"], ans: 0, exp: "Convex mirror: f = +25 cm, u = −75 cm. 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/25 − 1/(−75) = 1/25 + 1/75 = 3/75 + 1/75 = 4/75. v = 75/4 = +18.75 cm (virtual, behind mirror)." },
    { q: "A lens of power +4 D is combined with a lens of power −1.5 D. The focal length of the combination is:", opts: ["40 cm", "−40 cm", "25 cm", "66.7 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "P_total = P₁ + P₂ = 4 + (−1.5) = +2.5 D. f = 1/P = 1/2.5 m = 0.4 m = 40 cm. The combination acts as a converging lens of focal length 40 cm." },
    { q: "An object is placed 40 cm from a convex lens of focal length 20 cm. The nature and position of the image is:", opts: ["Real, inverted, at 40 cm on other side", "Virtual, erect, at 40 cm same side", "Real, inverted, at 20 cm on other side", "Real, erect, at 40 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "Using lens formula: 1/v − 1/u = 1/f → 1/v − 1/(−40) = 1/20 → 1/v = 1/20 − 1/40 = 2/40 − 1/40 = 1/40. v = +40 cm. Image is real, inverted, same size, on opposite side." },
    { q: "The refractive index of diamond is 2.42. The speed of light inside diamond is:", opts: ["1.24 × 10⁸ m/s", "2.42 × 10⁸ m/s", "3.0 × 10⁸ m/s", "0.41 × 10⁸ m/s"], ans: 0, exp: "n = c/v → v = c/n = (3 × 10⁸)/2.42 ≈ 1.24 × 10⁸ m/s. Diamond's very high refractive index gives it exceptional brilliance through total internal reflection." },
    { q: "A concave lens of focal length 30 cm forms an image of an object at infinity. The image is located:", opts: ["At the focus, 30 cm in front of lens", "At infinity", "At 30 cm behind lens", "At 60 cm in front of lens"], ans: 0, exp: "For a concave (diverging) lens with object at infinity: 1/v = 1/f + 1/u = 1/(−30) + 0 = −1/30. So v = −30 cm (30 cm in front, same side as object). The image is virtual, erect, and diminished." },
    { q: "A student measures the radius of curvature of a concave mirror as 40 cm. Its focal length and power are respectively:", opts: ["20 cm, −5 D", "40 cm, −2.5 D", "20 cm, +5 D", "10 cm, −10 D"], ans: 0, exp: "f = R/2 = 40/2 = 20 cm = 0.20 m. For mirrors, power P = 1/f (in meters). Since concave mirror has f = −0.20 m, P = −5 D. (Note: sign convention makes concave mirror power negative in some formulations.)" },
    { q: "When a ray strikes a plane mirror at 35° to the mirror surface (not the normal), the angle of reflection from the normal is:", opts: ["55°", "35°", "70°", "45°"], ans: 0, exp: "Angle to mirror surface = 35°, so angle of incidence (from normal) = 90° − 35° = 55°. By the law of reflection, angle of reflection = 55°." },
    { q: "An object 5 cm tall is placed 15 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 10 cm. The height of the image is:", opts: ["−10 cm (inverted, 10 cm tall)", "+10 cm (erect)", "−5 cm", "−20 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "1/v + 1/u = 1/f → 1/v = 1/(−10) − 1/(−15) = −1/10 + 1/15 = −3/30 + 2/30 = −1/30. v = −30 cm. m = −v/u = −(−30)/(−15) = −2. Image height = m × object height = −2 × 5 = −10 cm (inverted, 10 cm tall)." },
    { q: "Optical fibres work on the principle of:", opts: ["Total internal reflection", "Refraction at the core-cladding interface", "Diffraction of light", "Partial reflection"], ans: 0, exp: "In optical fibres, light undergoes repeated total internal reflection at the glass core–cladding interface (core has higher n than cladding). No light escapes, allowing signal transmission over long distances with minimal loss." },
    { q: "A concave mirror has a focal length of 15 cm. An object is placed between the focus and the pole. The image formed is:", opts: ["Virtual, erect, magnified, behind the mirror", "Real, inverted, magnified", "Real, inverted, diminished", "Virtual, inverted, magnified"], ans: 0, exp: "When object is between F and pole of a concave mirror, the rays after reflection diverge. Extended back, they meet behind the mirror. The image is virtual, erect, and magnified — same as using a concave mirror as a shaving/makeup mirror." },
    { q: "Two plane mirrors are inclined at an angle of 40°. The number of images formed of an object placed between them is:", opts: ["8", "9", "7", "4"], ans: 0, exp: "Number of images = (360°/θ) − 1 when 360°/θ is even. 360/40 = 9 (odd), so number = 9 − 1 = 8. This formula applies when the object is placed symmetrically; generally n = (360/θ) − 1 = 8." },
    { q: "Light goes from medium A (n = 1.6) to medium B (n = 1.2). Total internal reflection can occur because:", opts: ["A is denser; sin C = 1.2/1.6 = 0.75, C ≈ 48.6°", "B is denser; critical angle exists in B", "n_A < n_B so TIR occurs at any angle", "TIR only occurs in glass–air interfaces"], ans: 0, exp: "TIR occurs when going from optically denser to rarer medium. Here n_A = 1.6 > n_B = 1.2, so A is denser. sin C = n_B/n_A = 1.2/1.6 = 0.75 → C ≈ 48.6°. Beyond this angle, no refracted ray exits into B." },
    { q: "Using the mirror formula, if u = −f for a concave mirror, the image distance v is:", opts: ["Infinity (image at ∞)", "−2f", "+f", "0"], ans: 0, exp: "1/v + 1/u = 1/f → 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/(−f) − 1/(−f) = 0. So v = ∞. When the object is at the focus of a concave mirror, reflected rays are parallel and the image forms at infinity." },
    { q: "A ray of light goes from medium 1 to medium 2. In medium 1, θ = 60°; in medium 2, θ = 45°. The refractive index of medium 2 w.r.t. medium 1 is:", opts: ["√6/2 ≈ 1.22", "0.816", "1.5", "√3"], ans: 0, exp: "n₂₁ = n₁ sin θ₁ / n₁ = (n₁ sin 60°)/(n₂ sin 45°) is rearranged: n₂/n₁ = sin θ₁/sin θ₂ = sin 60°/sin 45° = (√3/2)/(1/√2) = √3·√2/2 = √6/2 ≈ 1.22. Medium 2 is optically denser." },
    { q: "A convex lens of focal length 10 cm is used as a magnifying glass. For maximum magnification at the near point (D = 25 cm), the magnification is:", opts: ["3.5", "2.5", "1.5", "4.5"], ans: 0, exp: "For a simple microscope (magnifying glass), m = 1 + D/f = 1 + 25/10 = 1 + 2.5 = 3.5. This is the angular magnification when image forms at the near point (25 cm)." },
    { q: "The magnification produced by a concave mirror when the object is beyond C is:", opts: ["Negative and less than 1 in magnitude", "Positive and greater than 1", "Negative and greater than 1", "Exactly −1"], ans: 0, exp: "When object is beyond C, image forms between F and C on same side. v < u (both negative, |v| < |u|), so m = −v/u → negative (inverted) and |m| < 1 (diminished)." },
    { q: "A glass slab of thickness 6 cm and refractive index 1.5 is placed in the path of a converging beam. The lateral shift of the emergent ray compared to the incident ray is:", opts: ["The slab shifts the convergence point but causes no lateral displacement for a ray normal to slab surfaces", "Lateral shift = t(1 − 1/n) = 6(1 − 1/1.5) = 2 cm at normal incidence", "6 cm", "4 cm"], ans: 1, exp: "Lateral shift for a ray through a glass slab = t(1 − cos i / cos r) for oblique incidence. For a ray perpendicular to surfaces, there is no lateral shift. For a general formula at oblique incidence the apparent shift is t(1 − 1/n) = 6(1 − 2/3) = 2 cm — the image appears shifted 2 cm toward the observer." },
    { q: "Which among the following correctly states the new Cartesian sign convention for mirrors?", opts: ["Distances measured in the direction of incident light are positive", "All distances are measured from the pole; opposite to incident light is positive", "All distances are measured from the focus", "Distances below principal axis are positive"], ans: 0, exp: "In the new Cartesian convention, the incident light travels left to right. Distances measured in the direction of incident light (right) are positive; opposite direction (left, toward object) are negative. Heights above principal axis are positive." },
    { q: "A student uses a concave mirror to observe that the image of a candle flame exactly coincides with the candle itself. This happens when the object is at:", opts: ["The centre of curvature", "The focus", "Infinity", "Between F and P"], ans: 0, exp: "When the object is at C, the image also forms at C (real, inverted, same size). If the image coincides with the object, the object is at the centre of curvature. This is used in practice to locate C of a concave mirror." },
    { q: "The power of a concave lens of focal length 50 cm is:", opts: ["−2.0 D", "+2.0 D", "−0.5 D", "+0.5 D"], ans: 0, exp: "f = −50 cm = −0.50 m (concave lens: negative focal length). P = 1/f = 1/(−0.50) = −2.0 D. A negative power indicates a diverging (concave) lens." },
    { q: "A light ray enters a rectangular glass block at one face and exits from the opposite face. Compared to the incident ray direction, the emergent ray is:", opts: ["Parallel but laterally shifted", "Perpendicular to the incident ray", "Deviated at an angle", "Coincident with incident ray"], ans: 0, exp: "In a glass slab (parallel sides), refraction at entry and exit faces are equal and opposite. The emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray but displaced laterally. The displacement depends on slab thickness and angle." },
    { q: "The critical angle for a medium is 45°. A ray strikes the boundary at 50°. The ray will:", opts: ["Undergo total internal reflection", "Refract and exit the medium", "Reflect partially and refract partially", "Stop at the boundary"], ans: 0, exp: "Since 50° > critical angle 45°, total internal reflection occurs. No refracted ray exits; all incident energy is reflected back into the denser medium. This is total, not partial, reflection." },
    { q: "The image formed by a convex mirror is always:", opts: ["Virtual, erect, and diminished", "Real, inverted, and diminished", "Virtual, erect, and magnified", "Real, erect, and same size"], ans: 0, exp: "For a convex mirror, the focal length is positive and all real object positions give v between 0 and f (positive). Magnification m = −v/u is positive (v positive, u negative) and < 1 — always virtual, erect, diminished." },
    { q: "A prism with apex angle 60° has n = 1.5. The minimum deviation is approximately:", opts: ["37°", "60°", "30°", "45°"], ans: 0, exp: "For minimum deviation: n = sin[(A + δₘ)/2] / sin(A/2) → 1.5 = sin[(60° + δₘ)/2] / sin 30° = sin[(60° + δₘ)/2] / 0.5. So sin[(60° + δₘ)/2] = 0.75 → (60° + δₘ)/2 = 48.6° → δₘ = 2(48.6) − 60 ≈ 37.2°." },
    { q: "For a concave mirror, if the magnification m = −3 and the image distance v = −45 cm, the object distance u is:", opts: ["−15 cm", "+15 cm", "−45 cm", "−60 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "m = −v/u → −3 = −(−45)/u = 45/u → u = 45/(−3) = −15 cm. Object is 15 cm in front of the mirror, between F and C (since |u| < |v|)." },
    { q: "Atmospheric refraction causes the apparent position of stars to:", opts: ["Shift slightly upward from their true position", "Shift downward", "Remain unchanged", "Shift sideways"], ans: 0, exp: "The atmosphere is denser near Earth's surface. Light from stars bends toward the normal (toward Earth) as it enters progressively denser layers. The apparent position of a star is slightly higher than its true geometric position." },
    { q: "A student plots the image distance v against object distance u for a concave mirror. For real images, the graph of v vs u (both negative per sign convention) is:", opts: ["A hyperbola in the third quadrant (u and v both negative)", "A straight line through origin", "A parabola", "An ellipse"], ans: 0, exp: "The mirror formula 1/v + 1/u = 1/f is a hyperbola in v–u space. For real images of concave mirror (u and v both negative), the curve lies in the third quadrant. The graph bends toward u = v = −2f at the vertex." },
    { q: "A convex lens (f = 20 cm) and concave lens (f = 30 cm) are placed in contact. The power of combination is:", opts: ["−1.67 D", "+1.67 D", "+8.33 D", "−8.33 D"], ans: 1, exp: "P₁ = 1/0.20 = +5 D, P₂ = 1/(−0.30) = −3.33 D. P_total = 5 − 3.33 = +1.67 D. The combination is converging with f = 1/1.67 = 60 cm." },
    { q: "In a lateral inversion experiment with text \"AMBULANCE\", the mirror image appears as:", opts: ["Reversed left-right but not upside-down", "Upside-down only", "Both reversed and upside-down", "Identical to the original"], ans: 0, exp: "A plane mirror produces lateral inversion — left and right are swapped but top and bottom are preserved. \"AMBULANCE\" is written in reverse on the front of ambulances so drivers see the correct text in their rear-view mirrors." },
    { q: "The refractive index of a medium is √3. The critical angle for total internal reflection from this medium to air is:", opts: ["35.3°", "45°", "60°", "30°"], ans: 0, exp: "sin C = 1/n = 1/√3 ≈ 0.577. C = sin⁻¹(0.577) ≈ 35.3°. Note: 30° would correspond to n = 2 (sin30° = 0.5), and 45° to n = √2. For n = √3, the critical angle is uniquely sin⁻¹(1/√3) ≈ 35.3°." },
    { q: "A ray of light passes through a triangular glass prism. As the angle of incidence on the prism increases from 0°, the angle of deviation first:", opts: ["Decreases then increases, with a minimum value", "Increases continuously", "Decreases continuously", "Remains constant then increases"], ans: 0, exp: "The angle of deviation D vs angle of incidence i curve is a U-shape with a minimum. At minimum deviation, the ray passes symmetrically through the prism. This minimum deviation δₘ is used to calculate n of the prism." },
    { q: "A lens forms a real image twice the size of the object. If the object is 30 cm from the lens, the focal length is:", opts: ["20 cm", "40 cm", "10 cm", "60 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "Real, inverted image → m = −2 = v/u (using lens sign convention m = v/u). u = −30 cm → v = +60 cm. Lens formula: 1/f = 1/v − 1/u = 1/60 − 1/(−30) = 1/60 + 2/60 = 3/60 = 1/20. f = 20 cm." },
    { q: "If an object is placed between F and 2F of a convex lens, the image is:", opts: ["Real, inverted, beyond 2F on other side", "Virtual, erect, same side as object", "Real, inverted, between F and 2F", "Real, erect, beyond 2F"], ans: 0, exp: "When f < |u| < 2f for a convex lens (object between F and 2F), the image is real, inverted, magnified, and beyond 2F on the other side. This is the principle used in projectors." },
    { q: "The speed of light in a medium of refractive index 1.6 is:", opts: ["1.875 × 10⁸ m/s", "4.8 × 10⁸ m/s", "3.0 × 10⁸ m/s", "1.6 × 10⁸ m/s"], ans: 0, exp: "v = c/n = (3 × 10⁸)/1.6 = 1.875 × 10⁸ m/s. Light slows down when entering an optically denser medium, which is the fundamental reason for refraction." },
    { q: "Which characteristic is INCORRECT for the image formed by a plane mirror?", opts: ["The image is at the same distance behind the mirror as the object is in front", "The image is real and can be projected on a screen", "The image is laterally inverted", "The image is of the same size as the object"], ans: 1, exp: "A plane mirror always forms a virtual image — it cannot be projected on a screen because the rays do not actually converge. The image appears to be behind the mirror. The other three statements are correct." },
    { q: "A concave mirror of radius of curvature 60 cm produces an image at twice the distance from the object. If the object is real, the object distance is:", opts: ["45 cm", "30 cm", "90 cm", "20 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "R = 60 cm → f = −30 cm. Image at twice object distance: v = 2u. Both real → u = −d, v = −2d. 1/(−2d) + 1/(−d) = 1/(−30) → −1/(2d) − 1/d = −1/30 → −3/(2d) = −1/30 → d = 45 cm. u = −45 cm." },
    { q: "For a convex lens, when the object is at 2F, the magnification is:", opts: ["−1 (real, inverted, same size)", "+1 (virtual, erect, same size)", "−2", "+0.5"], ans: 0, exp: "When u = −2f for a convex lens: 1/v = 1/f + 1/u = 1/f + 1/(−2f) = 1/(2f). So v = 2f. m = v/u = 2f/(−2f) = −1. Image is real, inverted, same size, at 2F on other side." },
    { q: "An object is placed 12 cm from a concave lens of focal length 8 cm. The image distance is:", opts: ["−4.8 cm", "+4.8 cm", "−24 cm", "+24 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "1/v − 1/u = 1/f → 1/v − 1/(−12) = 1/(−8) → 1/v + 1/12 = −1/8 → 1/v = −1/8 − 1/12 = −3/24 − 2/24 = −5/24. v = −24/5 = −4.8 cm. Virtual image, 4.8 cm from lens, same side as object." },
    { q: "The phenomenon responsible for the twinkling of stars (scintillation) is:", opts: ["Atmospheric refraction due to varying density layers", "Doppler effect", "Diffraction at the atmosphere", "Total internal reflection in the ionosphere"], ans: 0, exp: "Stars twinkle because atmospheric layers of varying temperature, pressure, and density continuously shift, causing the star's refracted path to vary rapidly. This random variation in apparent position and brightness is scintillation." },
    { q: "If the power of a lens is −4.5 D, the lens is concave with focal length:", opts: ["−22.2 cm", "+22.2 cm", "−45 cm", "+45 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "f = 1/P = 1/(−4.5) m = −0.222 m = −22.2 cm. A negative focal length confirms it is a diverging (concave) lens." },
    { q: "A ray of light makes an angle of 30° with the normal at the surface of a denser medium (n = 1.5). The angle of refraction inside the medium is:", opts: ["19.5°", "30°", "35.3°", "41.8°"], ans: 0, exp: "Angle of incidence = 30° (measured from normal). Snell's law: 1 × sin 30° = 1.5 × sin r → sin r = 0.5/1.5 = 0.333 → r = sin⁻¹(0.333) ≈ 19.5°. The ray bends toward the normal on entering the denser medium." },
    { q: "The mirror formula applies to spherical mirrors under which approximation?", opts: ["Paraxial (small angle) approximation — only rays close to principal axis", "All rays regardless of angle", "Only for real objects and real images", "Only for objects at C or beyond"], ans: 0, exp: "The mirror formula 1/v + 1/u = 1/f is derived assuming paraxial rays — rays that make small angles with the principal axis and lie close to it. Rays far from the axis cause spherical aberration and the formula breaks down." },
    { q: "An observer looks at a fish in water (n = 1.33). When the fish is 40 cm below the surface, the apparent depth is:", opts: ["30.1 cm", "53.2 cm", "40 cm", "26.7 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "Apparent depth = real depth / n = 40/1.33 ≈ 30.1 cm. The fish appears closer to the surface than it actually is because light bends away from normal on exiting water into air." },
    { q: "Why does a diamond sparkle more than glass?", opts: ["Diamond has a much higher refractive index (2.42 vs ~1.5), giving a much smaller critical angle (~24.4°) and facilitating TIR", "Diamond reflects more light from its flat surfaces", "Diamond absorbs UV and re-emits visible light", "Diamond has two refractive indices"], ans: 0, exp: "Diamond's critical angle is sin⁻¹(1/2.42) ≈ 24.4°, far smaller than glass (~42°). Light entering a cut diamond undergoes multiple total internal reflections before exiting from the top facets, creating intense brilliance." },
    { q: "A concave mirror produces a real image 4 times the size of the object. If the image is 80 cm from the mirror, the focal length of the mirror is:", opts: ["16 cm", "20 cm", "32 cm", "64 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "Real, inverted image → m = −4. Using m = −v/u with v = −80 cm: −4 = −(−80)/u → u = −20 cm. Mirror formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u = 1/(−80) + 1/(−20) = −1/80 − 4/80 = −5/80. So f = −16 cm; |f| = 16 cm." }
  ]
,

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════
     CH 10 — THE HUMAN EYE AND THE COLOURFUL WORLD
  ══════════════════════════════════════ */
  10: [
    { q: "The human eye lens changes its focal length (curvature) to focus near and far objects. This ability is called:", opts: ["Persistence of vision", "Accommodation", "Adaptation", "Presbyopia"], ans: 1, exp: "Accommodation: ciliary muscles contract (near object) → ciliary zonule fibres relax → lens becomes more curved/convex → shorter focal length → near object focused. Relax for distant objects." },
    { q: "The retina of the human eye contains photoreceptor cells called:", opts: ["Rods and cones", "Rods and bipolar cells", "Only cones", "Only rods"], ans: 0, exp: "Retina: contains rods (low-light/night vision, black and white) and cones (bright light, colour vision: red, green, blue). ~120 million rods, ~7 million cones per eye." },
    { q: "The blind spot of the eye is where:", opts: ["Vision is sharpest", "No photoreceptors exist — optic nerve exits the eye here (no rods or cones)", "Only cones are present", "Most rods are located"], ans: 1, exp: "Blind spot: the point where the optic nerve leaves the retina — no rods or cones → no vision. Any image falling here is not detected. Located medial to the yellow spot (macula)." },
    { q: "The yellow spot (fovea centralis / macula) of the retina is where:", opts: ["No photoreceptors are present", "Vision is sharpest — high density of cones, no rods", "Night vision is best", "Optic nerve connects"], ans: 1, exp: "Fovea centralis: point of maximum visual acuity, densely packed with cones (no rods). Colour vision, daytime vision. Macular degeneration destroys the fovea → central vision loss." },
    { q: "Which defect causes difficulty seeing distant objects clearly but near objects are clear?", opts: ["Hypermetropia", "Presbyopia", "Myopia (near-sightedness)", "Astigmatism"], ans: 2, exp: "Myopia: distant objects blurred, near objects clear. Cause: eyeball too long OR lens too converging → parallel rays focus IN FRONT of retina. Correction: concave (diverging) lens." },
    { q: "Hypermetropia (far-sightedness) is corrected using:", opts: ["Concave lens", "Convex (converging) lens", "Cylindrical lens", "No correction needed"], ans: 1, exp: "Hypermetropia: near objects blurred, distant OK. Eyeball too short/lens weak → image behind retina. Convex lens brings image forward onto retina." },
    { q: "Presbyopia affects people usually after age 40-45. It is caused by:", opts: ["Pupil shrinking permanently", "Loss of elasticity of the eye lens (inability to accommodate for near work)", "Cataract", "Retinal detachment"], ans: 1, exp: "Presbyopia: age-related loss of accommodation. The lens becomes less elastic (stiff) → cannot increase curvature for near work → difficulty reading. Corrected with reading glasses (convex) or bifocals." },
    { q: "Astigmatism is caused by:", opts: ["Lens being too powerful", "Uneven curvature of cornea or lens (different focal lengths in different planes)", "Cataract", "Short eyeball"], ans: 1, exp: "Astigmatism: irregular curvature of cornea/lens → different parts of field of view are in focus at different distances simultaneously → blurred, distorted vision. Corrected with cylindrical lenses." },
    { q: "Cataract is:", opts: ["Clouding of the retina", "Increased intraocular pressure (glaucoma)", "Clouding of the eye lens (opacity)", "Detachment of retina"], ans: 2, exp: "Cataract: lens proteins denature and become opaque (cloudy) → blurred vision → eventually blindness if untreated. Common with age, UV exposure, diabetes. Treated by surgical lens replacement." },
    { q: "The iris of the eye controls the:", opts: ["Shape of the lens", "Size of the pupil (amount of light entering)", "Focus on retina", "Production of aqueous humour"], ans: 1, exp: "Iris: coloured, muscular diaphragm. Pupil = hole in centre. Iris muscles control pupil diameter: bright light → iris dilates → pupil constricts (less light). Dark → iris contracts → pupil widens (more light)." },
    { q: "Persistence of vision is the time for which the impression of an image lasts on the retina after the image is removed. This time is approximately:", opts: ["0.01 seconds", "1/16th of a second (0.0625 s)", "1 second", "5 seconds"], ans: 1, exp: "Persistence of vision: ~1/16th second. This is exploited in movies/animation: if ≥16 frames/second are shown, brain perceives continuous motion (modern cinema uses 24+ fps)." },
    { q: "The scattering of light by small particles in the atmosphere is called:", opts: ["Dispersion", "Refraction", "Rayleigh scattering", "Total internal reflection"], ans: 2, exp: "Rayleigh scattering: small particles (~molecules) scatter shorter wavelengths more (blue ∝ 1/λ⁴). Blue scattered more than red → sky appears blue. At sunrise/sunset: long path → blue scattered away → sky appears red/orange." },
    { q: "The sky appears blue because:", opts: ["Air is blue", "Blue light from sun is absorbed by atmosphere", "Blue light (short wavelength) is scattered more by air molecules and reaches our eyes from all directions", "Water vapour in air is blue"], ans: 2, exp: "Blue sky: Rayleigh scattering — shorter wavelengths (blue, violet) scattered ∝ 1/λ⁴ much more than longer wavelengths. Blue reaches us from all directions across the sky (violet scattered too, but our eyes less sensitive to violet)." },
    { q: "Why does the sun appear red at sunrise and sunset?", opts: ["Sun is cooler then", "Clouds filter blue light", "Blue light scattered away over the long atmospheric path; only red/orange penetrates", "Sunlight gets weaker"], ans: 2, exp: "At sunrise/sunset, sunlight travels through a longer atmospheric path → most blue light scattered away before reaching us → only longer wavelengths (red, orange) remain → red/orange sun." },
    { q: "The twinkling of stars is due to:", opts: ["Stars actually blinking on and off", "Atmospheric refraction (varying density layers refract starlight constantly → apparent shift in position → twinkling)", "Light absorption by dust", "Planets interfering"], ans: 1, exp: "Stellar scintillation (twinkling): turbulent atmospheric layers of varying density and temperature continuously refract starlight differently → apparent position and brightness fluctuate → twinkling. Planets don't twinkle because they appear as discs." },
    { q: "Planets do not twinkle like stars because:", opts: ["Planets are closer so they appear as extended discs (scintillation effects average out)", "Planets are farther away", "Planets emit their own light", "Stars are cooler than planets"], ans: 0, exp: "Planets are much closer → appear as small discs (not points). Multiple points on the disc twinkle but not in synchrony → effects average out → steady light. Stars are so distant they appear as points → all scintillation adds up → twinkling." },
    { q: "The power of accommodation of the normal human eye is the difference in power between:", opts: ["Maximum and minimum power of the eye", "Power of lens only", "Power when viewing at 25cm vs infinity", "Power of cornea and lens combined"], ans: 0, exp: "Power of accommodation ≈ 4 dioptres for a young adult (between near point ~25cm → P=40D, far point at ∞ → P=36D). With age, this range decreases → presbyopia." },
    { q: "The near point of a normal human eye is approximately:", opts: ["10 cm", "25 cm (least distance of distinct vision)", "50 cm", "1 m"], ans: 1, exp: "Near point (least distance of distinct vision): 25 cm for young adult. This is the minimum distance at which an object can be seen clearly. For myope, near point < 25 cm. For hypermetrope, near point > 25 cm." },
    { q: "The far point of a normal (emmetropic) human eye is:", opts: ["25 cm", "50 cm", "Infinity", "1 m"], ans: 2, exp: "Far point of normal eye = infinity (relaxed eye focuses parallel rays from very distant objects on retina). For myope, far point is < infinity (a real point, maybe 2 m). For hypermetrope, far point is virtual (behind eye)." },
    { q: "Glaucoma is a condition where:", opts: ["Lens becomes cloudy", "Increased intraocular pressure damages the optic nerve → vision loss", "Retina detaches", "Cornea becomes inflamed"], ans: 1, exp: "Glaucoma: increased pressure inside the eye (intraocular pressure) damages the optic nerve. Often painless initially. Can cause permanent blindness. Treated with eye drops, laser or surgery." },
    { q: "Which phenomenon explains why a stick appears bent when placed in water?", opts: ["Reflection", "Dispersion", "Refraction (light bends at water-air interface → apparent position of stick bottom differs)", "Scattering"], ans: 2, exp: "Refraction: light from the submerged part of the stick bends away from normal as it exits water (denser) into air (rarer) → object appears shallower/bent. \"Objects in water appear closer to surface.\"" },
    { q: "The angular size of the sun and moon as seen from Earth are approximately equal. This explains:", opts: ["Why they are the same temperature", "Why total solar eclipses occur (moon can exactly cover the sun's disc)", "Why both are visible", "Why they have the same colour"], ans: 1, exp: "Sun diameter is ~400× larger but ~400× farther away → same angular size (~0.5°). This coincidence means the moon can exactly cover the sun's disc → total solar eclipses. Pure astronomical coincidence." },
    { q: "What is the function of aqueous humour in the eye?", opts: ["Provides shape to the eyeball only", "Nourishes the lens and cornea (avascular) and maintains intraocular pressure", "Absorbs light", "Contains photoreceptors"], ans: 1, exp: "Aqueous humour: clear fluid between cornea and lens. Nourishes lens and cornea (no blood supply). Maintains intraocular pressure. Vitreous humour: gel in posterior chamber (between lens and retina), maintains eye shape." },
    { q: "The danger of looking at the sun during a solar eclipse with naked eyes is:", opts: ["The moon is too bright", "During the eclipse, retinal damage occurs because intense sunlight burns rods and cones (especially fovea) — no pain felt", "Moonlight is harmful", "UV light is absent during eclipse"], ans: 1, exp: "Solar eclipse: corona during totality is safe, but partial eclipse — unfiltered sunlight (especially UV and IR) causes photochemical and thermal damage to retinal cells. No pain as retina has no pain receptors. Causes \"eclipse blindness.\"" },
    { q: "The cornea contributes approximately _____% of the eye's total refractive power.", opts: ["10%", "30%", "70% (about 43 of the total 60 dioptres)", "50%"], ans: 2, exp: "The cornea provides ~43 D of the eye's total ~60 D refractive power (~70%). The lens adds the remaining ~17 D and can vary (accommodation). Cornea is fixed; lens is adjustable." },
    { q: "Chromatic aberration in lenses is caused by:", opts: ["The lens being dirty", "Different wavelengths of light bending by different amounts (dispersion in lens)", "Spherical aberration", "Reflection losses"], ans: 1, exp: "Chromatic aberration: different colours refract by different amounts → different focal lengths → coloured fringes around images. Corrected by achromatic doublet: convex (crown glass) + concave (flint glass) lens combination." },
    { q: "A person can see clearly between 15 cm and 150 cm. They are suffering from:", opts: ["Only myopia", "Only hypermetropia", "Both myopia and hypermetropia (near point 15 cm < 25 cm suggests hypermetropia; far point 150 cm < ∞ suggests myopia)", "Only presbyopia"], ans: 2, exp: "Far point 150 cm (< infinity) → myopic. Near point 15 cm (< 25 cm) actually suggests they can see near objects — this is the myope's situation. But if question means near point farther than 25 cm, that would be hypermetropia. With both defects simultaneously → bifocal lenses needed." },
    { q: "The defect in which a person cannot distinguish between certain colours (usually red and green) is:", opts: ["Astigmatism", "Nyctalopia (night blindness)", "Colour blindness (achromatopsia)", "Diplopia"], ans: 2, exp: "Colour blindness: absence or malfunction of one or more types of cone cells. Red-green colour blindness is most common (X-linked recessive). More common in males (1 in 12) than females (1 in 200)." },
    { q: "Night blindness (nyctalopia) is caused by deficiency of:", opts: ["Vitamin C", "Vitamin D", "Vitamin A (needed for rhodopsin — visual pigment in rods)", "Iron"], ans: 2, exp: "Night blindness: rods contain rhodopsin (visual purple). Rhodopsin synthesis requires retinal (derived from Vitamin A). Vitamin A deficiency → insufficient rhodopsin → inability to see in dim light." },
    { q: "The refractive power of a combination of two thin lenses in contact is:", opts: ["P₁ × P₂", "P₁ − P₂", "P₁ + P₂", "P₁/P₂"], ans: 2, exp: "Thin lenses in contact: 1/f = 1/f₁ + 1/f₂ → P = P₁ + P₂. Powers add (not focal lengths). Used in camera lenses, eyeglasses, and microscopes where multiple lenses are combined." },
    { q: "LASIK surgery corrects myopia by:", opts: ["Replacing the lens", "Using laser to reshape (flatten) the cornea, reducing its converging power", "Injecting fluid to lengthen the eye", "Inserting a lens implant"], ans: 1, exp: "LASIK (Laser-Assisted In Situ Keratomileusis): excimer laser ablates corneal tissue → cornea flattened → reduced refractive power → corrects myopia. For hypermetropia: cornea is steepened." },
    { q: "The cornea and the lens of the eye together act as a:", opts: ["Plane mirror", "Diverging system", "Converging lens system forming a real, inverted image on retina", "Prism dispersing light"], ans: 2, exp: "The eye's optical system (cornea + lens) forms a real, inverted, diminished image on the retina. The brain interprets it right-side up. Total refracting power ≈ 60 dioptres." },
    { q: "Strabismus (squint) is a condition where:", opts: ["Both eyes see the same image", "Eyes are not aligned properly (one or both turn in/out/up/down)", "The lens is cloudy", "Intraocular pressure is raised"], ans: 1, exp: "Strabismus: misalignment of eyes — one eye may turn inward (esotropia), outward (exotropia), up or down. Causes double vision or suppression of one eye's image (amblyopia). Treated with glasses, patching, or surgery." },
    { q: "The intensity of scattered light is inversely proportional to the 4th power of wavelength. Red light is scattered LEAST because:", opts: ["Red has highest frequency", "Red has longest wavelength → 1/λ⁴ is smallest → least scattered", "Red photons have less energy", "Red is absorbed by atmosphere"], ans: 1, exp: "Rayleigh scattering: I ∝ 1/λ⁴. Red (λ ≈ 700 nm) vs violet (λ ≈ 400 nm): ratio = (700/400)⁴ ≈ 9.4. Red is scattered ~9.4× less than violet, so red penetrates long atmospheric distances." },
    { q: "What is the role of the choroid layer in the eye?", opts: ["Provides shape", "Transparent covering", "Absorbs stray light (prevents internal reflections) and provides blood supply to retina", "Responsible for focusing"], ans: 2, exp: "Choroid: dark brown vascular layer between retina and sclera. Contains melanin (absorbs stray light → prevents internal reflections → sharper image) + blood vessels (nourishes retina)." },
    { q: "Intraocular lens (IOL) implant is used in the surgical treatment of:", opts: ["Glaucoma", "Myopia surgically", "Cataract (cloudy lens removed and replaced with IOL)", "Night blindness"], ans: 2, exp: "Cataract surgery: opaque natural lens removed → replaced with clear intraocular lens (IOL) implant. Very effective; one of the most commonly performed surgeries worldwide." },
    { q: "If a person has myopia of −3 D (power of corrective lens), their far point is at:", opts: ["3 m", "−3 m", "1/3 m ≈ 33 cm", "3 cm"], ans: 2, exp: "Far point for a −3 D myope: the corrective concave lens of −3 D has f = −1/3 m ≈ −33 cm. The far point is approximately 1/3 m ≈ 33 cm in front of the eye (the maximum distance they see clearly)." },
    { q: "Deep sea water appears deep blue because:", opts: ["Sea water is chemically blue", "Red and orange light is absorbed by water in the first few metres; blue light penetrates deepest", "Refraction disperses blue light downward", "Bioluminescent organisms make it blue"], ans: 1, exp: "Water selectively absorbs longer wavelengths (red, orange) within a few metres. Blue light has the shortest wavelength of the visible spectrum that penetrates water deeply → deep sea appears deep blue." },
    { q: "Why is the sky near the horizon less blue (more whitish) than overhead?", opts: ["Less scattering near horizon", "Sunlight near horizon passes through more atmosphere → more wavelengths scattered → white/hazy appearance; overhead has more pure blue", "Clouds are white", "Different atmospheric composition"], ans: 1, exp: "Near the horizon, sunlight has a longer atmospheric path → all short wavelengths are scattered multiple times → white/hazy. Overhead: shorter path → blue dominates. This is why the zenith sky is deeper blue." },
    { q: "The total power of the human eye (cornea + lens + aqueous + vitreous) is approximately:", opts: ["20 D", "40 D", "60 D (about 60 dioptres)", "100 D"], ans: 2, exp: "Total optical power of the human eye ≈ 60 dioptres. Cornea contributes ~43 D (fixed), crystalline lens ~17 D (variable, 14-30 D with accommodation). Focal length ≈ 1.67 cm (distance to retina)." },
    { q: "The image of a distant object formed on the retina is:", opts: ["Erect and magnified", "Erect and diminished", "Inverted and real (brain corrects for orientation)", "Virtual and inverted"], ans: 2, exp: "The eye forms a real, inverted, diminished image on the retina (just like a camera). The visual cortex processes and interprets this inverted signal as right-side up. Verified by experiments with inverting goggles." },
    { q: "What is the approximate wavelength range of visible light?", opts: ["100-400 nm (UV)", "400-700 nm (VIBGYOR range)", "700-1000 nm (infrared)", "1-100 nm (X-ray)"], ans: 1, exp: "Visible light: ~400 nm (violet) to ~700 nm (red). Below 400 nm: UV light. Above 700 nm: infrared. Human eye is most sensitive to green light (~555 nm)." },
    { q: "A fish inside water looking at an object above water surface sees it:", opts: ["At its actual position", "Closer to the surface than it actually is (due to refraction)", "Deeper in the water", "Inverted"], ans: 1, exp: "Refraction at water-air interface: light bends when going from water (denser) to air (rarer). Fish looking up: objects above water appear elevated/closer to the surface than they actually are (apparent depth effect)." },
    { q: "Bifocal lenses are used for correction of:", opts: ["Myopia only", "Presbyopia combined with myopia or hypermetropia (upper half for distance, lower for near)", "Astigmatism", "Colour blindness"], ans: 1, exp: "Bifocal lenses: upper portion is concave (for myopic distance correction) or no power (emmetrope), lower portion is convex (for near reading). Invented by Benjamin Franklin for people with both distance and near vision problems." },
    { q: "The sclera (white of the eye) is the:", opts: ["Inner light-sensitive layer", "Tough outer protective coat of the eyeball", "Transparent front layer", "Pigmented layer"], ans: 1, exp: "Sclera: the tough, white, outer coat of the eyeball that gives it shape and protects the delicate inner layers. The transparent cornea is the anterior (front) portion of the sclera." },
    { q: "Optical illusions occur because:", opts: ["Eyes are defective", "The brain misinterprets visual information based on context, prior experience, or ambiguous cues", "Light bends abnormally", "Photoreceptors malfunction"], ans: 1, exp: "Optical illusions: the visual cortex makes incorrect inferences about visual input. The brain uses shortcuts/assumptions based on experience → can be misled by ambiguous or misleading visual patterns." },
    { q: "The Snellen chart is used to measure:", opts: ["Colour vision", "Visual acuity (sharpness of vision at distance)", "Intraocular pressure", "Accommodation range"], ans: 1, exp: "Snellen chart: letters of decreasing size read from 6m distance. 6/6 vision (or 20/20 in USA) = normal. 6/12 means you can see at 6m what a normal person sees at 12m. Standard eye test." },
    { q: "Progressive addition lenses (varifocals) differ from bifocals in that they:", opts: ["Only correct one vision problem", "Have a gradual change in lens power from top to bottom (no visible dividing line)", "Are only for myopia", "Have three distinct zones"], ans: 1, exp: "Varifocals (progressive lenses): gradual transition from distance correction (top) through intermediate to reading correction (bottom) — no abrupt line visible. More aesthetically pleasing than bifocals." },
    { q: "A magnifying glass is a:", opts: ["Concave lens", "Plane mirror", "Convex lens used to produce a virtual, magnified, erect image when object is within focal length", "Concave mirror"], ans: 2, exp: "Magnifying glass: convex lens. Object placed between lens and focus → virtual, erect, magnified image on same side as object. Angular magnification ≈ D/f + 1 (where D = 25 cm = least distance of distinct vision)." },
    { q: "The \"red-eye\" effect in flash photography occurs because:", opts: ["Camera flash is red", "Flash reflects off the red choroid/retina blood vessels through the dilated pupil (low light → large pupil)", "The eye reflects all colours as red", "Film is red-sensitive"], ans: 1, exp: "Red-eye: in dim conditions, pupil dilates. Flash enters through large pupil → reflects off the reddish blood vessels of the choroid/retina → exits back through pupil → appears red in photo." },
    { q: "A person's near point is 50 cm. The power of the corrective lens needed to read a book at 25 cm is:", opts: ["+2 D", "+4 D", "−2 D", "+1 D"], ans: 0, exp: "The person needs an image at −50 cm (their near point) when holding the book at 25 cm (u = −25 cm). 1/v − 1/u = 1/f → 1/(−50) − 1/(−25) = 1/f → −1/50 + 2/50 = 1/50. P = 1/f = +2 D. The lens is converging (convex)." },
    { q: "A myopic person has a far point of 80 cm. The power of the corrective spectacle lens is:", opts: ["−1.25 D", "+1.25 D", "−0.8 D", "+0.8 D"], ans: 0, exp: "For myopia correction, the lens must form a virtual image of a distant object (u = ∞) at the far point (v = −0.80 m). 1/f = 1/v − 1/u = 1/(−0.80) − 0 = −1.25 m⁻¹. So P = −1.25 D. The negative sign confirms a concave (diverging) lens is needed." },
    { q: "The phenomenon of Rayleigh scattering states that the intensity of scattered light is proportional to:", opts: ["1/λ⁴ (inverse fourth power of wavelength)", "1/λ² ", "λ²", "λ"], ans: 0, exp: "Rayleigh scattering: I ∝ 1/λ⁴. Shorter wavelengths (violet, blue) scatter far more than longer wavelengths (red, orange). This is why the sky appears blue — blue light scatters most in all directions." },
    { q: "At sunset, the sun appears red because:", opts: ["Blue and violet are scattered away leaving red to travel the long atmospheric path", "Red light refracts more", "The sun emits more red light at sunset", "Dust absorbs blue light only at sunrise/sunset"], ans: 0, exp: "At sunrise/sunset, sunlight travels through a much longer atmospheric path. Blue/violet components (short λ) are largely scattered away, and the remaining transmitted light is enriched in red/orange wavelengths, making the sun and sky appear red-orange." },
    { q: "Which part of the human eye contains the maximum density of cone cells?", opts: ["Fovea (yellow spot)", "Optic disc (blind spot)", "Peripheral retina", "Iris"], ans: 0, exp: "The fovea centralis (yellow spot) is the region of sharpest vision and contains the highest density of cone cells. It is the area where the lens focuses light during direct (foveal) vision. The optic disc has no photoreceptors." },
    { q: "A hypermetropic person has a near point of 1 m. The power of lens needed for reading at 25 cm is:", opts: ["+3 D", "+4 D", "+1 D", "+5 D"], ans: 0, exp: "The corrective lens must produce a virtual image at 1 m (near point) from a book at 25 cm. u = −0.25 m, v = −1.0 m. 1/f = 1/v − 1/u = 1/(−1.0) − 1/(−0.25) = −1 + 4 = +3. P = +3 D." },
    { q: "Dispersion of white light through a glass prism occurs because:", opts: ["Different wavelengths have different refractive indices in glass (n_violet > n_red)", "White light splits because of interference", "The prism absorbs some colours", "Different colours travel at different speeds in vacuum"], ans: 0, exp: "Glass shows chromatic dispersion: n increases as wavelength decreases (VIBGYOR). Violet (λ ≈ 400 nm) bends most (highest n), red (λ ≈ 700 nm) bends least. This separates white light into its spectrum. Light speeds in vacuum are identical for all wavelengths." },
    { q: "The phenomenon of accommodation refers to the eye's ability to:", opts: ["Adjust the curvature of the ciliary-lens system to focus objects at different distances", "Adjust pupil size for different light intensities", "Adapt to darkness over time", "Distinguish between colours"], ans: 0, exp: "Accommodation: the ciliary muscles change the tension on the zonular fibres, altering the curvature (and hence focal length) of the crystalline lens to focus near or far objects sharply on the retina." },
    { q: "In presbyopia, both near and distant vision are blurred because:", opts: ["The lens loses elasticity with age and cannot change curvature effectively for either distance", "The cornea thickens", "The retina degenerates", "The pupil cannot dilate"], ans: 0, exp: "Presbyopia is an age-related condition where the crystalline lens hardens and loses elasticity. Ciliary muscles cannot change its curvature effectively. The range of accommodation narrows, making both near and far vision poor. Bifocal lenses correct both." },
    { q: "A rainbow is always seen in the direction _____ the sun:", opts: ["Opposite to (observer faces away from sun)", "Same as (observer faces the sun)", "Perpendicular to", "At 42° toward the sun"], ans: 0, exp: "A rainbow is seen when the sun is behind the observer and rain/drops are in front. Light enters the drops, undergoes total internal reflection inside, and exits back toward the observer. The primary rainbow subtends about 42° from the anti-solar point." },
    { q: "Stars do not twinkle but planets do not. The primary reason planets do not twinkle is:", opts: ["Planets are extended sources; the fluctuations in many light paths average out", "Planets emit their own light", "Planets are closer and their light is not refracted", "Planets reflect infrared light"], ans: 0, exp: "Planets, being much closer, subtend a small but finite disc (extended source). The random atmospheric fluctuations affecting different parts of the disc average out, giving a steady apparent brightness. Stars are point sources so no such averaging occurs." },
    { q: "The angle of deviation of violet light in a prism is greater than that of red light. This means:", opts: ["n_violet > n_red, so violet refracts more at each surface", "Violet light travels faster in glass", "Violet wavelength is longer", "Violet undergoes total internal reflection at smaller angles"], ans: 0, exp: "In glass, n_violet > n_red (normal dispersion). Greater n means more bending at the glass surfaces per Snell's law. So violet deviates more overall through the prism, appearing at the bottom of the VIBGYOR spectrum." },
    { q: "A student uses a corrective lens of power +1.5 D. The far point of this person without glasses is:", opts: ["66.7 cm", "150 cm", "25 cm", "200 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "If the person uses +1.5 D (converging) for distant vision correction (hypermetropia), the lens forms a virtual image of infinity at the person's far point: v = 1/P = 1/1.5 m = 0.667 m = 66.7 cm behind (virtual)." },
    { q: "In the human eye, the iris functions to:", opts: ["Control the amount of light entering by varying pupil size", "Focus light onto the retina", "Detect colour", "Convert light to electrical signals"], ans: 0, exp: "The iris is a muscular diaphragm that controls the diameter of the pupil (the opening). In bright light, the iris constricts the pupil; in dim light, it dilates. This regulates the amount of light reaching the retina, protecting photoreceptors." },
    { q: "The order of colours in a primary rainbow from inner to outer arc is:", opts: ["Violet inside, red outside (VIBGYOR from inside)", "Red inside, violet outside", "Green inside and outside, red in middle", "Random order"], ans: 0, exp: "In a primary rainbow, violet light (refracted most) exits at a smaller angle (~40°) and appears on the inner arc. Red (refracted least) exits at ~42° and appears on the outer arc. The order inside to outside is VIBGYOR." },
    { q: "For the correction of astigmatism, which type of lens is used?", opts: ["Cylindrical lens", "Bifocal lens", "Concave spherical lens", "Convex spherical lens"], ans: 0, exp: "Astigmatism is caused by unequal curvature of the cornea in different planes, causing different focal lengths in horizontal and vertical planes. A cylindrical lens, which has curvature in only one plane, corrects the difference in refraction between axes." },
    { q: "The resolving power of the eye depends mainly on the density of:", opts: ["Cone cells in the fovea", "Rod cells in the periphery", "Lens curvature", "Size of the pupil"], ans: 0, exp: "Visual acuity (resolving power) is maximum at the fovea where cone density is highest (~150,000/mm²). Two closely spaced points can be resolved only if their images fall on different cones. Rods provide low-resolution, monochromatic peripheral vision." },
    { q: "A person uses lenses of power +2.5 D for distance vision. The person suffers from:", opts: ["Hypermetropia (far-sightedness)", "Myopia (near-sightedness)", "Presbyopia only", "Astigmatism"], ans: 0, exp: "Power +2.5 D is a converging (convex) lens. Convex lenses are used to correct hypermetropia, where the far point is virtual (behind the eye) — the eyeball is too short or cornea too flat, so parallel rays focus behind the retina." },
    { q: "In scattering, which colour is scattered least by atmospheric particles?", opts: ["Red (λ ≈ 700 nm)", "Blue (λ ≈ 450 nm)", "Violet (λ ≈ 400 nm)", "Green (λ ≈ 550 nm)"], ans: 0, exp: "By Rayleigh scattering (I ∝ 1/λ⁴), red light with the longest wavelength in the visible spectrum is scattered least. This is why red is used for danger signals — it is transmitted through fog and haze with minimal scattering." },
    { q: "The secondary rainbow has colours in what order compared to the primary rainbow?", opts: ["Reversed — red inside, violet outside", "Same order as primary", "No colour — white band", "Only red and violet visible"], ans: 0, exp: "In the secondary rainbow, light undergoes two internal reflections inside the water drop. This reverses the colour sequence: red is on the inside arc (~51°) and violet on the outside (~53°). The secondary rainbow is also fainter than the primary." },
    { q: "What is the far point of a normal (emmetropic) human eye?", opts: ["Infinity", "25 cm", "250 cm", "15 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "For a normal eye, the far point is at optical infinity — the eye can focus on objects very far away with its ciliary muscles fully relaxed. The near point of a young adult is typically 25 cm." },
    { q: "The power of the cornea in the human eye is approximately:", opts: ["40 D", "20 D", "60 D", "10 D"], ans: 0, exp: "The cornea contributes about 40 D of the eye's total refracting power (~60 D). The crystalline lens adds the remaining ~20 D, with range for accommodation. The cornea is thus the primary refracting surface of the eye." },
    { q: "A person cannot see objects beyond 2 m clearly. Without glasses, the person can see objects at 25 cm. The person needs:", opts: ["Concave lens of −0.5 D", "Convex lens of +0.5 D", "Concave lens of −2 D", "Convex lens of +4 D"], ans: 0, exp: "Far point = 2 m = 200 cm. For correction, distant objects (u = ∞) must be imaged at 2 m (v = −2 m). 1/f = 1/(−2) − 0 = −0.5. P = −0.5 D. A concave lens of −0.5 D corrects the myopia." },
    { q: "Dispersion of light is NOT observed when white light passes through a:", opts: ["Glass slab with parallel faces", "Prism", "Convex lens", "Diffraction grating"], ans: 0, exp: "A glass slab with parallel faces disperses light at entry but the second surface reverses the dispersion, so the emergent white beam has the same direction as incident (only shifted). The colours do not separate permanently — no net dispersion occurs." },
    { q: "The blind spot (optic disc) in the human eye:", opts: ["Contains no photoreceptors — where the optic nerve exits", "Has the highest concentration of cones", "Has only rod cells", "Is responsible for colour vision"], ans: 0, exp: "The optic disc is the point where all nerve fibres from retinal ganglion cells converge to form the optic nerve. There are no rods or cones here, making it insensitive to light. Objects imaged on this spot cannot be seen — the blind spot." },
    { q: "The sky appears dark (black) from outer space because:", opts: ["There are no air molecules to scatter sunlight", "The sun is not visible from space", "Space absorbs all light", "Gravity bends light away from space travelers"], ans: 0, exp: "Rayleigh scattering requires atmospheric molecules. In outer space, there is no atmosphere, so sunlight is not scattered in all directions. The sky appears black (no scattered light), and the sun, stars, and planets are visible as intense point sources." },
    { q: "A myopic student with far point at 60 cm wears −2 D lenses. After wearing glasses, the effective far point is:", opts: ["Infinity", "40 cm", "80 cm", "120 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "The −2 D lens: f = −50 cm. An object at infinity forms virtual image at v = −50 cm (50 cm from lens). The student's far point is 60 cm, but the image from the −2 D lens is at 50 cm — closer than their unaided far point. Strictly: for exact correction, far point = 60 cm, P = 1/(−0.60) = −1.67 D. With −2 D, distant objects are imaged slightly closer than the far point, so the effective range extends to infinity for practical purposes." },
    { q: "In the human eye, accommodation is brought about by:", opts: ["Ciliary muscles changing the tension on the crystalline lens", "Movement of the eyeball", "Pupil dilation only", "Cornea changing its curvature"], ans: 0, exp: "When ciliary muscles contract, the tension on the suspensory (zonular) fibres decreases, allowing the elastic crystalline lens to become more curved (shorter focal length) for near objects. Relaxation flattens the lens for distant objects." },
    { q: "The colour addition of red, green, and blue light in equal intensities produces:", opts: ["White light", "Yellow light", "Cyan light", "Black"], ans: 0, exp: "Red, green, and blue are primary colours of light (additive mixing). Their equal combination produces white light. This is the principle behind RGB screens. In contrast, pigment (subtractive) primaries are cyan, magenta, and yellow." },
    { q: "Why does the sky appear deep blue rather than violet, even though violet is scattered more by Rayleigh scattering?", opts: ["The sun emits less violet light; human eyes are more sensitive to blue; and much violet is absorbed in upper atmosphere", "Violet light is completely absorbed by ozone", "The eye cannot detect violet at all", "Violet and blue mix to form blue"], ans: 0, exp: "Three factors: (1) The solar spectrum has less violet than blue energy. (2) Human cone cells are less sensitive to violet. (3) Some violet is absorbed by atmospheric ozone. The net effect makes scattered blue light the dominant colour perceived." },
    { q: "The minimum distance of distinct vision for a normal human eye is:", opts: ["25 cm", "10 cm", "50 cm", "15 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "The near point of a normal (young adult) human eye is 25 cm — the minimum distance at which the eye can focus without strain. This is also called the least distance of distinct vision (D) and is used in magnification calculations." },
    { q: "A person's corrective lens for myopia has focal length −25 cm. The far point of the person's unaided eye is:", opts: ["25 cm", "50 cm", "−25 cm", "100 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "The lens images distant objects (u = ∞) at the far point: v = f = −25 cm (25 cm in front of lens/eye). So the far point of the unaided eye is 25 cm. Without glasses, the person cannot see anything beyond 25 cm clearly." },
    { q: "Which phenomenon causes the advance in the sun's apparent position (the sun is visible ~2 minutes before actual sunrise)?", opts: ["Atmospheric refraction bends light from below-horizon sun toward the observer", "Reflection from the upper atmosphere", "Diffraction around Earth's curvature", "Gravitational lensing"], ans: 0, exp: "Atmospheric refraction: as the sun is just below the horizon, its light enters the progressively denser atmosphere and bends downward (toward Earth). This allows us to see the sun about 2 minutes before it geometrically rises and 2 minutes after it geometrically sets." },
    { q: "When yellow light (secondary colour) is mixed with blue light, the result is:", opts: ["White light", "Green light", "Red light", "Magenta light"], ans: 0, exp: "Yellow = Red + Green (in additive mixing). Yellow + Blue = Red + Green + Blue = White. These complementary colour pairs (yellow–blue, magenta–green, cyan–red) combine to give white light." },
    { q: "The critical angle for glass (n = 1.5) to air is approximately 41.8°. If light strikes the glass-air interface at 45°, it:", opts: ["Undergoes total internal reflection (45° > 41.8°)", "Partially refracts into air", "Refracts fully into air", "Is absorbed at the surface"], ans: 0, exp: "Since 45° > critical angle 41.8°, the condition for total internal reflection is met. All light is reflected back into the glass; no light is transmitted into air. This is exploited in optical fibres and prism-based periscopes." },
    { q: "A person has a near point of 40 cm and a far point of 200 cm. To correct both defects, they need:", opts: ["Bifocal glasses — concave top for distance, convex bottom for reading", "Only a concave lens", "Only a convex lens", "Contact lenses only"], ans: 0, exp: "Far point 200 cm < ∞ → myopia (needs concave lens for distance). Near point 40 cm > 25 cm → hypermetropia (needs convex lens for reading). This combination is presbyopia-like and requires bifocal (or progressive) lenses with different powers for distance and near vision." },
    { q: "The primary rainbow is observed at approximately _____° from the anti-solar point:", opts: ["42°", "51°", "90°", "180°"], ans: 0, exp: "The primary rainbow is seen at ~42° from the anti-solar direction (the point directly opposite the sun from the observer's perspective). This angle arises from two refractions and one total internal reflection in each water droplet." },
    { q: "Dark adaptation of the human eye involves:", opts: ["Regeneration of rhodopsin (visual purple) in rod cells in low light", "Pupil constriction", "Increased cone activity", "Cornea thickening"], ans: 0, exp: "In bright light, rhodopsin (the photopigment in rods) is bleached. In darkness, it regenerates gradually (over ~20–30 minutes). As rhodopsin levels rise, rod sensitivity increases enormously, allowing vision in very dim conditions — dark adaptation." },
    { q: "Which of the following describes a person with 6/60 vision?", opts: ["They see at 6 m what a normal person sees at 60 m (severely impaired)", "They see at 60 m what normal see at 6 m (excellent)", "They have 6 mm pupil and 60 mm lens", "Their near point is 6 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "6/60 (Snellen notation): the person can only read at 6 m what a person with normal vision can read at 60 m. Normal vision is 6/6. This represents significant visual impairment (legally blind threshold in many countries is ≤ 6/60)." },
    { q: "Monochromatic red light passes through a glass prism. The emerging light is:", opts: ["A single red beam, deviated but not dispersed", "A spectrum of colours with red dominant", "Dispersed into VIBGYOR", "Unchanged in direction"], ans: 0, exp: "Dispersion only occurs with polychromatic (multi-wavelength) light. Monochromatic red light has a single wavelength, so it refracts (deviates) at a single angle with no splitting into colours — only a single red beam emerges, deviated by the prism." },
    { q: "The sky on the moon appears black even during daytime because:", opts: ["The moon has no atmosphere, so there are no molecules to scatter sunlight", "The moon is too far from the sun", "The moon's gravity is too low to hold light", "Moonlight is not strong enough to illuminate the sky"], ans: 0, exp: "The blue sky on Earth is due to Rayleigh scattering by atmospheric molecules. The moon has essentially no atmosphere. Without molecules to scatter sunlight in all directions, the sky is black, and stars are visible even during lunar daytime." },
    { q: "For a myopic person, using a concave lens shifts the far point to infinity. If the lens creates a virtual image of a distant object 150 cm in front of the lens, the power of the lens is:", opts: ["−0.67 D", "+0.67 D", "−1.5 D", "+1.5 D"], ans: 0, exp: "v = −150 cm = −1.5 m (virtual image, same side as object), u = ∞. 1/f = 1/v − 1/u = 1/(−1.5) − 0 = −1/1.5. P = −1/1.5 ≈ −0.67 D." },
    { q: "The dispersion of white light through a prism and its recombination using a second inverted prism was demonstrated by:", opts: ["Newton (two-prism experiment)", "Huygens", "Young", "Maxwell"], ans: 0, exp: "Isaac Newton used two prisms — the first to disperse white light into VIBGYOR, the second (inverted) to recombine the spectrum back into white light. This proved that white light contains all colours and that the prism does not add colour." },
    { q: "The photoreceptors responsible for colour vision and functioning in bright light are:", opts: ["Cone cells (three types: S, M, L for blue, green, red)", "Rod cells only", "Ganglion cells", "Bipolar cells"], ans: 0, exp: "Cone cells contain three types of photopigments sensitive to short (blue), medium (green), and long (red) wavelengths. They function in bright (photopic) light and enable colour discrimination. Rods (contain rhodopsin) work in dim (scotopic) light, providing monochromatic vision." },
    { q: "A child's near point is 7 cm (too close). This condition is called:", opts: ["Myopia — the ciliary muscles are too tense even at rest", "Hypermetropia", "Presbyopia", "Astigmatism"], ans: 0, exp: "An unusually close near point (less than 10 cm) in children can indicate myopia or simply high accommodation power. Myopia causes the far point to be closer than infinity and often involves the near point being unusually close. A near point of 7 cm with a close far point is consistent with myopia." },
    { q: "In the formation of a rainbow, the role of total internal reflection is to:", opts: ["Reflect light back inside the water drop before it exits, separating colours by exit angle", "Prevent any light from exiting", "Increase the wavelength of light", "Create white light"], ans: 0, exp: "Light enters the raindrop, refracts (dispersing colours), undergoes one total internal reflection off the back surface (primary rainbow), then refracts again on exiting. The double refraction + TIR separates colours by exit angle, creating the rainbow arc." },
    { q: "A spectacle lens has a power of +3.5 D. The focal length in cm is:", opts: ["28.6 cm", "35 cm", "3.5 cm", "350 cm"], ans: 0, exp: "f = 1/P = 1/3.5 m = 0.286 m = 28.6 cm. The positive power confirms it is a converging (convex) lens used to correct hypermetropia." },
    { q: "Two persons A and B have far points of 40 cm and 100 cm respectively. Person A needs a lens of power:", opts: ["−2.5 D (more powerful concave)", "−1 D", "+2.5 D", "+1 D"], ans: 0, exp: "For person A: far point = 40 cm = 0.4 m. Corrective lens: 1/f = 1/(−0.4) = −2.5 D. Person B: 1/f = 1/(−1.0) = −1 D. Person A is more severely myopic and needs the stronger (more negative) lens." },
    { q: "The \"twinkling\" of stars is NOT observed for stars directly overhead (zenith). The reason is:", opts: ["At zenith, light passes through minimum atmosphere and minimum turbulent path, reducing scintillation", "Stars overhead emit steady light", "The eye adjusts for overhead stars", "Gravity straightens the light path"], ans: 0, exp: "At the zenith, the line of sight passes through the minimum column of atmosphere (approximately one atmosphere mass). Atmospheric turbulence is minimized, so the bending/fluctuation is minimal and twinkling is greatly reduced compared to stars near the horizon." },
    { q: "The image on the retina is:", opts: ["Real, inverted, and much smaller than the object", "Virtual, erect, and same size", "Real, erect, and magnified", "Virtual, inverted, and smaller"], ans: 0, exp: "The eye's optical system (cornea + lens) forms a real, inverted, and diminished image on the retina — similar to a camera. The brain inverts the signal to perceive the world as right-side-up. The retinal image is much smaller (millimeters for objects meters away)." }
  ]
,

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════
     CH 11 — ELECTRICITY
  ══════════════════════════════════════ */
  11: [
    { q: "Ohm's Law states that the current through a conductor is:", opts: ["Directly proportional to resistance at constant voltage", "Directly proportional to the potential difference (V) at constant temperature", "Inversely proportional to potential difference", "Independent of voltage"], ans: 1, exp: "Ohm's Law: V = IR (V ∝ I at constant T). Current is directly proportional to potential difference and inversely proportional to resistance. SI unit of resistance: ohm (Ω)." },
    { q: "The SI unit of electric charge is the:", opts: ["Ampere", "Coulomb", "Volt", "Watt"], ans: 1, exp: "Coulomb (C) is the SI unit of charge. 1 coulomb = charge carried by 6.24 × 10¹⁸ electrons. Current I = Q/t (charge per unit time). 1 ampere = 1 coulomb/second." },
    { q: "The resistance of a conductor depends on its length (L), cross-sectional area (A) and resistivity (ρ). The formula is:", opts: ["R = ρLA", "R = ρL/A", "R = L/ρA", "R = A/ρL"], ans: 1, exp: "R = ρL/A. Resistance increases with length (longer → more collisions), decreases with area (wider → more paths). ρ (resistivity): material property, depends on material and temperature." },
    { q: "A 5 Ω and 10 Ω resistor are connected in series. The equivalent resistance is:", opts: ["2 Ω", "15 Ω", "3.33 Ω", "50 Ω"], ans: 1, exp: "Series: R_total = R₁ + R₂ + ... = 5 + 10 = 15 Ω. Current is same through all; total voltage = sum of individual voltage drops." },
    { q: "A 5 Ω and 10 Ω resistor are connected in parallel. The equivalent resistance is:", opts: ["15 Ω", "7.5 Ω", "3.33 Ω", "2 Ω"], ans: 2, exp: "1/R = 1/5 + 1/10 = 2/10 + 1/10 = 3/10. R = 10/3 ≈ 3.33 Ω. Parallel resistance is always less than the smallest individual resistor. Voltage is same across all; currents differ." },
    { q: "In a parallel circuit, which quantity is the same across all branches?", opts: ["Current", "Resistance", "Voltage (potential difference)", "Power"], ans: 2, exp: "Parallel circuit: voltage (p.d.) is the same across all branches. Current divides inversely proportional to resistance (I ∝ 1/R). In series: current is same, voltage divides." },
    { q: "The potential difference across a 10 Ω resistor carrying 3 A of current is:", opts: ["0.3 V", "13 V", "30 V", "3 V"], ans: 2, exp: "V = IR = 3 × 10 = 30 V. Ohm's law directly applied. Always check units: V (volts) = I (amperes) × R (ohms)." },
    { q: "Electric power is defined as:", opts: ["P = V/I", "P = VI = I²R = V²/R", "P = VR", "P = V²R"], ans: 1, exp: "P = VI = I²R = V²/R. SI unit: watt (W). 1 W = 1 J/s = 1 V × 1 A. Power depends on both voltage and current. Higher voltage and current → more power." },
    { q: "A 60 W bulb is used for 2 hours. The energy consumed in kWh (units) is:", opts: ["0.12 kWh", "120 kWh", "0.03 kWh", "30 kWh"], ans: 0, exp: "Energy = Power × Time = 60 W × 2 h = 120 Wh = 0.12 kWh (units). 1 unit = 1 kWh = 1000 W × 1 hour. If electricity costs ₹6/unit, cost = 0.12 × 6 = ₹0.72." },
    { q: "The heating effect of electric current is given by Joule's law: H = ?", opts: ["H = I²Rt", "H = V/It", "H = VR/t", "H = V²t/R²"], ans: 0, exp: "Joule's heating law: H = I²Rt (joules). Heat produced is proportional to square of current, resistance, and time. Basis of electric heaters, toasters, geysers, electric irons, fuses." },
    { q: "A fuse wire in an electric circuit works by:", opts: ["Increasing current when overloaded", "Melting and breaking the circuit when current exceeds safe limit (high resistance, low melting point)", "Reducing voltage automatically", "Blocking current always"], ans: 1, exp: "Fuse: thin wire of low melting point (often tin-lead alloy). Excess current → I²R heating → fuse melts → circuit breaks → protects appliances from overload/short circuit. MCB (miniature circuit breaker) is modern alternative." },
    { q: "The resistance of nichrome (used in heaters) is high. The advantage of using nichrome is:", opts: ["It doesn't get hot", "High resistance + high melting point → high heat production without melting", "It is cheap", "It is a superconductor"], ans: 1, exp: "Nichrome (Ni-Cr alloy): high resistivity → significant heat generated (H = I²Rt). High melting point (~1400°C) → doesn't melt at operating temperatures. Used in electric heaters, toasters, hair dryers." },
    { q: "A voltmeter is always connected in _____ with the component being measured.", opts: ["Series", "Parallel", "Perpendicular", "Either"], ans: 1, exp: "Voltmeter measures potential difference → connected in PARALLEL with the component. It should have very high resistance to draw negligible current (avoid affecting the circuit). Ammeter: in series, very low resistance." },
    { q: "An ammeter is always connected in _____ in a circuit.", opts: ["Parallel", "Series", "Either", "Between two components but in parallel"], ans: 1, exp: "Ammeter measures current → in SERIES (all current passes through it). Very low resistance to minimise voltage drop. If connected in parallel → short circuit (most current bypasses the component)." },
    { q: "The resistivity of conductors, semiconductors, and insulators compares as:", opts: ["Conductors > Semiconductors > Insulators", "Insulators > Semiconductors > Conductors", "All equal", "Conductors = Semiconductors < Insulators"], ans: 1, exp: "Resistivity: insulators (10⁸-10¹⁶ Ω·m) >> semiconductors (10⁻⁴-10³ Ω·m) >> conductors (10⁻⁸-10⁻⁶ Ω·m). Copper: ~1.7×10⁻⁸ Ω·m. Silicon: ~640 Ω·m. Glass: ~10¹⁰-10¹⁴ Ω·m." },
    { q: "When temperature of a metallic conductor increases, its resistance:", opts: ["Decreases", "Stays the same", "Increases (more thermal vibrations → more electron collisions)", "Becomes zero"], ans: 2, exp: "Metals: resistance ∝ temperature (positive temperature coefficient). Higher T → more lattice vibrations → more collisions of electrons → greater resistance. Exception: semiconductors (resistance decreases with T)." },
    { q: "Superconductivity is the phenomenon where some materials have:", opts: ["Infinite resistance at low T", "Zero electrical resistance below a critical temperature (T_c)", "Maximum resistivity at absolute zero", "High resistance at any temperature"], ans: 1, exp: "Superconductivity: below critical temperature (T_c), resistance becomes exactly zero. First observed by Heike Kamerlingh Onnes (1911) in mercury at 4.2 K. No energy loss → used in MRI magnets, maglev trains." },
    { q: "1 kWh (kilowatt-hour) equals how many joules?", opts: ["1000 J", "3.6×10³ J", "3.6×10⁶ J", "1×10⁶ J"], ans: 2, exp: "1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6×10⁶ J = 3.6 MJ. This is the commercial unit of energy (1 \"unit\" of electricity). Energy consumed = Power × Time in appropriate units." },
    { q: "Two bulbs are rated 60 W/220 V and 100 W/220 V. Connected in series to 220 V, which one glows brighter?", opts: ["100 W bulb", "60 W bulb", "Both equally bright", "Neither glows"], ans: 1, exp: "60 W bulb at 220 V has higher resistance: R = V²/P = 220²/60 ≈ 807 Ω. 100 W: R = 220²/100 = 484 Ω. In series, same current → H = I²R → higher R (60 W bulb) glows brighter." },
    { q: "Domestic electrical appliances in India are connected in:", opts: ["Series", "Parallel (each gets 220 V independently; failure of one doesn't affect others)", "Mixed series-parallel", "No fixed arrangement"], ans: 1, exp: "Domestic wiring: parallel connection. Each appliance receives 220 V (full mains voltage) independently. Advantages: each switched individually, failure of one doesn't affect others, each draws its own current." },
    { q: "The live wire (phase wire) in domestic wiring is insulated with which colour? (as per Indian standards)", opts: ["Black", "Green", "Red or brown", "Blue"], ans: 2, exp: "Indian/international colour codes: Live (phase) = Red (old) or Brown (new). Neutral = Black (old) or Blue (new). Earth = Green/yellow. Live is at 220 V; neutral at 0 V (ground reference)." },
    { q: "An electric motor converts:", opts: ["Electrical energy to mechanical energy", "Mechanical energy to electrical energy", "Chemical energy to electrical energy", "Heat to electrical energy"], ans: 0, exp: "Electric motor: uses magnetic force on current-carrying conductor → mechanical rotation. Electrical → mechanical. Generator (dynamo): mechanical rotation → electrical (reverse)." },
    { q: "The commercial unit of electrical energy is:", opts: ["Joule", "Watt", "Kilowatt-hour (kWh)", "Volt-ampere"], ans: 2, exp: "kWh (kilowatt-hour) is the commercial/billing unit. Electricity meters measure in kWh (units). 1 unit = 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J. Monthly electricity bill = (total kWh) × (rate per unit)." },
    { q: "Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL) states that at any junction in a circuit:", opts: ["Voltage is constant", "Sum of currents entering = Sum of currents leaving (charge conservation)", "Current increases", "Resistance is zero"], ans: 1, exp: "KCL: ΣI_in = ΣI_out at any node. Based on conservation of charge — charge cannot accumulate at a junction. Important for analysing complex circuits." },
    { q: "A device that converts chemical energy to electrical energy is called a:", opts: ["Motor", "Transformer", "Electrochemical cell/battery", "Capacitor"], ans: 2, exp: "Electrochemical cell/battery (e.g., dry cell, lead-acid battery): chemical reactions drive electrons through external circuit → electrical energy. EMF = energy supplied per coulomb of charge." },
    { q: "The electric field inside a conductor in static equilibrium is:", opts: ["Maximum at the centre", "Equal to the applied voltage", "Zero (free electrons redistribute until field = 0)", "Positive at surface"], ans: 2, exp: "In electrostatic equilibrium: free electrons in conductor redistribute until internal E-field = 0. All excess charge resides on the outer surface. Net E-field inside = 0." },
    { q: "What happens to the equivalent resistance when more resistors are added in parallel?", opts: ["Increases", "Stays the same", "Decreases (more current paths available)", "Doubles each time"], ans: 2, exp: "More resistors in parallel → more current paths → lower equivalent resistance. 1/R_eq = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + ... R_eq is always less than the smallest individual resistor." },
    { q: "A wire of resistance R is stretched to double its length. Its new resistance is:", opts: ["R/2", "2R", "4R", "R"], ans: 2, exp: "Stretching doubles L and halves A (volume constant). R = ρL/A. New R = ρ(2L)/(A/2) = 4ρL/A = 4R. Resistance quadruples when length doubles (volume constant)." },
    { q: "The voltage of a standard dry cell (Leclanche cell) is approximately:", opts: ["6 V", "12 V", "1.5 V", "9 V"], ans: 2, exp: "Standard dry cell (zinc-carbon or alkaline): ~1.5 V EMF. A 9 V battery = 6 cells × 1.5 V in series. Car battery (lead-acid): 6 cells × 2 V = 12 V." },
    { q: "The internal resistance of an ideal voltage source is:", opts: ["Very high", "Equal to external resistance", "Zero (ideal voltage source maintains constant V regardless of current)", "Equal to 1 Ω"], ans: 2, exp: "Ideal voltage source: zero internal resistance → terminal voltage = EMF regardless of current. Real batteries have internal resistance → terminal voltage drops under load: V = EMF − Ir." },
    { q: "Earthing (grounding) of electrical appliances is done to:", opts: ["Increase efficiency", "Provide a safe path for leakage current to the ground, preventing electric shock to users", "Reduce power consumption", "Improve insulation"], ans: 1, exp: "Earthing: metal body of appliance connected to earth via green/yellow wire. If live wire touches the metal body → current flows to ground safely → fuse/MCB blows → user not electrocuted." },
    { q: "The heating element of an electric geyser is made of a material with:", opts: ["Low resistivity and low melting point", "High resistivity and high melting point", "Zero resistivity", "Variable resistivity"], ans: 1, exp: "Geyser heating element: high resistivity → generates more heat (H = I²Rt). High melting point → can operate at high temperatures without melting. Nichrome is commonly used." },
    { q: "If the voltage is doubled and resistance is halved, current becomes:", opts: ["Same", "4 times the original", "2 times", "Half"], ans: 1, exp: "I = V/R. New I = 2V/(R/2) = 4V/R = 4I. Current becomes 4 times the original. This is a common problem combining both Ohm's law variables." },
    { q: "The diode is a device that allows current to flow in:", opts: ["Both directions equally", "Only one direction (forward bias)", "Neither direction", "Alternating directions only"], ans: 1, exp: "Semiconductor diode: allows current in one direction (anode +, cathode −: forward bias). Blocks current in reverse bias. Used in rectifiers (AC → DC), LEDs, solar cells." },
    { q: "Fleming's left-hand rule gives the direction of force on a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field. The thumb points in the direction of:", opts: ["Magnetic field", "Current", "Force (motion) on the conductor", "Electric field"], ans: 2, exp: "Fleming's LHR: Index finger → magnetic field (B), middle finger → current (I), thumb → force/motion (F). Used for motors. Fleming's RHR: used for generators (induced current direction)." },
    { q: "An LED (Light Emitting Diode) converts:", opts: ["Light to electricity", "Electrical energy to light (electroluminescence)", "Heat to light", "Mechanical energy to light"], ans: 1, exp: "LED: semiconductor diode that emits light when forward-biased. Electrons and holes recombine → release energy as photons (electroluminescence). Very energy-efficient (LEDs use 80-90% less energy than incandescent bulbs)." },
    { q: "What is the effect of temperature on the resistance of a semiconductor (like silicon)?", opts: ["Resistance increases with temperature", "Resistance decreases with temperature (negative temperature coefficient)", "No effect", "Resistance becomes zero at high T"], ans: 1, exp: "Semiconductors: higher temperature → more electron-hole pairs thermally generated → more charge carriers → lower resistance. Negative temperature coefficient (NTC) — opposite of metals." },
    { q: "In a household circuit with 220 V supply, what is the current drawn by a 1100 W air conditioner?", opts: ["0.5 A", "220 A", "5 A", "242 A"], ans: 2, exp: "I = P/V = 1100/220 = 5 A. This determines the appropriate fuse rating. A 6 A or 10 A fuse would be appropriate for this appliance." },
    { q: "Electric charge is measured in coulombs. One electron has a charge of approximately:", opts: ["1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C (negative)", "1.6×10¹⁹ C", "1.6×10⁻²³ C", "9.1×10⁻³¹ C"], ans: 0, exp: "Charge of one electron: e = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C (negative). Charge of proton: +1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C. Mass of electron: 9.1×10⁻³¹ kg. These are fundamental constants." },
    { q: "If 10 C of charge flows through a circuit in 5 seconds, the current is:", opts: ["50 A", "0.5 A", "2 A", "15 A"], ans: 2, exp: "I = Q/t = 10/5 = 2 A. Current (ampere) = charge (coulomb) / time (second). One ampere = 1 coulomb per second." },
    { q: "The resistance of a nichrome wire compared to a copper wire of same length and area is:", opts: ["Much lower", "About the same", "Much higher (ρ_nichrome ≈ 100× ρ_copper)", "Temperature dependent only"], ans: 2, exp: "Nichrome resistivity: ~1×10⁻⁶ Ω·m. Copper resistivity: ~1.7×10⁻⁸ Ω·m. Ratio ≈ 60-100. Nichrome wire generates far more heat per unit length than copper wire of same dimensions." },
    { q: "Coulomb's law states the force between two charges is:", opts: ["F = kQ₁Q₂r", "F = kQ₁Q₂/r²", "F = kQ₁+Q₂/r", "F = k(Q₁+Q₂)/r²"], ans: 1, exp: "Coulomb's law: F = kQ₁Q₂/r², where k = 9×10⁹ N·m²/C². Force is proportional to product of charges and inversely proportional to square of distance. Like charges repel; unlike attract." },
    { q: "Why does the filament of an electric bulb glow, but the connecting wires do not?", opts: ["Different current flows", "Filament (tungsten) has much higher resistance → I²R heating → incandescence; copper wire has very low resistance → negligible heating", "Filament is longer", "Different voltage"], ans: 1, exp: "Tungsten filament: very high resistance → I²R generates extreme heat (~2500°C) → glows white. Copper connecting wires: very low resistance → I²R negligible → barely warm. Resistance is the key difference." },
    { q: "Three 6 Ω resistors are connected in parallel. The equivalent resistance is:", opts: ["18 Ω", "6 Ω", "2 Ω", "3 Ω"], ans: 2, exp: "1/R = 1/6 + 1/6 + 1/6 = 3/6 = 1/2. R = 2 Ω. For n identical resistors in parallel: R_eq = R/n = 6/3 = 2 Ω." },
    { q: "A 60 W, 220 V bulb has filament resistance:", opts: ["3.67 Ω", "13200 Ω", "806.7 Ω", "220 Ω"], ans: 2, exp: "R = V²/P = 220²/60 = 48400/60 ≈ 806.7 Ω. This is the hot resistance. Cold resistance of tungsten is much lower (resistance increases with temperature)." },
    { q: "The cost of running a 1500 W electric iron for 2 hours at ₹5 per unit is:", opts: ["₹15", "₹5", "₹10", "₹7.50"], ans: 0, exp: "Energy = 1500 W × 2 h = 3000 Wh = 3 kWh (units). Cost = 3 × ₹5 = ₹15." },
    { q: "Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL) states that in any closed loop:", opts: ["Current is constant", "Sum of all voltages around any closed loop is zero (conservation of energy)", "Resistance decreases", "Power increases"], ans: 1, exp: "KVL: ΣV = 0 around any closed loop. Based on conservation of energy — EMF sources = sum of voltage drops across resistors. Algebraic sum of all potential differences (including EMFs) = 0." },
    { q: "When two bulbs (different wattages) are connected in parallel to the same supply, which one is brighter?", opts: ["Lower wattage", "Higher wattage bulb (draws more current at same voltage)", "Both equally bright", "Cannot be determined"], ans: 1, exp: "In parallel, both get same voltage. Higher wattage bulb (lower resistance) draws more current → consumes more power → glows brighter. (Series: lower wattage = higher R = glows brighter.)" },
    { q: "The minimum number of cells needed to make a battery (by definition) is:", opts: ["1 (a single cell is sometimes called a battery)", "2", "3", "6"], ans: 0, exp: "A battery consists of one or more electrochemical cells. Technically, a single cell can be called a battery (though common usage distinguishes them). A 1.5 V \"battery\" (dry cell) is actually a single cell." },
    { q: "Resistance of a 12 V, 60 W car headlight bulb is:", opts: ["5 Ω", "0.2 Ω", "2.4 Ω", "0.72 Ω"], ans: 2, exp: "R = V²/P = 12²/60 = 144/60 = 2.4 Ω. This is the operating (hot) resistance. Current drawn: I = P/V = 60/12 = 5 A." },
    { q: "A wire of resistivity ρ, length L and cross-sectional area A is drawn into a wire of length 3L (volume unchanged). The new resistance compared to the original R is:", opts: ["3R", "6R", "9R", "R/3"], ans: 2, exp: "Volume = AL is constant, so new area = A/3. New R = ρ(3L)/(A/3) = 9ρL/A = 9R. Resistance scales as the SQUARE of the length ratio when volume is fixed, because both L increases and A decreases." },
    { q: "Three resistors of 4 Ω, 6 Ω, and 12 Ω are connected in parallel. Their equivalent resistance is:", opts: ["22 Ω", "2 Ω", "4 Ω", "1.5 Ω"], ans: 1, exp: "1/R = 1/4 + 1/6 + 1/12 = 3/12 + 2/12 + 1/12 = 6/12 = 1/2. R = 2 Ω. In parallel, equivalent resistance is always less than the smallest individual resistor (here 4 Ω)." },
    { q: "A cell of EMF 12 V and internal resistance 2 Ω is connected to an external resistor of 10 Ω. The terminal voltage of the cell is:", opts: ["12 V", "10 V", "14 V", "2 V"], ans: 1, exp: "I = EMF/(r + R) = 12/(2+10) = 1 A. Terminal voltage V = EMF − Ir = 12 − 1×2 = 10 V. The 2 V drop occurs across the internal resistance. Terminal voltage is always less than EMF when current flows." },
    { q: "In a series circuit, resistors R₁ = 3 Ω and R₂ = 6 Ω are connected to a 9 V battery. The power dissipated in R₂ is:", opts: ["3 W", "6 W", "2 W", "18 W"], ans: 1, exp: "Series: I = V/(R₁+R₂) = 9/(3+6) = 1 A. P₂ = I²R₂ = 1² × 6 = 6 W. Alternatively, V₂ = IR₂ = 6 V, P₂ = V₂²/R₂ = 36/6 = 6 W." },
    { q: "Two resistors 5 Ω and 20 Ω are connected in parallel across a 10 V supply. The total current drawn from the supply is:", opts: ["0.5 A", "2 A", "2.5 A", "4 A"], ans: 2, exp: "I₁ = V/R₁ = 10/5 = 2 A; I₂ = V/R₂ = 10/20 = 0.5 A. Total I = 2 + 0.5 = 2.5 A. Or: R_eq = (5×20)/(5+20) = 4 Ω; I = 10/4 = 2.5 A." },
    { q: "A 100 W, 200 V bulb and a 200 W, 200 V bulb are connected in series to a 400 V supply. The total power consumed is:", opts: ["300 W", "200 W", "266.7 W", "100 W"], ans: 2, exp: "R₁ = 200²/100 = 400 Ω; R₂ = 200²/200 = 200 Ω. Series R_total = 600 Ω. P = V²/R_total = 400²/600 = 160000/600 ≈ 266.7 W." },
    { q: "A 2 kW electric heater is used for 3 hours per day for 30 days. The electricity bill at ₹4 per unit (kWh) is:", opts: ["₹720", "₹360", "₹240", "₹480"], ans: 0, exp: "Energy = Power × Time = 2 kW × 90 h = 180 kWh. Cost = 180 × ₹4 = ₹720." },
    { q: "A wire of resistance R is cut into 3 equal pieces and all pieces are connected in parallel. The equivalent resistance is:", opts: ["R/3", "R/9", "3R", "R"], ans: 1, exp: "Each piece has resistance R/3. Three equal pieces in parallel: R_eq = (R/3)/3 = R/9. General rule: n equal pieces in parallel give R_eq = R/n²." },
    { q: "The resistivity of copper is 1.7 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m. The resistance of a copper wire of length 100 m and cross-sectional area 1 mm² (= 1 × 10⁻⁶ m²) is:", opts: ["1.7 Ω", "0.17 Ω", "17 Ω", "0.017 Ω"], ans: 0, exp: "R = ρL/A = (1.7×10⁻⁸ × 100) / (1×10⁻⁶) = 1.7×10⁻⁶ / 1×10⁻⁶ = 1.7 Ω. Direct application of R = ρL/A with careful unit conversion." },
    { q: "A heating element dissipates 1000 J of heat in 10 seconds when carrying 5 A. The resistance of the element is:", opts: ["40 Ω", "4 Ω", "20 Ω", "10 Ω"], ans: 1, exp: "Joule heating: H = I²Rt → R = H/(I²t) = 1000/(25×10) = 1000/250 = 4 Ω." },
    { q: "The potential difference between two points in a circuit is 6 V. The work done in moving 3 C of charge between these points is:", opts: ["2 J", "18 J", "0.5 J", "9 J"], ans: 1, exp: "W = QV = 3 × 6 = 18 J. Potential difference is defined as work done per unit charge: V = W/Q → W = QV." },
    { q: "In a parallel circuit: R₁ = 4 Ω and R₂ = 8 Ω. If the total current is 6 A, the current through R₂ is:", opts: ["4 A", "2 A", "3 A", "1 A"], ans: 1, exp: "Parallel branches have the same voltage. I₁R₁ = I₂R₂ → I₁/I₂ = R₂/R₁ = 8/4 = 2. Also I₁ + I₂ = 6. So 2I₂ + I₂ = 6 → I₂ = 2 A, I₁ = 4 A." },
    { q: "A 60 W bulb is switched on for 1 minute. The heat produced (in joules) is:", opts: ["60 J", "1 J", "3600 J", "360 J"], ans: 2, exp: "H = P × t = 60 W × 60 s = 3600 J. The watt equals joule per second, so power × time in seconds gives energy in joules. 3600 J = 1 Wh." },
    { q: "Two cells, each of EMF 1.5 V and internal resistance 1 Ω, are connected in series. The total EMF and total internal resistance are:", opts: ["3 V, 2 Ω", "1.5 V, 2 Ω", "3 V, 0.5 Ω", "3 V, 1 Ω"], ans: 0, exp: "Cells in series: total EMF = 1.5 + 1.5 = 3 V. Total internal resistance = 1 + 1 = 2 Ω. EMFs add (when aiding) and so do internal resistances. Used in battery packs to increase voltage." },
    { q: "A fuse is rated at 5 A. The maximum power it can safely handle at 240 V is:", opts: ["48 W", "240 W", "1200 W", "2400 W"], ans: 2, exp: "P_max = V × I_max = 240 × 5 = 1200 W. When load exceeds 1200 W on a 240 V circuit, current exceeds 5 A and the fuse blows." },
    { q: "R₁ = 2 Ω in series with a parallel combination of R₂ = 6 Ω and R₃ = 3 Ω, connected to 12 V. The total current from the supply is:", opts: ["6 A", "3 A", "1.5 A", "4 A"], ans: 1, exp: "Parallel R₂₃ = (6×3)/(6+3) = 2 Ω. Total R = 2 + 2 = 4 Ω. I = V/R = 12/4 = 3 A." },
    { q: "The drift velocity of free electrons in a typical copper conductor carrying household current is approximately:", opts: ["3 × 10⁸ m/s", "10⁶ m/s", "10⁻⁴ m/s", "1 m/s"], ans: 2, exp: "Drift velocity in copper is ~10⁻⁴ m/s (fractions of a millimetre per second). This is surprisingly slow, yet the electric signal propagates near the speed of light because the electric field instantaneously pushes all conduction electrons simultaneously." },
    { q: "The resistance of a conductor is doubled and the voltage across it is simultaneously halved. The power dissipated becomes what fraction of the original?", opts: ["Same", "4 times", "1/8 of original", "1/4 of original"], ans: 2, exp: "P = V²/R. New P = (V/2)²/(2R) = V²/(4×2R) = V²/8R = P/8. Power becomes one-eighth of the original." },
    { q: "A 2200 W geyser and a 1000 W refrigerator both operate on a 220 V supply. The minimum fuse rating for the combined load is:", opts: ["5 A", "15 A", "20 A", "25 A"], ans: 1, exp: "Total current = (2200 + 1000)/220 = 3200/220 ≈ 14.5 A. The next standard fuse rating above 14.5 A is 15 A." },
    { q: "Which graph correctly shows resistivity (ρ) vs temperature (T) for a pure metal conductor?", opts: ["Straight line with negative slope", "Straight line with positive slope (ρ increases with T)", "Parabolic curve", "Horizontal line (ρ constant)"], ans: 1, exp: "For metals, ρ = ρ₀(1 + αΔT) where α > 0 (positive temperature coefficient). This is approximately linear, giving a straight line with positive slope. Semiconductors show the opposite — ρ decreases with T." },
    { q: "A 40 W, 220 V bulb and a 60 W, 220 V bulb are connected in parallel to a 220 V supply. The total current drawn is:", opts: ["0.45 A", "1 A", "2.75 A", "0.023 A"], ans: 0, exp: "I_total = P_total/V = (40+60)/220 = 100/220 ≈ 0.45 A. In parallel, total current = sum of individual currents: 40/220 + 60/220 = 100/220 ≈ 0.45 A." },
    { q: "A 100 W, 230 V tungsten lamp at operating temperature has filament resistance approximately:", opts: ["529 Ω", "230 Ω", "23 Ω", "52.9 Ω"], ans: 0, exp: "R = V²/P = 230²/100 = 52900/100 = 529 Ω. This is the hot resistance. Cold resistance of tungsten is about 10–15 times lower — tungsten has a high positive temperature coefficient." },
    { q: "If the cross-sectional area of a wire is halved and its length is doubled, the resistance changes by a factor of:", opts: ["2", "4", "8", "1/4"], ans: 1, exp: "Original R = ρL/A. New R = ρ(2L)/(A/2) = 4ρL/A = 4R. Halving area doubles resistance; doubling length also doubles resistance. Combined effect: 4× increase." },
    { q: "Resistors 5 Ω, 10 Ω, and 15 Ω are in series across 60 V. The voltage drop across the 10 Ω resistor is:", opts: ["10 V", "20 V", "30 V", "40 V"], ans: 1, exp: "R_total = 5+10+15 = 30 Ω. I = 60/30 = 2 A. V₁₀ = IR = 2×10 = 20 V. Voltage divides in proportion to resistance in a series circuit." },
    { q: "The charge that flows through a circuit if a current of 3 A flows for 2 minutes is:", opts: ["6 C", "1.5 C", "360 C", "120 C"], ans: 2, exp: "Q = It = 3 A × 120 s = 360 C. Time must be in seconds for Q = It to give coulombs. 2 minutes = 120 seconds." },
    { q: "In Joule's law, if the current through a resistor is tripled (R and t constant), the heat produced is:", opts: ["3 times", "6 times", "9 times", "27 times"], ans: 2, exp: "H = I²Rt. If I → 3I: H_new = (3I)² Rt = 9I²Rt = 9H. Heat is proportional to the SQUARE of current. This is why even modest overcurrents cause disproportionately large heating." },
    { q: "Three identical cells, each of EMF 1.5 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω, are connected in parallel. The equivalent internal resistance of the battery is:", opts: ["1.5 Ω", "0.5 Ω", "0.17 Ω", "3 Ω"], ans: 2, exp: "Cells in parallel: total EMF = 1.5 V (unchanged). Internal resistances in parallel: r_eq = 0.5/3 ≈ 0.17 Ω. Parallel cells increase current capacity and reduce internal resistance without changing the EMF." },
    { q: "Short circuit is dangerous primarily because:", opts: ["Voltage becomes zero", "Resistance drops to near zero → current becomes enormous → I²R heating → fire", "Current becomes zero", "Voltage doubles"], ans: 1, exp: "Short circuit: R ≈ 0 Ω, so I = V/R → very large current (theoretically infinite). This causes extreme I²R heating in wires and could cause fire. The fuse or MCB must interrupt the circuit before this happens." },
    { q: "A 12 V car headlight rated at 48 W has a hot resistance of:", opts: ["0.25 Ω", "576 Ω", "3 Ω", "4 Ω"], ans: 2, exp: "R = V²/P = 144/48 = 3 Ω. Current drawn: I = P/V = 48/12 = 4 A. Verification: P = I²R = 16×3 = 48 W ✓." },
    { q: "A copper wire has n = 10²⁸ free electrons per m³, cross-sectional area A = 10⁻⁶ m², and drift velocity vd = 10⁻⁴ m/s. The current is:", opts: ["1.6 A", "0.16 A", "16 A", "0.016 A"], ans: 0, exp: "I = nAve = 10²⁸ × 10⁻⁶ × 10⁻⁴ × 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ = 10²⁸⁻⁶⁻⁴ × 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ = 10¹⁸ × 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ = 1.6 A." },
    { q: "An MCB (Miniature Circuit Breaker) uses which dual mechanism to protect a circuit?", opts: ["Heating effect only (bimetallic strip)", "Magnetic effect only (solenoid)", "Both: bimetallic strip for slow overloads AND electromagnetic solenoid for sudden short circuits", "Chemical effect"], ans: 2, exp: "MCB dual protection: (1) bimetallic strip for prolonged overloads — heats, bends, trips; (2) electromagnetic solenoid for sudden large overcurrents (short circuits) — instant magnetic trip. Far superior to a simple fuse." },
    { q: "A resistor consumes 36 W when connected to 12 V. If the same resistor is connected to 6 V, the power consumed is:", opts: ["18 W", "9 W", "72 W", "4 W"], ans: 1, exp: "R = V²/P = 144/36 = 4 Ω. At 6 V: P = V²/R = 36/4 = 9 W. Power ∝ V², so halving voltage reduces power to (1/2)² = 1/4: 36/4 = 9 W." },
    { q: "In a combination: R₁ = 10 Ω in series with a parallel pair R₂ = R₃ = 10 Ω, connected to 30 V. The current through R₁ is:", opts: ["1 A", "2 A", "3 A", "0.5 A"], ans: 1, exp: "R₂₃ parallel = 10/2 = 5 Ω. R_total = 10 + 5 = 15 Ω. I = 30/15 = 2 A. This 2 A passes through R₁, then splits equally (1 A each) through R₂ and R₃." },
    { q: "The number of electrons that pass through a cross-section of wire per second when the current is 1 A is approximately:", opts: ["1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹", "6.25 × 10¹⁸", "6.25 × 10²³", "1 × 10²³"], ans: 1, exp: "Q per second = 1 C. Number of electrons = Q/e = 1/(1.6×10⁻¹⁹) = 6.25×10¹⁸. This is NOT Avogadro's number; Avogadro's number would require about 25,600 A." },
    { q: "A 100 W, 250 V lamp is connected to a 200 V supply. The actual power consumed is:", opts: ["100 W", "64 W", "80 W", "156 W"], ans: 1, exp: "R = V²/P = 250²/100 = 625 Ω. P_actual = V_actual²/R = 200²/625 = 40000/625 = 64 W. Power ∝ V²; ratio = (200/250)² = 0.64; P = 64 W." },
    { q: "The earthing (grounding) wire in domestic wiring is connected to:", opts: ["Live terminal of the meter", "Metal body of the appliance AND a copper plate buried in moist earth", "Neutral wire only", "The fuse box alone"], ans: 1, exp: "Earthing: green/yellow wire connects the metal body of the appliance to a copper plate buried deep in moist earth. If a live wire touches the casing, the low-resistance earth path carries fault current safely to earth, blowing the fuse." },
    { q: "An aluminium wire and a copper wire of the same resistance and length — which has the larger cross-sectional area? (ρ_Al = 2.8×10⁻⁸ Ω·m; ρ_Cu = 1.7×10⁻⁸ Ω·m)", opts: ["Copper wire", "Aluminium wire (higher ρ requires larger A for same R)", "Both equal", "Cannot be determined"], ans: 1, exp: "From R = ρL/A: A = ρL/R. For same R and L, A ∝ ρ. Since ρ_Al > ρ_Cu, the aluminium wire needs a larger cross-section. Aluminium is still used for overhead lines because it is much lighter than copper." },
    { q: "The voltage drop across the internal resistance of a cell of EMF 3 V when the terminal voltage is 2.4 V is:", opts: ["5.4 V", "0.6 V", "3.6 V", "1.2 V"], ans: 1, exp: "V_internal = EMF − V_terminal = 3 − 2.4 = 0.6 V. This is the Ir drop. If internal resistance r = 0.5 Ω, then I = 0.6/0.5 = 1.2 A." },
    { q: "A 500 W, 220 V electric iron draws current through a 1 Ω lead wire. The voltage drop across the lead wire is approximately:", opts: ["2.27 V", "1.14 V", "500 V", "0.23 V"], ans: 0, exp: "I = P/V = 500/220 ≈ 2.27 A. V_drop = I × R_lead = 2.27 × 1 ≈ 2.27 V. This shows that long, thin extension leads waste energy and reduce the voltage reaching the appliance." },
    { q: "Conductor A has twice the resistivity, half the length, and double the area of conductor B. The resistance of A compared to B is:", opts: ["Equal to R_B", "4R_B", "R_B/2", "2R_B"], ans: 2, exp: "R_A = (2ρ)(L/2)/(2A) = ρL/(2A) = R_B/2. Doubling ρ doubles R; halving L halves R; doubling A halves R. Combined: 2 × 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/2." },
    { q: "Which connection of three 6 Ω resistors gives the smallest equivalent resistance?", opts: ["All in series (18 Ω)", "Two in series, one in parallel (~4 Ω)", "All three in parallel (2 Ω)", "Two in parallel, one in series (9 Ω)"], ans: 2, exp: "All in parallel: R_eq = 6/3 = 2 Ω — smallest possible. All in series gives 18 Ω. Parallel always gives R_eq < smallest individual resistor. n identical resistors in parallel: R_eq = R/n." },
    { q: "An electric motor with 90% efficiency draws 5 A from a 200 V supply. The mechanical power output is:", opts: ["900 W", "1000 W", "1100 W", "111 W"], ans: 0, exp: "Electrical input = V × I = 200 × 5 = 1000 W. Mechanical output = 0.90 × 1000 = 900 W. The remaining 100 W is wasted as heat in motor windings and due to friction." },
    { q: "A 24 V battery (negligible internal resistance) is connected to: 4 Ω in series with a parallel combination of 6 Ω and 3 Ω. The voltage across the parallel combination is:", opts: ["8 V", "16 V", "12 V", "24 V"], ans: 0, exp: "Parallel R₂₃ = (6×3)/(6+3) = 2 Ω. Total R = 4 + 2 = 6 Ω. I = 24/6 = 4 A. V_parallel = I × R_parallel = 4 × 2 = 8 V. V_series = 4 × 4 = 16 V. Check: 8+16 = 24 V ✓." },
    { q: "A conductor has resistance 100 Ω at 0°C and 130 Ω at 100°C. Its temperature coefficient of resistance α is:", opts: ["0.003 /°C", "0.004 /°C", "0.001 /°C", "0.03 /°C"], ans: 0, exp: "α = (R_T − R₀)/(R₀ × ΔT) = (130−100)/(100×100) = 30/10000 = 0.003 /°C. Using this: R at any temperature T = R₀(1 + αT) = 100(1 + 0.003T)." },
    { q: "Overloading in a household circuit occurs when:", opts: ["Too many appliances draw current exceeding the circuit's safe rated limit", "Voltage drops below 200 V", "The fuse is removed intentionally", "Earthing is connected"], ans: 0, exp: "Overloading: total load current exceeds the rated current of the wiring (e.g., 15 A circuit draws 20 A). Excess current causes I²R heating in wires → insulation melts → fire hazard. Fuse/MCB prevent overloading damage." },
    { q: "A 40 W fluorescent tube and a 40 W incandescent bulb operate for the same time at the same voltage. Which is brighter and why?", opts: ["Incandescent: converts more power to light", "Fluorescent: same power consumed but higher luminous efficiency (more light per watt)", "Both equally bright", "Incandescent is brighter because it is hotter"], ans: 1, exp: "Both consume 40 W. However, fluorescent converts ~25% to light; incandescent converts only ~5%. Fluorescent gives roughly 5× more visible light per watt (60–80 lm/W vs 10–15 lm/W). Same energy, very different light output." },
    { q: "The hot resistance of a 1000 W, 230 V electric kettle element is:", opts: ["52.9 Ω", "0.23 Ω", "230 Ω", "23 Ω"], ans: 0, exp: "R = V²/P = 230²/1000 = 52900/1000 = 52.9 Ω. Current drawn = P/V = 1000/230 ≈ 4.35 A. Check: I²R = 4.35² × 52.9 ≈ 18.9 × 52.9 ≈ 1000 W ✓." },
    { q: "Two identical resistors are first in series across a battery, then in parallel. The ratio of total power (series : parallel) is:", opts: ["1 : 4", "4 : 1", "1 : 2", "2 : 1"], ans: 0, exp: "Let each R, battery V. Series: P_s = V²/(2R). Parallel: P_p = V²/(R/2) = 2V²/R. Ratio P_s : P_p = [V²/2R] : [2V²/R] = 1:4. Parallel combination dissipates 4 times the power of the series combination." },
    { q: "The watt-hour (Wh) is a unit of:", opts: ["Power", "Energy (1 Wh = 3600 J)", "Current", "Charge"], ans: 1, exp: "1 Wh = 1 W × 3600 s = 3600 J. 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J. The watt-hour measures electrical energy (not power). The watt (W) is the SI unit of power." },
    { q: "A 230 V, 1150 W electric stove is used for 2 hours. The cost at ₹5 per unit (kWh) is:", opts: ["₹11.50", "₹5.75", "₹23", "₹1.15"], ans: 0, exp: "Energy = 1.15 kW × 2 h = 2.3 kWh. Cost = 2.3 × ₹5 = ₹11.50." }
  ]
,

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════
     CH 12 — MAGNETIC EFFECTS OF ELECTRIC CURRENT
  ══════════════════════════════════════ */
  12: [
    { q: "A compass needle placed near a straight current-carrying conductor will:", opts: ["Point toward the wire", "Be deflected (magnetic field around conductor deflects the needle)", "Not be affected", "Point away from the wire"], ans: 1, exp: "Oersted's discovery (1820): current produces a magnetic field around it. Compass needle (magnet) responds to this field and deflects. This was the first evidence of electromagnetism." },
    { q: "The right-hand thumb rule (for a straight conductor) states:", opts: ["If the right thumb points in the direction of current, the curled fingers show the direction of magnetic field lines (circles around the wire)", "Left hand for current direction", "The thumb shows magnetic field direction", "Fingers show current, thumb shows magnetic field"], ans: 0, exp: "Right-hand thumb rule: hold wire in right hand; thumb points in current direction; curled fingers indicate the direction of circular magnetic field lines around the wire." },
    { q: "Inside a bar magnet, the magnetic field lines go from:", opts: ["N to S pole (outside and inside)", "S to N pole (inside only)", "Randomly", "There are no field lines inside"], ans: 1, exp: "Magnetic field lines: OUTSIDE magnet: N to S (by convention). INSIDE magnet: S to N (to form closed loops). Field lines are always closed loops — no beginning or end (unlike electric field lines)." },
    { q: "The SI unit of magnetic field (magnetic flux density) is the:", opts: ["Gauss", "Tesla (T)", "Oersted", "Weber"], ans: 1, exp: "Tesla (T) is the SI unit of magnetic flux density (B). 1 T = 1 Wb/m² (Weber per square metre). The gauss is a CGS unit: 1 T = 10,000 gauss. Earth's magnetic field ≈ 50 microtesla." },
    { q: "The force on a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field is maximum when the conductor is:", opts: ["Parallel to the field", "At 30° to the field", "At 45° to the field", "Perpendicular to the field (F = BIL)"], ans: 3, exp: "Force F = BIL sinθ. F is maximum when θ = 90° (conductor ⊥ field): F_max = BIL. F = 0 when conductor is parallel to field (θ = 0°). The direction is given by Fleming's LHR." },
    { q: "Fleming's Right-Hand Rule is used to find the direction of:", opts: ["Force on a motor", "Induced current in a generator", "Magnetic field around a wire", "Force on a magnet"], ans: 1, exp: "Fleming's RHR (dynamo rule): for generators. Index finger → field (B), thumb → motion of conductor, middle finger → induced current direction. Used in electromagnetic induction." },
    { q: "An electromagnet is a temporary magnet made by passing current through a solenoid. Its strength is increased by:", opts: ["Using AC instead of DC", "Increasing number of turns, current, and using a soft iron core", "Reducing current", "Using an air core"], ans: 1, exp: "Electromagnet strength ∝ number of turns (N) × current (I) = Ampere-turns. Soft iron core → large relative permeability → concentrates magnetic field. Soft iron demagnetises easily when current stops." },
    { q: "The magnetic field inside a solenoid is:", opts: ["Zero", "Uniform and parallel to the axis", "Circular", "Radial"], ans: 1, exp: "Inside a long solenoid: B is uniform (constant magnitude) and parallel to the solenoid's axis. It behaves like a bar magnet — one end is N, other is S. B = μ₀nI (n = turns/length)." },
    { q: "Faraday's law of electromagnetic induction states that the induced EMF is:", opts: ["Proportional to the current in the conductor", "Equal to the rate of change of magnetic flux through the circuit", "Proportional to resistance", "Inversely proportional to time"], ans: 1, exp: "Faraday's law: EMF = −dΦ/dt (rate of change of magnetic flux). The negative sign indicates Lenz's law. Basis of generators, transformers, electric motors." },
    { q: "Lenz's law states that the induced current always:", opts: ["Flows in the direction of the changing flux", "Opposes the change in magnetic flux that caused it (conservation of energy)", "Has the same direction as the inducing current", "Flows from high to low potential always"], ans: 1, exp: "Lenz's law: induced current direction is such that its magnetic effect opposes the change in flux causing it. This is a consequence of conservation of energy. Increases effective resistance (back EMF)." },
    { q: "An AC generator works on the principle of:", opts: ["Magnetic attraction", "Electromagnetic induction (rotating coil in a magnetic field)", "Electrostatics", "Thermoelectric effect"], ans: 1, exp: "AC generator: rotating coil in magnetic field → flux changes → EMF induced (Faraday's law). Output: sinusoidal alternating current. DC generator: same principle + commutator to convert AC to DC." },
    { q: "The device that converts mechanical energy into electrical energy is:", opts: ["Electric motor", "Electric generator (dynamo)", "Transformer", "Battery"], ans: 1, exp: "Generator/dynamo: mechanical energy (rotation of turbine/coil) → electrical energy via electromagnetic induction. Motor: reverse → electrical → mechanical. Transformer: changes AC voltage levels." },
    { q: "A transformer works on the principle of:", opts: ["DC electromagnetic induction", "Mutual electromagnetic induction (changing current in primary coil → changing flux → induces EMF in secondary coil)", "Electrostatics", "Thermoelectric effect"], ans: 1, exp: "Transformer: AC in primary coil → changing magnetic flux → induces AC in secondary coil (mutual induction). V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p. Transformers only work with AC (not DC). Used in power transmission." },
    { q: "In a step-up transformer:", opts: ["N_s < N_p; V_s < V_p", "N_s > N_p; V_s > V_p (secondary voltage is greater)", "N_s = N_p always", "Current increases"], ans: 1, exp: "Step-up transformer: N_s > N_p → V_s > V_p. Voltage steps up. Since P = VI (ideal transformer), if V doubles, I halves. Used at power stations to increase voltage for long-distance transmission (reduces I²R losses)." },
    { q: "Power is transmitted at very high voltages (e.g., 400,000 V) over long distances because:", opts: ["It travels faster at high voltage", "High voltage → low current → very low I²R heating losses in transmission lines", "High voltage is safer", "Transformers require high voltage"], ans: 1, exp: "P_loss = I²R. At high voltage, current I is small for the same power (P = VI). Small I → dramatically lower I²R losses in the resistance of transmission cables. Efficiency of long-distance power transfer increases." },
    { q: "Earth's magnetic field at the geographic north pole has its:", opts: ["Magnetic north pole", "Magnetic south pole (geographic north = magnetic south)", "No magnetic pole", "Both poles at once"], ans: 1, exp: "Earth's geographic North Pole coincides with a magnetic south pole (because the north end of a compass needle is attracted to it). Earth's magnetic field runs from geographic south to north inside Earth." },
    { q: "A current-carrying solenoid behaves like a bar magnet. The end through which the current flows anti-clockwise is the:", opts: ["South pole", "North pole", "Neither", "Depends on current strength"], ans: 1, exp: "Solenoid poles: looking at the end face, if current flows anticlockwise → that face is N pole (by right-hand curl rule or by remembering N looks like anticlockwise arrow when facing it)." },
    { q: "A moving charge in a magnetic field experiences a force called the:", opts: ["Gravitational force", "Coulomb force", "Lorentz force", "Nuclear force"], ans: 2, exp: "Lorentz force: F = qv×B = qvB sinθ. A charge q moving at velocity v in field B experiences a force perpendicular to both v and B. Basis of cyclotron, CRT, aurora borealis." },
    { q: "The working principle of a galvanometer is based on:", opts: ["Heating effect of current", "Magnetic effect of current (current-carrying coil in magnetic field experiences torque → deflects pointer)", "Electrostatic effect", "Photoelectric effect"], ans: 1, exp: "Galvanometer: moving coil in permanent magnet field. Current → torque → coil rotates against spring → pointer deflects. Deflection ∝ current. Can be converted to ammeter (shunt) or voltmeter (multiplier)." },
    { q: "The device used to measure very small currents (microamperes) is a:", opts: ["Ammeter", "Voltmeter", "Galvanometer", "Wattmeter"], ans: 2, exp: "Galvanometer: highly sensitive current detector. Can detect very small currents (μA). Converted to ammeter by adding a low-resistance shunt in parallel. To voltmeter: high-resistance multiplier in series." },
    { q: "To convert a galvanometer into an ammeter, a shunt resistor is connected:", opts: ["In series with the galvanometer", "In parallel with the galvanometer (low resistance to bypass most current)", "In a separate branch", "Nowhere — it needs no modification"], ans: 1, exp: "Ammeter conversion: shunt (low R) in parallel. Most current bypasses through shunt; only a small fraction goes through galvanometer (which has high resistance). Shunt R = I_g × G / (I − I_g)." },
    { q: "Which of the following is used in speakers and electric bells based on magnetic effect?", opts: ["Step-up transformer", "Electromagnet (current → magnetic field → attraction/vibration)", "Galvanometer", "Capacitor"], ans: 1, exp: "Electric bell and speaker: electromagnet attracts striker/diaphragm when energised. Bell: AC or pulsed DC → rapid on/off → hammers bell. Speaker: coil in field → current changes → cone vibrates → sound." },
    { q: "Magnetic field lines never intersect each other because:", opts: ["They are parallel", "At any point, there is only ONE direction of magnetic field (if lines crossed, field would have two directions — impossible)", "They are straight lines", "They are circular"], ans: 1, exp: "If field lines intersected, the field would have two directions at that point — impossible. This applies to both electric and magnetic field lines. Field lines also have no start or end (magnetic field lines are closed loops)." },
    { q: "The aurora borealis (northern lights) is caused by:", opts: ["Reflection of sunlight by polar ice", "Charged solar wind particles guided by Earth's magnetic field → ionise atmosphere → emit light", "Moon's gravitational effect", "Volcanic eruptions at north pole"], ans: 1, exp: "Aurora: charged particles (protons, electrons) from solar wind spiral along Earth's magnetic field lines toward poles → collide with O₂, N₂ in upper atmosphere → excite atoms → emit colourful light." },
    { q: "A magnetic compass works because the compass needle:", opts: ["Is attracted to the geographic north by gravity", "Aligns with Earth's magnetic field (N pole points toward geographic north)", "Repels all metals", "Points toward the sun"], ans: 1, exp: "Compass: magnetised needle pivoted at centre. N pole of needle attracted to geographic north (Earth's magnetic south is near geographic north). Navigational tool for thousands of years." },
    { q: "A relay is an electrically controlled switch that uses:", opts: ["Capacitor to switch high current", "Electromagnetic effect: small current in coil → magnetic field → attracts armature → closes switch in high-power circuit", "Resistance change", "Piezoelectric effect"], ans: 1, exp: "Relay: small input current energises electromagnet → attracts iron armature → closes contacts in high-current circuit. Allows low-power signal to control high-power device. Used in car starters, industrial machinery." },
    { q: "Induced current in a generator increases when:", opts: ["Speed of rotation decreases", "Number of turns in coil decreases", "Strength of magnetic field or speed of rotation increases", "Core is removed"], ans: 2, exp: "Induced EMF ∝ N × dΦ/dt. Faster rotation → more rapid flux change → greater EMF → more current. Stronger magnetic field → greater flux change → greater EMF. More turns → greater EMF." },
    { q: "A circuit breaker (MCB) differs from a fuse in that it:", opts: ["Destroys itself when tripped", "Can be reset (not replaced) after tripping; uses electromagnetic or bimetallic mechanism", "Only works with DC", "Increases resistance"], ans: 1, exp: "MCB (Miniature Circuit Breaker): trips electromagnetically when current exceeds rated value → breaks circuit. After fault is fixed, simply reset by pressing the button. Unlike fuse: reusable." },
    { q: "The concept of magnetic flux is:", opts: ["The number of field lines per unit area (B × A × cosθ)", "The force on a conductor", "The EMF induced in a coil", "The current through a conductor"], ans: 0, exp: "Magnetic flux Φ = B × A × cosθ, where θ is angle between B and area vector (normal to surface). Unit: Weber (Wb). Faraday's law: EMF = rate of change of flux (ΔΦ/Δt)." },
    { q: "Eddy currents are induced currents in:", opts: ["Coil windings of transformers", "Solid metallic cores of transformers (and are losses — reduced by using laminated cores)", "Water", "Air cores"], ans: 1, exp: "Eddy currents: induced currents in solid metallic conductors (transformer cores, motor cores) due to changing flux. They produce heat (I²R loss). Minimised using laminated iron cores (thin sheets with insulation between them)." },
    { q: "The principle behind an induction cooker is:", opts: ["Resistance heating (conventional)", "Eddy currents induced in the cooking vessel → heat generated", "Microwave radiation", "Conduction from a heating element"], ans: 1, exp: "Induction cooker: AC flows through coil below glass surface → alternating magnetic field → eddy currents induced in the iron/steel cookware → I²R heating of the vessel itself. Very efficient (vessel heats directly)." },
    { q: "The motor effect (force on current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field) formula is:", opts: ["F = BIL sinθ", "F = BIL/sinθ", "F = BI sinθ/L", "F = B/IL"], ans: 0, exp: "F = BIL sinθ, where B = magnetic flux density (T), I = current (A), L = length of conductor in field (m), θ = angle between conductor and field. Maximum force when θ = 90°." },
    { q: "A dynamo (generator) and motor are related in that:", opts: ["They are completely unrelated", "They are the reverse of each other — same machine can act as motor or generator depending on input/output", "A dynamo is more efficient", "A motor uses DC only"], ans: 1, exp: "Motor and generator are related by electromagnetic induction. A motor (electrical → mechanical) will act as a generator (mechanical → electrical) if the shaft is turned mechanically. Same fundamental principles." },
    { q: "Magnetic field lines around a straight current-carrying conductor are:", opts: ["Straight lines parallel to conductor", "Concentric circles centred on the conductor", "Straight lines perpendicular to conductor", "Parabolic curves"], ans: 1, exp: "Magnetic field around a straight conductor: concentric circles centred on the conductor, in planes perpendicular to it. Closer to the wire → denser field lines → stronger field. Right-hand thumb rule gives direction." },
    { q: "The north pole of a solenoid can be identified by:", opts: ["Looking at which end current enters", "Looking at the face where current flows anticlockwise (N) or using right-hand rule", "The end with more turns", "The end facing geographic north"], ans: 1, exp: "Solenoid N pole: looking at the face, if current goes anticlockwise → that face is N pole. Alternatively: right-hand rule — wrap right hand around solenoid with fingers in direction of current → thumb points to N pole." },
    { q: "Which of the following is NOT a source of electromagnetic waves?", opts: ["Oscillating charges", "Accelerating electrons", "A stationary charge", "An alternating current"], ans: 2, exp: "Electromagnetic waves are produced by oscillating/accelerating charges. A stationary charge has a static electric field only (no oscillation → no EM wave emission). EM waves from stationary charges: only static fields." },
    { q: "The magnitude of the force between two parallel current-carrying conductors depends on:", opts: ["The product of the currents and the distance between them", "F/L = μ₀I₁I₂/(2πd) — force per unit length depends on both currents and separation", "Only the individual currents", "Only the separation"], ans: 1, exp: "Force per unit length between parallel conductors: F/L = μ₀I₁I₂/(2πd). Parallel currents attract; antiparallel currents repel. This was used to define the ampere (now redefined using fundamental constants)." },
    { q: "In a DC electric motor, the commutator is used to:", opts: ["Measure current", "Reverse the direction of current through the coil every half turn — ensuring continuous rotation", "Maintain constant voltage", "Reduce speed"], ans: 1, exp: "Commutator: split ring that reverses current direction through the coil every half revolution → torque always acts in the same direction → continuous rotation. Without commutator → coil would oscillate back and forth." },
    { q: "The phenomenon of electromagnetic induction was discovered by:", opts: ["Oersted", "Ampere", "Michael Faraday (and independently by Joseph Henry)", "Maxwell"], ans: 2, exp: "Michael Faraday (1831) discovered electromagnetic induction: changing magnetic flux induces an EMF. Joseph Henry independently discovered the same phenomenon. Faraday's law quantifies this." },
    { q: "The frequency of AC supply in India is:", opts: ["50 Hz", "60 Hz", "100 Hz", "25 Hz"], ans: 0, exp: "India uses 50 Hz AC supply (50 cycles per second) at 220-240 V. USA/Canada use 60 Hz at 120 V. 50 Hz means the current reverses direction 100 times per second (2 reversals per cycle)." },
    { q: "Earth's magnetic field is generated by:", opts: ["The iron in Earth's crust", "Electric currents in the liquid outer core (dynamo theory)", "The sun's gravity", "The moon's magnetic field"], ans: 1, exp: "Geodynamo theory: Earth's liquid outer core (molten iron-nickel) undergoes convection + Earth's rotation → circulating electric currents → magnetic field. This dynamo effect generates Earth's magnetosphere." },
    { q: "The strength of the magnetic field (B) at the centre of a circular current loop is:", opts: ["Proportional to current and inversely proportional to radius: B = μ₀I/(2R)", "Independent of radius", "B = μ₀I×R", "Zero at centre"], ans: 0, exp: "B at centre of circular loop = μ₀I/(2R). Field increases with current, decreases with larger radius. Multiple loops (N turns): B = Nμ₀I/(2R)." },
    { q: "Magnetic declination is the angle between:", opts: ["True north and magnetic north at a given location", "Horizontal and vertical components of Earth's field", "Earth's axis and the ecliptic", "Field and the conductor"], ans: 0, exp: "Magnetic declination: angle between geographic north (true north) and magnetic north (direction compass points) at any location. Varies with location and changes over time. Navigators must correct for it." },
    { q: "An MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) machine uses:", opts: ["X-rays to image tissue", "Strong magnetic fields and radiofrequency waves (no ionising radiation) to image soft tissue", "Ultrasound only", "Nuclear radiation"], ans: 1, exp: "MRI: uses strong superconducting magnets (1-3 T) to align hydrogen nuclei in body. RF pulses flip them → they emit signals → computer reconstructs 3D images. No X-rays, excellent soft tissue contrast." },
    { q: "Magnetic braking (eddy current braking) is used in:", opts: ["Steam trains", "Roller coasters and high-speed trains (non-contact deceleration using induced eddy currents)", "Bicycles", "Piston engines"], ans: 1, exp: "Eddy current braking: conducting disc/rail moves through magnetic field → eddy currents induced → oppose motion (Lenz's law) → smooth deceleration without mechanical contact. Used in maglev and roller coasters." },
    { q: "The slip rings in an AC generator are used to:", opts: ["Convert AC to DC", "Maintain continuous electrical contact between rotating coil and external circuit", "Reverse current direction", "Reduce back EMF"], ans: 1, exp: "Slip rings: two continuous rings connected to each end of the rotating coil. Brushes press on rings → current can flow continuously in/out of the rotating coil. AC generator uses slip rings; DC generator uses commutator." },
    { q: "Curie point (Curie temperature) is:", opts: ["Temperature at which a magnet gains maximum strength", "Temperature above which ferromagnetic material loses permanent magnetism and becomes paramagnetic", "Melting point of magnetic material", "Temperature of superconductivity"], ans: 1, exp: "Curie temperature: above this, ferromagnetic materials (Fe, Co, Ni) become paramagnetic (lose permanent magnetism). Iron: ~770°C. Thermal energy disrupts magnetic domain alignment." },
    { q: "Hall effect is the production of voltage difference across an electrical conductor when:", opts: ["It is heated", "Current flows through it in the presence of a magnetic field (perpendicular to both)", "It is cooled to low temperature", "It is stretched"], ans: 1, exp: "Hall effect: current in conductor + perpendicular magnetic field → force on charge carriers → accumulate on one side → voltage across conductor perpendicular to both. Used in Hall sensors to measure B-field and in motor control." },
    { q: "Which type of magnet retains magnetism without any external field?", opts: ["Electromagnet", "Temporary magnet", "Permanent magnet (made from hard ferromagnetic materials like steel, Alnico)", "Paramagnetic material"], ans: 2, exp: "Permanent magnets: made from hard ferromagnetic materials (steel, Alnico, ferrite, rare earth magnets like NdFeB). Retain magnetism without external field. Used in motors, speakers, MRI." },
    { q: "The transformer equation V_p/V_s = N_p/N_s assumes:", opts: ["100% efficiency", "All flux from primary links the secondary (ideal transformer — no energy losses)", "DC input", "Resistance in the coils"], ans: 1, exp: "Ideal transformer: assumes 100% flux linkage (no leakage) and no resistance losses. In reality, transformers have small losses (core losses + copper losses) → efficiency typically 95-99%." },
    { q: "A solenoid has 1000 turns/m and carries 2 A. The magnetic field inside (μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ T·m/A) is:", opts: ["4π × 10⁻⁴ T", "8π × 10⁻⁴ T", "2π × 10⁻³ T", "4π × 10⁻³ T"], ans: 1, exp: "B = μ₀nI = 4π×10⁻⁷ × 1000 × 2 = 8π×10⁻⁴ T ≈ 2.51×10⁻³ T. n = turns per metre. B inside a solenoid is uniform and parallel to the axis." },
    { q: "A conductor of length 0.5 m carrying 4 A is placed perpendicular to a 0.2 T magnetic field. The force on it is:", opts: ["0.4 N", "4 N", "0.04 N", "0.1 N"], ans: 0, exp: "F = BIL sinθ = 0.2 × 4 × 0.5 × sin90° = 0.4 N. The conductor is perpendicular to the field (θ = 90°, maximum force). Direction given by Fleming's left-hand rule." },
    { q: "A transformer has 500 primary turns and 50 secondary turns. If 220 V AC is applied to the primary, the secondary voltage is:", opts: ["2200 V", "22 V", "220 V", "44 V"], ans: 1, exp: "V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p → V_s = 220 × 50/500 = 22 V. This step-down transformer reduces voltage by factor 10. For the same power, current in secondary is 10× larger." },
    { q: "An ideal transformer has 1200 primary turns, 60 secondary turns. Primary voltage is 240 V, primary current is 2 A. The secondary current is:", opts: ["10 A", "40 A", "0.1 A", "0.5 A"], ans: 1, exp: "V_s = V_p × N_s/N_p = 240 × 60/1200 = 12 V. Power conservation: V_p I_p = V_s I_s → I_s = (240 × 2)/12 = 40 A. Step-down in voltage means step-up in current." },
    { q: "An AC generator produces maximum EMF when the plane of the rotating coil is:", opts: ["Perpendicular to the magnetic field (coil ⊥ B)", "Parallel to the magnetic field (coil ∥ B)", "At 45° to the field", "At 30° to the field"], ans: 1, exp: "EMF = NBAω sinθ, where θ is the angle between coil PLANE and B. EMF is maximum when sinθ = 1, i.e., when the coil plane is parallel to B. At this position, the flux is zero but the rate of change of flux is maximum." },
    { q: "A step-up transformer at a power station increases voltage from 11 kV to 220 kV. If transmitted power is 100 MW, the current in the 220 kV line is:", opts: ["9090 A", "455 A", "100 A", "220 A"], ans: 1, exp: "I = P/V = 100×10⁶ / (220×10³) = 10⁸/2.2×10⁵ ≈ 455 A. High transmission voltage drastically reduces current, cutting I²R heating losses in the cables." },
    { q: "A coil of 200 turns and area 10 cm² (= 10⁻³ m²) is in a magnetic field that drops from 0.5 T to 0 T in 0.1 s. The induced EMF is:", opts: ["1 V", "10 V", "100 V", "0.1 V"], ans: 0, exp: "EMF = N × ΔΦ/Δt = N × A × ΔB/Δt = 200 × 10⁻³ × (0.5/0.1) = 200 × 10⁻³ × 5 = 1 V." },
    { q: "By Lenz's law, a bar magnet dropped through a vertical copper tube falls:", opts: ["Faster than free fall", "At the same rate as free fall", "Slower than free fall due to opposing eddy currents", "It cannot pass through copper"], ans: 2, exp: "As the magnet falls, it induces eddy currents in the copper tube. By Lenz's law, these currents create an upward magnetic force opposing the fall. The magnet falls slower than free fall — a striking demonstration of electromagnetic braking." },
    { q: "The magnetic field at the centre of a circular loop of radius 5 cm carrying 2 A is: (μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ T·m/A)", opts: ["8π × 10⁻⁶ T", "4π × 10⁻⁵ T", "2π × 10⁻⁵ T", "8π × 10⁻⁵ T"], ans: 0, exp: "B = μ₀I/(2R) = (4π×10⁻⁷ × 2)/(2 × 0.05) = 8π×10⁻⁷/0.1 = 8π×10⁻⁶ T ≈ 25.1 μT." },
    { q: "A transformer with 96% efficiency receives 1000 W at 200 V primary. If secondary voltage is 50 V, the secondary current is approximately:", opts: ["19.2 A", "1.92 A", "20 A", "5 A"], ans: 0, exp: "Output power = 0.96 × 1000 = 960 W. V_s = 50 V. I_s = P_out/V_s = 960/50 = 19.2 A. The 4% loss (40 W) appears as heat in core and copper windings." },
    { q: "In an AC generator, the output EMF is zero when the plane of the coil is:", opts: ["Parallel to the field (flux = 0, rate of change is maximum)", "Perpendicular to the field (flux is maximum, dΦ/dt = 0)", "At 45° to the field", "At 30° to the field"], ans: 1, exp: "EMF = 0 when dΦ/dt = 0. Flux Φ = NBAcosα is maximum when the coil plane is perpendicular to B (α = 0). At maximum flux, the rate of change is zero → EMF = 0." },
    { q: "A transformer steps 230 V down to 11.5 V. The turns ratio N_p : N_s is:", opts: ["20 : 1", "1 : 20", "0.05 : 1", "200 : 1"], ans: 0, exp: "N_p/N_s = V_p/V_s = 230/11.5 = 20. This 20:1 step-down transformer reduces voltage by 20×. For every 20 turns on the primary, there is 1 turn on the secondary." },
    { q: "A galvanometer deflects because of:", opts: ["Joule heating of the coil", "Torque on a current-carrying coil in a radial magnetic field (τ = NIAB)", "Electromagnetic induction only", "Electrostatic attraction"], ans: 1, exp: "Galvanometer: a current-carrying coil in a radial permanent magnetic field experiences torque τ = NIAB. The coil rotates against a hairspring until equilibrium. Deflection is directly proportional to current I." },
    { q: "The commutator in a DC motor reverses the current direction in the armature coil every half revolution in order to:", opts: ["Increase motor speed", "Ensure torque always acts in the same rotational direction for continuous rotation", "Convert mechanical to electrical energy", "Reduce friction"], ans: 1, exp: "Without the commutator, the torque would reverse direction every half turn (coil would oscillate). The split-ring commutator reverses current every half revolution, keeping the torque always in the same direction → continuous rotation." },
    { q: "Doubling the number of turns in a coil while keeping all other variables the same causes the induced EMF to:", opts: ["Halve", "Double", "Quadruple", "Stay the same"], ans: 1, exp: "EMF = N × dΦ/dt. EMF ∝ N directly. Doubling N doubles the EMF. This is the fundamental reason transformers and multi-turn coils in generators produce higher voltages than single-turn loops." },
    { q: "A DC motor draws 10 A from a 200 V supply. Its back EMF is 180 V. The armature resistance is:", opts: ["18 Ω", "2 Ω", "20 Ω", "0.1 Ω"], ans: 1, exp: "Net voltage driving current = supply − back EMF = 200 − 180 = 20 V. I = 10 A. R_armature = 20/10 = 2 Ω. At startup (back EMF = 0), current would be 200/2 = 100 A without a starter resistor." },
    { q: "Soft iron is preferred over steel for electromagnet cores because:", opts: ["Steel has higher electrical resistance", "Soft iron has low retentivity — easily magnetised and demagnetised when current switches", "Soft iron has higher retentivity", "Steel does not become magnetic"], ans: 1, exp: "Soft iron has low retentivity (little residual magnetism) and low coercivity → demagnetises immediately when current stops. Steel has high retentivity → stays magnetised (good for permanent magnets, bad for electromagnets that must switch rapidly)." },
    { q: "A conductor of length 1 m moves at 10 m/s perpendicular to a 0.5 T magnetic field. The induced (motional) EMF is:", opts: ["0.5 V", "5 V", "50 V", "0.05 V"], ans: 1, exp: "Motional EMF = BLv sinθ = 0.5 × 1 × 10 × sin90° = 5 V. This formula applies when a conductor moves through a magnetic field (basis of the DC generator/dynamo)." },
    { q: "The slip rings in an AC generator are used to:", opts: ["Convert AC to DC", "Maintain continuous electrical contact between rotating coil and stationary external circuit", "Reverse the current every half turn", "Cool the generator windings"], ans: 1, exp: "Slip rings are two continuous copper rings, each connected to one end of the rotating coil. Carbon brushes press on the rings, allowing current to flow in and out without interruption. They preserve the AC nature of the output (unlike commutator which produces DC)." },
    { q: "Laminated cores in transformers reduce eddy current losses because:", opts: ["They increase magnetic permeability of the core", "Thin sheets with insulation between them restrict eddy current paths → much smaller eddy currents → lower I²R loss", "They cool the core faster", "They increase the core cross-section"], ans: 1, exp: "Eddy current loss ∝ (thickness)². Laminations force eddy currents to flow only within thin individual sheets, creating much smaller current loops with much greater resistance → losses are reduced dramatically. Power transformers use grain-oriented silicon steel laminations." },
    { q: "The time period of one complete cycle of 50 Hz AC mains supply is:", opts: ["0.02 s", "0.05 s", "50 s", "0.002 s"], ans: 0, exp: "T = 1/f = 1/50 = 0.02 s = 20 ms. In each cycle, the voltage goes: 0 → +325 V (peak) → 0 → −325 V → 0. This happens 50 times per second." },
    { q: "Magnetic field lines around a current-carrying solenoid most closely resemble:", opts: ["Field lines around a straight wire", "Field lines of a bar magnet (uniform inside, dipole outside)", "Field of a point charge", "Field of a parallel-plate capacitor"], ans: 1, exp: "A solenoid behaves exactly like a bar magnet: uniform, parallel field inside along the axis; dipole field pattern outside. One end is North (anticlockwise current viewed from that end); other end is South." },
    { q: "The force on a proton (q = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C) moving at 10⁶ m/s perpendicular to a 1 T magnetic field is:", opts: ["1.6×10⁻¹³ N", "1.6×10⁻¹⁹ N", "1.6×10⁻¹² N", "1.6 N"], ans: 0, exp: "F = qvB sinθ = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ × 10⁶ × 1 × 1 = 1.6×10⁻¹³ N. This Lorentz force acts perpendicular to the velocity → causes circular motion. No work is done by the magnetic force on a moving charge." },
    { q: "In a DC motor at startup, a starter resistance is connected in series with the armature because:", opts: ["It increases back EMF quickly", "Back EMF = 0 at start; without starter R, full supply voltage across low armature resistance → dangerously large current", "It reduces rotation speed permanently", "It magnetises the field coil"], ans: 1, exp: "At startup, motor is stationary → back EMF = 0. With armature R = 2 Ω and 200 V supply: I = 200/2 = 100 A (extreme!). Starter adds series resistance to limit current. As speed builds, back EMF rises and starter resistance is gradually cut out." },
    { q: "Electromagnetic induction is the working principle of:", opts: ["Electric motor only", "Generators, transformers, and induction coils", "Only transformers", "Battery chargers but not generators"], ans: 1, exp: "Electromagnetic induction (Faraday's law) underlies: AC/DC generators (motion → electricity), transformers (mutual induction), induction cookers (eddy currents), metal detectors, and microphones. Faraday discovered this in 1831." },
    { q: "Rapidly thrusting a bar magnet into a solenoid produces a larger galvanometer deflection than a slow thrust because:", opts: ["Slow insertion creates more total flux change", "Rapid insertion means greater rate of flux change (dΦ/dt) → larger induced EMF → larger current", "The magnet strength increases with speed", "The number of coil turns increases"], ans: 1, exp: "EMF = N × ΔΦ/Δt. Rapid insertion reduces Δt → increases dΦ/dt → larger EMF → larger deflection. The TOTAL flux change may be the same, but the RATE determines the EMF and hence the deflection." },
    { q: "A transmission line of resistance 10 Ω carries 100 A. The power lost as heat is:", opts: ["1 kW", "10 kW", "100 kW", "1 MW"], ans: 2, exp: "P_loss = I²R = 100² × 10 = 10,000 × 10 = 100,000 W = 100 kW. This illustrates why high current in transmission lines wastes enormous power. Doubling voltage and halving current would reduce this loss by a factor of 4." },
    { q: "The right-hand grip rule for a solenoid: thumb points toward which pole when fingers curl in the direction of current flow?", opts: ["South pole", "North pole", "East direction always", "Centre of the solenoid"], ans: 1, exp: "Right-hand grip rule for solenoid: wrap right hand around solenoid with fingers pointing in the direction of current through the turns; the thumb points toward the North pole. Equivalently: the N-pole end has anticlockwise current when viewed from that end." },
    { q: "Maxwell's corkscrew rule relates the direction of current in a wire to the direction of:", opts: ["Force on a motor", "The surrounding magnetic field lines", "Induced EMF", "Transformer output"], ans: 1, exp: "Maxwell's corkscrew (right-hand screw) rule: if a right-handed screw advances in the direction of current flow, its rotation gives the direction of circular magnetic field lines around the conductor. It is equivalent to the right-hand thumb rule." },
    { q: "A transformer primary has 200 turns at 400 V. To step up to 8000 V, the secondary needs:", opts: ["4000 turns", "10 turns", "400 turns", "800 turns"], ans: 0, exp: "N_s = N_p × V_s/V_p = 200 × 8000/400 = 200 × 20 = 4000 turns. This 20:1 step-up transformer is typical of power station transmission transformers." },
    { q: "In Fleming's left-hand rule, the index (fore) finger represents:", opts: ["Current direction", "Force on the conductor", "Magnetic field direction (N → S)", "Voltage direction"], ans: 2, exp: "Fleming's LHR mnemonic FBI: Forefinger = B (magnetic field, N→S), Middle finger = I (current direction), Thumb = force/motion. Used for electric motors (force on current-carrying conductor in a field)." },
    { q: "A galvanometer (full-scale = 1 mA, internal resistance G = 50 Ω) is converted to an ammeter reading 0–10 A. The shunt resistance is approximately:", opts: ["0.005 Ω", "50,000 Ω", "0.5 Ω", "500 Ω"], ans: 0, exp: "I_g × G = (I − I_g) × S. 0.001 × 50 = (10 − 0.001) × S. 0.05 ≈ 10 × S → S = 0.005 Ω. Most of the 10 A passes through the shunt; only 1 mA through the galvanometer." },
    { q: "Lenz's law is ultimately a statement of conservation of:", opts: ["Charge", "Momentum", "Energy", "Mass"], ans: 2, exp: "Lenz's law: induced current opposes the change producing it. If induced current AIDED the change, it would amplify the flux further, creating unlimited energy. The opposition is required by energy conservation — you must do work to move the magnet." },
    { q: "The peak voltage of India's 230 V (RMS) AC supply is approximately:", opts: ["230 V", "325 V", "163 V", "460 V"], ans: 1, exp: "V_peak = V_rms × √2 = 230 × 1.414 ≈ 325 V. The RMS value (230 V) is the equivalent DC heating value. The instantaneous voltage actually swings between +325 V and −325 V, 50 times per second." },
    { q: "A conductor carrying current I is in a field B at 30° to B, experiencing force F. When the angle is changed to 60°, the new force is:", opts: ["F√3", "F/√3", "F", "2F"], ans: 0, exp: "F = BIL sinθ. F₁ = BIL sin30° = BIL/2. F₂ = BIL sin60° = BIL√3/2. Ratio F₂/F₁ = (√3/2)/(1/2) = √3. New force = F√3." },
    { q: "Short circuit in household wiring occurs when:", opts: ["Too many appliances are connected", "Live wire directly contacts neutral wire — near-zero resistance → very large fault current", "Earth wire is disconnected", "Voltage drops below 200 V"], ans: 1, exp: "Short circuit: direct contact between live and neutral (or live and earth) → path resistance ≈ 0 → I = V/R → extremely large current. Extreme I²R heating → fire within seconds. The fuse or MCB must rupture the circuit in milliseconds." },
    { q: "Hysteresis losses in a transformer core are minimised using a core with:", opts: ["Large B-H loop area (hard ferromagnet)", "Small B-H loop area (soft ferromagnet, e.g. silicon steel)", "Zero permeability core", "High coercivity material"], ans: 1, exp: "Hysteresis loss per AC cycle = area enclosed by the B-H (hysteresis) loop. Soft ferromagnets (silicon steel, ferrites) have narrow loops → low loss per cycle. Hard ferromagnets (steel, Alnico) have wide loops → high loss (good for permanent magnets, bad for transformer cores)." },
    { q: "An electric bell's electromagnet repeatedly attracts and releases the iron striker because:", opts: ["AC current reverses polarity", "The striker movement breaks the contact → current off → demagnetisation → spring restores contact → current on → cycle repeats", "The permanent magnet alternates polarity", "The bell uses inductive kicks only"], ans: 1, exp: "Bell mechanism: current → electromagnet → attracts striker+hammer → hammer strikes gong → armature breaks contact screw → current interrupted → electromagnet demagnetises → spring restores armature → contact remade → repeats. Self-interrupting circuit." },
    { q: "A rectangular coil (50 turns, area 0.02 m²) rotates at 50 Hz in a 0.1 T field. The peak (maximum) EMF is:", opts: ["π V", "2π V", "10π V", "20π V"], ans: 2, exp: "E_peak = NBAω = NBAω = 50 × 0.1 × 0.02 × (2π×50) = 50 × 0.1 × 0.02 × 100π = 0.1 × 100π = 10π V ≈ 31.4 V." },
    { q: "The device that converts alternating current (AC) to direct current (DC) is called a:", opts: ["Inverter", "Rectifier (uses diodes)", "Transformer", "Amplifier"], ans: 1, exp: "Rectifier: uses diodes to allow current in only one direction. Half-wave rectifier (1 diode) passes only half cycles. Full-wave bridge rectifier (4 diodes) rectifies both half cycles → smoother DC. Transformers change AC voltage; inverters convert DC to AC." },
    { q: "A proton moves along the central axis of a solenoid (parallel to B). The magnetic force on it is:", opts: ["Zero (v ∥ B, sinθ = 0)", "1.6×10⁻¹⁷ N perpendicular to axis", "Directed radially outward", "Directed along B"], ans: 0, exp: "F = qvB sinθ. Since the proton moves parallel to B (along the axis), θ = 0° → sin0° = 0 → F = 0. Magnetic force is only non-zero when the charge has a velocity component perpendicular to B." },
    { q: "When a magnet is pulled away from a solenoid, the induced current creates a face of the solenoid that:", opts: ["Repels the magnet (same pole faces the magnet)", "Attracts the magnet (opposite pole faces the magnet, opposing withdrawal)", "Has no pole", "Alternates polarity rapidly"], ans: 1, exp: "Lenz's law: removing the magnet reduces flux → induced current tries to MAINTAIN flux → solenoid face nearest to receding magnet becomes the OPPOSITE pole (attracting the magnet, resisting its removal). Work must be done against this attractive force." },
    { q: "Iron cores are replaced with ferrite cores in high-frequency (kHz–MHz) switching power supply transformers because:", opts: ["Iron is too heavy", "Eddy current loss ∝ f² — at high frequency, iron core losses are enormous; ferrite (ceramic, high resistivity) has negligible eddy current losses", "Iron is non-magnetic at high frequency", "Ferrite has higher saturation flux density"], ans: 1, exp: "Eddy current power loss ∝ f². At 100 kHz, iron core eddy losses are (100,000/50)² = 4×10⁶ times larger than at 50 Hz — completely unusable. Ferrite ceramics have resistivity 10⁶–10⁸ × higher than iron → negligible eddy currents even at high frequency." },
    { q: "Increasing the rotational speed of an AC generator while keeping all other parameters the same causes:", opts: ["Decreased peak EMF and frequency", "Both peak EMF and frequency to increase", "Only frequency to increase (not peak EMF)", "Only peak EMF to increase (not frequency)"], ans: 1, exp: "E_peak = NBAω and f = ω/(2π). Both are directly proportional to ω (angular speed). Increasing rotation speed increases BOTH the peak EMF and the output frequency simultaneously." },
    { q: "A moving-coil galvanometer: 100 turns, area 2 cm² = 2×10⁻⁴ m², radial field B = 0.1 T. The torque when 1 mA flows is:", opts: ["2×10⁻⁶ N·m", "0.02 N·m", "2×10⁻⁵ N·m", "2×10⁻³ N·m"], ans: 0, exp: "τ = NIAB = 100 × 0.001 × 2×10⁻⁴ × 0.1 = 10⁻¹ × 2×10⁻⁵ = 2×10⁻⁶ N·m. The spring constant k (N·m/rad) determines full-scale deflection. Smaller k → more sensitive galvanometer." },
    { q: "Fleming's right-hand rule is used to determine the direction of induced current in a:", opts: ["DC motor", "Electromagnetic generator (conductor moving in a magnetic field)", "Transformer", "Electromagnet"], ans: 1, exp: "Fleming's RHR (dynamo rule): thumb = direction of conductor's motion, forefinger = magnetic field direction, middle finger = direction of induced current. Used exclusively for generators (electromagnetic induction). Fleming's LHR is for motors." },
    { q: "Overloading a household circuit is dangerous primarily because it causes:", opts: ["Voltage to drop to zero", "Excess current → I²Rt heating in wires → insulation melts → fire or electrocution risk", "Magnetic fields to weaken significantly", "EMF of the mains supply to increase"], ans: 1, exp: "Overloading: current exceeds the wiring's rated capacity. I²Rt heating causes wiring insulation to soften, melt, or ignite. Melted insulation exposes live conductors → fire or shock. Fuses and MCBs protect against this by interrupting the circuit." },
    { q: "A step-down transformer has a turns ratio of 10:1. If the primary current is 2 A, the secondary current (ideal transformer) is:", opts: ["20 A", "0.2 A", "2 A", "10 A"], ans: 0, exp: "For an ideal transformer, V_p I_p = V_s I_s. Since N_p/N_s = 10, V_p/V_s = 10, so I_s/I_p = V_p/V_s = 10 → I_s = 10 × 2 = 20 A. Step-down in voltage means step-up in current." },
    { q: "Two identical bar magnets are placed end-to-end (N–N) and brought near a solenoid connected to a galvanometer. Compared to using one magnet, using two in this arrangement produces:", opts: ["Same deflection", "Double the deflection (double the flux change rate)", "Zero deflection because fields cancel", "Half the deflection"], ans: 1, exp: "Two identical magnets N–N facing the same coil doubles the total flux change entering the solenoid per unit time → doubles dΦ/dt → doubles induced EMF → doubles galvanometer deflection. Flux is additive here since both contribute in the same direction." },
    { q: "The efficiency of a transformer is given by (P_out/P_in)×100%. For a transformer receiving 5000 W with 4750 W output, the efficiency and losses are:", opts: ["95%, 250 W losses", "90%, 500 W losses", "75%, 1250 W losses", "99%, 50 W losses"], ans: 0, exp: "Efficiency = (P_out/P_in) × 100 = (4750/5000) × 100 = 95%. Losses = P_in − P_out = 5000 − 4750 = 250 W (as heat in core and copper windings). Modern large power transformers typically achieve 97–99.5% efficiency." }
  ]
,

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════
     CH 13 — OUR ENVIRONMENT
  ══════════════════════════════════════ */
  13: [
    { q: "An ecosystem includes:", opts: ["Only living organisms", "Only non-living (abiotic) components", "Both biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) components interacting", "Only the food chain"], ans: 2, exp: "Ecosystem: community of biotic (organisms) + abiotic (air, water, soil, temperature, light) components interacting as a system. Examples: forest, pond, grassland, ocean." },
    { q: "Producers in an ecosystem are:", opts: ["Animals that eat plants", "Organisms that eat producers", "Organisms that produce their own food via photosynthesis (green plants, algae, cyanobacteria)", "Decomposers"], ans: 2, exp: "Producers (autotrophs): make organic food from inorganic materials using solar energy (photosynthesis). They form the base of every food chain and capture solar energy into the ecosystem." },
    { q: "Primary consumers (herbivores) feed on:", opts: ["Other animals (carnivores)", "Producers (plants)", "Decomposers", "Tertiary consumers"], ans: 1, exp: "Primary consumers = herbivores: feed directly on producers (plants). Examples: rabbit, deer, caterpillar, grasshopper. They are at the second trophic level." },
    { q: "The 10% law (energy rule) in ecology states that when energy is transferred from one trophic level to the next:", opts: ["100% is transferred", "50% is transferred", "Only about 10% of the energy is available (90% lost as heat, movement, and metabolic processes)", "10% is lost and 90% transferred"], ans: 2, exp: "Lindemann's 10% rule: only ~10% of energy at one trophic level transfers to the next (90% lost to respiration, heat, waste). Therefore, food chains rarely exceed 4-5 levels (insufficient energy at top)." },
    { q: "Decomposers in an ecosystem include:", opts: ["Herbivores", "Carnivores", "Bacteria and fungi that break down dead organic matter", "Plants"], ans: 2, exp: "Decomposers (saprotrophic microorganisms): bacteria and fungi that break down dead organisms and organic waste → simple inorganic substances (CO₂, H₂O, minerals) → returned to environment. Essential for nutrient cycling." },
    { q: "A food web differs from a food chain in that it:", opts: ["Has fewer organisms", "Is simpler", "Shows multiple interconnected feeding relationships in an ecosystem (more realistic)", "Only shows producers"], ans: 2, exp: "Food chain: linear sequence (Producer → Consumer₁ → Consumer₂). Food web: complex network of interconnected food chains. More realistic — most organisms eat multiple food sources." },
    { q: "The trophic level of an organism in a food chain indicates:", opts: ["Its body size", "Its age", "Its feeding level/position (producers=1st, herbivores=2nd, etc.)", "Its reproductive rate"], ans: 2, exp: "Trophic level: feeding position in the food chain. 1st = producers, 2nd = primary consumers, 3rd = secondary consumers, 4th = tertiary consumers. Energy decreases at each higher level." },
    { q: "Biodegradable waste is material that:", opts: ["Cannot be broken down by microorganisms", "Can be broken down by biological organisms (microorganisms) into natural substances", "Is only plastic", "Is hazardous always"], ans: 1, exp: "Biodegradable: organic materials decomposed by microbes over time (food scraps, paper, cotton, wood). Non-biodegradable: materials that persist (plastic, glass, heavy metals, DDT). Biodegradable = preferred for environment." },
    { q: "The ozone layer is located in the:", opts: ["Troposphere", "Mesosphere", "Stratosphere (15-35 km altitude)", "Thermosphere"], ans: 2, exp: "Ozone (O₃) layer: stratosphere, ~15-35 km altitude (peak ~25 km). Absorbs 97-99% of harmful UV-B and UV-C radiation from the sun, protecting life on Earth." },
    { q: "Ozone depletion is primarily caused by:", opts: ["CO₂", "Methane", "Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)", "Water vapour"], ans: 2, exp: "CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons) from refrigerants, aerosols, and foam. In stratosphere, UV breaks CFC → releases Cl radicals → catalytic chain reaction destroying O₃: Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂ (each Cl can destroy 100,000 O₃ molecules)." },
    { q: "The Montreal Protocol (1987) was signed to:", opts: ["Reduce CO₂ emissions", "Phase out CFCs and other ozone-depleting substances", "Protect forests", "Reduce nuclear weapons"], ans: 1, exp: "Montreal Protocol: international treaty to phase out production and use of ozone-depleting substances (CFCs, HCFCs, halons). Most successful environmental treaty — ozone layer now slowly recovering." },
    { q: "Biological magnification (biomagnification) means that the concentration of harmful chemicals (e.g., DDT) in organisms:", opts: ["Decreases at higher trophic levels", "Stays constant through food chain", "Increases at higher trophic levels (accumulates in fatty tissues, not excreted)", "Is highest in producers"], ans: 2, exp: "Biomagnification: chemicals like DDT/mercury accumulate in fatty tissues and are not metabolised. As organisms eat many organisms below them, concentration multiplies. Highest concentration in top predators (eagles, humans)." },
    { q: "DDT (dichloro-diphenyl-trichloroethane) was banned primarily because:", opts: ["It was too expensive", "It accumulated in food chains (biomagnification) causing population crashes in birds (eggshell thinning)", "It didn't kill insects", "It evaporated too quickly"], ans: 1, exp: "DDT biomagnification: concentrated at top of food chains. In birds: DDT → thinner eggshells → egg breakage → population crash. Rachel Carson's \"Silent Spring\" (1962) exposed this. Banned in most countries." },
    { q: "What is the main cause of the \"greenhouse effect\" on Earth?", opts: ["Oxygen in the atmosphere", "Greenhouse gases (CO₂, CH₄, N₂O, H₂O) trapping infrared radiation emitted by Earth", "The ozone layer", "Nitrogen gas"], ans: 1, exp: "Greenhouse effect: solar radiation enters → Earth absorbs and re-emits IR. Greenhouse gases (CO₂, CH₄, N₂O, H₂O) absorb and re-emit IR → warm Earth's surface. Essential for life; enhanced effect causes global warming." },
    { q: "Which gas is primarily responsible for enhanced greenhouse effect (global warming)?", opts: ["Oxygen", "Nitrogen", "Carbon dioxide (CO₂) from fossil fuel burning", "Argon"], ans: 2, exp: "CO₂ is the primary driver of anthropogenic (human-caused) enhanced greenhouse effect. Sources: burning fossil fuels, deforestation. CO₂ levels have risen from ~280 ppm (pre-industrial) to >420 ppm today." },
    { q: "Eutrophication is a process where:", opts: ["Water becomes pure", "Excess nutrients (N, P from fertilisers) cause algal bloom → oxygen depletion → death of aquatic life", "Soil nutrients are lost", "Forests grow too densely"], ans: 1, exp: "Eutrophication: excess nitrogen/phosphorus (agricultural runoff) → algal bloom → algae die → bacterial decomposition uses O₂ → hypoxia (O₂ depletion) → fish and other aquatic organisms die. Creates \"dead zones\"." },
    { q: "Acid rain is caused by:", opts: ["CO₂ dissolving in rainwater", "SO₂ and NOₓ from fossil fuel combustion dissolving in water → H₂SO₄ and HNO₃", "Ozone reacting with water", "Chlorine gas"], ans: 1, exp: "Acid rain: SO₂ (from coal/oil burning) + H₂O → H₂SO₃/H₂SO₄. NOₓ (from car engines) + H₂O → HNO₃. pH of acid rain < 5.6. Damages forests, lakes, buildings, soil pH." },
    { q: "Which of the following is a renewable source of energy?", opts: ["Coal", "Petroleum", "Solar energy (replenished continuously)", "Natural gas"], ans: 2, exp: "Renewable energy: solar, wind, hydropower, tidal, geothermal, biomass — naturally replenished on human timescales. Non-renewable: coal, petroleum, natural gas (formed over millions of years → finite)." },
    { q: "Chipko movement in India was a protest to:", opts: ["Stop water pollution", "Protect trees from being cut (literally \"hugging trees\" to prevent deforestation) in the Himalayas", "Promote solar energy", "Stop mining"], ans: 1, exp: "Chipko movement (1973, Garhwal Himalayas): villagers hugged trees to prevent commercial logging. Led by Sundarlal Bahuguna and Gaura Devi. \"Chipko\" = \"to hug\" in Hindi. Successful grassroots environmental movement." },
    { q: "The Ganga Action Plan was launched to:", opts: ["Build dams on the Ganga", "Clean up pollution in the Ganga river", "Divert the Ganga to other states", "Study Ganga ecosystem"], ans: 1, exp: "Ganga Action Plan (1985, Government of India): aimed at reducing pollution in the Ganga river (industrial effluents, sewage, religious waste). Considered largely unsuccessful due to inadequate implementation." },
    { q: "The food chain: Grass → Rabbit → Fox → Tiger. Grass is at the first trophic level. Tiger is at the:", opts: ["2nd trophic level", "3rd trophic level", "4th trophic level", "5th trophic level"], ans: 2, exp: "Trophic levels: Grass (1st) → Rabbit (2nd) → Fox (3rd) → Tiger (4th). Tiger is a tertiary consumer at the 4th trophic level. Very little energy reaches this level (~0.1% of original solar energy in grass)." },
    { q: "Sustainable development means meeting the needs of the present generation without:", opts: ["Using any resources", "Compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs", "Using renewable energy", "Developing industry"], ans: 1, exp: "Sustainable development definition (Brundtland Commission, 1987): \"development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.\" Balance: economic growth + environmental protection." },
    { q: "Soil pollution is caused by excessive use of:", opts: ["Water", "Pesticides and chemical fertilisers", "Sunlight", "Organic compost"], ans: 1, exp: "Soil pollution: excess fertilisers (N, P) → eutrophication of water bodies when washed away. Pesticides → kill non-target organisms, bioaccumulate. Also: heavy metals, plastic waste, industrial effluents." },
    { q: "The term \"ecosystem services\" refers to:", opts: ["Economic services provided to ecosystems", "Benefits that humans derive from ecosystems (clean air, water, pollination, food, climate regulation)", "Tourism services", "Agricultural services only"], ans: 1, exp: "Ecosystem services: benefits ecosystems provide to humans. Types: provisioning (food, water, timber), regulating (climate, water purification, flood control), cultural (recreation, spiritual), supporting (soil formation, nutrient cycling)." },
    { q: "Carbon dioxide (CO₂) is a greenhouse gas. Which process removes CO₂ from the atmosphere?", opts: ["Respiration", "Decomposition", "Photosynthesis (and also ocean absorption)", "Combustion"], ans: 2, exp: "Photosynthesis removes CO₂: 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂. Forests are major carbon sinks. Oceans absorb ~30% of human CO₂ emissions (but causes ocean acidification). Deforestation reduces carbon sequestration." },
    { q: "Which ecosystem has the highest biodiversity (greatest number of species per unit area)?", opts: ["Tundra", "Desert", "Tropical rainforest", "Temperate grassland"], ans: 2, exp: "Tropical rainforests (Amazon, Congo, Southeast Asia): ~50% of world's species in ~6% of Earth's land area. High year-round temperature, rainfall, and sunlight → enormous biodiversity. Called \"lungs of the Earth.\"" },
    { q: "Ozone in the troposphere (ground level) is:", opts: ["Protective like stratospheric ozone", "A pollutant (causes respiratory problems) formed from NOₓ + VOCs + sunlight", "Beneficial for plants", "Part of the ozone layer"], ans: 1, exp: "Tropospheric ozone: a pollutant! Forms via photochemical reactions of NOₓ + VOCs + sunlight. Causes: respiratory irritation, reduced crop yields, smog formation (urban areas). Unlike stratospheric ozone, it is harmful." },
    { q: "The water cycle (hydrological cycle) is driven by:", opts: ["Gravity alone", "Solar energy (evaporation) and gravity (precipitation and runoff)", "Wind alone", "Volcanic activity"], ans: 1, exp: "Water cycle: Solar energy → evaporation of ocean/lake water → water vapour rises → cools → condensation → clouds → precipitation (rain/snow) → runoff → rivers → back to ocean. Gravity drives the return flow." },
    { q: "Deforestation leads to all of the following EXCEPT:", opts: ["Soil erosion", "Increased CO₂ in atmosphere", "Loss of biodiversity", "Increased oxygen production"], ans: 3, exp: "Deforestation: trees removed → less photosynthesis → LESS oxygen (not more). Also: soil erosion (no roots), CO₂ increases (decomposition + fewer trees), biodiversity loss, flooding, climate change." },
    { q: "The nitrogen cycle involves which process that converts atmospheric N₂ into usable form (ammonia)?", opts: ["Nitrification", "Denitrification", "Nitrogen fixation by Rhizobium and lightning", "Decomposition"], ans: 2, exp: "Nitrogen fixation: N₂ → NH₃/NH₄⁺. By: Rhizobium in legume root nodules, free-living Azotobacter, and lightning (creates NOₓ). This converts inert N₂ into forms plants can absorb (ammonium, nitrate)." },
    { q: "Biogas (gobar gas) is produced by:", opts: ["Burning wood", "Anaerobic fermentation of organic waste (mainly CO₂ + CH₄) by methanogenic bacteria", "Solar energy", "Wind energy"], ans: 1, exp: "Biogas: anaerobic decomposition of organic matter (cattle dung, sewage, food waste) by methanogenic archaea → ~60% methane (CH₄) + 40% CO₂. Used for cooking and electricity. Slurry used as fertiliser." },
    { q: "Which of the following is an example of a non-biodegradable pollutant that biomagnifies?", opts: ["Orange peel", "Cotton cloth", "DDT, mercury, PCBs", "Food waste"], ans: 2, exp: "DDT, mercury (methylmercury), PCBs (polychlorinated biphenyls): non-biodegradable, fat-soluble → not excreted → accumulate in fatty tissue → biomagnify through food chains. Found in highest concentrations in apex predators." },
    { q: "The concept of \"reduce, reuse, recycle\" (3Rs) in waste management prioritises:", opts: ["Recycling above all", "Reduce (generate less waste) first, then Reuse, then Recycle as last resort", "Reuse above reduce", "All three equally"], ans: 1, exp: "3R hierarchy: Reduce (least waste generation) is most important (prevents problem). Reuse (use items multiple times) is second. Recycle (processing materials into new products) is third. Waste-to-energy and landfill are least preferred." },
    { q: "UV radiation reaching Earth's surface increases due to ozone depletion. This can cause:", opts: ["Increased crop yields", "Skin cancer, cataracts, immune suppression, and reduced phytoplankton productivity", "Cooler temperatures", "Decreased biodiversity indirectly but no direct effects"], ans: 1, exp: "UV-B (280-315 nm) effects of ozone depletion: skin cancer (melanoma), cataracts (lens damage), immune system suppression, damage to DNA. Also reduces phytoplankton productivity (base of marine food chain)." },
    { q: "Which human activity is the LARGEST contributor to greenhouse gas emissions globally?", opts: ["Agriculture", "Deforestation", "Burning fossil fuels (coal, oil, gas) for energy and transport", "Waste decomposition"], ans: 2, exp: "Burning fossil fuels accounts for ~75% of global greenhouse gas emissions (mainly CO₂). This powers electricity generation, industry, transportation, and heating. Agriculture (~11%), deforestation (~6%)." },
    { q: "Biomass as an energy source refers to:", opts: ["Solar energy stored in cells", "Organic matter from plants and animals used as fuel", "Fossil fuels", "Nuclear energy"], ans: 1, exp: "Biomass: organic material (wood, crop residues, dung, algae) that stores solar energy via photosynthesis. When burned or fermented → releases energy. Renewable but generates CO₂ (considered carbon-neutral if sustainably managed)." },
    { q: "The removal of top soil by wind or water is called:", opts: ["Eutrophication", "Leaching", "Soil erosion", "Salinisation"], ans: 2, exp: "Soil erosion: removal of topsoil by wind, water, or ice. Topsoil contains humus and nutrients → vital for agriculture. Deforestation, overgrazing, poor farming practices accelerate soil erosion. Prevention: afforestation, contour farming, windbreaks." },
    { q: "Waterborne diseases like cholera, typhoid, and dysentery are caused by drinking water contaminated with:", opts: ["Industrial chemicals", "Faecal pathogens (bacteria, viruses, protozoa from sewage)", "Excess fluoride", "Heavy metals only"], ans: 1, exp: "Waterborne diseases: water contaminated with human/animal faeces contains pathogens. Cholera (Vibrio cholerae), typhoid (Salmonella typhi), hepatitis A (virus), dysentery (bacteria/amoeba). Prevention: water treatment, sanitation." },
    { q: "Ozone is represented by the formula O₃. It is formed in the stratosphere by:", opts: ["O₂ + CO₂ reaction", "O₂ absorbing UV radiation → O atoms → O + O₂ → O₃", "Water vapour decomposing", "Nitrogen reacting with oxygen"], ans: 1, exp: "Ozone formation: O₂ + UV (< 242 nm) → 2O (atomic oxygen). Then: O + O₂ + M → O₃ + M (M = third molecule). Also: ozone absorbs UV (280-320 nm) → O₂ + O → cycle continues. Net: absorbs UV, protecting Earth." },
    { q: "The Paris Agreement (2015) aims to limit global temperature rise to:", opts: ["1°C above pre-industrial levels", "1.5-2°C above pre-industrial levels", "3°C above pre-industrial levels", "5°C above pre-industrial levels"], ans: 1, exp: "Paris Agreement (2015, 196 parties): limit global average temperature rise to well below 2°C, preferably 1.5°C, above pre-industrial levels. Requires reducing greenhouse gas emissions to net zero by ~2050." },
    { q: "Which of the following represents a complete food chain in a pond ecosystem?", opts: ["Algae → Phytoplankton → Fish → Eagle", "Phytoplankton → Zooplankton → Small fish → Large fish", "Fish → Frog → Algae → Sun", "Water → Algae → Sun → Fish"], ans: 1, exp: "Correct food chain: Phytoplankton (producers) → Zooplankton (primary consumers) → Small fish (secondary consumers) → Large fish/Bird (tertiary consumers). Energy flows from producer upward through the chain." },
    { q: "A keystone species is one that:", opts: ["Is most abundant in the ecosystem", "Has a disproportionately large effect on ecosystem structure relative to its abundance", "Is at the bottom of the food chain", "Produces the most offspring"], ans: 1, exp: "Keystone species: has outsized ecological impact disproportionate to its numbers. Removing it causes ecosystem collapse. Examples: sea otters (control sea urchin → protect kelp forests), wolves in Yellowstone, elephants (create water holes)." },
    { q: "Integrated pest management (IPM) involves:", opts: ["Using only chemical pesticides", "Using maximum pesticide doses", "Combining biological, cultural, and minimal chemical controls to manage pests sustainably", "Eliminating all insects"], ans: 2, exp: "IPM: uses multiple strategies — biological control (natural predators), crop rotation, resistant varieties, traps, and minimal targeted pesticides only when necessary. Reduces environmental impact and resistance development." },
    { q: "Which process in the carbon cycle converts organic carbon back to CO₂?", opts: ["Photosynthesis", "Nitrogen fixation", "Respiration and decomposition", "Transpiration"], ans: 2, exp: "Carbon cycle: CO₂ → organic carbon (photosynthesis). Organic carbon → CO₂: respiration (all organisms), decomposition (decomposers), combustion (fossil fuels, fires). Maintains carbon balance in the atmosphere." },
    { q: "The ecological pyramid of energy is always:", opts: ["Inverted", "Upright (energy always decreases at higher trophic levels due to 10% rule)", "Oval shaped", "Sometimes inverted"], ans: 1, exp: "Pyramid of energy is always upright: energy decreases at each successive trophic level (10% law). Unlike pyramids of numbers or biomass, the energy pyramid can never be inverted." },
    { q: "Which of the following is a characteristic of a stable ecosystem?", opts: ["Maximum species diversity with interconnected food webs", "Very few species with simple food chains", "No decomposers", "Only producers and one consumer"], ans: 0, exp: "Stable ecosystem: high biodiversity with complex, interconnected food webs → more resilient. If one species is lost, others can fill its role. Simple ecosystems (low diversity) are fragile and easily disrupted." },
    { q: "The United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) was established to:", opts: ["Manage global trade", "Coordinate UN's environmental activities and address environmental challenges", "Regulate nuclear energy", "Manage world fisheries only"], ans: 1, exp: "UNEP (established 1972, Nairobi, Kenya): coordinates environmental work within the UN, monitors global environment, facilitates environmental treaties (including Montreal Protocol, Basel Convention)." },
    { q: "Bioaccumulation refers to the accumulation of substances (like pollutants) in an organism due to:", opts: ["Fast metabolism", "Intake rate exceeding elimination rate — substance builds up over the organism's lifetime", "Excretion of substances", "Active pumping out of cells"], ans: 1, exp: "Bioaccumulation: pollutant (e.g., mercury, DDT) is taken in faster than it can be excreted or metabolised → builds up in tissue over lifetime. Differs from biomagnification (which is increase across trophic levels)." },
    { q: "The ozone hole is most pronounced over which region?", opts: ["Equatorial Africa", "The Arctic", "Antarctica (especially in southern spring: August–October)", "Europe"], ans: 2, exp: "Antarctic ozone hole: polar stratospheric clouds (PSCs) form in the extreme cold → CFCs accumulate on ice crystals → when sunlight returns in spring, catalytic destruction of O₃ is rapid. Largest and most severe over Antarctica." },
    { q: "A completely terrestrial ecosystem that is self-sustaining would require at minimum:", opts: ["Only producers", "Only consumers", "Producers + decomposers (or producers + consumers + decomposers)", "Only abiotic factors"], ans: 2, exp: "Minimum ecosystem: producers (create organic matter via photosynthesis) + decomposers (recycle nutrients back to inorganic form). Without decomposers, nutrients remain locked in dead organic matter → producers starve." },
    { q: "In the food chain Grass → Grasshopper → Frog → Snake → Eagle, if 10,000 J of energy is available at the producer level, how many joules reach the eagle (5th trophic level)?", opts: ["10 J", "1 J", "0.1 J", "100 J"], ans: 1, exp: "Applying the 10% law at each trophic level transfer: Grass (1st) = 10,000 J → Grasshopper (2nd) = 1,000 J → Frog (3rd) = 100 J → Snake (4th) = 10 J → Eagle (5th) = 1 J. Eagle at the 5th trophic level receives 10,000 × (0.1)⁴ = 1 J. Only 0.01% of the original energy reaches the top predator — this is why food chains rarely exceed 4-5 levels." },
    { q: "Which statement about the pyramid of numbers in a grassland ecosystem is CORRECT?", opts: ["It is always upright", "It may be inverted if a single large tree supports many insects", "It is always inverted", "It is always a perfect triangle"], ans: 1, exp: "Pyramid of numbers can be inverted: in a forest, one tree (1 organism) may support thousands of insects (caterpillars), which support fewer birds. The pyramid of energy is ALWAYS upright, but numbers and biomass can invert." },
    { q: "An ecological pyramid of biomass is inverted in which type of ecosystem?", opts: ["Grassland", "Tropical rainforest", "Ocean/marine ecosystem (phytoplankton biomass < zooplankton biomass at a given time)", "Desert"], ans: 2, exp: "In marine ecosystems, phytoplankton (producers) reproduce very rapidly but are consumed quickly — their standing biomass at any instant is less than the biomass of zooplankton consuming them. The pyramid of biomass is inverted. This does NOT violate the energy pyramid (production rate, not standing crop, matters)." },
    { q: "CFCs are harmful to the ozone layer because each Cl atom can destroy approximately how many ozone molecules before being deactivated?", opts: ["100", "1,000", "100,000", "1,000,000"], ans: 2, exp: "Each Cl radical acts catalytically: Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂; ClO + O → Cl + O₂. The Cl is regenerated and can destroy ~100,000 O₃ molecules before it is eventually removed from the stratosphere by reactions forming HCl or ClONO₂. This catalytic amplification makes CFCs so dangerous at tiny concentrations." },
    { q: "The term \"eutrophication\" literally means \"well-nourished.\" In ecological context, which sequence correctly describes cultural eutrophication?", opts: ["Algal bloom → excess nutrients → oxygen depletion → fish die", "Excess nutrients (N, P) → algal bloom → algae die → bacterial decomposition consumes O₂ → hypoxia → fish die", "Fish die → algal bloom → nutrient increase", "Oxygen depletion → algal bloom → nutrient excess"], ans: 1, exp: "Cultural eutrophication (caused by human activities): agricultural runoff/sewage provides excess N and P → rapid algal bloom covers the surface (blocks light) → algae die off → heterotrophic bacteria decompose algae, consuming dissolved O₂ → BOD (biological oxygen demand) spikes → hypoxic/anoxic conditions → fish and invertebrate die-off. Creates dead zones (e.g., Gulf of Mexico)." },
    { q: "Biomagnification of DDT was first scientifically documented by studying which bird species, whose eggshells became too thin to support the weight of incubating adults?", opts: ["Sparrows", "Pigeons", "Bald eagles and peregrine falcons", "Ostriches"], ans: 2, exp: "DDT is metabolised by birds to DDE, which inhibits calcium ATPase involved in eggshell formation. Bald eagles and peregrine falcons, at the top of food chains, accumulated the highest concentrations. Their populations crashed dramatically in the 1950s-70s, triggering the ban on DDT in many countries. Rachel Carson documented this in \"Silent Spring\" (1962)." },
    { q: "The Montreal Protocol is considered the most successful international environmental treaty. Which of the following best explains why?", opts: ["It had strong penalties for non-compliance", "It achieved near-universal ratification and CFCs have been phased out, leading to measurable ozone layer recovery", "It was financially supported by CFCs manufacturers", "It banned all greenhouse gases"], ans: 1, exp: "Montreal Protocol (1987): ratified by 197 parties (universal) — the only UN treaty with this distinction. Production of CFCs reduced by >99%. Stratospheric chlorine peaked around 2000 and is now slowly declining. The ozone hole has been slowly shrinking. Estimated to prevent 2 million cases of skin cancer per year by 2030." },
    { q: "In the nitrogen cycle, which process converts nitrates (NO₃⁻) back to atmospheric nitrogen (N₂)?", opts: ["Nitrification", "Ammonification", "Nitrogen fixation", "Denitrification by anaerobic bacteria (Pseudomonas, Thiobacillus)"], ans: 3, exp: "Denitrification: anaerobic bacteria (Pseudomonas denitrificans, Thiobacillus) in waterlogged/oxygen-depleted soils convert NO₃⁻ → NO₂⁻ → N₂O → N₂ gas, which returns to atmosphere. Nitrification is the reverse (NH₄⁺ → NO₃⁻). Denitrification closes the nitrogen cycle." },
    { q: "Acid rain with pH 4 is how many times more acidic than normal rain with pH 5.6?", opts: ["1.6 times", "16 times", "About 40 times", "About 400 times"], ans: 2, exp: "pH is a logarithmic scale. pH 5.6 → [H⁺] = 10⁻⁵·⁶ ≈ 2.5×10⁻⁶ M. pH 4.0 → [H⁺] = 10⁻⁴ = 1×10⁻⁴ M. Ratio: 1×10⁻⁴ / 2.5×10⁻⁶ = 40. So pH 4 rain is approximately 40 times more acidic than normal rain. Note: some estimates range from 25-40× depending on the exact reference pH used." },
    { q: "The water cycle moves approximately how much freshwater from the ocean to land each year (in km³)?", opts: ["1,000 km³", "500,000 km³", "About 40,000 km³ (net transfer by precipitation over land minus evapotranspiration)", "1,000,000 km³"], ans: 2, exp: "Global water cycle: ~502,800 km³ of water evaporates from oceans annually; ~458,000 km³ falls back on ocean. ~62,800 km³ evaporates from land; ~106,000 km³ falls on land as precipitation. Net transfer from ocean to land: ~40,000 km³/year, which runs off through rivers back to ocean. This drives the global freshwater supply." },
    { q: "Phytoplankton in the ocean are critical to the global carbon cycle because they:", opts: ["Consume CO₂ via chemosynthesis", "Fix approximately 50% of global CO₂ through photosynthesis (biological pump — organic C sinks to deep ocean)", "Release CO₂ through respiration only", "Form the base of terrestrial food chains"], ans: 1, exp: "Phytoplankton (microscopic algae, cyanobacteria) perform roughly half of Earth's photosynthesis despite covering only ocean surfaces. They fix ~50 Pg C/year. When they die, organic carbon sinks to the deep ocean (biological pump), sequestering CO₂ for hundreds to thousands of years. Ozone depletion reduces phytoplankton productivity by increasing UV-B at ocean surface." },
    { q: "The concept of \"trophic cascade\" means:", opts: ["Energy flows up through trophic levels", "Changes at one trophic level (esp. top predator removal) cascade down to alter lower levels dramatically", "Food chains cascade into food webs", "Carbon cascades through the ecosystem"], ans: 1, exp: "Trophic cascade: removing a top predator causes a cascade. Classic example: removal of wolves from Yellowstone (1926) → deer population exploded → overgrazing → riverside vegetation lost → stream erosion → changed river channels. Reintroduction of wolves (1995) reversed these changes. Also: sea otter removal → sea urchin boom → kelp forest destruction." },
    { q: "Polar stratospheric clouds (PSCs) accelerate ozone depletion over Antarctica because:", opts: ["They block sunlight", "Ice crystal surfaces allow heterogeneous reactions that release Cl₂ from reservoir species (HCl, ClONO₂) — more reactive than gas-phase reactions", "PSCs emit Cl₂ directly", "PSCs warm the stratosphere and increase CFC breakdown"], ans: 1, exp: "PSC mechanism: Antarctic winter temperatures drop below −78°C → PSCs form. On ice surfaces: ClONO₂ + HCl → Cl₂ + HNO₃. Cl₂ is stored in ice; when spring UV arrives, Cl₂ → 2Cl radicals → explosive O₃ destruction. Heterogeneous chemistry on ice surfaces is ~1000× more efficient than gas-phase reactions. The Arctic has fewer/smaller PSCs, hence smaller ozone hole." },
    { q: "Which greenhouse gas has the highest Global Warming Potential (GWP) over a 100-year timeframe?", opts: ["CO₂ (GWP = 1, the reference)", "CH₄ (GWP ≈ 28-34)", "N₂O (GWP ≈ 265-298)", "SF₆ (sulfur hexafluoride, GWP ≈ 23,500)"], ans: 3, exp: "GWP measures heat-trapping relative to CO₂ over 100 years. SF₆ (used in electrical switchgear): GWP ≈ 23,500. N₂O: 265-298. CH₄: 28-34. CO₂ = reference (GWP 1). However, CO₂ is the most significant driver of climate change because of its enormous volume of emissions. SF₆ is emitted in tiny quantities." },
    { q: "The concept of \"carbon footprint\" refers to:", opts: ["The amount of carbon in soil", "Total greenhouse gas emissions (in CO₂ equivalent) directly and indirectly associated with an activity or product", "Footprints left by carbon particles", "Carbon content of fossil fuels"], ans: 1, exp: "Carbon footprint: total GHG emissions (CO₂, CH₄, N₂O, etc., expressed as CO₂-equivalent) caused directly or indirectly by an individual, organisation, event, or product. Includes direct emissions (car exhaust) and indirect (manufacturing emissions embedded in goods). Average Indian: ~2 tonnes CO₂/year; average American: ~16 tonnes." },
    { q: "In the Serengeti ecosystem, wildebeest migrations are driven by rainfall and grass growth. When wildebeest populations were reduced by rinderpest disease, what happened to vegetation?", opts: ["Grass disappeared", "Trees and shrubs invaded grassland (without grazing pressure), changing the ecosystem type", "More predators appeared", "Rainfall increased"], ans: 1, exp: "This is a documented trophic cascade / bottom-up effect. When wildebeest (grazers) are reduced by disease, grass grows tall → accumulates as dry fuel → fires become more frequent and intense → trees unable to establish → paradoxically: fewer wildebeest = more fires. When rinderpest was eliminated in the 1960s, wildebeest recovered → less dry grass accumulation → fewer fires → more trees. Shows complex ecosystem interactions." },
    { q: "The Ramsar Convention (1971) relates to the conservation of:", opts: ["Tropical rainforests", "Wetlands (marshes, swamps, mangroves, river deltas, lakes, peatlands)", "Desert ecosystems", "Mountain ecosystems"], ans: 1, exp: "Ramsar Convention: international treaty for conservation and sustainable use of wetlands. Named after Ramsar, Iran, where it was signed. Wetlands provide ecosystem services: water purification, flood control, carbon storage (peatlands store 2× more C than all forests), nurseries for fish, habitat for migratory birds." },
    { q: "Which statement about decomposers and nutrient cycling is MOST accurate?", opts: ["Decomposers only break down plant material", "Without decomposers, nutrients would remain locked in dead organic matter and ecosystems would eventually run out of available nutrients", "Decomposers produce oxygen as a by-product", "Decomposers are found only in soil"], ans: 1, exp: "Without decomposers (bacteria, fungi), dead organic matter (leaves, carcasses, excreta) would accumulate. Carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus, sulfur, and other nutrients would remain trapped in organic form and be unavailable to producers. Nutrient cycles would halt. Decomposers mineralize organic matter → inorganic nutrients → re-absorbed by plants → cycle continues." },
    { q: "In 1984, Bhopal gas tragedy killed thousands due to release of:", opts: ["CO₂ and CO", "Methyl isocyanate (MIC) — an extremely toxic industrial chemical", "Chlorine gas", "Sulfur dioxide"], ans: 1, exp: "Bhopal disaster (December 1984, Union Carbide pesticide plant): ~40 tonnes of methyl isocyanate (CH₃NCO) gas leaked. MIC reacts with moisture in lungs → methylamine + CO₂ → severe pulmonary edema. >3,700 immediate deaths; estimated 15,000-20,000 total deaths from exposure. Largest industrial disaster in history. Led to stricter chemical plant regulations worldwide." },
    { q: "Primary productivity differs from secondary productivity in that:", opts: ["Primary productivity is by consumers; secondary by producers", "Primary productivity (GPP) = rate of photosynthesis by producers; secondary productivity = rate of biomass production by consumers", "They are identical concepts", "Primary productivity occurs at night; secondary during day"], ans: 1, exp: "Gross Primary Productivity (GPP): total rate of photosynthesis (including plant respiration). Net Primary Productivity (NPP) = GPP − plant respiration = energy available to herbivores. Secondary productivity: rate of energy storage by consumers. NPP of tropical forests ≈ 2000 g C/m²/year; deserts ≈ 3 g C/m²/year." },
    { q: "The role of mangrove forests in coastal ecosystems includes all EXCEPT:", opts: ["Nursery habitat for marine fish", "Coastal erosion protection (wave attenuation)", "Increasing ocean salinity", "Carbon storage (blue carbon) at high rates per unit area"], ans: 2, exp: "Mangroves do NOT increase ocean salinity. They are highly productive ecosystems: nurseries for 80% of tropical fish species, protect coastlines from waves/storms (demonstrated in 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami — areas with mangroves had significantly less damage), store carbon in sediments (up to 4× more than tropical forests per unit area — \"blue carbon\")." },
    { q: "Global warming at 2°C above pre-industrial levels is predicted to cause all of the following EXCEPT:", opts: ["Coral reef bleaching and widespread reef loss", "Sea level rise from ice melt and thermal expansion", "Increased frequency of extreme weather events", "Immediate total extinction of all polar species"], ans: 3, exp: "At 2°C: coral reefs decline 70-90%; sea level rises 30-60 cm by 2100; more intense storms, droughts, heatwaves. However, polar species face habitat loss and population pressure but do NOT immediately go extinct — some may adapt, migrate, or persist in refugia. Mass extinction risk increases significantly at higher temperatures." },
    { q: "The concept of \"ecological succession\" refers to:", opts: ["The feeding sequence in a food chain", "The directional change in community composition over time (from pioneer species to climax community)", "The annual migration of animals", "The reproductive succession of generations"], ans: 1, exp: "Ecological succession: predictable, directional change in species composition over time. Primary succession: begins on bare rock (lichens → mosses → ferns → shrubs → trees). Secondary succession: after disturbance (fire, clearing) — faster because soil remains. Climax community: stable final state in equilibrium with climate. Lakes undergo hydrosere succession → eventually terrestrial ecosystem." },
    { q: "The ozone layer absorbs which specific types of ultraviolet radiation?", opts: ["UV-A (320-400 nm) only", "UV-B (280-315 nm) and UV-C (< 280 nm)", "All of the infrared spectrum", "Visible light only"], ans: 1, exp: "Stratospheric ozone absorbs UV-B (95% filtered) and virtually all UV-C (extremely harmful, absorbed by O₂ and O₃ together). UV-A passes through largely unaffected (lower energy). UV-C would be lethal to all surface life without the ozone shield. UV-B causes DNA damage (thymine dimers) → skin cancer, cataracts." },
    { q: "What distinguishes a \"closed\" ecosystem from an \"open\" ecosystem?", opts: ["Closed ecosystems have no biotic components", "In a closed ecosystem, no matter enters or leaves (energy can); open ecosystems exchange both energy and matter with surroundings", "Closed ecosystems have no predators", "Open ecosystems have no producers"], ans: 1, exp: "Thermodynamics in ecology: most natural ecosystems are open systems — they exchange both energy and matter with their surroundings. A sealed terrarium can approximate a closed system (no matter exchange, only energy via light). True closed ecosystems are extremely rare; even sealed biosphere experiments had leakages." },
    { q: "In the context of global warming, \"albedo\" refers to:", opts: ["The rate of photosynthesis", "The reflectivity of a surface (ice/snow has high albedo ~0.8; ocean has low albedo ~0.06)", "The absorption of infrared radiation", "The emission of CO₂"], ans: 1, exp: "Albedo: fraction of solar radiation reflected by a surface (0=perfect absorber, 1=perfect reflector). Arctic sea ice: albedo ~0.8 (reflects 80% of sunlight). When ice melts → dark ocean exposed (albedo ~0.06) → absorbs more heat → more melting. This \"ice-albedo feedback\" is a major positive feedback loop amplifying Arctic warming." },
    { q: "The International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List categorises species as \"Critically Endangered\" when the probability of extinction within 10 years or 3 generations is at least:", opts: ["10%", "25%", "50% (IUCN criterion E)", "Over 80% (incorrect — 50% applies to CR)"], ans: 2, exp: "IUCN Red List Criteria E for Critically Endangered: quantitative analysis shows probability of extinction ≥50% within 10 years or 3 generations. For Endangered: ≥20% in 20 years. For Vulnerable: ≥10% in 100 years. India's CR species include Bengal Florican, Forest Owlet, and Pygmy Hog." },
    { q: "Which correctly describes the difference between food chain efficiency and ecological efficiency?", opts: ["They are the same concept", "Food chain efficiency = energy transfer between two consecutive trophic levels; ecological efficiency = ratio of production at one level to production at level below (typically ~10%)", "Ecological efficiency is always 50%", "Food chain efficiency is always higher"], ans: 1, exp: "Ecological efficiency (Lindemann efficiency): production at trophic level n / production at trophic level n-1, typically ~10% (range 5-20%). Assimilation efficiency = energy assimilated / energy ingested. Trophic level transfer efficiency = net production passed up / net production of previous level. These distinctions matter in ecosystem modeling." },
    { q: "Silent Valley National Park controversy in Kerala (1970s-80s) was significant because it led to the protection of:", opts: ["A coral reef ecosystem", "One of India's last pristine tropical rainforests with a silent river (no rapids)", "A desert ecosystem", "A mangrove forest"], ans: 1, exp: "Silent Valley controversy: Kerala government proposed a hydroelectric dam on the Kunthipuzha river that would have submerged Silent Valley — one of India's last untouched tropical rainforests with exceptional biodiversity (including lion-tailed macaque). The Save Silent Valley movement (1970s) and scientific advocacy led to cancellation of the dam. Silent Valley National Park established 1984." },
    { q: "The process by which dead organic matter is converted into humus (stable organic matter in soil) is called:", opts: ["Nitrification", "Humification", "Calcification", "Podsolisation"], ans: 1, exp: "Humification: complex process by which dead organic matter (leaves, wood, carcasses) is broken down and transformed by microorganisms, fungi, and invertebrates into humus — dark, stable, complex organic material. Humus improves soil structure, water-holding capacity, and provides slow-release nutrients. Earthworms are crucial in this process." },
    { q: "Thermal pollution of rivers occurs when power plant cooling water discharge:", opts: ["Increases dissolved oxygen", "Raises water temperature → decreases dissolved O₂ → kills cold-water fish → promotes algal growth", "Adds chemical pollutants", "Lowers river temperature"], ans: 1, exp: "Thermal pollution: warm water discharged from cooling towers of thermal/nuclear power plants → river temperature rises → O₂ solubility decreases (warm water holds less O₂) → fish (especially salmonids/trout that need cold oxygenated water) die → algae and warm-water opportunists thrive. Changes species composition fundamentally." },
    { q: "In the carbon cycle, the largest carbon reservoir on Earth is:", opts: ["Atmosphere (CO₂)", "Terrestrial vegetation (forests)", "Ocean dissolved inorganic carbon (DIC)", "Fossil fuels (coal, oil, gas)"], ans: 2, exp: "Earth's carbon reservoirs: Lithosphere (rocks, fossil fuels) = largest overall (~80 million Pg). In the \"active\" cycle: Ocean DIC ≈ 38,000 Pg C (largest active reservoir). Atmosphere ≈ 860 Pg C. Soil organic carbon ≈ 1,500 Pg. Vegetation ≈ 560 Pg C. The ocean's huge capacity to absorb CO₂ is a critical buffer but causes ocean acidification." },
    { q: "Which pair of abiotic factors MOST strongly determine the type of terrestrial biome in a region?", opts: ["Soil type and elevation", "Annual mean temperature and annual precipitation (Whittaker biome diagram)", "Wind speed and soil pH", "Latitude and altitude only"], ans: 1, exp: "Whittaker's biome classification uses mean annual temperature (x-axis) vs mean annual precipitation (y-axis). Desert: low precipitation regardless of temperature. Tropical rainforest: high T + high rainfall. Tundra: very low T. Temperate grassland: moderate T, low-moderate precipitation. Temperature and rainfall are the primary determinants of biome type." },
    { q: "E-waste (electronic waste) is particularly hazardous because it contains:", opts: ["Mainly biodegradable plastics", "Toxic heavy metals (Pb, Cd, Hg, Cr) and flame retardants (PBBs, PBDEs) that leach into groundwater", "Only CO₂ when burned", "Only recyclable metals like Fe and Al"], ans: 1, exp: "E-waste: discarded electronics (phones, computers, TVs). Contains lead (solder), cadmium (batteries, pigments), mercury (LCDs), chromium (VI), and brominated flame retardants. When improperly recycled (heating, acid baths in informal sector), these toxins contaminate soil and water, causing neurotoxicity, kidney damage, cancer. India is 3rd largest e-waste producer globally." },
    { q: "The 10% law of energy transfer was proposed by:", opts: ["Charles Elton", "Raymond Lindemann (1942) based on Cedar Bog Lake, Minnesota", "Eugene Odum", "Tansley"], ans: 1, exp: "Raymond Lindemann (1942): studied energy flow in Cedar Bog Lake and proposed that ~10% of energy at one trophic level is available to the next (Lindemann's trophic-dynamic aspect of ecology). Earlier, Charles Elton described the food pyramid concept (1927). A.G. Tansley coined the term \"ecosystem\" (1935)." },
    { q: "Soil erosion is MOST effectively prevented by which combination of strategies?", opts: ["Removing vegetation and ploughing deeply", "Afforestation, contour ploughing, terracing on slopes, and cover crops", "Irrigation and monoculture farming", "Deep borewell drilling"], ans: 1, exp: "Soil erosion prevention: (1) Afforestation — roots bind soil, canopy breaks raindrop impact. (2) Contour ploughing — furrows follow slope contours, trapping water. (3) Terracing — reduces slope gradient. (4) Cover crops — keep soil covered year-round. (5) Windbreaks/shelterbelts — reduce wind erosion. India loses ~5.3 billion tonnes of soil/year to erosion." },
    { q: "The term \"albedo feedback loop\" in climate science describes a situation where melting Arctic ice:", opts: ["Reflects more sunlight, cooling the Arctic", "Exposes dark ocean water that absorbs more heat → more warming → more ice melt → more dark ocean → accelerating warming (positive feedback)", "Causes precipitation to increase, rebuilding ice", "Has no effect on global temperature"], ans: 1, exp: "Ice-albedo positive feedback: Arctic ice melts (due to warming) → dark ocean surface exposed → ocean absorbs ~6× more solar energy than ice → more warming → more melting. This amplifies climate change in the Arctic. The Arctic is warming at 3-4× the global average rate (Arctic amplification) partly because of this feedback." },
    { q: "In the water cycle, transpiration by plants represents approximately what fraction of evapotranspiration over land?", opts: ["Less than 10%", "About 10-20%", "About 50-65%", "Nearly 100%"], ans: 2, exp: "Global estimates: transpiration by vegetation contributes approximately 50-65% of total evapotranspiration over land (the remainder being soil evaporation, interception, and open water evaporation). Forests are particularly important: Amazon rainforest generates its own rainfall through transpiration (biotic pump) — deforestation disrupts regional rainfall patterns." },
    { q: "Which is NOT a consequence of acid rain on terrestrial ecosystems?", opts: ["Leaching of Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺ from soil → nutrient depletion of trees", "Release of Al³⁺ from soil minerals → toxic to tree roots and aquatic invertebrates", "Increased soil fertility due to sulphate deposition", "Damage to waxy cuticle of leaves → increased water loss"], ans: 2, exp: "Acid rain effects: (i) Leaches Ca, Mg, K from soil → nutrient-poor soil → forest decline. (ii) Releases toxic Al³⁺ from minerals → damages fine roots and gill membranes of fish. (iii) Damages leaf cuticles → stress. (iv) Acidifies lakes → kills aquatic life. It does NOT increase soil fertility — excess sulfate as SO₄²⁻ may marginally increase sulfur (a plant nutrient) but acid rain's harmful effects far outweigh any minor fertilization." },
    { q: "Project Tiger (1973) was launched in India primarily to:", opts: ["Increase tiger hunting sustainably", "Conserve the Bengal tiger and its habitat through establishing tiger reserves with core and buffer zones", "Create a national tiger census only", "Export tigers for international zoos"], ans: 1, exp: "Project Tiger (1973): launched under PM Indira Gandhi. India's tiger population had fallen from ~40,000 (1900) to ~1,827 (1972 census). Established tiger reserves with strict protection of core areas and regulated buffer zones. By 2022: India's tiger population reached 3,167 — over 70% of wild tigers in the world. Major conservation success." },
    { q: "The concept of \"edge effect\" in ecology refers to:", opts: ["The extinction of species at geographic edges", "Increased species diversity at the boundary (ecotone) between two different habitats", "Reduction of prey at forest edges", "Decreased predation at forest boundaries"], ans: 1, exp: "Edge effect: where two different habitats meet (ecotone), species diversity is often higher than in either adjacent habitat alone, because: (i) species of both habitats are present, plus (ii) edge-specialist species. Ecotones: forest-grassland boundary, mangrove-ocean interface. However, human-created edges (fragmentation) often increase invasive species and predation rates, harming forest-interior specialists." },
    { q: "Secondary succession after a forest fire would begin with:", opts: ["Climax tree species immediately", "Pioneer species such as annual weeds, grasses, and herbs that colonize bare but intact soil", "Lichens (like primary succession)", "Large shrubs and trees only"], ans: 1, exp: "Secondary succession: begins where an existing community was destroyed but soil remains intact (fire, flood, agriculture abandonment). Faster than primary succession because soil, seed bank, and nutrients are present. Sequence: annual weeds → perennial grasses/herbs → shrubs → pioneer trees → climax forest. Full recovery may take 100-200 years depending on climate." },
    { q: "Which best explains why decomposition is faster in tropical ecosystems than in tundra?", opts: ["Tropical decomposers are more numerous by species only", "Warm, moist conditions in tropics → high enzyme reaction rates → faster microbial metabolism; tundra: cold, frozen ground → negligible decomposer activity, organic matter accumulates as peat", "Tropical soils have no minerals", "Tundra has no organic matter"], ans: 1, exp: "Temperature controls enzyme activity (Q₁₀ effect): metabolic rates roughly double per 10°C rise. Tropical soils: high T (~25-30°C), moisture → rapid decomposition → nutrients quickly recycled → nutrient-poor soil (nutrients held in biomass). Tundra/boreal: cold, waterlogged (permafrost) → slow decomposition → peat accumulation (peatlands store massive amounts of organic carbon — more than tropical forests combined)." },
    { q: "Which human activity directly increases atmospheric methane (CH₄) concentrations?", opts: ["Deforestation only", "Rice cultivation, livestock (enteric fermentation), and landfill decomposition", "Solar panel installation", "Wind energy generation"], ans: 1, exp: "CH₄ sources: (i) Rice paddies — anaerobic paddy soil → methanogenic archaea → CH₄ (2nd largest agricultural GHG source). (ii) Livestock — rumen fermentation (enteric fermentation) by cattle, sheep → belch CH₄. (iii) Landfills — anaerobic decomposition of organic waste. (iv) Natural gas leaks. (v) Wetlands (natural source). CH₄ GWP = 28-34 over 100 years." },
    { q: "The \"missing sink\" in the carbon cycle refers to:", opts: ["Carbon lost to space", "Carbon that appears to be removed from the atmosphere by terrestrial ecosystems (forests, soil) beyond what oceans account for — mechanism still being studied", "CO₂ emissions that are unmeasured", "Carbon in the ocean not counted"], ans: 1, exp: "Carbon budget: humans emit ~10 Pg C/year. Atmosphere absorbs ~5 Pg (measurable rise in CO₂). Oceans absorb ~2.4 Pg. That leaves ~2.6 Pg \"missing.\" This missing sink is attributed to Northern hemisphere forests (especially regrowing forests in North America, Europe, and Siberia) and enhanced photosynthesis from CO₂ fertilization. This \"terrestrial carbon sink\" is being studied as a potentially fragile buffer." },
    { q: "The \"Safe Planet\" concept involves defining planetary boundaries. The 9 planetary boundaries include all EXCEPT:", opts: ["Climate change", "Ozone depletion", "Biodiversity loss", "GDP growth rates"], ans: 3, exp: "Planetary Boundaries framework (Rockström et al., 2009): 9 boundaries within which humanity can operate safely: climate change, biosphere integrity (biodiversity loss), land-system change, freshwater use, biogeochemical flows (N, P), ocean acidification, atmospheric aerosol loading, ozone depletion, novel entities (chemical pollution). GDP is an economic measure, NOT a planetary boundary." },
    { q: "In ecology, \"r-strategists\" vs \"K-strategists\" differ in that:", opts: ["r-strategists are larger, longer-lived; K-strategists are small, short-lived", "r-strategists: small, rapid reproduction, many offspring, short lifespan (weeds, insects); K-strategists: large, slow reproduction, few offspring, long parental care (elephants, whales)", "Both have the same reproductive strategy", "K-strategists are found only in deserts"], ans: 1, exp: "r vs K selection (MacArthur-Wilson): r-strategists (r = intrinsic rate of increase): rapidly colonise disturbed/unpredictable environments, many small offspring, little parental care (bacteria, annual weeds, mice, most insects). K-strategists (K = carrying capacity): stable environments, few offspring with heavy investment, long lifespan (elephants, humans, whales, eagles). Most organisms fall on a continuum between these extremes." },
    { q: "The Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) established which international financial mechanism to support conservation in biodiversity-rich but financially poor countries?", opts: ["World Bank", "Global Environment Facility (GEF)", "International Monetary Fund", "UNEP fund"], ans: 1, exp: "Global Environment Facility (GEF): established alongside CBD at Rio Earth Summit (1992). Provides grants to developing countries for environmental projects: biodiversity, climate, land degradation, international waters, chemicals. Has funded over $21 billion in grants since inception. CBD also established the Nagoya Protocol (2010) on access and benefit sharing of genetic resources." },
    { q: "Vermicomposting uses earthworms to decompose organic matter. Compared to conventional composting, vermicompost:", opts: ["Takes longer and produces lower quality compost", "Produces nutrient-richer compost faster, with higher microbial activity and better soil structure", "Produces same quality compost", "Requires higher temperature"], ans: 1, exp: "Vermicompost (produced by Eisenia fetida — red wigglers): (i) higher nutrient content than conventional compost. (ii) Rich in plant growth hormones (humic acids, auxin-like compounds). (iii) Better aeration and water-holding capacity when added to soil. (iv) Process occurs at ambient temperature (unlike thermophilic composting). Used in organic farming as \"black gold.\"" },
    { q: "The greenhouse effect is essential for life on Earth because without it, Earth's average surface temperature would be approximately:", opts: ["0°C", "−18°C (Earth would be mostly frozen)", "−100°C", "+5°C"], ans: 1, exp: "Natural greenhouse effect: without any greenhouse gases, Earth's average temperature would be approximately −18°C (Stefan-Boltzmann calculation for a bare rocky planet at Earth's distance from the sun). The natural greenhouse effect raises this to the actual average of +15°C — a difference of 33°C. This natural warming is essential for life. The problem is enhanced greenhouse effect from human emissions." }
  ]
};
